    "chunk_text": "The United States celebrated\u00a0 250 years of freedom on the Fourth of July in spectacular style\n\nJuly 4, 2026 marked the 250th anniversary of the Declaration of Independence by the Second Continental Congress in 1776, announcing that the 13 American colonies free from British rule.\n\nThe first July 4 celebrations were hardly as elaborate as those of the present day, the main theme being mock funerals for King George III, who was King of England at the time.\n\nJuly 4 was also called Freedom Day, a gross misnomer. President Barack Obama brought the significance of the date into perspective, while a self-educated slave described eloquently what July 4 meant to the African-American, then, and even to some extent, today.\n\nPresident Obama said, \u201cOut of the fire and steel of a revolution, a declaration was made that we are all created equal, endowed by our creator with certain inalienable rights, and in the newly independent United States, there will be no kings or lords, no serfs or subjects, but only citizens, each of us free to pursue our own version of happiness and determine our collective fate through an elected representative government.\n\n\u201cThe success of this experiment was never a given. The founders fell terribly short of the Declaration\u2019s promise, leaving slavery intact, allowing the states to restrict the franchise to white men\u201d.\n\nBut they did have the foresight to provide a framework to make the union and democracy more perfect as the years went by. Sadly, a framework that is proving to be too fragile to protect citizens\u2019 rights and their democracy in the hands of the most evil white supremacists from the good old days of slavery, which, according to them, did not exist. People in power with the mentality of the founders, to whom the words \u201cWe the people\u201d encompassed white men only."
