    "md5": "ff153b35a2d592981fbed100825ee520",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Erdo\u011fan\u2019s New Republic\n\nSoon after dominating global headlines by hosting one of NATO\u2019s most consequential summits in recent years, Recep Tayyip Erdo\u00f0an once again ensured that the closing images were unmistakably Turkish. Each visiting leader left Ankara carrying an engraved G\u00fcm\u00fc\u00feay .357 Magnum revolver produced by the state-owned defence manufacturer MKE, complete with six cartridges, personalised stationery and a biography celebrating Erdo\u00f0an\u2019s political journey. Erdo\u00f0an, however, had already achieved what mattered. The gift signalled that Turkey no longer wished to be seen merely as NATO\u2019s south-eastern flank. It sought recognition as an independent military power capable of manufacturing weapons, shaping alliances and dictating conversations.\n\nDays later, another story placed Erdo\u00f0an at the centre of regional geopolitics. An investigation published by Haaretz reported claims by the former Mossad chief, David Barnea, that a conversation between Donald Trump and Erdo\u00f0an influenced American reluctance to support a proposed Kurdish operation during the recent conflict with Iran. The report also included sharp disagreement from former senior Israeli intelligence officials, one dismissing Barnea\u2019s explanation as a convenient attempt to explain operational failure. What matters is that Turkey\u2019s president now features in discussions of covert regional strategy not as an observer but as a leader whose views are regarded as consequential by allies and adversaries alike.\n\nTen years after the failed coup attempt of 15 July 2016, Erdo\u00f0an stands as one of the most durable political figures of the twenty-first century. Few leaders have experienced a military uprising, survived it, transformed it into the defining narrative of national politics and then used its aftermath to redesign the architecture of the state. It emerged with a fundamentally different political order."
