    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "The relationship between prosperity and political dominance became increasingly reciprocal. Electoral victories strengthened executive authority. Executive authority facilitated greater influence over institutions. Institutions, in turn, reinforced electoral advantage. Scholars increasingly describe Turkey as a competitive authoritarian system rather than a conventional liberal democracy. Elections remain meaningful and opposition parties continue to govern major municipalities, yet the political playing field has become progressively more uneven.\n\nHis foreign policy has displayed similar adaptability. Erdo\u00f0an purchased Russian S-400 missile systems while supplying armed drones to Ukraine, criticised Israel in uncompromising language while maintaining channels of communication with Washington, repaired relations with Gulf monarchies after years of confrontation, and expanded Turkey\u2019s defence exports, military footprint and diplomatic influence across Africa, the Caucasus and the eastern Mediterranean. Few contemporary leaders have demonstrated such willingness to occupy apparently contradictory positions when doing so advances national leverage.\n\nHis rapport with Donald Trump reflects the same pragmatism. During Trump\u2019s first presidency, sanctions threatened Turkey over the detention of an American pastor and Ankara\u2019s acquisition of Russian missile systems. Yet both leaders repeatedly emphasised their personal relationship, allowing dialogue to continue even during periods of acute disagreement. If the recent  Haaretz investigation accurately reflects internal Israeli discussions, Erdo\u00f0an\u2019s conversations with Trump remain sufficiently significant to feature in assessments of regional security planning. Turkey\u2019s president is increasingly regarded as a strategic actor whose calculations cannot easily be ignored."
