    "chunk_size_bytes": 1456,
    "chunk_text": "In the early 1960s, travelling by CTB double-decker bus from Panadura to an A-Level class in Dehiwala, I watched a lean, fair-skinned gentleman, neatly dressed in a white shirt, red tie, and a white drill coat draped over one arm boarding the bus. A fellow passenger recognised him at once and offered his seat: \u201cWadivenna, Merrile Sahodaraya, ada parlimenthu wageyi.\u201d\n\nAn ideologue to the end, Fernando broke away with fellow revolutionaries when the LSSP leadership entered into coalition with \u201ccapitalist\u201d Sirimavo Bandaranaike in 1964, and duly lost the Moratuwa seat to his former party colleague Wimalasiri de Mel in 1965.\n\nHenry Peiris, MP for Panadura from 1947 to 1952, campaigned across his electorate on the only vehicle he owned, a push bicycle. As a sitting MP, he travelled to Parliament by public bus, since the CTB did not yet exist. He was the father of the veteran journalist, the late Janadasa Peiris, and in the 1947 election he defeated the Deputy Speaker of the State Council, Sir Susantha de Fonseka, who stood for the UNP and came from one of Panadura\u2019s wealthiest landowning families.\n\nSir Susantha toured his constituency, then stretching into what is now Bandaragama and Kesbewa, on horseback, his chauffeur-driven limousine trailing empty behind him. The clatter of hooves on the road would wake the neighbourhood children, myself among them, then just four years old, and we would run to the garden to watch him pass."
