    "chunk_size_bytes": 1864,
    "chunk_text": "India\u2019s cockroach protesters set to march to parliament\n\nBBC - Thousands of students, parents, activists and political leaders are set to march to the Indian parliament following a call from the Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) for reforms in the education system.\n\nThe protest venue in Delhi is thronging with protesters and several hundred police and paramilitary personnel have been deployed.\n\nChaos erupted on Saturday when activist and educator Sonam Wangchuk, who was on day 21 of his hunger-strike in CJP's support, was forcibly taken to hospital. He continues fasting there.\n\nThe Delhi Police have said that protesters don't have permission for the march but a CJP spokesperson told the BBC that they will go ahead anyway.\n\nFounded by Abhijeet Dipke, the CJP emerged in May as an online satirical movement to protest against paper leaks and irregularities in exams and has gained a huge following. They have been demanding that Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan take responsibility and resign.\n\nPradhan has dismissed the CJP and its supporters as \"the B-team of disruptive elements\". The government of Prime Minister Narendra Modi has not engaged with the protesters yet.\n\nOn Monday morning, the atmosphere at the protest site - Jantar Mantar, a 300-year-old observatory in Delhi - was lively and spirited. The pouring rain did not dampen the spirit of hundreds of protesters, many of whom were shouting slogans demanding Pradhan's resignation.\n\nMany protesters were waving the national flag while others held plastic roses as a peace offering.\n\nHundreds of policemen had been deployed and all roads leading to the venue have been sealed to traffic. Security has been stepped up across the city with barricades on a number of main streets.\n\nPolicemen were seen checking vehicles on roads leading to the area and asking questions to drivers and passengers."
