    "md5": "e0111fea5265df67533f4cc1d11a74df",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "One area where the US still gets higher ratings than China is \u201cpersonal freedoms.\u201d More say that the US government respects the personal freedoms of its people. Still, the gap has been narrowing \u2013 driven by \u201clarge drops\u201d in the shares saying the US government respects personal freedoms.\n\nAs China and the US compete for influence around the world, Pew also examined how people in six Latin American countries see their foreign policy roles. People in these countries previously tended to see the US as a more reliable partner than China, but are now split.\n\nThe Americans\u2019 nearest neighbours \u2013 Canadians and Mexicans \u2013 also view China more positively than the US. A majority of Canadians (57%) had a positive view of the US in 2023, while 14% viewed China positively. In 2025, Canadians were equally favourable towards the US and China. But now, more Canadians have a favourable view of China (44%) than of the US (33%). The same pattern is seen in a number of other countries.\n\nBe that as it may, confidence in both Trump and Xi is generally low, though people in many of the 36 countries surveyed view Xi more favourably. In European countries, for example, neither leader gets a majority-positive rating, but views of Xi tend to be more favourable than the views of Trump.\n\nIn Germany, Greece, Italy, the Netherlands, Spain, Sweden and the United Kingdom, Xi leads Trump by double digits, though his highest favourability rating is just 37% (in the UK).\n\nIn Mexico around a third (35%) say that the Chinese government respects its people\u2019s personal freedoms, while 20% say the same about the US government.\n\n80% of Israelis say the US respects the freedoms of its people, while just 15% say the same of China. Japan shows another sizable difference (61% vs. 6%).\u00a0 In Bangladesh, Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, and the West Bank and East Jerusalem, more say China respects personal freedoms than the US."
  },
