    "chunk_size_bytes": 1726,
    "chunk_text": "Speaking on the government\u2019s drug prevention strategy at an event in Kurunegala on Sunday, Wijepala said authorities would adopt a different approach towards drug users and traffickers. He said that simply arresting and imprisoning drug users would not provide a lasting solution, and that users should instead be treated as individuals requiring medical assistance and directed towards rehabilitation programmes.\n\nHowever, he said strict legal action would be taken against drug traffickers and distributors who contribute to the spread of narcotics.\n\nThe Minister also highlighted the long-standing links between the drug trade, organised crime and political influence, stating that narcotics trafficking had continued for decades with the backing of certain political elements. He claimed that the present administration had succeeded in breaking the connections between politicians, drug networks and underworld groups.\n\nWijepala said a nationwide campaign has been launched to combat the drug menace, including the establishment of anti-drug councils at national and district levels, along with regional committees. He said that more than 14,000 public security committees have also been formed to work with communities and help protect young people from substance abuse.\n\nRejecting criticism and allegations made against the government\u2019s efforts, the Minister said the administration would not retreat from its anti-drug measures. He stressed that the law would be enforced equally against all individuals regardless of their position or influence.\n\nWijepala said that eliminating drugs should not become a matter of political division and warned that no person would be allowed to take the law into their own hands."
