    "chunk_text": "The 40m-long (131ft) vessel was built in 1939, according to a listing on VesselFinder.\n\nA survivor, Elena Moonsammy, told local news site Kiskadee Watch that the ferry had tilted after being hit by a large wave, with water quickly rushing in.\n\nMoonsammy said she had survived by clinging to a box floating amidst the debris.\n\nShe had tried to hold on to the hand of her two-year-old child but they had been separated.\n\nMoonsammy said she had managed to save a woman, whom she urged to also hold on to the floating box.\n\nAt least 15 children were among those rescued. Small private boats were key in pulling at least 17 survivors from the sea.\n\nThe French army sent 12 divers, two doctors and a mechanic from nearby French Guiana to aid the search-and-rescue operation.\n\nGuyana's Health Minister, Dr Frank Anthony, said three of the survivors remained in hospital with minor injuries but the others had already been discharged.\n\nMost, he added, had cuts and bruises from clinging to debris for hours."
