    "chunk_size_bytes": 1781,
    "chunk_text": "Pakistan steps in as US-Iran war widens and Hormuz shipping stalls\n\nINDIA TODAY - Diplomatic efforts intensified on Tuesday to revive the collapsed interim agreement between Iran and the United States, even as both sides continued attacks for a 10th day of renewed fighting. Iranian Interior Minister Eskandar Momeni travelled to Pakistan, which has been trying to mediate between the two countries, but it was not immediately clear what fresh arrangement could emerge to end the war.\n\nThe conflict has also deepened disruption in the Strait of Hormuz, where shipping has largely stalled and both sides have targeted civilian infrastructure used by millions across the region. Iran kept up attacks on US allies, with Bahrain and Kuwait issuing alerts over incoming projectiles, while higher oil prices added to the wider impact of the fighting.\n\nMomeni met Pakistan's army chief, Field Marshal Asim Munir, according to two officials who spoke on condition of anonymity because the talks were held behind closed doors. He later met Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif.\n\nPakistan's political leadership and Munir, who serves as both the chief of army staff and the head of the defence forces, played a key role in brokering last month's interim agreement between the US and Iran. Islamabad has since been trying to persuade both sides to return to talks and resolve outstanding issues under the June memorandum of understanding.\n\nNo details of Momeni's meetings were immediately released. The visit came as Tehran continued attacks on Gulf Arab allies of the US.\n\nUS Secretary of State Marco Rubio said Washington remained open to negotiations with Iran, but that \"it has to be real\". It remains unclear whether Tehran will offer anything that President Donald Trump would consider \"real\"."
