    "chunk_id": "2026-07-22-dailymirrorlk-5526d6af-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "968e8b97c057a3f01100a6984af34735",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "Dengue control cannot be seasonal. It must be constant. Drains need to be cleaned before the rains begin. Waste must be collected on time. High-risk areas must be monitored regularly. There must also be accountability. When a particular area reports a spike in cases, the relevant local council should be able to explain why. But if we are honest, the blame does not lie only with the authorities.\n\nThe mosquito does not need a canal or a marsh. It needs only a few centimetres of water. Keeping one\u2019s surroundings clean is not simply a matter of personal choice. It is a civic duty.\n\nWe cannot expect municipal workers to enter every home and inspect every garden. That responsibility rests with individuals. A few minutes each week to clear stagnant water can make a difference. Yet, this basic discipline is often missing.\n\nPublic awareness campaigns are carried out regularly. The message is repeated year after year. But awareness without action achieves very little.\n\nSchools, workplaces and communities must become part of the solution. Children need to be taught that cleanliness is not optional. Offices must ensure their premises are properly maintained. Every dengue patient adds pressure to an already stretched healthcare system. Doctors and nurses are forced to manage increasing numbers with limited resources. Beds fill up quickly. Attention is diverted from other critical health needs. Sri Lanka has faced a severe dengue crisis before. The 2017 outbreak should have been a turning point. It exposed the weaknesses in our systems and highlighted the importance of sustained action. But nearly a decade later, we find ourselves dealing with the same issues.\n\nThis is no longer just about mosquitoes. It is about mindset. Local councils must do better. That is non-negotiable. Waste management, drainage and public sanitation cannot be treated as secondary concerns. But the public must also change. Blame alone will not solve this problem."
  },
