    "md5": "4a751d54a1db96a7fd59da14eb402470",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "Clean Sri Lanka has so far failed to produce tangible results, except for news headlines. Sri Lankan cities, towns, and hamlets are dirtier than they were before. Some commonsense initiatives such as relocating hawkers in the city of Kandy are welcomed, but by and large, inefficient local governments and the central government have become the problem, rather than problem solvers.\n\nHowever, if the current lethargy persists, Sri Lankan cities and hamlets would unravel into decay and squalor. Garbage\u00a0 piles up in your neighbourhood\u00a0 for several reasons.\n\nOne is political crookedness, which is a historical malady, not unique to this government.\n\nSuccessive governments have deliberately let the projects undertaken by their predecessors and rivals fall into disrepair as a strategy to politically delegitimise not just their rivals, but also their development initiatives. Yahapalanaya was an extreme example of this case\u2014not only the Port City, the Mattala airport, Floating Market, and many other projects, but also the entire economic momentum that clocked 6.5 per cent annualised growth over the previous decade was\u00a0 discarded simply to delegitimise the Rajapaksas.\n\nWhen the Colombo Municipal Council and Urban Development Ministry fail to maintain even the walking paths \u2014 which, a\u00a0 satire account on X has renamed as hiking trails \u2014\u00a0 if it is not its inefficiency, it is political crookedness at play.\n\nSecond is incompetence. Even a deputy minister in local government was on record as admitting that local government bodies have failed to provide basic services to the public.\n\nThis is a side effect when voters mistake grievance-hyping as proof of future administrative competence.\n\nThird, incompetence of elected representatives is magnified by an incompetent bureaucracy at the local government level. That\u00a0 is a major shortfall in Sri Lankan administrative structure, which many advocates of devolution have often overlooked."
  },
