    "chunk_size_bytes": 1602,
    "chunk_text": "Trade agreements should reflect the same philosophy. Their success should not be measured solely by tariff schedules negotiated between governments. They should include practical mechanisms that make it easier for smaller exporters to participate through simplified procedures, standards recognition, digital customs cooperation, technical assistance, and business matchmaking.\n\nEmbassies should increasingly be evaluated by the export partnerships, technology transfers, and investment opportunities they help create.\n\nOur SMEs do not need another generation of sympathy. They do not need to remain permanent recipients of programs designed merely to sustain them. They need larger markets, stronger technology partnerships, deeper integration into global value chains, and a foreign policy that recognises them not as beneficiaries of development, but as creators of national prosperity\n\nSri Lanka has capable entrepreneurs, strategic geography, competitive industries, and access to global markets. What we lack is continuity. Lasting prosperity is not built by first shipments or isolated programs, but by institutions that help businesses grow year after year.\n\nOur SMEs do not need another generation of sympathy. They do not need to remain permanent recipients of programs designed merely to sustain them. They need larger markets, stronger technology partnerships, deeper integration into global value chains, and a foreign policy that recognises them not as beneficiaries of development, but as creators of national prosperity.\n\nThat is the economic diplomacy Sri Lanka should aspire to build."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-22-dailyftlk-38f91513",
    "num": "dailyftlk-38f91513",
    "date_str": "2026-07-22",
    "description": "Sri Lankan brands don\u2019t have a sponsorship problem: They have an imagination problem",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Sri-Lankan-brands-don-t-have-a-sponsorship-problem-They-have-an-imagination-problem/4-794932",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1784679300.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-22-dailyftlk-38f91513-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "582eed408b3e1026bd8ded420d8cbc25",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1952,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan brands don\u2019t have a sponsorship problem: They have an imagination problem\n\nSri Lanka has never lacked creativity. What it has often lacked is the confidence to think bigger about the role brands can play in culture. The next generation of successful marketing campaigns will not be built around the biggest media buy or the most prominent logo. They will be built around experiences that people actively choose to be part of\n\nI have yet to sit in a sponsorship meeting where someone has said, \"Forget the logo, banners and flags. Tell me how we're going to make people feel.\"\n\nInstead, the conversation almost always follows the same predictable script. How many people will attend? Where will our branding appear? How much media coverage can we expect? How many VIP tickets are included?\n\nBy the end of the meeting, everyone knows how many banners will be printed, yet nobody has asked what is arguably the only question consumers actually care about.\n\nHaving spent more than twenty years producing television, destination campaigns, food festivals and live events, I have watched sponsorship evolve very little, while consumer behaviour has evolved dramatically. Marketing departments continue to measure reach, impressions and visibility, yet today's consumers have become remarkably adept at filtering traditional advertising. They skip commercials, scroll past sponsored posts, install ad blockers and increasingly distrust overt brand messaging.\n\nYet those same consumers will happily spend hours at a music festival, queue to enter an immersive brand activation, photograph a beautifully designed installation, beg for branded merchandise or travel across the country for an experience that feels authentic and memorable.\n\nThe problem is not that people have stopped engaging with brands. The problem is that many brands are still trying to engage consumers in ways that no longer reflect how people choose to spend their attention."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
