    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7e23d993931398c14564e85d32371ba2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1896,
    "chunk_text": "The problem is not that people have stopped engaging with brands. The problem is that many brands are still trying to engage consumers in ways that no longer reflect how people choose to spend their attention.\n\nThose figures tell us something profound. Experience is no longer an enhancement. It has become an expectation.\n\nAround the world, marketing has shifted from interrupting consumers to inviting them to participate. The brands winning today are not simply buying media; they are creating moments that people actively choose to spend time with.\n\nMany sponsorships still prioritise visibility over participation. Budgets are allocated to logo placement, stage branding, hospitality and media exposure, while the consumer experience itself often receives comparatively little investment. That approach may deliver awareness, but awareness alone rarely creates affinity.\n\nConsumers rarely remember the biggest logo. They remember how a brand made them feel.\n\nWhen producing the Electric Peacock Festival, we worked with relatively modest sponsorship budgets. Like many event organisers, we wanted to create memorable brand experiences but often had limited resources to do so.\n\nAt the Ceylon Tea Festival, we invited chefs to cook with Ceylon Tea, encouraging visitors to discover the ingredient in an unexpected and engaging way rather than simply offering another product sample. It was a small activation, but it demonstrated the power of experience over exposure.\n\nIt also taught me something else. The limitation was never creativity. The limitation was timing. Too often, brands entered the conversation after the event had already been designed. Sponsorship became a commercial transaction rather than a creative collaboration.\n\nImagine instead if brand managers, event producers, architects, set designers, chefs, storytellers and content creators were around the table from day one."
  },
