    "chunk_size_bytes": 1881,
    "chunk_text": "For decades, U.S. maritime dominance in the Indian Ocean relied heavily on its fortified base at Diego Garcia. However, China\u2019s expanding naval capabilities and growing presence through strategic port investments, combined with emerging uncertainties over the Chagos Archipelago, have encouraged Washington to diversify its regional approach. The 2026 conflict involving the United States, Israel, and Iran further highlighted the vulnerabilities of relying on a small number of fixed military facilities.\n\nThe United States is strengthening its maritime strategy in South Asia through deeper engagement with key commercial infrastructure, particularly the Port of Colombo in Sri Lanka and the Thilafushi Port in the Maldives. Instead of relying only on traditional military facilities, Washington is using economic partnerships, diplomacy, and development finance to expand its influence over critical maritime hubs.\n\nSri Lanka has emerged as a key focus of this strategy. In June, US Deputy Assistant Secretary of State Dr. Paul Kapur visited the Port of Colombo. Publicly centered on expanding economic cooperation, the visit strategically reflected Washington's growing interest in shaping the future of one of the Indian Ocean's most important transshipment hubs alongside other global partners.\n\nThe Port of Colombo is South Asia\u2019s largest transshipment hub and ranks among the world\u2019s leading container ports. Operating above 90 per cent capacity since 2021, its strategic location along the main east-west shipping route between Asia, Europe, and the Americas makes it a vital regional maritime asset. The port is also crucial for India, handling around 45 per cent of its container transshipment traffic.\n\nAs trade volumes continue to expand, Colombo's importance has grown beyond its commercial role, placing it at the centre of increasing geopolitical competition."
