    "chunk_size_bytes": 1872,
    "chunk_text": "The project is being developed through a joint venture led by India's Adani Ports, which holds a 51 per cent stake, alongside John Keells Holdings with 34 per cent and the Sri Lanka Ports Authority with 15 per cent. Strong demand has accelerated planning for the proposed West Container Terminal 2, with detailed designs already being fast-tracked.\n\nWhile Sri Lanka welcomes foreign direct investment as it recovers from the severe 2022 economic crisis, linking its commercial ports with major-power strategic interests carries significant risks. The escalation of the US-Iran conflict in early 2026 highlighted these vulnerabilities, demonstrating how infrastructure with geopolitical significance can become exposed during regional tensions.\n\nOn March 4, 2026, the Indian Ocean became the scene of a major military confrontation when a US fast-attack submarine torpedoed the Iranian frigate 'IRIS Dena' off Sri Lanka's southern coast near Galle, killing 84 Iranian sailors. The Sri Lankan Navy rescued 32 survivors, while the damaged \u2018IRIS Bushehr\u2019 later sought emergency assistance near Colombo.\n\nIn the tense aftermath, Washington pressed Colombo for logistical support, including access to Mattala Rajapaksa International Airport to deploy fighter jets armed with anti-ship missiles. Meanwhile, Tehran requested a Colombo port call for survivor evacuation and emergency repairs.\n\nFacing competing demands from two rival powers, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake denied military access to both sides, reaffirming Sri Lanka\u2019s policy of neutrality and non-alignment.\n\nAs competition in the Indian Ocean grows, the Port of Colombo will continue to attract major powers pursuing economic and strategic interests. While foreign partnerships can bring investment, Sri Lanka must ensure they align with national priorities and do not compromise its strategic autonomy."
