    "md5": "6a61b24a1bfbd0325f8c477a5e2f0a85",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "As competition in the Indian Ocean grows, the Port of Colombo will continue to attract major powers pursuing economic and strategic interests. While foreign partnerships can bring investment, Sri Lanka must ensure they align with national priorities and do not compromise its strategic autonomy.\n\nThe Thilafushi Port Development Project, led by Maldives Ports Limited (MPL), aims to modernize the Maldives\u2019 maritime trade network by relocating major cargo and bulk handling operations from congested Mal\u00e9 to the artificial island of Thilafushi. With a planned capacity of 400,000 twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs), the project is expected to reduce port congestion, improve vessel efficiency, and lower shipping costs.\n\nThe United States has signalled growing interest in the project as part of its broader effort to strengthen maritime partnerships across South Asia. During an official visit to the Maldives, US Assistant Secretary of State for South and Central Asian Affairs Paul Kapur toured the Thilafushi Port project and described it as a future logistics and distribution hub for international commerce.\n\nFollowing the visit, Kapur said the United States looked forward to working with the Maldives on the Thilafushi Port project and helping unlock its long-term economic potential. During his visit, he also met President Mohamed Muizzu, Economic Development Minister Mohamed Saeed and Foreign Minister Iruthisham Adam.\n\nKapur's visit followed an earlier inspection of the project in May by Nicole Chulick, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary in the Bureau of South and Central Asian Affairs at the US State Department. During her visit, Chulick met senior officials of Maldives Ports Limited (MPL), toured the existing Male' Commercial Port and visited the Thilafushi development site. She also expressed US interest in supporting infrastructure development projects across the Maldives."
