    "chunk_text": "Despite the influx of investment and diplomatic attention, host nations across South Asia must remain explicit and cautious about the underlying motives of the U.S. maritime strategy. For Washington, this approach frequently prioritizes American geopolitical interests, specifically deterring adversarial influence and securing vital maritime logistics far above the immediate, pressing developmental needs of local economies. When critical infrastructure decisions are heavily influenced by the strategic objectives of a distant superpower, it fundamentally complicates sovereign decision-making. Developing nations, many of which are intensely focused on sustainable growth and debt recovery, find themselves pressured to align their commercial maritime policies with Western security agendas, risking artificial policy divisions that do little to address their actual domestic priorities.\n\nThe developments in Colombo and Thilafushi illustrate a broader trend in which maritime infrastructure is becoming closely linked to regional and global strategic competition. Ports and logistics networks remain essential for economic growth, but they are also gaining importance as countries seek greater influence and connectivity across the Indian Ocean. For smaller states, the challenge is to attract investment and strengthen development while ensuring that these projects continue to serve national interests rather than becoming instruments of wider geopolitical competition."
