    "md5": "39c15c9ab0bfd26e63b51ac064ac27ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1571,
    "chunk_text": "The last of these options is possible only when there is no wind. In our case, however, the Southwest monsoon winds have already set in. These masses of air have travelled literally thousands of miles over the sea and are laden with moisture.\n\nThe Trailing Edge of the wing with Silver Iodide Flares working\n\nIn \u2018Operation Popeye\u2019, from 1967 to 1972, USA scientists modified the monsoon weather in Vietnam by cloud seeding, to harass the Viet Cong on their supply routes along the Ho Chi Minh Trail, with the extra 30 to 45 days of rain producing mud and landslides. More recently, on the eve of the 2008 Olympic Games, the Chinese fired rockets into clouds to force heavy rains in order to improve air quality and provide good weather on the opening day.\n\nHowever, if the wind is not anticipated correctly, disastrous results could occur. Once, in the early 1980s, Saudi Arabia seeded its clouds and the wind unexpectedly changed, bringing heavy rainfall, including hail, to the United Arab Emirates and Oman. The southwesterly winds in Sri Lanka are usually steady. Observing the clouds from both the ground and air, they are characterised by considerable vertical development and, therefore, ripe for seeding.\n\nThe easiest way to seed clouds would be from the air. The Sri Lanka Air Force has aircraft and experienced pilots who can carry out such an operation effectively. If we seed the clouds to the south of Ratmalana, rain will fall somewhere northeast of that point. This will, of course, be predicated by the prevalent wind direction and speed for that day."
  },
