    "chunk_size_bytes": 1717,
    "chunk_text": "The country has come a long way since then. People have seen the best and worst of times, in the form of ethnic divisions, authoritarian rulers, political plots, people\u2019s movements in the form of Aragalaya,\u00a0 and how corrupt regimes were eventually overthrown. In fact,\u00a0 Sri Lanka inspired citizens from many other countries to realise the power they have in their hands. Gen-Z protests in Bangladesh and Nepal are examples from the South Asian region.\u00a0 The Gen-Z uprising in Kenya is another example from another part of the world. In each one of these uprisings, people and mostly the Gen-Z stood up against political corruption, police brutality, and ban on social media and so on.\n\nSupporting the youth-led protests is Sonam Wangchuk, a well-known Indian activist and educator, the inspiration behind the 2009 super hit Bollywood film \u2018Three Idiots\u2019. He is one of best-known public figures from Ladakh. His work has earned international recognition, including the Ramon Magsaysay Award in 2018, often referred to as Asia\u2019s Nobel Prize. His deteriorating health has sparked widespread concern among his supporters,\u00a0 but he claims that he would end the hunger strike \u2018only if the government took accountability for the recent failures in the education system.\u2019\n\nIn Sri Lanka, many individuals have launched hunger strikes for serious causes \u2013 demanding accountability for enforced disappearances and various atrocities committed during the armed conflict,\u00a0 to various other struggles demanding justice. These hunger strikes are unlike those that have been politically motivated. Some local political figures have now become infamous for launching hunger strikes only to end it within a day or two."
  },
