    "chunk_text": "\"Every time you step onboard an airplane today, you are safer because of what happened to Clipper Endeavor and her passengers three-quarters of a century ago,\" said Expedition Unknown host Josh Gates.\n\nHe said in a statement that he had been \"astonished that such an important story had largely faded from public memory\".\n\nHe then began piecing together the aircraft's final moments using reports from the Civil Aeronautics Board, the predecessor to the US Federal Aviation Administration.\n\nThe breakthrough came after researchers uncovered records from a public hearing in Puerto Rico, including a map drawn by air force pilots who had witnessed the crash, which helped narrow down the search area.\n\nResearchers used historical records and weather data from the time of the crash to identify the 10 square nautical miles of Atlantic Ocean seabed where the wreckage was likely resting.\n\nAn attempt to locate the wreckage in 2024 was abandoned due to poor weather - but Matthews said his team had been determined to return before the 75th anniversary of the crash.\n\nA drone found the wreckage - which had broken into two sections - during its first pass of the search area on 2 June.\n\nA follow-up photographic survey confirmed the wreckage was of the Clipper Endeavor, providing images that revealed the Pan Am logo and aircraft's name were still visible on the fuselage.\n\n\"To have a front-row seat to an expedition like this as it's unfolding is tremendous, and so I feel really lucky that we were able to be a part of it,\" said Gates, who was aboard the surface ship when the wreckage appeared on screen.\n\nThe discovery could offer fresh hope for the families of passengers of other planes that are presumed to have crashed in the ocean, such as Malaysia Airlines Flight 370.\n\nIt disappeared 12 years ago with 269 people onboard and, despite numerous searches in the Indian Ocean, has not yet been located."
