    "chunk_size_bytes": 1704,
    "chunk_text": "Supply chain responsibility has also become part of the social pillar. Businesses are expected to ensure ethical labour practices, fair wages, safe working conditions, and respect for human rights throughout their supplier networks. International buyers increasingly conduct ESG audits before selecting suppliers.\n\nTechnology supports inclusion through accessible digital platforms, remote work opportunities, assistive technologies, and digital learning programmes. Digital transformation enables organisations to create more flexible workplaces while expanding employment opportunities across geographical regions.\n\nInvestors increasingly examine social metrics including employee turnover, training hours, workplace safety, gender balance, board diversity, employee engagement, and community investment. Transparent reporting demonstrates accountability while strengthening investor confidence.\n\nCorporate governance provides the foundation for effective DEI initiatives. Leadership commitment, measurable targets, transparent reporting, and accountability mechanisms ensure diversity strategies deliver meaningful results rather than symbolic commitments.\n\nSmall and medium-sized enterprises can also benefit significantly from inclusive business practices. Even modest investments in employee wellbeing, training, flexible work arrangements, and fair employment policies can improve productivity while strengthening competitiveness.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s growing knowledge economy depends heavily on attracting and retaining skilled professionals. Organisations that create inclusive cultures will be better positioned to compete for talent while strengthening innovation and long-term business resilience."
