    "md5": "24f20bea43fcc59443ca8e31a60e0a5f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1952,
    "chunk_text": "Unlike the traditional linear model, the circular economy encourages businesses to design products that are durable, repairable, reusable, and recyclable. Materials are continuously circulated within the economy instead of being discarded after a single use.\n\nThis approach transforms waste into a valuable resource while reducing the need for virgin raw materials and lowering greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nCircular economy practices help address these challenges by improving resource productivity and reducing dependence on finite materials.\n\nFor businesses, sustainability is no longer simply about environmental responsibility\u2014it is becoming an important driver of operational efficiency and competitive advantage.\n\nSri Lanka generates significant volumes of municipal waste, agricultural residues, construction debris, electronic waste, and plastic packaging every year. Much of this material still ends up in landfills or unmanaged disposal sites.\n\nBy adopting circular economy strategies, these waste streams can become valuable economic resources.\n\nManufacturers can redesign production processes to reduce material waste, increase recycling rates, and use recycled inputs wherever possible. Cleaner production techniques improve efficiency while lowering environmental impact.\n\nIndustrial symbiosis\u2014where one company\u2019s waste becomes another company\u2019s raw material\u2014offers significant opportunities across manufacturing sectors.\n\nPlastic pollution remains one of the country\u2019s most visible environmental challenges. Expanding recycling infrastructure, promoting biodegradable alternatives, and encouraging responsible packaging design can significantly reduce plastic waste entering the environment.\n\nBusinesses can also adopt reusable packaging systems that minimise single-use plastics while strengthening customer engagement.\n\nConstruction generates large quantities of waste materials that often retain considerable economic value."
  },
