  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-422942ba",
    "num": "dailyftlk-422942ba",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "Growing Responsibly: The Next Stage of ESG",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Growing-Responsibly-The-Next-Stage-of-ESG/34-795032",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1784792886.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-422942ba-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7371db8e45f0a149d03285b8bbf4be2f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "Growing Responsibly: The Next Stage of ESG\n\nPorts exist to move trade, connect markets and support economic growth. At Hambantota International Port, that remains our primary responsibility. Every day we work to operate a safe, efficient and competitive commercial port that strengthens Sri Lanka\u2019s position as a regional maritime and logistics hub.\n\nBut today\u2019s ports are expected to do more than move cargo. They operate within communities, alongside sensitive marine environments and as part of increasingly complex global supply chains. As the expectations of customers, investors and society evolve, ports must also evolve. Growth and responsibility can no longer be treated as separate objectives. They must progress together.\n\nThat principle lies at the heart of Hambantota International Port Group\u2019s Environmental, Social and Governance (ESG) strategy.\n\nIt also reflects the broader ESG philosophy of China Merchants Port (CMPort), which integrates environmental, social and governance considerations into business strategy across its global port network. Importantly, CMPort recognises that every port operates in a different setting. ESG cannot be a standard programme replicated across countries. It must respond to local priorities, local communities and local environments.\n\nFor HIPG, that means our ESG priorities are shaped by the needs of Hambantota and Sri Lanka.\n\nOver the past eight years, our approach has gradually evolved. Traditional corporate responsibility often focused on giving back through donations or one-off community projects. Those initiatives continue to have value and will always remain part of being a responsible corporate citizen.\n\nESG, however, asks a different question. How can a business use its own expertise, partnerships and resources to create lasting value beyond the immediate intervention?\n\nThis shift, from project-based CSR to integrated ESG, has influenced the way we think about every initiative we undertake."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-422942ba",
    "num": "dailyftlk-422942ba",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "Growing Responsibly: The Next Stage of ESG",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Growing-Responsibly-The-Next-Stage-of-ESG/34-795032",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1784792886.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-422942ba-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5ff29d1c4eb9110fe9a9161f101e5a65",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "ESG, however, asks a different question. How can a business use its own expertise, partnerships and resources to create lasting value beyond the immediate intervention?\n\nThis shift, from project-based CSR to integrated ESG, has influenced the way we think about every initiative we undertake.\n\nHope Village in Kandagasmankada followed the same philosophy. By supporting community infrastructure, livelihoods and employability for more than 2,000 residents, the objective was to help build long-term resilience rather than provide temporary assistance.\n\nThe Save Ali Pancha programme represents another stage of that evolution. Supported by the China Merchants Foundation and implemented with the Department of Wildlife Conservation and the Wildlife and Nature Protection Society, the programme combines immediate support for orphaned elephant calves with scientific research and technology aimed at reducing human-elephant conflict over the long term.\n\nViewed individually, these initiatives may appear unrelated. Viewed together, they reveal a consistent philosophy.\n\nOur objective is not simply to respond to individual problems but to strengthen the systems surrounding them, whether those systems relate to education, livelihoods, conservation or scientific knowledge.\n\nThis is where ESG becomes different from traditional CSR. Rather than asking, \u201cWhat can we give?\u201d, ESG increasingly asks, \u201cWhat can we strengthen?\u201d\n\nFor a commercial port, that question also extends to the environment in which we operate. Every port interacts with its surrounding marine environment. The responsibility is not simply to minimise impacts, but to better understand them so that future decisions are informed by sound science rather than assumption."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-422942ba",
    "num": "dailyftlk-422942ba",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "Growing Responsibly: The Next Stage of ESG",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Growing-Responsibly-The-Next-Stage-of-ESG/34-795032",
    "lang": "en",
