    "md5": "a142a9ca31d0fdff7bb3cfdc1d2c4dca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "The CSRSA pathway is designed to develop professionals who can help organisations understand sustainability standards, identify relevant risks and opportunities, develop meaningful metrics and greenhouse gas reduction pathways, and integrate sustainability into governance, risk management and long-term strategy.\n\nThe summit brought together C-suite executives, board members, regulators, sustainability experts and leading professionals to examine how sustainability can move from a compliance exercise to a strategic priority.\n\nDiscussions explored how IFRS S1 and S2 are reshaping sustainability reporting, and how credible sustainability information can influence investor confidence, regulatory oversight and board accountability.\n\nThe summit also brought together perspectives from international and local organisations, including UNDP, the Colombo Stock Exchange, Deloitte and EY, alongside leading Sri Lankan businesses and professionals. The message was clear: sustainability is no longer a peripheral concern. It is increasingly connected to risk, investment, competitiveness, governance and long-term business resilience.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the challenge now is to ensure that the momentum generated by new standards, professional qualifications, stronger governance frameworks and industry dialogue translates into meaningful action across the economy.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s sustainable transformation will not be achieved by any one institution or sector. It will require businesses, professionals, policymakers, investors, public-sector institutions and other stakeholders to work together.\n\nFor organisations, the journey may begin with understanding their most significant environmental and social impacts, identifying the risks and opportunities that could affect their future, and strengthening the quality of the information they provide to stakeholders.\n\nFor professionals, it requires developing the expertise to support organisations through this transition."
  },
