    "md5": "c9fff67c4409180bfade71d37baa519f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "Farewell Ambassador Qi Zhenhong of China!\n\nColombo, July 25 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has always been a global trading hub and as result many Envoy appointments are recorded through its long history. Among notable envoys recorded are Emperor Ashoka's Envoys (3rd Century BC), Persian Envoys (2nd Century BC), Roman Envoys (1st Century AD), Chinese Buddhist Scholars (5th Century AD) and Chinese Admiral Zheng He (14th Century AD). Assistance provided by Admiral Zheng He is recorded in the trilingual plaque on display at the national museum in Colombo.\n\nAmong notable Sri Lankan Envoys are; trader brothers Thapassu and Balluka (who met the Buddha), Envoys of King Mahanama in China and Rome (1st Century AD) and Ambassador Jayapala of Chilaw in Great China during the Ming Dynasty (14th Century AD). Jayapala is believed to have travelled to China on 6 occasions. It was the peak of the maritime Silk Road when Sri Jayawardenepura became capital under Parakramabahu VI who resided close to \u2018Mirihana\u2019 in Jayawardenepura. Above indicates that Sino Lanka diplomatic ties have been stronger than most of Sri Lanka\u2019s bilateral relations.\n\nAmbassador Qi Zhenhong arrived in Sri Lanka in late 2020 when Sri Lanka was combating COVID \u2013 19 under President Gotabaya who ruled the country from Mirihana. Sri Lankans may remember the simple arrival ceremony Chinese Embassy held on advice of Ambassador Qi Zhenhong respecting the health guidelines of Sri Lanka to prevent the spread of COVID \u2013 19."
