    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "A Landmark Gathering of Minds: The Ceylon Journal Roundtable Revives the Spirit of Intellectual Dialogue\n\nIn an age when academic discourse is increasingly confined within disciplinary boundaries and universities rarely provide spaces for sustained interdisciplinary dialogue, The Ceylon Journal Roundtable marked a remarkable departure. Held recently at the serene Tulana Research Centre in Kelaniya, the inaugural Roundtable brought together more than fifty distinguished academics, researchers, professionals, clergy, writers, artists, architects, physicians, lawyers, journalists and independent scholars from across Sri Lanka for a full day of conversation under the theme History, Culture and Religion.\n\nConceived and principally organised by historian and Editor-in-Chief of The Ceylon Journal, Avishka Mario Senewiratne, the Roundtable represented months of careful planning and collaboration. Sponsored by The Ceylon Journal, the event sought not merely to present research papers, but to recreate a culture of civilised intellectual exchange that has become increasingly uncommon in Sri Lanka\u2019s academic landscape.\n\nIndeed, many participants remarked that it was difficult to recall a comparable interdisciplinary gathering organised independently outside the conventional university conference structure. In his virtual message played at the commencement of the event, eminent historian Professor C. R. de Silva (now domiciled in Virginia, USA) observed that the last comparable intellectual initiative had taken place during the late 1960s and early 1970s, inspired by the pioneering work of Professor Gananath Obeyesekere and a generation of scholars committed to rigorous public dialogue. Professor de Silva congratulated The Ceylon Journal for reviving this important tradition and expressed the hope that the Roundtable would become a permanent annual feature of Sri Lanka\u2019s intellectual calendar."
  },
