    "chunk_text": "Indeed, many participants remarked that it was difficult to recall a comparable interdisciplinary gathering organised independently outside the conventional university conference structure. In his virtual message played at the commencement of the event, eminent historian Professor C. R. de Silva (now domiciled in Virginia, USA) observed that the last comparable intellectual initiative had taken place during the late 1960s and early 1970s, inspired by the pioneering work of Professor Gananath Obeyesekere and a generation of scholars committed to rigorous public dialogue. Professor de Silva congratulated The Ceylon Journal for reviving this important tradition and expressed the hope that the Roundtable would become a permanent annual feature of Sri Lanka\u2019s intellectual calendar.\n\nFollowing the welcome, Dr. Praveen Weeratunga of The Ceylon Journal introduced the objectives of the Roundtable and outlined the format for the day\u2019s discussions before inviting participants to embrace open yet respectful intellectual engagement.\n\nThe first session, chaired by Dr. Praveen Weeratunga, focused on History and Heritage. Dr. Buddhika Dassanayake delivered a compelling presentation examining the forgotten royal burial grounds of the Kandyan Kingdom and argued that the destruction of this sacred landscape amounted to a deliberate erasure of historical memory. He combined archaeological evidence, historical records and colonial documentation to reconstruct one of the island\u2019s least appreciated heritage sites."
