    "chunk_text": "The second session on Art, Architecture and Shared Cultural Influences, chaired by architect Lakmith Fernando, highlighted the relationship between the built environment, culture and sustainability. Dr. Priyantha Udagedara explored the colonial transformation of Nuwara Eliya through paintings, travel accounts and visual culture, illustrating how British landscape aesthetics reshaped both physical space and cultural perceptions of the hill country. Together, the presentations generated considerable discussion on heritage conservation, identity and the relevance of traditional knowledge in the modern world. Architect Sunela Jayewardene delivered a thought-provoking address on Sri Lanka\u2019s traditional architectural heritage, demonstrating how centuries-old indigenous design principles provide practical solutions to contemporary environmental challenges, climate resilience and sustainable living.\n\nParticipants were then introduced to the history and mission of the Tulana Research Centre by its Director, Fr. Dilan Perera SJ. Founded in 1974 by the internationally renowned theologian and Buddhist scholar late Fr. Aloysius Pieris SJ, Tulana has long served as one of Sri Lanka\u2019s foremost centres for scholarship, interreligious encounter and dialogue. Described by many as a \u201cmini university,\u201d Tulana has welcomed generations of local and international scholars, while its remarkable libraries, research facilities and atmosphere of contemplation have made it one of the country\u2019s most respected intellectual institutions. Participants expressed particular appreciation that such a gathering was hosted in a venue whose very philosophy embodies dialogue across cultures, religions and academic disciplines."
