    "chunk_text": "Participants were then introduced to the history and mission of the Tulana Research Centre by its Director, Fr. Dilan Perera SJ. Founded in 1974 by the internationally renowned theologian and Buddhist scholar late Fr. Aloysius Pieris SJ, Tulana has long served as one of Sri Lanka\u2019s foremost centres for scholarship, interreligious encounter and dialogue. Described by many as a \u201cmini university,\u201d Tulana has welcomed generations of local and international scholars, while its remarkable libraries, research facilities and atmosphere of contemplation have made it one of the country\u2019s most respected intellectual institutions. Participants expressed particular appreciation that such a gathering was hosted in a venue whose very philosophy embodies dialogue across cultures, religions and academic disciplines.\n\nThe concluding academic session, chaired by Dr. Shane Halpe, shifted attention to contemporary society through discussions on Education, Compassion and Ethnic Harmony. Dr. Gauthami Kamalika Jayathilaka argued that compassion should become a central value within Sri Lanka\u2019s state universities, presenting practical examples of cultivating empathy, social responsibility and harmony among students. Ralston Joseph followed with a stimulating presentation exploring ethnic harmony through an economic rather than purely political lens, suggesting that shared prosperity and market participation may provide common ground capable of reducing social divisions. The discussion that followed demonstrated how the Roundtable successfully connected historical inquiry with pressing contemporary challenges."
