    "md5": "c4d5e358ce3b24b73c8e34afaeeeb6c8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1844,
    "chunk_text": "Second, writing in English, he applied the frameworks of the leading anthropologists of his time \u2013 the two most prominent being Bronislaw Malinowski and Ruth Benedict \u2013 to his community. As the anthropologist Sarath Amunugama noted in an interview with me, in delving into his society so deeply, he made Sinhala Buddhist culture \u201cas valid an object of study as any other rural traditional society in the world of his time.\u201d\n\nWe are living in Ceylon like an integrated community\u2026 An integrated independent community implies a language, culture, and tradition of their own which are, of course, continuously changing. Language and tradition are aspects of culture. The most important thing for an independent community is its changing culture, which unites them in spite of the diversities of class and social habits.\n\nThis was evident even in his fiction. In novels like Gamperaliya (1944), which has often been compared to Giuseppe Tomasi di Lampedusa\u2019s great saga of the Italian nobility, The Leopard, he charts the economic changes which transformed the country in the late 19th century. Like Lampedusa\u2019s novel, Gamperaliya portrays a clash between an old aristocracy moored in traditional values and a new elite moored in the capitalist ethic. Considered as the definitive realist Sinhala novel of the 20th century, it was hailed as a masterpiece almost right after it was first published by Ediriweera Sarachchandra.\n\nSecond, as a public figure, Wickramasinghe communicated his ideas through the popular press. At the time newspapers enjoyed a considerable circulation and were featuring essays on literature, art, and religion. Wickramasinghe made use of these platforms to convey his ideas on these subjects to a wide audience. In doing so, he enabled the transmission of new and modern ideas to a very receptive readership."
  },
