    "chunk_size_bytes": 2028,
    "chunk_text": "Kandy Club at 150: Colonial past, civic future\n\nMarking 150 years today (26), The Kandy Club stands as a living relic of Sri Lanka\u2019s colonial inheritance \u2014 a private sanctuary whose stone and timber recall an era of planters, governors and British civility that shaped the hill capital.\n\nPerched beside Kandy Lake since 1877, the Club has watched emperors of policy and potters of politics pass through the city\u2019s changing seasons. \u201cWe are custodians of a layered past,\u201d President Palitha Pethiyagoda said at the sesquicentennial ceremony, invoking the Club\u2019s continuity from colonial drawing rooms to post#independence civic life. \u201cOur mission is to preserve the heritage, traditions and cultural dignity of The Kandy Club while serving as a timeless institution of fellowship for future generations.\u201d\n\nArchitecturally restrained and quietly grand, the Club\u2019s symmetry and proportionality are textbook colonial, an aesthetic of functionality and permanence that its stewards have guarded faithfully. \u201cEach room is an archive,\u201d Secretary Rienzie Nanayakkara observed, pointing to portraits, billiard tables and timber rafters that bear the imprint of time. \u201cPreservation has not been nostalgia alone but a disciplined governance that ensured institutional continuity across generations.\u201d\n\nFounded as a social centre for British planters, civil servants and military officers, membership remained predominantly British until independence in 1948; all 13 colonial governors later served as patrons. The Club\u2019s roll reads like a social history of Kandy: planters turned professionals, administrators turned statesmen, and visitors including Leonard Woolf, who chronicled its social life in his memoir Growing.\n\nThat pedigre, presents both privilege and responsibility. \u201cWe acknowledge the origins frankly,\u201d Neville Abeysinghe, Manager of The Kandy Club said. \u201cThe Club\u2019s traditions are rooted in a colonial past, yet our role today is to make that legacy relevant, inclusive and civic-minded.\u201d"
