    "md5": "1704e1a170389895e73fb67179895016",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1903,
    "chunk_text": "Eyes aggressive route expansion across Europe, Asia and Africa\n\nAs SriLankan Airlines pushes to expand its fleet, Chairman Dimal Arandara has outlined a strategic roadmap to heavily increase flight frequencies to established long-haul markets while establishing a brand-new footprint in Africa.\n\nTestifying before the parliamentary committee, he argued that the airline is currently constrained by a severe lack of capacity rather than a lack of market demand, noting that wide body aircraft would serve as the backbone of this future route expansion.\n\nArandara revealed ambitious plans to penetrate the African aviation market to serve as a transit hub for Chinese traffic, heavily leveraging Colombo\u2019s geographical positioning.\n\n\u201cWe are looking at new markets. Africa, because Sri Lanka is the shortest distance destination from China to Africa. We should look at Kenya, Addis Ababa, or Pretoria. We are studying this,\u201d he explained to the committee, indicating that feasibility studies for these intercontinental routes are currently underway.\n\nEmphasising immediate demand on key existing routes, the Chairman also noted the massive potential in East Asia, Australia, and Europe. \u201cLooking at London, we can easily add another aircraft.\n\nHe added that the airline has the potential to run daily flights to South Korea and can significantly increase flight frequencies to China.\n\nChairman of the Sectoral Oversight Committee on Infrastructure and Strategic Development, MP Marikkar acknowledged the lost revenue opportunities caused by the current depleted fleet, specifically highlighting the lack of connectivity to major global financial hubs.\n\n\u201cFor example, there is no direct flight to Shanghai. A large scale of businessmen travel to Shanghai, but their airline has direct flights while ours doesn\u2019t. We understand that\u2019s because we don\u2019t have enough aircraft,\u201d Marikkar noted."
  },
