    "language": "en",
    "md5": "165270e863e6a2f14549d4d943236c8d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1812,
    "chunk_text": "This divergence stems from tactical moves by vehicle importers to bypass fiscal regulations. Authorities currently levy a monthly penalty equivalent to the percent of a vehicle\u2019s CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) value if it remains unregistered beyond 90 days after importation. Introduced to prevent overstocking and current account pressures, the policy has instead led dealers to register vehicles prior to retail sale. This effectively clears the statutory window while masking true leasing and mortgage volumes in official registration records.\n\nHe noted that lower recorded financing shares reflect dealers registering inventory before sales are finalized.\n\nThe accumulation of floor stock follows a sharp rebound in imports during May 2026, when traders rushed to open letters of credit ahead of fiscal tightening. \u201cVehicle imports peaked in November and December 2025 before moderating in the subsequent months.\n\nHowever, imports rebounded noticeably in May 2026, likely reflecting a surge in letters of credit opened ahead of the 50 percent duty surcharge introduced by the fiscal authorities to cool demand,\u201d Jafferjee explained.\n\nThe temporary surcharge, initially enacted for three months, expires in mid-August 2026 and is widely expected to be extended.\n\nCrucially, Jafferjee\u2019s analysis highlights that the rapid credit growth bankrolling vehicle purchases is being fuelled by a balance sheet expansion across non-bank financial institutions.\n\nSectoral credit data indicates that lending to financial services, consumer finance, and pawning has grown at rates well above the banking system average. Rather than matching loan growth with deposit mobilisation, many licensed finance companies have increasingly turned to commercial bank borrowings to fund their portfolios, according to him."
  },
