    "description": "Integrity Begins Within:  Internal Affairs Units,  the Keystone of a  New Public Service Culture",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Integrity-Begins-Within-Internal-Affairs-Units-the-Keystone-of-a-New-Public-Service-Culture/172-346689",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785097546.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-27-dailymirrorlk-81bc6da9-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a9bb6e36df295b60463b676f09b6acce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "If properly empowered, adequately resourced and protected from political interference, these units have the potential to shift Sri Lanka\u2019s anti corruption efforts from reactive enforcement to proactive prevention.\n\nThese reforms emerged largely in response to public demands for cleaner governance following the economic crisis and the widespread calls for accountability that followed. The Government has subsequently reinforced these legal reforms through the National Anti Corruption Action Plan, recognising that institutional integrity must become an integral part of everyday public service rather than an occasional compliance exercise.\n\nAmong the most innovative features of the current reform programme is the establishment of Internal Affairs Units within government institutions. Unlike traditional disciplinary\u00a0 divisions that often become active only after allegations emerge, IAUs are intended to function as \u2018institutional guardians of integrity\u2019. Their responsibilities extend far beyond handling complaints. They are expected to identify corruption risks through systematic assessments, recommend practical measures to minimise those risks, monitor compliance with anti-corruption laws and ethical standards, oversee conflict-of-interest management, encourage compliance with asset declaration requirements, coordinate institutional integrity assessments, and serve as the principal liaison with CIABOC. Equally important, they provide secure channels through which employees and members of the public can report corruption-related concerns. This represents a significant conceptual shift. Corruption prevention is no longer viewed solely as the responsibility of a central investigative agency. Every public institution is expected to become accountable for identifying its own vulnerabilities and strengthening its own systems."
