    "chunk_text": "Among the most innovative features of the current reform programme is the establishment of Internal Affairs Units within government institutions. Unlike traditional disciplinary\u00a0 divisions that often become active only after allegations emerge, IAUs are intended to function as \u2018institutional guardians of integrity\u2019. Their responsibilities extend far beyond handling complaints. They are expected to identify corruption risks through systematic assessments, recommend practical measures to minimise those risks, monitor compliance with anti-corruption laws and ethical standards, oversee conflict-of-interest management, encourage compliance with asset declaration requirements, coordinate institutional integrity assessments, and serve as the principal liaison with CIABOC. Equally important, they provide secure channels through which employees and members of the public can report corruption-related concerns. This represents a significant conceptual shift. Corruption prevention is no longer viewed solely as the responsibility of a central investigative agency. Every public institution is expected to become accountable for identifying its own vulnerabilities and strengthening its own systems.\n\nThese countries increasingly view corruption prevention as a core responsibility of every public institution rather than the exclusive mandate of specialised enforcement agencies. The common lesson is clear. Countries achieve lasting improvements not because they prosecute more offenders, but because they build institutions where corruption becomes increasingly difficult to commit and easier to detect."
