    "md5": "92db8099d87c52d451f1bfa9749552e4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1145,
    "chunk_text": "The union also claimed that teachers had been instructed to report for duty and travel to Jaffna to participate in the distribution programme despite the project allegedly lacking Education Ministry approval.\n\nThe parents' union has also requested the Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA) to inspect the quality of the donated goods before they are distributed.\n\nSome parents had previously claimed that individual classes collected goods worth more than Rs. 24,000, while also complaining about the financial burden imposed on families. The union had further alleged that students were earlier asked to contribute Rs. 500 each for Vesak lantern decorations.\n\nA senior Education Ministry official previously told the Daily Mirror that the ministry was unaware of the programme and reiterated that no approval had been granted for such an initiative.\n\nThe Daily Mirror previously made several attempts to obtain a response from the Thurstan College principal, but those efforts were unsuccessful. Government policy maintains that while voluntary donations cannot be prohibited, schools have no legal authority to compel parents to make contributions."
