    "chunk_text": "He said that many of the individuals believed to be orchestrating the attacks are currently operating from overseas, while the intended victims are often relatives or associates of rival gang members. Such attacks, he said, are carried out to intimidate opponents and send a message to competing criminal networks.\n\nReferring to the fatal shooting in Bellanwila on July 25, he said that police arrested two suspects within an hour of the incident. According to investigators, one of those arrested is believed to be the gunman while the other allegedly rode the motorcycle used in the attack. Police also found evidence indicating that both suspects were heavily addicted to narcotics.\n\nThe Police Spokesman further revealed that investigators are increasingly finding that drugs, rather than cash, are being used as payment for contract killings. He said some suspects are allegedly willing to carry out murders in exchange for small quantities of narcotics.\n\nExplaining why shootings appear to occur in clusters after relatively quiet periods, ASP Wootler said criminal groups wait until they have located their intended targets and identified opportunities when police presence is limited before carrying out attacks.\n\nHe acknowledged that it is impossible to deploy police officers at every location but said law enforcement agencies continue to rely on intelligence-led operations to prevent violent crime. He said that police will continue to take strict action against organized criminal groups and those involved in drug-related violence.\n\nShootings reported across the country between July 11 - July 25\n\nGunmen riding a motorcycle opened fire at a house in the Neboda, Matugama on July 11, although no injuries were reported.\n\nA suspected drug trafficker snatched a traffic police officer's service firearm and shot at him during a narcotics raid in Katunayake on July 15. The officer was injured."
