    "description": "Unregulated digital currency use by tourists and expats sparks concerns over formal forex drain",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Unregulated-digital-currency-use-by-tourists-and-expats-sparks-concerns-over-formal-forex-drain/273-346761",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785172763.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-27-dailymirrorlk-f828e716-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0f6cd905bcfc10f7bdf1dc9bd6591f64",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "Unregulated digital currency use by tourists and expats sparks concerns over formal forex drain\n\nA leading legal expert has raised concerns over the growing use of cryptocurrency in Sri Lanka\u2019s informal sector, warning that widespread digital asset transactions by foreign tourists and emigrating locals are bypassing the formal banking channels and directly impacting the country\u2019s foreign exchange inflows.\n\nSudath Perera Associates Partner and Head of Corporate and Commercial Law Dushyantha Perera recently observed that popular tourist destinations such as Unawatuna are seeing a proliferation of unregulated crypto exchanges.\n\n\u201cOne thing that struck me is how prevalent cryptocurrency use is for cross-border capital transfers and current transactions, particularly among the Russian and Ukrainian tourists,\u201d Perera noted, following a recent visit to the southern coast.\n\nEstablishments openly offering to buy and sell crypto have emerged alongside traditional tourist infrastructure, enabling the visitors to bring funds in and out of the country outside the formal financial system.\n\nThis parallel financial ecosystem is believed to be impacting the state tax revenues and domestic economic indicators. Although Sri Lanka is aggressively pursuing national targets of 5 million arrivals and US $ 8 billion in tourism income, the authorities have been attempting to understand a recent decline in individual tourist spending.\n\n\u201cMost of these Russian and Ukrainian visitors trade in crypto to get funds in and out of the country and to pay for things locally,\u201d Perera stated.\n\nHe suggested that this shift to the crypto space should be closely evaluated alongside the depreciation of the rupee and changing tourist demographics."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-27-dailymirrorlk-f828e716",
