    "md5": "f62165cdfd6419d565753ba8975d3254",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1612,
    "chunk_text": "Findings of COPE, COPA, COPF \u2013 are they media shows?\n\nTwo amendments to the Parliamentary Standing Orders (SO) were passed on April 7 allowing the Committee on Public Accounts (COPA) and the Committee on Public Enterprises (COPE), to directly report to the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) or the Inspector General of Police (IGP) when they find evidence of fraud, corruption or bribery during their deliberations, for taking necessary action regarding such matters.\n\nAccordingly, a new paragraph was inserted into SO 119(4) related to COPA, stating: \u201cIf the Committee on Public Accounts, in examining the reports presented to the Committee by the Auditor-General, comes to the conclusion that serious financial frauds, acts of bribery or corruption have occurred, the Committee may refer such matters directly to the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption or to the Inspector-General of Police.\u201d\n\nA separate paragraph is also added to SO 120 (4) related to COPE, stating the same in respect of findings during its sessions.\n\nAccordingly, Parliament on July 21 approved two motions to refer reports submitted to it by the COPA and the COPE, containing findings from investigations into various institutions, to the Attorney General and the CIABOC, respectively.\n\nReport of COPA inquiry conducted on October 24 last year on the scheme implemented from 1 May 2022 to 15 September 2023 for issuing permits to Sri Lankans employed overseas to import fully electric vehicles using foreign remittances is thus to be referred to the Attorney General."
  },
