    "md5": "865d97c90bf0e813a7c4c4dd6ec31a67",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "To Solve Street Dog Issue; Government must work with animal welfare groups\n\nIndia\u2019s Supreme Court passed a series of key rulings on street dogs between August 2025 and May 2026 (photo credit\u00a0 Pic 1 Mani Gautam(L) Pic 2 Anurag Jamwal(R))\n\nDrives launched by governments to eliminate certain undesirable phenomena are politically very convenient.\u00a0 Such programmes bolster people\u2019s faith in government at times when it begins to wane.\n\nWe already have the drive to eliminate dengue by 2030, and now the Department of Animal Production and Health (DAPH) and the National Rabies Control Programme have announced a drive to completely eliminate rabies by the same year.\n\nThis is laudable. But a public outcry arose after this announcement because it was linked to a \u2018don\u2019t feed street dogs\u2019 media campaign, causing animal welfare activists to react swiftly.\n\nSocial media posts by various government sources, including some pradeshiya sabhas (local government bodies) warned that feeding stray dogs was an anti-social, retrogressive activity because it was the main reason behind the \u2018street dog population explosion\u2019 we have seen in Sri Lanka over the past two decades.\n\nThere has always been a very vocal anti street dog lobby, and these official announcements fed fuel to the fire, with private social media posts urging people to photograph those seen feeding street dogs, and report them to the police. It looked like the start of a witch hunt.\n\nSri Lanka officially banned the culling of dogs twenty years ago, in 2006, replacing extermination by the most inhuman methods with a more humane CNVR (Catch-Neuter-Vaccinate-Release), Initially,\u00a0 most street dogs were caught, treated and released, with several foreign organisations, too, helping."
  },
