    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9344a1fb09c4ad6c9113753b45dbb367",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1697,
    "chunk_text": "SJB, Govt. trade barbs over BASL amid judges\u2019 retirement age controversy\n\nOpposition Leader Sajith Premadasa,\u00a0Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Deputy Minister Chathuranga Abeysinghe\n\nThe Main Opposition Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) yesterday rejected allegations by the Government that it offered political inducements, including the post of Chief Minister, to Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL) President Rajeev Amarasuriya in exchange for opposing the Government\u2019s proposal to increase the retirement age of Supreme Court judges, as the Government confirmed it intends to pursue a constitutional amendment to implement the reform.\n\nAddressing the opening of an SJB office in Ratnapura, Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa described the allegation as \u201cthe latest lie of the NPP\u201d and denied that the SJB had ever offered political favours.\n\n\u201cThe SJB has never given political bribes to anyone. We stand with a straight backbone for an independent judiciary,\u201d he said.\n\nPremadasa accused NPP MPs of using parliamentary privilege to make false allegations, referring to claims that the SJB had promised the Chief Minister\u2019s position in return for opposition to the Government\u2019s proposal to extend the retirement age of Supreme Court judges.\n\nHe alleged that the proposal would undermine judicial independence by prolonging the tenure of incumbent judges while existing vacancies in the Superior Courts remained unfilled, delaying the progression of judicial officers through the judicial hierarchy.\n\nPremadasa also said the SJB had consistently defended the independence of the judiciary and had never attempted to exert pressure on judges through parliamentary committees."
  },
