    "chunk_text": "Leading Presidents\u2019 Counsel voice serious concerns\n\nColombo, July 28 (Daily Mirror) - Five leading Presidents' Counsel, including a member of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Leadership Council and the Executive Council Lal Wijenayake, yesterday protested the government's move to extend the retirement age of judges by two years.\n\nMr Wijenayake, flanked by four others, told a media briefing that the NPP leadership asked him to be silent about the move. \u201cNPP as a party has not made any decision to extend the retirement age of judges. I was asked to keep silent when I mentioned it to the party hierarchy. I have informed the party leadership that I am not agreeable to the move. Then the party called for a meeting of its most powerful committee. I told this committee that I am against this move. I have been in the legal profession for more than 50 years and have been engaged in leftist politics for many years. Our duty is to create a civilised society,\u201d Mr Wijenayake said.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Saliya Peiris said the extension of the retirement age of judges could result in a conflict of interest in the event a petition is filed against the amendment once it is presented to Parliament. \u201cThere will be a question as to how judges themselves could come out with a judgment on their own retirement age,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cThe reasons which have been put forward by the government for the constitutional amendment pertaining to the retirement age of judges are that it is done to do away with the backlog of cases. There is no point in extending the retirement age of Supreme Court and Appeal Court judges as most of the backlog cases are pending in magistrate and district courts. Also, there are a number of laws which can be amended to ease out the backlog. One is the amendment of divorce laws to ease the pending divorce cases,\u201d he said."
