    "chunk_text": "US$2.5Mn cyber heist; COPF covers up the top brass: UNP\n\nColombo, July 28 (Daily Mirror) - The United National Party (UNP) yesterday charged that the Committee on Public Finance (COPF) had failed to identify the shortcomings of senior officials in connection with the cybercrime, while highlighting that the US$2.5 million heist had occurred due to the failure of the Finance Minister and the Secretary to the Finance Ministry to monitor the transition of powers from the Central Bank to the Public Debt Management Office.\n\nThe UNP said in a statement that the COPF report discusses the hacking and the theft, but has defended the higher officials and the top brass whose shortcomings led to the theft.\n\n\"The report uses terms such as 'systematic failure' and 'procedural gaps' but has been careful not to mention anyone who could be held accountable. The easiest way to escape accountability in today's politics is to blame the system for any shortcoming rather than identify those who are responsible.\n\n\"The Treasury Secretary is responsible for public funds, while the Finance Minister must take responsibility on behalf of the political leadership of the government.\" The report clearly states that there were shortcomings in the internal handling of the transition, the provision of guidelines and the administration. However, no allegations have been made against any senior official. Instead, only lower-ranking officers have been blamed. Accordingly, four officials have already been interdicted for neglect of duty. The report refers only to issues relating to language and coordination. This amounts to double standards. Higher officials who designed the systems appear to have defended themselves. Negligence relating to IT systems actually invited cyber thieves to act.\n\n\"The government has attempted to portray the theft as a sophisticated cybercrime. This is not the truth."
  },
