    "chunk_size_bytes": 1743,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe are at a point of fragility,\u201d Eric Brocardi, spokesperson for France\u2019s national firefighters federation, told BFMTV on Tuesday. \u201cWe will continue attacking this fire despite the forecast rise in temperatures.\u201d The fire, burning since last week, has at times turned into a self-feeding firestorm as it spread across an area four times the size of Paris and forced 220,000 people to evacuate.\n\nThe overnight progress in Gironde stood in contrast with clearer gains in Landes, where authorities said a separate wildfire had been fixed and more than 15,000 people were allowed to return home on Monday night in Sanguinet, Parentis-en-Born and parts of Biscarrosse. Residents of Biscarrosse\u2019s aviation village and a large campsite were among those allowed back, though several other neighbourhoods remained out of bounds. \u201cThe fire is fixed, which is good news in the Landes,\u201d French President Emmanuel Macron said on Monday evening. Authorities warned that the fire there had not been extinguished and that hot spots, wind and drought could still cause flare-ups for days or even weeks.\n\nBoth French fire zones faced a fresh test as temperatures started rising again on Tuesday. France\u2019s national weather service placed Gironde under a yellow heat warning from midday, forecasting inland temperatures of 33 to 35C. South-east winds were expected across the department, along with westerly sea breezes on the coast. According to the Interior Ministry, more than 116,000 hectares have burned across France so far this year. The European Union\u2019s Copernicus climate service said western Europe recorded its hottest June on record, with extreme heat and widespread dryness helping fires spread and intensify in France and Spain."
