    "chunk_text": "No crime should go unpunished\n\nPoliticians uphold the rule of law only when they are out of power. Their respect for the law and their love for democracy last only until an opportunity comes their way to savour power. Thereafter, they lay bare their true faces and bulldoze their way through; they even consider the Constitution an obstacle in their path. This is the name of the game in Sri Lankan politics.\n\nThe Badulla High Court (HC) yesterday sentenced SJB MP Chaminda Wijesiri to one-and-a-half years\u2019 imprisonment for assaulting and injuring two police officers in February 2019. The prosecution stated that MP Wijesiri, his driver and a police officer attached to the Ministerial Security Division had stopped a police vehicle and issued death threats to the victims before setting upon them in the middle of the Bandarawela town. The MP was angry that the police vehicle had overtaken his SUV.\n\nMP Wijesiri may not have thought he would have to face the consequences of his action. All he can do now is to appeal against the HC judgement.\n\nJVP-NPP propagandists are in overdrive, condemning MP Wijesiri and the entire Opposition, which they say is full of lawbreakers. However, Wijesiri committed the offence at issue when he was in the UNP, which was on honeymoon with the JVP. The UNP-led UNF, which formed the Yahapalana government in 2015 with the SLFP-led UPFA as a partner, was dependent on the JVP for survival in 2019. The JVP threw its weight behind the UNP when the then President Maithripala Sirisena fell out with Prime Minister Ranil Wickremesinghe, sacked the latter, appointed former President Mahinda Rajapaksa as Prime Minister and tried to dissolve Parliament as he could not muster a working majority, in late 2018."
