    "language": "en",
    "md5": "85ae8177f9773779d9d9a3fc4a2a90ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1694,
    "chunk_text": "Delivering the Chairman\u2019s Address, CTTA Chairman Lushantha de Silva reflected on the association\u2019s distinguished 132-year legacy in promoting governance, transparency and integrity within Sri Lanka\u2019s tea trade.\n\nHighlighting the industry\u2019s future priorities, he stressed that Sri Lanka must increasingly focus on value addition, premium branding, innovation and product diversification to maximise the value of every kilogramme of tea produced. Strengthening branded Ceylon Tea, he said, would generate higher export earnings, improve the livelihoods of producers and smallholders and further enhance Sri Lanka\u2019s reputation as the global benchmark for premium-quality tea.\n\nDelivering the keynote address, Dr. Woldemichael described Sri Lanka\u2019s tea industry as one of the country\u2019s strongest ambassadors on the global stage and acknowledged its significant contribution to national economic recovery. Reflecting on the country\u2019s progress since the economic crisis of 2022, she noted that inflation has declined substantially, tax revenue has more than doubled, foreign reserves have strengthened, public debt has fallen and debt restructuring is nearing completion through sustained reforms, fiscal discipline and accountability.\n\nWhile acknowledging these achievements, she cautioned that the external challenges, including the geopolitical tensions in the Middle East and disruptions to the international shipping routes, continue to create uncertainty for the export-oriented industries. She reaffirmed the IMF\u2019s commitment to supporting Sri Lanka in achieving resilient, durable and inclusive economic growth while safeguarding the hard-earned gains of the recent reforms."
  },
