    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ae04f67898010fdfdb8206e5ad730a1c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "A taste of Yaal plated in Colombo\n\nThere are meals that simply satisfy hunger, and then there are those that transport you. At Sheraton Colombo this week, the journey begins with a bowl of pala kanji, gathers heat over a Jaffna crab curry and ends on the gentle sweetness of pongal.\n\n\u2018Yaal: A Journey Through Jaffna\u2019 is Colombo Kitchen\u2019s week-long exploration of northern Sri Lanka\u2019s culinary character. Led by Sheraton Colombo\u2019s Executive Chef Ramesh Kularatnam, the festival opened to a full house on Monday, which shows that Colombo\u2019s appetite for regional food experiences is growing.\n\nThe festival does not ease diners cautiously into Jaffna cuisine. It presents the north in all its boldness: crab rasam, sura puttu, chicken poriyal, Jaffna pittu, mutton curry, fish head curry and palmyrah-based sweets. These are dishes with presence, built around spice, texture and cooking traditions that resist being reduced to the milder comforts of a conventional hotel buffet.\n\nMuch of the evening unfolds around a series of dedicated stations. Pala kanji offers a soothing beginning before the Jaffna-style fried street-food counter shifts the mood towards something livelier. At the action station, paper-thin dosas are prepared before diners, bringing the rhythm and theatre of live cooking into the room.\n\nBut it is difficult to walk past the curries without stopping.\n\nThe Jaffna crab curry is the kind of centrepiece that demands both attention and a willingness to get one\u2019s hands involved. Close behind are the mutton curry and much sought-after fish head curry, completing a trio that captures the depth and intensity for which northern Sri Lankan cooking is celebrated.\n\nThere is also a quiet visual code running through the dining room. The chefs in red aprons have travelled from Jaffna; those in black belong to Sheraton\u2019s Colombo team."
  },
  {
