    "md5": "d128b90a342479b6ff874c927a024a0b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1597,
    "chunk_text": "Prison overcrowding:  Revision of remand prisoners key to solution\n\nThe most practical is to first focus on remand prisoners who amount to around 70 percent of the total\n\nThe Department reported to Parliament that there was a backlog of 17,671 reports due from the Narcotics Division by\u00a0end 2024\n\n\u201cThe degree of civilisation of a society can be judged by entering its prisons.\u201d\n\nThis aphorism is sometimes attributed to the Russian author Fyodor Dostoevsky and sometimes to the French philosopher Voltaire. Whoever said it, it rings true.\n\nDescribing the horrific overcrowding in our prisons is no longer necessary. The Negombo calamity has done that better than anyone could. What is now needed is quick action to prevent a recurrence and thereby drag our civilisation out from the ditch.\n\nThe most practical is to first focus on remand prisoners who amount to around 70 percent of the total. Just in Colombo, New Magazine and Colombo Remand Prisons held 29,745 unconvicted men in 2024, more than double the 12,592 housed in 2020. Women remandees were housed in Welikada: 2,692 in 2024 (more than double the number remanded in 2020). All these numbers are likely to be higher in 2026 because of the police wanting to score points under the President\u2019s anti-drug campaign.\n\nRemand prisoners are presumed innocent. There can be no debate about the need to treat remand prisoners in a humane manner. If effective measures can be taken to reduce the overcrowding in remand prisons, it will be easier to address problems affecting the minority who have been found guilty.\u00a0 \u00a0Sections 54 and 83"
  },
  {
