    "chunk_size_bytes": 1377,
    "chunk_text": "In 2019, 60,347 devices were active in Brazil, which amounted to under 9 percent of the prison population in that country. Jair Bolsanaro, the former President of Brazil was restricted to his home wearing an electronic anklet while awaiting trial. According to The National Secretariat of Penal Policies, the cost of an electronic bracelet in Brazil was around R$200 (around Rs 10,800) per remandee. It is stated that the cost per monitoring a prisoner through this method is 70 percent lower than remanding that individual in prison.\n\nFrom the above, it can be seen that actions such as a new act or amendments, the establishment of the necessary technical and human facilities at the police stations, the procurement of service providers must be taken. They should, ideally, be taken in parallel.\n\nThis solution can complement the legislative changes proposed above and the improvement of the Government Analyst\u2019s functions. In combination, they will reduce the horrific overcrowding of remand prisons. They will not by themselves fix all that is wrong with our prisons. But they will create the space for retraining\u00a0 prison and police officials, and for ensuring Sri Lankan prisons meet the UN\u2019s Standard Minimum Rules for the Treatment of Prisoners (the Mandela Rules). By this means we will hopefully get prisons that reflect our aspirations to become more civilised."
