    "md5": "f0f1ea39637d68b9f0f8f87bed5052a3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "These are important signals. However, bold announcements and high-profile raids alone will not deliver a sustainable victory. It is easy to announce measures; it is far harder to implement them effectively. Much of what has been done in the past, and even some current operations,\u00a0 remains essentially reactive, responding after harm has already occurred. While it is better late than never to mount a serious national effort, these processes must now be made genuinely meaningful. They require clearly defined immediate, mid-term and long-term goals with measurable milestones and regular evaluation. If the campaign is to succeed, it must urgently strengthen four critical institutional pillars while placing prevention at the centre, driven by proactive and strategic intelligence.\n\nSuccess demands that rehabilitation be treated as a professional, evidence-based discipline. This requires standardised protocols, adequate trained counsellors, meaningful family engagement, robust aftercare and independent evaluation of long-term outcomes. Capacity must be scaled dramatically. Without these, the rehabilitative pillar risks becoming a revolving door that wastes resources and undermines public confidence.\n\nThe drug trade has evolved. Traffickers use encrypted communications, digital finance and sophisticated concealment. Relying primarily on traditional raid-and-arrest tactics guarantees diminishing returns.\n\nSri Lanka needs accelerated investment in advanced forensic drug analysis for network mapping, expanded digital and cyber forensics to clear backlogs, strengthened financial investigation for asset tracing, and data analytics for intelligence fusion across agencies. Officers must be equipped and trained for this more complex environment. Without this technical upgrade, the campaign will remain one step behind adaptive criminal networks."
  },
  {
