    "md5": "d94ddc94336029f706d4620b4a866d45",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "Pathfinder Foundation, Indian High Commission and ICCR hold symposium on strengthening Pali language studies\n\nThe Pathfinder Foundation, in collaboration with the High Commission of India in Colombo and the Indian Council for Cultural Relations (ICCR), held a symposium on \u201dStrengthening Pali Language Studies in Sri Lanka\u201d on 24 July in Colombo.\n\nThe symposium brought together eminent members of the Maha Sangha, distinguished academics, policymakers, and education specialists to discuss the future of Pali language studies in Sri Lanka and explore practical strategies for preserving and promoting one of South Asia\u2019s most significant classical languages.\n\nThe event was held with the blessings and participation of leading members of the Maha Sangha, including Most Venerable Dr. Niyangoda Vijithasiri, Anunayaka Thero of the Malwatta Mahavihara; Most Venerable Dr. Ittapana Dhammalankara Maha Nayaka Thero of the Kotte Sri Kalyani Samagri Dharma Maha Sangha Sabha; Most Venerable Shastrapathi Ganthune Assaji Maha Nayaka Thero of the Amarapura Chulagandhi Chapter; Senior Professor Ven. Kotapitiye Rahula, Anunayaka of the Kotte Sri Kalyani Samagri Dharma Maha Sangha Sabha; Most Venerable Dr. Waleboda Gunasiri Anunayaka Thero of the Sri Lanka Ramanna Nikaya; and Most Venerable Waskaduwe Mahindawansa Thero of the Amarapura Sambuddha Sasanodaya Maha Nikaya.\n\nThe symposium also brought together representatives of the three main Nikayas, leading scholars in Pali and Buddhist Studies, Indian High Commissioner Santosh Jha, Deputy High Commissioner Maitrey Kulkarni, Pathfinder Foundation Founder Milinda Moragoda, Swami Vivekananda Cultural Centre Acting Director Roshni Thomson, Urumayake Ulpatha General Secretary R.A.D. Sirisena, university academics, Pirivena educators, and education experts."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailyftlk-0ae650be",
    "num": "dailyftlk-0ae650be",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Pathfinder Foundation, Indian High Commission and ICCR hold symposium on strengthening Pali language studies",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Pathfinder-Foundation-Indian-High-Commission-and-ICCR-hold-symposium-on-strengthening-Pali-language-studies/56-795292",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785271788.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailyftlk-0ae650be-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9ce3c67de32a7d3dd454be313c955438",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1660,
    "chunk_text": "The symposium also brought together representatives of the three main Nikayas, leading scholars in Pali and Buddhist Studies, Indian High Commissioner Santosh Jha, Deputy High Commissioner Maitrey Kulkarni, Pathfinder Foundation Founder Milinda Moragoda, Swami Vivekananda Cultural Centre Acting Director Roshni Thomson, Urumayake Ulpatha General Secretary R.A.D. Sirisena, university academics, Pirivena educators, and education experts.\n\n\u201cPirivena education remains the centre of Pali knowledge transmission, while universities represent its highest level of academic learning,\u201d Ven. Vijithadhamma Thera said. He observed that several important Pali traditions continue to be preserved within specific monastic lineages in Sri Lanka.\n\nHe noted that Pali education is currently divided among schools, Pirivenas, and universities, emphasising the need for closer collaboration among these institutions to strengthen the discipline.\n\n\u201cThe decline of the humanities in higher education is largely due to limited employment opportunities. Symposiums such as this are therefore essential to strengthening Pali language studies,\u201d he said.\n\nDrawing attention to international examples, Ven. Vijithadhamma Thera noted that countries such as Thailand and Cambodia conduct national examinations in Pali and suggested that Sri Lanka should consider introducing a similar system. He also called on universities to provide greater recognition to creative scholarly works produced in Pali. Highlighting the opportunities offered by emerging technologies, he said artificial intelligence and digital tools could play a transformative role in preserving the language."
  },
