    "chunk_size_bytes": 1674,
    "chunk_text": "Drawing attention to international examples, Ven. Vijithadhamma Thera noted that countries such as Thailand and Cambodia conduct national examinations in Pali and suggested that Sri Lanka should consider introducing a similar system. He also called on universities to provide greater recognition to creative scholarly works produced in Pali. Highlighting the opportunities offered by emerging technologies, he said artificial intelligence and digital tools could play a transformative role in preserving the language.\n\nAddressing the gathering, Indian High Commissioner Santosh Jha said that participating in Buddhist events was much more than a diplomatic responsibility, as it held deep personal significance for him, having been born and raised in Bihar, close to the place where Lord Buddha attained Enlightenment.\n\nHe noted that Pali forms the very foundation of Buddhist knowledge and recalled that in October 2024 the Government of India conferred Classical Language status on Pali. Referring to the remarks of Prime Minister Narendra Modi, he said it is humanity\u2019s responsibility to keep the Pali language alive in order to preserve the original teachings of the Buddha.\n\nHigh Commissioner Jha emphasised that Pali is the language of the earliest Buddhist scriptures, including the Tripitaka, which embodies the timeless message of Dhamma. In a world increasingly affected by conflict, he said, the Buddha\u2019s teachings provide a path towards peace, compassion, and the welfare of all humanity.\n\nHe further observed that Sri Lanka continues to play a unique role in preserving Pali through its use in religious rituals, chanting, and Theravada Buddhist traditions."
