    "md5": "b81a33557207c216d2af52e463cde56a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "If an extension applies to current judges, it changes the rules mid-game. Even if done with no ill intent, it creates a subtle incentive structure where judges approaching retirement could be perceived as aligning with the executive to secure an extension.\n\n\u2022 The 18th Amendment (2010) removed presidential term limits to consolidate executive control.\n\n\u2022 The 20th Amendment (2020) recentralized power over independent commissions and the judiciary.\n\nHistory shows a consistent outcome: every regime that tampered with constitutional safeguards to entrench its authority ultimately suffered severe public backlash and lost power. The electorate inevitably rejects governments that manipulate state institutions for political convenience. Short-term institutional fixes routinely lead to long-term political failure.\n\nDemocratic legitimacy does not come from parliamentary strength alone; it comes from adherence to foundational principles. The independence of the judiciary is not an abstract concept\u2014it relies entirely on public trust.\n\nFor justice to function, the courts must not only be independent; they must be seen to be independent. When a government uses its majority to alter judicial tenures, it weakens public trust in the neutral administration of justice. Once public confidence in the courts is eroded, no parliamentary majority can restore it. Enduring institutional values must always take precedence over temporary political power.\n\nReforms driven by administrative efficiency can still lead to damaging consequences if core institutional boundaries are crossed. A government may argue that extending judges' terms retains experienced legal minds or helps clear court backlogs.\n\nHowever, when a good intention is executed by departing from constitutional norms, the systemic damage far outweighs the short-term benefits. This can create a precedent that future administrations can exploit. Good intentions cannot cure a flawed process."
  },
