    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-938de27d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fac97afc72c4f75ae4efc5d82dc4b247",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "Jay Jay Textile Lanka knitting a brighter, greener future for Batticaloa\n\nMarking a major milestone in Sri Lanka\u2019s regional industrialization journey, Jay Jay Textile Lanka (Pvt) Ltd has officially commenced operations at the Board of Investment (BOI) Textile Manufacturing Zone in Punnakudah, Eravur Pattu.\n\nRepresenting the largest recent investment in the Eastern Province\u2019s textile sector, the state-of-the-art facility is engineered to deliver high-performance knitting, dyeing, and finishing operations built on eco-friendly technology.\n\nCommitted to fostering a balanced regional development, the project has already generated over 350 direct employment opportunities for individuals in the Batticaloa District, offering stable and dignified career opportunities closer to home.\n\nBeyond direct employment, the facility has revitalized the regional micro-economy. By integrating local service providers for daily operational logistics including employee transport, catering and accommodation, the facility indirectly impacts a substantial number of community members, breathing fresh economic vigour into Eravur Pattu.\n\nFurthermore, by establishing advanced local fabric production, the venture contributes directly to national macroeconomic stability by reducing foreign exchange outflow on imported fabrics.\n\nJay Jay Textile Lanka places employee well-being at the core of its operational strategy. Recognizing that workforce health directly drives productivity, the company provides 100 percent free daily meals and comprehensive transport facilities for all staff members. This initiative eliminates logistical and financial burdens for workers, ensuring a safe, dignified, and supportive environment.\n\nOperating in line with the highest global environmental standards, the Punnakudah plant demonstrates that large-scale industrial growth can coexist with resource conservation, by implementing the following measures."
  },
