    "description": "Arrogance of power",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/arrogance-of-power-6/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785345120.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-islandlk-cb1a0d66-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "695bd41b56477630e173b0ed7253723e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1608,
    "chunk_text": "Arrogance of power\n\nSri Lankans are gluttons for punishment. All governments they elect with steamroller majorities invariably undermine democracy. They voted overwhelmingly for the SLFP-led United Front in 1970, the UNP in 1977, the SLFP-led UPFA in 2010, and the SLPP in 2020 only to be left with regrets. But they did so again in 2024, when they elected the JVP-led NPP with a mammoth majority, which is to be misused to amend the Constitution to extend the retirement ages of the superior court judges.\n\nThe JVP-NPP government is impervious to reason. The extension of the retirement ages of sitting judges is bound to raise concerns about a quid pro quo, severely eroding public confidence in the judiciary. Worse, if the government goes ahead with its plan, petitions against the constitutional amendment to be presented will be heard by the judges who are to benefit from the government move at issue. This will give rise to a conflict of interest and violate a core rule of natural justice that prevents bias\u2014\u2019no one should be a judge in his or her own case\u2019. Therefore, the proposed constitutional amendment must be presented to the people at a referendum for their approval besides being passed by a special majority in Parliament, legal experts have argued. This argument is convincing.\n\nInitially, the government tried to extend the retirement ages of only the judges of the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court. Now, in a desperate bid to cover up its true intention, it is going to make a bigger mess; it is trying to extend the retirement ages of all judges, according to media reports."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-islandlk-cb1a0d66",
    "num": "islandlk-cb1a0d66",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Arrogance of power",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/arrogance-of-power-6/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785345120.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-islandlk-cb1a0d66-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5ee199777113529ecc26ab2dfdaf4626",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1288,
    "chunk_text": "Initially, the government tried to extend the retirement ages of only the judges of the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court. Now, in a desperate bid to cover up its true intention, it is going to make a bigger mess; it is trying to extend the retirement ages of all judges, according to media reports.\n\nThe JVP-NPP government seems to have a very low opinion of the intelligence of the public. Otherwise, it would not have claimed that its move to raise the retirement ages of judges was intended to clear the backlog of cases. Judicial delays cannot be blamed on the existing retirement ages of judges. Most of all, the government has no moral right to make such a claim while refusing to fill four vacancies each in the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court. Vacancies that remains unfilled aggravate judicial delays, which are mainly due to a multitude of interconnected causes, including the inability of the Government Analyst\u2019s Department to issue reports expeditiously, procedural delays and outdated practices, lack of alternative dispute resolution mechanisms, shortages of judges, delays on the part of the police and the Attorney General\u2019s Department, inadequate court infrastructure and resources, lawyers\u2019 practices that prolong litigation, and frequent adjournments."
  },
