    "md5": "6dd654e2c4bb820d5db577dd69846b29",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1956,
    "chunk_text": "The JVP-NPP government seems to have a very low opinion of the intelligence of the public. Otherwise, it would not have claimed that its move to raise the retirement ages of judges was intended to clear the backlog of cases. Judicial delays cannot be blamed on the existing retirement ages of judges. Most of all, the government has no moral right to make such a claim while refusing to fill four vacancies each in the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court. Vacancies that remains unfilled aggravate judicial delays, which are mainly due to a multitude of interconnected causes, including the inability of the Government Analyst\u2019s Department to issue reports expeditiously, procedural delays and outdated practices, lack of alternative dispute resolution mechanisms, shortages of judges, delays on the part of the police and the Attorney General\u2019s Department, inadequate court infrastructure and resources, lawyers\u2019 practices that prolong litigation, and frequent adjournments.\n\nIt behoves the JVP-NPP government to take cognisance of the fate of previous individual-centric constitutional amendments. The 18th Amendment enabled the then President Mahinda Rajapaksa to seek a third term, and the 20th Amendment paved the way for Basil Rajapaksa\u2019s return to Parliament while enhancing the executive powers of the then President Gotabaya Rajapaksa. In 2015, Mahinda failed to secure a third term, and in 2022, Basil, Gotabaya and Mahinda had to head for the hills with angry mobs in close pursuit. The two constitutional amendments have been relegated to the dustbin of history, and their architects, beneficiaries and backers are struggling to remain politically relevant. Will the JVP/NPP leaders, blinded by the arrogance of power, make the same mistake?\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
