    "chunk_text": "FOB prices, when measured in Sri Lankan Rupees, showed year-on-year improvement across the cumulative period, rising from Rs. 1,745.10 in 2025 to Rs. 1,810.28 in 2026, largely reflecting the currency depreciation. FOB values in USD terms remained under slight pressure throughout the period, declining from USD 5.86 in 2025 to USD 5.70 in 2026. Consequently, equivalent US Dollar export earnings for the cumulative period declined by approximately USD 41.87 Mn (5.63 percent), falling from USD 743.12 Mn in 2025 to USD 701.25 Mn in 2026.\n\nHigh Grown teas recorded a marginal increase of 0.44 Mn/Kgs, reaching 30.90 Mn/Kgs during the first six months of 2026 compared with 30.46 Mn/Kgs in the corresponding period of 2025. Medium Grown teas experienced a notable decline, decreasing by 1.15 Mn/Kgs to 24.31 Mn/Kgs, compared with 25.46 Mn/Kgs recorded during the same period in 2025.\n\nLow Grown teas, which continued to account for the largest share of national tea production at 58.14 percent, recorded the most significant volume decline. Production fell by 3.17 Mn/Kgs, decreasing to 76.68 Mn/Kgs in 2026 from 79.85 Mn/Kgs in 2025.\n\nAlthough High Grown tea production recorded a slight increase during the first half of 2026, this improvement was insufficient to offset the declines in Medium and Low Grown production. The substantial reduction in Low Grown teas, which account for the largest share of Sri Lanka\u2019s total tea output, was the primary factor contributing to the overall decline in national tea production compared with the corresponding period of 2025.\n\nThe comparison of CTC Tea Production for the January\u2013June 2026 period against the corresponding period in 2025 reflects a strong overall improvement across all elevations, with total production increasing by 1.45 Mn/Kgs, from 11.95 Mn/Kgs in 2025 to 13.41 Mn/Kgs in 2026."
