    "chunk_text": "In the Low Grown category, which continues to command the highest average price among the elevation categories, the Rupee value remained largely stable, declining marginally by Rs.3.79, from Rs. 1,228.85 in 2025 to Rs.1,225.06 in 2026. However, the US Dollar average declined by 0.28 from USD 4.13 to USD 3.85, reflecting a decrease in export value.\n\nFor the period January to June 2026, Sri Lanka\u2019s tea exports declined in volume to 123.10 Mn/Kgs, compared to 126.81 Mn/Kgs during the corresponding period of 2025, reflecting a decrease of approximately 3.71 Mn/Kgs (2.9%). Despite the lower export volume, total export earnings increased to Rs.222.85 billion from Rs.221.29 billion recorded in the same period of 2025.\n\nThe average FOB price also improved to Rs.1,810.28 per kg in 2026, compared to Rs.1,745.10 per kg in 2025. However, in US dollar terms, export earnings declined to approximately USD 701.25 Mn from USD 743.12 Mn, while the average FOB price decreased to USD 5.70 per kg from USD 5.86 per kg, largely reflecting the depreciation of the US dollar value relative to the Sri Lankan Rupee.\n\nSegment-wise, tea in bulk recorded declines in both export quantity and value in Rupee terms, although the average FOB price increased to Rs.1,528.80 per kg from Rs.1,497.12 per kg. Tea in packets, which remained the largest export category, also experienced lower export volumes and a slight decline in export value, while the average FOB price improved to Rs.1,666.07 per kg from Rs.1,623.36 per kg.\n\nTea in bags remained relatively stable in volume but recorded a notable increase in export value, supported by a significant rise in the average FOB price to Rs.2,909.40 per kg. Instant tea recorded strong growth in both export volume and value. However, its average FOB price eased slightly compared to the previous year. Green tea exports declined marginally in volume but registered higher export earnings, with the average FOB price increasing to Rs.4,088.96 per kg from Rs.3,808.94 per kg."
