    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "S&P rating exposes limits of Sri Lanka\u2019s post-crisis recovery\n\nSri Lanka may have moved beyond the immediate threat of another debt or payment crisis; however, its sovereign credit profile remains highly vulnerable to the external shocks, with the pressure on the reserves, rupee and foreign inflows, leaving little room\u00a0for policy error.\n\nS&P Global Ratings this week affirmed Sri Lanka\u2019s long and short-term sovereign credit ratings at \u2018CCC+/C\u2019, with a stable outlook, reflecting expectations that the economic growth and stronger government revenue will sustain fiscal repair over the next six to 12 months, despite the worsening external conditions.\n\nYet, the rating remains deep in speculative-grade territory, indicating that Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to meet its obligations continues to depend on the favourable economic and financial conditions.\n\nThe stable outlook is therefore not a clean bill of health. S&P does not expect a material improvement in sovereign credit support over the next six to 12 months, as the risks to the external demand, inflation and financing conditions remain significant.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s vulnerability is also shifting from the budget to balance of payments.\n\nThe government revenue surged 34 percent in 2025 to 16.7 percent of gross domestic product (GDP), helping narrow the fiscal deficit to 2.3 percent of GDP and lift the primary surplus to a record 5.4 percent. Revenue rose a further 30 percent during the first five months of 2026, despite a high comparison base.\n\nThis has eased the debt-servicing burden. S&P expects the interest payments to decline from 64 percent of revenue in 2024 to 39 percent by 2029, supported by a continued revenue growth and the phasing out of the expensive debt incurred during the 2022 crisis.\n\nEven then, nearly four of every 10 rupees collected by the government would be absorbed by the interest payments, constraining spending on infrastructure, healthcare, education"
