    "chunk_text": "This has eased the debt-servicing burden. S&P expects the interest payments to decline from 64 percent of revenue in 2024 to 39 percent by 2029, supported by a continued revenue growth and the phasing out of the expensive debt incurred during the 2022 crisis.\n\nEven then, nearly four of every 10 rupees collected by the government would be absorbed by the interest payments, constraining spending on infrastructure, healthcare, education\n\nMeanwhile, the fiscal deficit is expected to widen to 5 percent of GDP this year, from 2.3 percent in 2025, as spending rises on post-disaster reconstruction, energy subsidies and assistance to the households hit by the higher living costs. S&P expects the deficit to narrow towards\n\nThe more immediate pressure, however, is external, as the fuel imports more than doubled year-on-year in rupee terms during April and May, while the rupee depreciated around 8 percent against the US dollar in the first half. The gross official reserves fell to US $ 6.45 billion in June, from US $ 6.88 billion in May.\n\nS&P said it expects the current account to swing to a deficit of 1.7 percent of GDP in 2026, as the imports rise and uncertainty clouds the remittances and tourism, two of Sri Lanka\u2019s main foreign-exchange earners. The Middle East accounts for around 40 percent of remittance inflows, while roughly one-third of tourists transit through the aviation hubs in the region. A prolonged conflict could therefore hit Sri Lanka through a higher energy bill, weaker remittances and disrupted tourist traffic."
