    "chunk_text": "S&P said it expects the current account to swing to a deficit of 1.7 percent of GDP in 2026, as the imports rise and uncertainty clouds the remittances and tourism, two of Sri Lanka\u2019s main foreign-exchange earners. The Middle East accounts for around 40 percent of remittance inflows, while roughly one-third of tourists transit through the aviation hubs in the region. A prolonged conflict could therefore hit Sri Lanka through a higher energy bill, weaker remittances and disrupted tourist traffic.\n\nThe authorities have also allowed the exchange rate to absorb part of the shock, instead of aggressively defending the rupee with reserves. The Central Bank raised its policy rate by 100 basis points in May, while the period allowed for converting the export proceeds was shortened.\n\nWhile these measures could protect the reserves and anchor the inflation expectations, they also carry domestic costs through higher borrowing rates, imported inflation and weaker household purchasing power. Consumer inflation accelerated to 6.8 percent by end-June. Economic activity has nevertheless remained stronger than S&P previously expected. Real GDP expanded 4.8 percent in the fourth quarter of 2025 and 5.1 percent in the first quarter of 2026.\n\nGrowth is forecast to slow to 3.8 percent this year, as the higher energy and input costs weigh on the activity, before recovering to 4.2 percent in 2027, as the supply-chain conditions normalise."
