    "md5": "423ad043144f3b69a3943b8344ed95c4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1778,
    "chunk_text": "JKH operating earnings rise  26% to Rs.16.35bn\n\nPremier blue-chip John Keells Holdings PLC (JKH) reported stronger operating earnings for the June quarter, powered by its ports, bunkering, supermarkets and property businesses; however, the rupee depreciation and higher taxes absorbed much of the improvement before it reached the shareholders.\n\nGroup revenue for the first quarter of 2026/27 rose 24 percent year-on-year to Rs.141.65 billion, while the earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation (EBITDA) increased 26 percent to Rs.16.35 billion.\n\nProfit after tax grew by a more modest 8 percent to Rs.773 million. The profit attributable to the equity holders was just Rs.62 million, although this marked a turnaround from the Rs.804 million loss reported a year earlier.\n\nThe sharp divergence between the operating earnings and bottom-line profit was largely caused by a Rs.1.89 billion net exchange loss, up from Rs.388 million in the corresponding quarter. The loss arose mainly from the translation impact of the weaker rupee on the foreign currency-denominated long-term debt of City of Dreams Sri Lanka.\n\nThe rupee fell to Rs.337 against the US dollar at end-June, from Rs.315 at end-March, creating an immediate translation impact on JKH\u2019s net foreign currency exposure of about US $ 100 million.\n\nGroup profit before tax rose 31 percent to Rs.4.08 billion, despite the currency loss. Excluding the net exchange losses, pre-tax profit climbed to Rs.5.97 billion, from Rs.3.49 billion a year earlier, offering a clearer view of the improvement in the underlying earnings. The profit attributable to the shareholders, excluding the exchange losses, reached Rs.1.95 billion, reversing the adjusted loss of Rs.418 million recorded in the previous year."
  },
