    "chunk_text": "Retail continued to generate the largest divisional EBITDA at Rs.6.16 billion; however, this was 16 percent below the previous year, as the lower profitability from the vehicle business outweighed growth at the Keells supermarkets.\n\nThe financial services EBITDA increased 9 percent to Rs.2.02 billion, supported by Nations Trust Bank\u2019s loan growth and the integration of HSBC Sri Lanka\u2019s retail banking business from May. Union Assurance also recorded a double-digit growth in gross written premiums.\n\nLeisure remained the principal drag on the group\u2019s broader recovery. The segment recorded a negative EBITDA of Rs.53 million, compared with a negative Rs.170 million a year earlier. Excluding City of Dreams Sri Lanka, however, leisure swung to a Rs.442 million EBITDA loss, from a profit of Rs.830 million.\n\nThe travel disruption and weaker sentiment arising from the Middle East conflict affected both Sri Lankan and the Maldivian properties. The tourist arrivals during the quarter fell 9 percent in Sri Lanka and 11 percent in the Maldives, with the latter also facing higher fuel and energy costs.\n\nCity of Dreams Sri Lanka moved to a positive EBITDA of Rs.389 million, from a Rs.1.00 billion loss. The comparison, however, is not like-for-like, as the previous-year period included only the Cinnamon Life hotel while the latest quarter covered the hotel, mall, Nuwa hotel and casino-related operations.\n\nAt pre-tax level, City of Dreams remained heavily loss-making. It reported a loss of Rs.5.85 billion, including a Rs.3.72 billion exchange loss on its US dollar-denominated loan as well as Rs.2.52 billion in depreciation, amortisation and interest expenses.\n\nChairperson Krishan Balendra said the project\u2019s net profit continued to be affected by the depreciation and interest charge, while the operating performance across the integrated resort was expected to improve progressively."
