    "md5": "2776e59b5ade94e1241861b7b41c6c9b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "Reviving Sri Lanka\u2019s chequered trilingual literary heritage\n\nThe burning of the Jaffna public library in 1981 destroyed over 97,000 volumes\n\nColombo\u2019s literature scene came back to life with the inaugural Colombo Literary Festival which concluded on Wednesday (29). This trilingual celebration of literature, cinema, history and music brought together authors, artistes and literature lovers from across the country under one roof. At the core of this event is the commitment to provide an equal space to celebrate literary works done in Sinhala, Tamil and English.\n\nSri Lanka carries with it a rich literary heritage that spans over two millennia.\u00a0 From ancient histories and poetry to contemporary novels and books of fiction, the literary past of the island is a reflection of its dense social fabric. The literature scene has evolved, from ancient chronicles and religious writings to modern day fiction and non-fiction books.\n\nBut,\u00a0 over the course of history, Sri Lanka has witnessed some brutal events such as the armed conflict, the burning of the Jaffna Library to repression faced by Tamil writers from time to time in the post-war context. These events have challenged the coexistence of trilingual literary works.\n\nThe burning of the Jaffna public library in 1981 destroyed over 97,000 volumes, including irreplaceable palm-leaf manuscripts, dealing a devastating and permanent blow to Tamil literature, historical records, and cultural identity in Sri Lanka. According to scholars, Jaffna witnessed one of the most flourishing periods of Tamil literary celebration prior to the war. However, amidst the armed conflict, much of the post-war literary works revolved around injustices faced by their own people during the conflict. Many of these literary works were based on eyewitness accounts, reflecting the brutal past."
  },
