    "chunk_text": "The burning of the Jaffna public library in 1981 destroyed over 97,000 volumes, including irreplaceable palm-leaf manuscripts, dealing a devastating and permanent blow to Tamil literature, historical records, and cultural identity in Sri Lanka. According to scholars, Jaffna witnessed one of the most flourishing periods of Tamil literary celebration prior to the war. However, amidst the armed conflict, much of the post-war literary works revolved around injustices faced by their own people during the conflict. Many of these literary works were based on eyewitness accounts, reflecting the brutal past.\n\nThis is why Sri Lanka needs more trilingual literary festivals that celebrate literary works done in all three languages, at least now.\n\nThere\u2019s much emphasis made on the concept of reconciliation, but how could different communities reconcile if they are forced to learn just one language while other languages have been excluded? Apart from Tamil, different communities such as the Malay community in Sri Lanka speak what is called Sri Lankan Malay. This is neither found in Malaysia nor Indonesia. But this unique language too,\u00a0 is at risk of extinction since it is only being conversed by a particular community in the deep\u00a0 South.\n\nBut that doesn\u2019t mean that people should be aware of all languages in a country. India has 22 official languages, 121 major languages and over 19,000 mother tongues and dialects. In Sri Lanka, the Sinhalese dialect differs in different regions of the country and this is the same when it comes to Tamil as well. The Tamil dialect spoken by the Malaiyaha community is different to that spoken in Jaffna. The Tamil dialect in the Eastern province is even more different. This is the beauty of this culturally diverse nation."
  },
