    "md5": "c396297a3b52de3a05ad7721e92cfd4e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "Is retirement age extension issue aimed at a particular judge?\n\nOpposition parties are not really concerned about the independence of the judiciary, given their track record\n\nThe government, after its announcement on the Cabinet decision, cannot deny the allegation that it did not consult the stakeholders on the matter\n\nExperience matters when it comes to efficiency in any sector, but it is not something that the government is going to infuse into the judiciary by extending judges\u2019 retirement age\n\nDisregarding weeks-long agitations by the Opposition parties and a section of the legal fraternity against its move to extend the retirement age of the judges, the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government at last has clarified its stance on the matter.\n\nThe Cabinet had on Monday approved a proposal to go ahead with the move, allaying speculations on the existence of such a move and whether it concerns judges of all courts or only the superior courts.\n\nHealth and Media Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa stated that the purpose of the proposal was to accelerate the disposal of the large backlog of court cases using the experience of senior judges.\n\nCertain individuals of the Opposition and the media outlets aligned to them started the debate on this matter weeks ago, accusing that the proposed Constitutional amendment is aimed at giving service extension to a particular individual, namely\u00a0 Chief Justice Preethi Padman Surasena. Their whole protest campaign seemed to have been based on this premise which they never substantiated.\n\nThey initially questioned as to why the government was interested in extending only the retirement age of the judges of the Supreme Court and the Court of Appeal -- instead of judges of all courts. The leaders of the government were tightlipped until the opposing groups built a considerable public opinion in favour of their premise. The government\u2019s deafening silence on the matter created suspicion even in the minds of its loyalists."
  },
