    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "They initially questioned as to why the government was interested in extending only the retirement age of the judges of the Supreme Court and the Court of Appeal -- instead of judges of all courts. The leaders of the government were tightlipped until the opposing groups built a considerable public opinion in favour of their premise. The government\u2019s deafening silence on the matter created suspicion even in the minds of its loyalists.\n\nThe government, after its announcement on the Cabinet decision, cannot deny the allegation that it did not consult the stakeholders on the matter. Before the announcement it was only an idea just mooted, when there were opportunities to consider other views.\n\nYet, neither the government\u2019s main contention that extension of the judges\u2019 retirement age would help accelerate the disposal of the aforementioned backlog,\u00a0 nor the opposing groups\u2019 argument that\u00a0 judicial independence would be threatened by such an extension, is very convincing.\n\nExperience matters when it comes to efficiency in any sector, but it is not something that the government is going to infuse into the judiciary by extending judges\u2019 retirement age. Experience already exists. The backlog of cases was gathered while that experience was in practice.\n\nExperience of the judges, especially that of the superior court judges would help accelerate disposal of cases when it is coupled with new strategies implemented with new resources pumped into the system. Even then, newcomers who are not so experienced would take over a part of the job again, once the extension period of the current judges is lapsed, i.e. new judges would be promoted to the superior courts from the lower courts."
