    "md5": "1dd978896a8361dd1d01a2da988a4fdc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "Experience of the judges, especially that of the superior court judges would help accelerate disposal of cases when it is coupled with new strategies implemented with new resources pumped into the system. Even then, newcomers who are not so experienced would take over a part of the job again, once the extension period of the current judges is lapsed, i.e. new judges would be promoted to the superior courts from the lower courts.\n\nIf they fail to prove their allegation, it would backfire \u2013 meaning it is they who want to prevent a particular judge from being given an extension. This is an allegation that would prevent such extensions in the future as well, as it could be levelled at any time against any government that attempts to do the same. However, it is too late for the government to allay that fear as the Opposition has already sold that idea to a considerable segment of the people.\n\nOpposition parties demand a referendum to approve the government\u2019s move. However, the Article 83 of the Constitution clearly specifies the circumstances when people\u2019s approval has to be sought through a referendum. It provides for the President to hold referendums to amend the Articles of it concerning the name of the country, nature of the state, sovereignty of the people, national flag, National Anthem, National Day, place of Buddhism, religious freedom, freedom from torture, term of the President and the term of the Parliament.\n\nBesides, it is obvious that\u00a0 Article 83 if itself\u00a0 requires a referendum to amend,\u00a0 those articles also need a referendum to be amended.\n\nAlso, Bills or provisions of Bills that are certified by the Cabinet or determined by the Supreme Court as requiring the approval of the people, and those matters which, in the opinion of the President, are of national importance have to be put to the people through a referendum. The president too,\u00a0 can get a Bill that is already rejected by Parliament passed through a referendum."
