    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785347126.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-f84a7a59-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d7afbb9566f0a7fe4ca8ff55197e380a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1689,
    "chunk_text": "In emergency medicine and surgery, confirming proper endotracheal tube placement is a matter of life and death. Traditional gold-standard capnography takes roughly 30 seconds and carries risks of gastric inflation or delayed detection. Dr. Kumaru Wijeratne, an anaesthetist at the National Hospital of Sri Lanka, has spent five years developing a ground-breaking solution: the Rapid Intubation Detector (RID). By measuring tissue resistance differences between the trachea and esophagus, this compact, affordable device confirms correct positioning in under one second without artificial ventilation. Internationally patented and acclaimed by leading global experts, Dr. Wijeratne\u2019s invention promises to revolutionize emergency airway management worldwide.\n\nI am an old boy of Nalanda College, Colombo. After completing my secondary education, I entered the Faculty of Medicine (Ragama), University of Kelaniya, where I obtained my first medical degree.\n\nBeyond my clinical work, I have always had a strong passion for invention. From a young age, I was fascinated by solving practical problems and developing new ideas. That lifelong curiosity eventually inspired me to create this medical device, with the goal of improving patient safety and advancing healthcare in Sri Lanka and internationally.\n\nQMany people know about the surgeon who performs an operation, but public awareness of the role of an anaesthetist is relatively low. Why is that?\n\nA surgical operation is a team effort. While many people believe that the surgeon alone is responsible for a successful operation, the anaesthetist plays a critical role in ensuring the patient\u2019s safety throughout the entire procedure."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-f84a7a59",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f84a7a59",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Saving Seconds, Saving Lives:  The Sri Lankan Doctor Behind a Life-Saving Invention",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Saving-Seconds-Saving-Lives-The-Sri-Lankan-Doctor-Behind-a-Life-Saving-Invention/334-346908",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785347126.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-f84a7a59-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2d5b066fbf3a07d517acc33c9f154b83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1926,
    "chunk_text": "A surgical operation is a team effort. While many people believe that the surgeon alone is responsible for a successful operation, the anaesthetist plays a critical role in ensuring the patient\u2019s safety throughout the entire procedure.\n\nThis responsibility continues throughout the operation and into the recovery period. During surgery, the anaesthetist administers anaesthesia, continuously monitors the patient\u2019s breathing, blood pressure, heart function, and oxygen levels; provides the necessary medications, intravenous fluids, and blood when required; manages pain during and after surgery; and responds immediately to any life-threatening emergency that may arise.\n\nIn other words, the anaesthetist is responsible not only for keeping the patient asleep but also for continuity of life, thereby monitoring the patient\u2019s vital organ functions throughout the operation.\n\nTherefore, it is no exaggeration to say that the safe and successful performance of surgery depends not only on the surgeon\u2019s technical expertise but also on the anaesthetist\u2019s ability to maintain the patient\u2019s physiological stability and respond rapidly to unexpected complications.\n\nFrom a young age, I have had a strong interest in emergency medical care, critical thinking, and patient safety. Anaesthesiology is a speciality that brings together general medicine, pharmacology, physiology, physics, technology, and rapid clinical decision-making.\n\nEvery patient is different, and every second can be critical in saving a life. It was the speciality\u2019s challenging, highly responsible, and time-critical nature that inspired me to pursue a career in anaesthesiology. Since making that decision, I have had the privilege of serving as an anaesthetist at the National Hospital of Sri Lanka in Colombo, a role I have held since 2015.\n\nThe idea first came to me in 2014, when I began my anaesthesia training at Negombo General Hospital."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-f84a7a59",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f84a7a59",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Saving Seconds, Saving Lives:  The Sri Lankan Doctor Behind a Life-Saving Invention",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Saving-Seconds-Saving-Lives-The-Sri-Lankan-Doctor-Behind-a-Life-Saving-Invention/334-346908",
    "lang": "en",
