    "language": "en",
    "md5": "62e9626ac443b86d0bd9e870989f0daf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "Every patient is different, and every second can be critical in saving a life. It was the speciality\u2019s challenging, highly responsible, and time-critical nature that inspired me to pursue a career in anaesthesiology. Since making that decision, I have had the privilege of serving as an anaesthetist at the National Hospital of Sri Lanka in Colombo, a role I have held since 2015.\n\nThe idea first came to me in 2014, when I began my anaesthesia training at Negombo General Hospital.\n\nIn addition, capnography enables continuous monitoring of the patient\u2019s ventilation, circulation, and certain physiological parameters throughout anaesthesia. However, during my training, I realised several limitations in using this technology to confirm the correct placement of the endotracheal tube. It was my conviction that correct tube placement should be ascertained as rapidly and as reliably as possible, ideally without subjecting the patient to unnecessary interventions that could cause harm. When I studied the existing technology in greater depth, I became convinced that it had several important limitations.\n\nThe first limitation is that after endotracheal tube insertion, the patient must be connected to a mechanical ventilation system before capnography can confirm correct placement. To measure exhaled carbon dioxide, the patient must first receive several artificial breaths. Only then does the monitor display whether carbon dioxide is present. If carbon dioxide is detected, ventilation through the tube can safely continue. If no carbon dioxide is detected, the anaesthetist must remove the tube, reinsert it correctly, and repeat the confirmation process. This process usually takes approximately 20 to 30 seconds. However, in emergencies, even those few seconds can be critical to a patient\u2019s survival."
  },
