    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7fd17e3b16bbd6b7bb6ee04654938af9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1502,
    "chunk_text": "The first limitation is that after endotracheal tube insertion, the patient must be connected to a mechanical ventilation system before capnography can confirm correct placement. To measure exhaled carbon dioxide, the patient must first receive several artificial breaths. Only then does the monitor display whether carbon dioxide is present. If carbon dioxide is detected, ventilation through the tube can safely continue. If no carbon dioxide is detected, the anaesthetist must remove the tube, reinsert it correctly, and repeat the confirmation process. This process usually takes approximately 20 to 30 seconds. However, in emergencies, even those few seconds can be critical to a patient\u2019s survival.\n\nIn addition, the position of the tube may be assessed by listening to breath sounds over the lungs using a stethoscope. However, this too is not an accepted gold-standard method and also requires artificial ventilation. Furthermore, it is considered a more basic and less reliable technique than capnography.\n\nAnother important limitation arises during cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR). During cardiac arrest, there is little or no blood circulation, and therefore carbon dioxide is not transported to the lungs. Under these circumstances, if the tube has been misplaced, carbon dioxide monitoring cannot reliably help identify its location. Delayed recognition or failure to recognise oesophageal intubation in such situations may place the patient at serious risk and can even cause death."
