    "md5": "5fb8e9814d1cb9a8c93ee018f62635f9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1786,
    "chunk_text": "Another important limitation arises during cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR). During cardiac arrest, there is little or no blood circulation, and therefore carbon dioxide is not transported to the lungs. Under these circumstances, if the tube has been misplaced, carbon dioxide monitoring cannot reliably help identify its location. Delayed recognition or failure to recognise oesophageal intubation in such situations may place the patient at serious risk and can even cause death.\n\nDespite these limitations, even in healthcare settings where capnography is unavailable, many patients in Sri Lanka continue to receive safe care because of the knowledge, experience, and unwavering dedication of our doctors. However, regardless of how skilled a clinician may be, unexpected human errors can occur at any time. I therefore realised that there was a pressing need to develop a simple, reliable, and affordable technological solution that would not replace the clinician\u2019s expertise but rather strengthen it by introducing a device that facilitates access to advanced airway confirmation in every healthcare setting.\n\nWith that realisation, one simple yet very important question came to my mind:\n\nQ\u201cShouldn\u2019t there be a way to confirm quickly and reliably that the endotracheal tube is in the correct position before ventilation even begins\u2014without having to wait for carbon dioxide detection?\u201d\n\nMy invention was born in my quest to answer that single question.\n\nQHow difficult was the journey from an idea to a working device?\n\nIt was an extremely challenging journey. However, my passion for electronics, which stemmed from childhood, together with the software development and information technology skills I acquired later, buttressed my resolve throughout this journey."
