    "md5": "4879b5af4815657c754efaca9d67c3be",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "It was an extremely challenging journey. However, my passion for electronics, which stemmed from childhood, together with the software development and information technology skills I acquired later, buttressed my resolve throughout this journey.\n\nOne unique feature of this invention is that I personally designed the entire system, including the hardware, software, and mobile application. I consider this to be one of the greatest achievements of my life.\n\nMy invention is called the Rapid Intubation Detector (RID). It is a device that works by measuring the tissue resistance encountered as the endotracheal tube passes through either the trachea or the oesophagus. Its most distinctive feature is that it does not rely on carbon dioxide (CO\u2082) detection in any way.\n\nBecause the tissue resistance characteristics of the trachea and the oesophagus are different, the device can instantly and accurately identify which passage the endotracheal tube has entered in less than one second.\n\nQ\u201cIf the correct position of the endotracheal tube could be confirmed reliably at the very moment it is inserted, why should we continue using existing techniques, thereby losing precious seconds that could save a life?\u201d\n\nToday, I am proud to introduce the Rapid Intubation Detector (RID) as my answer to that question.\n\nThe primary purpose of this device is to help clinicians confirm, quickly and reliably, that the endotracheal tube has been correctly placed in the patient\u2019s airway.\n\n1. It does not require artificial ventilation to confirm the position of the endotracheal tube.\n\n2. It does not rely in any way on carbon dioxide measurement.\n\n3. It provides results immediately at the moment the tube is inserted.\n\n4. No part of the device needs to be inserted into the patient\u2019s body.\n\n5. It has a simple design and can be manufactured at an affordable cost.\n\nTogether, these features enable clinicians to make the correct clinical decision within a very short time while further enhancing patient safety."
