    "chunk_text": "5. It has a simple design and can be manufactured at an affordable cost.\n\nTogether, these features enable clinicians to make the correct clinical decision within a very short time while further enhancing patient safety.\n\nI designed this device to be a practical application in any clinical setting. It is compact, lightweight, and highly portable\u2014small enough to be carried even in a pocket.\n\nI believe that this device will not be limited to anaesthetists alone but will soon become an essential tool for every doctor involved in emergency airway management. Today, the stethoscope is recognised as both a symbol of the medical profession and an indispensable instrument in everyday clinical practice. In the same way, I believe that the Rapid Intubation Detector (RID) will become an essential, costomary piece of equipment for clinicians providing emergency care.\n\nBy carrying this device at all times, clinicians will be able to make the correct clinical decision within seconds during an emergency, thereby making a significant contribution to improving patient safety.\n\nI have been granted a Sri Lankan patent (LK/P/1/22891) for this invention by the National Intellectual Property Office of Sri Lanka. In addition, I filed an international patent application under the Patent Cooperation Treaty (PCT) administered by the World Intellectual Property Organisation (WIPO) (PCT/IB2024/050518).\n\nSubsequently, I entered the national phase by filing patent applications in the United States (19/581,052), Australia (P03937AU1), the United Kingdom (GB2612074.1), India (202617054614), and China (202480068343.3).\n\nBefore commencing this research, I obtained ethical approval from the Ethics Review Committee of the Faculty of Medicine, University of Kelaniya, Ragama."
