    "chunk_text": "In 2025, I was also allowed to present this invention at the World Airway Management Meeting (WAMM) in Florence, Italy. During the meeting, I received encouraging feedback from Professor Patrick Olomu, a consultant anaesthetist from the United States. One of the most memorable milestones of my professional career was meeting Professor Archie Brain, the British anaesthetist who invented the Laryngeal Mask Airway (LMA)\u2014one of the most revolutionary invention in modern anaesthesia\u2014and having the opportunity to discuss my invention with him in person.\n\nMy message to young inventors is very simple: never be afraid to fail. Failure is not the opposite of success; it is an essential step on the path to success. Every failed attempt teaches you a lesson that no textbook can ever teach.\n\nTherefore, have self-belief. Keep learning continuously. Improve yourself and your invention every single day. Never be discouraged by obstacles. Always remember that every great invention that has changed the world began with one person who had an unwavering belief in their own idea.\n\nMy dream is for the Rapid Intubation Detector (RID) to become a standard medical device that helps to save lives around the world. If this invention can save even a single life that might otherwise have been lost, it will be the greatest reward for the many years of dedication, hard work, and research that have been infused into this journey.\n\nI also believe that bringing this technology to the global market will not only improve patient safety worldwide but also enable Sri Lanka to export a high-value medical technology product, generating foreign exchange and thereby contributing to the country\u2019s economy.\n\nMy next goal is to further develop this technology and work with government institutions, universities, research organisations, and private industry to ensure it meets international standards and becomes available to benefit patients worldwide."
