    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "Increase  in issuance of dishonoured cheques  in transactions\n\nA cheque issued from an account where transactions are no longer made\n\nSmall and medium-sized enterprises in grave danger due to dishonoured cheques\n\nSome cheques have been issued from accounts where transaction activity had ceased\n\nIf a quick solution isn\u2019t found, the entire business sector is at risk\n\nSri Lanka was recently officially reclassified by the World Bank as an \u201cUpper Middle-Income Country.\u201d This new classification is based on the country\u2019s 5% economic growth recorded in 2025 and the macroeconomic recovery achieved following the economic crisis of 2022. Accordingly, Sri Lanka has been upgraded from the \u201cLower Middle-Income\u201d category to the \u201cUpper Middle-Income\u201d category under the World Bank\u2019s income classification.\n\nA country\u2019s income level is determined using the Atlas Method, which is based on its Gross National Income (GNI) per capita. International financial institutions have also noted in their recent reports that Sri Lanka, which declared bankruptcy in 2022, has now achieved a strong\n\nAgainst this backdrop of economic growth, however, the manner in which business transactions are being conducted in the country is regrettable. This is due to the issuance of dishonoured cheques during commercial transactions. The practice of issuing dishonoured cheques has now become widespread across Sri Lanka. At times, it even appears to be part of an organised fraudulent scheme, given the extent to which dishonoured cheque transactions have spread throughout the country.\n\nBusiness owners, who have fallen victim to dishonoured cheque frauds, told the Daily Mirror that the issue poses a serious threat to the future of their businesses. They further stated that, as a result of this type of fraud, many small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) are at risk of being forced to cease operations."
