    "md5": "99b064e0751c8fd606f57b1a266e1df7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "Business owners, who have fallen victim to dishonoured cheque frauds, told the Daily Mirror that the issue poses a serious threat to the future of their businesses. They further stated that, as a result of this type of fraud, many small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) are at risk of being forced to cease operations.\n\nThe legal provisions relating to dishonoured cheques were strengthened through the Bills of Exchange (Amendment) Act, No. 13 of 2025. Under the Act, a person responsible for issuing a dishonoured cheque may be liable to a fine equivalent to the value of the cheque, imprisonment for a term of up to two years, or both. A cheque may be dishonoured for several reasons, including insufficient funds in the account, exceeding an overdraft facility, issuing a cheque from a closed account, or instructing the bank to stop payment without reasonable cause. Such practices are commonly encountered in the business sector. However, when these actions are carried out deliberately as part of a fraudulent scheme, they constitute a serious offence. The dishonour of a cheque, issued by a company, partnership or corporation, as well as in the course of a business transaction, constitutes a serious financial and legal issue.\n\nAlthough penalties for dishonoured cheques were strengthened under the Bills of Exchange (Amendment) Act, No. 13 of 2025, those engaged in this fraud appear to have little regard for the law. Lodging a complaint with the police often proves futile, as the perpetrators evade the authorities. Even when the police summon them to appear for investigations, some fail to comply. Business owners said that these individuals have no fear of legal consequences.\n\nA notice issued under Act Number 15 of 1972 pertaining to the Criminal Procedure Code Number\u00a0 109 (06) to a person in case of a dishonoured cheque"
