    "md5": "e6f88cfb5d91526e7fa30ecf442dd27b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1631,
    "chunk_text": "Although penalties for dishonoured cheques were strengthened under the Bills of Exchange (Amendment) Act, No. 13 of 2025, those engaged in this fraud appear to have little regard for the law. Lodging a complaint with the police often proves futile, as the perpetrators evade the authorities. Even when the police summon them to appear for investigations, some fail to comply. Business owners said that these individuals have no fear of legal consequences.\n\nA notice issued under Act Number 15 of 1972 pertaining to the Criminal Procedure Code Number\u00a0 109 (06) to a person in case of a dishonoured cheque\n\nEvidence of this can be observed in a letter issued on 05.06.2026 by an individual from Tangalle to a business owner in relation to a commercial transaction. According to the business owner, the issuer has neither paid the amount due under the dishonoured cheque nor complied with the payment schedule set out in the letter. Only a single payment of LKR 25,000 has been made. The business owner further alleged that the remaining amount has not been paid.\n\nThe individual, who provided the letter in relation to a transaction worth LKR 492,000, had issued two cheques. Both cheques, drawn on an account maintained by the individual at a well-known bank in Tangalle, were dishonoured.\n\nAnother business owner told us that dishonoured cheque frauds have now spread across the country and is having a severe impact on businesses. According to him, some individuals issue dishonoured cheques for relatively small amounts. However, when these unpaid cheques accumulate, the total financial loss can reach between LKR 5-10 million."
