    "md5": "6d4d41b2b57bcaf950af0c96e7fc6158",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1285,
    "chunk_text": "Another business owner told us that dishonoured cheque frauds have now spread across the country and is having a severe impact on businesses. According to him, some individuals issue dishonoured cheques for relatively small amounts. However, when these unpaid cheques accumulate, the total financial loss can reach between LKR 5-10 million.\n\nInquiries made by this scribe further revealed that although complaints regarding dishonoured cheques are lodged with the police, the individuals who issued the cheques frequently fail to appear for the relevant investigations. The Daily Mirror also revealed that those who issued the dishonoured cheques have been evading the police investigations. It also appears that some of those individuals do not even accept summons issued to them under Section 109(6) of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, No. 15 of 1979. If a person refuses to accept or deliberately evades such a summons, the Officer-in-Charge (OIC) of the police station that issued the summons has the authority to seek a warrant for that person\u2019s arrest. In addition, legal proceedings may be instituted under Section 172 of the Penal Code.\n\nIt\u2019s appalling that in the backdrop of an economic growth, the way business transactions are conducted in the country is regrettable"
