    "chunk_text": "Inquiries made by this scribe further revealed that although complaints regarding dishonoured cheques are lodged with the police, the individuals who issued the cheques frequently fail to appear for the relevant investigations. The Daily Mirror also revealed that those who issued the dishonoured cheques have been evading the police investigations. It also appears that some of those individuals do not even accept summons issued to them under Section 109(6) of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, No. 15 of 1979. If a person refuses to accept or deliberately evades such a summons, the Officer-in-Charge (OIC) of the police station that issued the summons has the authority to seek a warrant for that person\u2019s arrest. In addition, legal proceedings may be instituted under Section 172 of the Penal Code.\n\nIt\u2019s appalling that in the backdrop of an economic growth, the way business transactions are conducted in the country is regrettable\n\nIn addition, instructing a bank to stop payment on a cheque without reasonable cause in the course of a legitimate commercial transaction may also be regarded as a fraudulent act. A cheque issued on a closed bank account cannot be used to complete a transaction or obtain payment. When such a cheque is presented to the bank, payment will be refused because the account has already been closed. This constitutes a dishonoured (bounced) cheque. Issuing cheques from a closed bank account and attempting to obtain money through such cheques are unlawful acts under Sri Lanka\u2019s Bills of Exchange Act."
