    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "In addition, instructing a bank to stop payment on a cheque without reasonable cause in the course of a legitimate commercial transaction may also be regarded as a fraudulent act. A cheque issued on a closed bank account cannot be used to complete a transaction or obtain payment. When such a cheque is presented to the bank, payment will be refused because the account has already been closed. This constitutes a dishonoured (bounced) cheque. Issuing cheques from a closed bank account and attempting to obtain money through such cheques are unlawful acts under Sri Lanka\u2019s Bills of Exchange Act.\n\nWith these documents, a complaint may be lodged at the relevant police station or the Fraud Investigation Division, and legal proceedings may be instituted before the Magistrate\u2019s Court through an attorney-at-law. A cheque remains valid for a period of six months from the date of issue, or until the last date on which it may be presented to the bank, as applicable. Accordingly, it is important to commence legal proceedings without undue delay.\n\nUnder the new legal amendments, the dishonouring of a valid cheque due to reasons such as insufficient funds in the account constitutes an offence. Furthermore, where the value of a dishonoured cheque is less than LKR 1 million, the dispute must first be referred to a Mediation Board in an attempt to resolve the matter before legal proceedings are instituted in court. If the police refuse to accept a complaint relating to a dishonoured cheque or fail to take appropriate action, a private plaint can be filed before the Magistrate\u2019s Court. This may be done either through an Attorney-at-Law or personally with the assistance of the relevant court officials.\n\nIn addition, under the Bills of Exchange (Amendment) Act, No. 13 of 2025, it is an offence to issue a cheque that is dishonoured due to a stop-payment instruction or insufficient funds (a bounced cheque)."
