    "md5": "ebe19834f607f5b8f98fffc56c9d8f5e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1433,
    "chunk_text": "In addition, under the Bills of Exchange (Amendment) Act, No. 13 of 2025, it is an offence to issue a cheque that is dishonoured due to a stop-payment instruction or insufficient funds (a bounced cheque).\n\nIn addition, instructing a bank to stop payment on a cheque without reasonable cause in the course of a legitimate commercial transaction may be regarded as a fraudulent act. Likewise, a cheque drawn on a bank account that has been closed cannot be used to complete a transaction or obtain payment. When such a cheque is presented to the bank, payment will be refused because the account has already been closed. This constitutes a dishonoured (bounced) cheque. Issuing cheques from a closed bank account and attempting to obtain money through such cheques are unlawful acts under Sri Lanka\u2019s Bills of Exchange Act.\n\nTo recover the amount due under a dishonoured cheque (cheque bouncing), the holder should first obtain a Return Memo from the bank stating the reason why the cheque was dishonoured. The holder should then notify the relevant individual or business in writing and demand payment. If the drawer fails to settle the amount, legal proceedings may be instituted. The bank-issued Return Memo, which specifies the reason for the dishonour, should be obtained together with the returned cheque. The drawer should be informed of the dishonour both by telephone and in writing, and payment should be formally demanded."
