    "chunk_text": "To recover the amount due under a dishonoured cheque (cheque bouncing), the holder should first obtain a Return Memo from the bank stating the reason why the cheque was dishonoured. The holder should then notify the relevant individual or business in writing and demand payment. If the drawer fails to settle the amount, legal proceedings may be instituted. The bank-issued Return Memo, which specifies the reason for the dishonour, should be obtained together with the returned cheque. The drawer should be informed of the dishonour both by telephone and in writing, and payment should be formally demanded.\n\nIf the value of the cheque is less than LKR 1 million, the dispute must first be referred to the relevant Mediation Board for the area, and an attempt must be made to resolve the matter before commencing court proceedings. If the police refuse to accept a complaint relating to a dishonoured cheque or fail to take appropriate action, the aggrieved party may file a private plaint before the Magistrate\u2019s Court. This may be done either through an Attorney-at-Law or by the complainant personally, with the assistance of the relevant\n\nTo ascertain what action is taken against those who continue to issue dishonoured cheques in disregard of all applicable laws, including subsection (6) of section 109 of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, and what the maximum measures available to the police are against individuals who fail to appear for investigations, this writer sought the views of Acting Police Media Spokesperson, Senior Superintendent of Police Chandana Herath. The response was as follows: \u201cThis matter involves both criminal and civil law aspects. I will need to consult the Legal Division before providing a formal response. Please send us the relevant information, and someone from the Legal Division will get in touch.\u201d"
