    "chunk_text": "To ascertain what action is taken against those who continue to issue dishonoured cheques in disregard of all applicable laws, including subsection (6) of section 109 of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, and what the maximum measures available to the police are against individuals who fail to appear for investigations, this writer sought the views of Acting Police Media Spokesperson, Senior Superintendent of Police Chandana Herath. The response was as follows: \u201cThis matter involves both criminal and civil law aspects. I will need to consult the Legal Division before providing a formal response. Please send us the relevant information, and someone from the Legal Division will get in touch.\u201d\n\n\u201cEnterprises are facing severe financial hardship as a result of dishonoured cheque transactions\u201d\n\nCommenting on the issue, Tania Abeysundara, Chairman of Sri Lanka United National Businesses Alliance, stated: \u201cThis has been happening for quite some time. We raised this issue with previous governments and repeatedly called for action. It was at our request that a decision was taken to deactivate a bank account when dishonoured cheques are issued. Even now, if several cheques issued by a person are dishonoured, that person\u2019s bank account is closed. However, the individual simply opens a new account at another bank and, after some time, continues issuing dishonoured cheques. That is extremely unfair.\n\n\u201cThere should be a system whereby, before a new bank account is opened, the individual\u2019s transaction history is examined. Just as banks check whether a person has been blacklisted before granting a loan. Such a system would make it far more difficult for repeat offenders to commit fraud. It would also encourage cheque issuers to conduct their business responsibly and honour their financial obligations. As a result, when a business receives a cheque, it can have confidence that the cheque will be honoured within the agreed credit period, whether two weeks or one month."
