    "description": "UK\u00a0launches \u00a3\u00a02.34\u00a0m\u00a0\u2018Resilient Waves\u2019\u00a0 to build climate resilient ecosystems, inclusive fishing economies",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/UKlaunches-2-34m-Resilient-Waves-to-build-climate-resilient-ecosystems-inclusive-fishing-economies/34-795347",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785369203.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-30-dailyftlk-77d2c792-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6375214077b6839cbcd779e160c554c4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "UK\u00a0launches \u00a3\u00a02.34\u00a0m\u00a0\u2018Resilient Waves\u2019\u00a0 to build climate resilient ecosystems, inclusive fishing economies\n\nFrom left: Acted Country Director Ciaran Lavery, British High Commissioner Andrew Patrick and Environment Minister Dr. Dammika Patabendi at the launch\n\nProject to support coastal fishing communities in Batticaloa and Mullaitivu till March 2029\n\nFocuses on habitat restoration, climate-smart fisheries, livelihood diversification and inclusive governance\n\nAquaculture, mangrove and coral reef restoration, and women\u2019s economic participation among key interventions\n\nProject selected from over 170 global applications, with only four receiving funding\n\nBritish High Commissioner Andrew Patrick says program supports Sri Lanka\u2019s own environmental priorities, not externally imposed initiatives\n\nA new \u00a32.34 million UK-funded initiative, \u2018Resilient Waves\u2019, was launched last week aimed at strengthening the climate resilience of Sri Lanka\u2019s coastal fishing communities, while protecting the country\u2019s rich marine biodiversity.\n\nThe initiative is funded by the UK Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (DEFRA) through the Ocean Community Empowerment and Nature (OCEAN) grant under the Blue Planet Fund, will be implemented by Acted and other partners till March 2029 in the coastal districts of Batticaloa and Mullaitivu. The stakeholders described the move as a\u00a0 step towards sustainable fisheries, ecosystem restoration, and climate adaptation.\n\nThe program seeks to restore critical marine habitats, promote climate-smart fisheries, diversify livelihoods through aquaculture, strengthen fisher governance and increase the participation of women in coastal economies, which can contribute to reducing multidimensional poverty.\n\nLaunching the program alongside Environment Minister Dr. Dammika Patabendi, British High Commissioner Andrew Patrick said the initiative builds on the strong environmental partnership between the United Kingdom and Sri Lanka."
  },
