    "md5": "f5339ff9b61f0a4d519cb773cc6b3dec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2012,
    "chunk_text": "Launching the program alongside Environment Minister Dr. Dammika Patabendi, British High Commissioner Andrew Patrick said the initiative builds on the strong environmental partnership between the United Kingdom and Sri Lanka.\n\nDescribing Sri Lanka as one of the world\u2019s most biodiverse countries, the High Commissioner said many people underestimate the natural wealth the country possesses.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is one of the most biodiverse countries in the world. You really are gifted this extraordinary legacy of biodiversity,\u201d he said.\n\nThe High Commissioner also stressed that the program has been designed in line with Sri Lanka\u2019s own environmental priorities.\n\n\u201cWe are trying to support the lead given by the Government,\u201d the High Commissioner said, thanking the Environment Ministry and its officials for their close collaboration.\n\nActed Country Director Ciaran Lavery said the award represents another milestone in the organisation\u2019s longstanding partnership with the Environment Ministry, following earlier collaboration on plastics recycling under an EU-funded programme.\n\nHe disclosed that the Resilient Waves Program was selected from over 370 applications worldwide, with only four projects ultimately receiving funding. \u201cI think that is really down to the quality of its design, something we can all be very proud of,\u201d Lavery said.\n\nThe project is being implemented through a consortium comprising Humanity and Inclusion, Blue Resources Trust Green Movement of Sri Lanka, and Biodiversity Sri Lanka bringing together expertise in biodiversity conservation, habitat restoration, inclusion and community development.\n\nLavery said implementation has been aligned with Sri Lanka\u2019s Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs), the National Adaptation Plan, the National Biodiversity Strategic Action Plan and other national environmental priorities.\n\nHe also pledged close coordination with Government agencies, academia and other development partners to maximise the programme\u2019s impact."
  },
