    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dc130c30a519ded661d4ea21aaeb8d5b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "Lavery said implementation has been aligned with Sri Lanka\u2019s Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs), the National Adaptation Plan, the National Biodiversity Strategic Action Plan and other national environmental priorities.\n\nHe also pledged close coordination with Government agencies, academia and other development partners to maximise the programme\u2019s impact.\n\nHe said Batticaloa and Mullaitivu were selected because fishing remains the backbone of local livelihoods, while communities face growing challenges from poverty, degraded coral reefs and mangroves, climate change and declining fish stocks.\n\nNoting that Sri Lanka ranks among the world\u2019s most climate-vulnerable countries, De Silva said the ultimate objective is to help coastal communities build long-term resilience \u201con their own terms\u201d, while safeguarding the country\u2019s marine ecosystems for future generations.\n\nHe also said women engaged in coastal livelihoods continue to face significant barriers to economic participation, making inclusion a central component of the programme.\n\nHe said the project will focus on three main pillars. \u201cThe first involves restoring degraded coral reefs and mangrove ecosystems through scientific assessments and targeted rehabilitation activities. The second and largest component will promote aquaculture, as an alternative livelihood, reducing dependence on traditional marine fishing. Potential livelihood options include cage culture, sea bass, tilapia and sea cucumber farming, with activities tailored to local conditions following community consultations and collaboration with relevant Government agencies. The third pillar aims to strengthen community participation in coastal governance by enhancing the role of Fisheries Cooperative Societies and newly established aquaculture groups in policy discussions and local decision-making,\u201d he explained."
