    "chunk_text": "\"Behind many of these scams, there may also be a victim,\" he said, emphasizing that individuals appearing to commit online crimes may in fact be acting under coercion after being deprived of their freedom, threatened, abused and compelled to deceive others by organized criminal groups.\n\nAir Vice Marshal Thuyacontha reaffirmed that the National Anti-Human Trafficking Task Force remains committed to preventing trafficking in persons, identifying and protecting victims, strengthening investigations and prosecutions against offenders, and supporting the sustainable reintegration of survivors into society.\n\nHowever, he emphasized that the fight against human trafficking cannot be won by the Government alone, calling for the active participation and cooperation of law enforcement agencies, civil society organizations, the private sector, international partners and the public.\n\nMarking the World Day Against Trafficking in Persons, he urged all Sri Lankans to stand together in safeguarding human dignity, supporting survivors and strengthening collective efforts to dismantle the criminal networks that profit from human exploitation.\n\n\"Together, we can build a future where no person is deceived, controlled, trafficked or exploited,\" he said."
