    "md5": "740673a8bcc1441f496a70d0b56546c4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Seylan Bank 2Q earnings climb 16%\n\nSeylan Bank PLC reported a 16.4 percent increase in profit after tax to Rs.3.17 billion for the second quarter of 2026, supported by higher fee income and trading gains, despite slower growth in core interest earnings and a sharp rise in impairment charges.\n\nProfit before income tax rose 11.9 percent year-on-year to Rs.4.75 billion, while earnings per share increased to Rs.4.99 from Rs.4.29.\n\nQuarterly operating income grew 14.1 percent to Rs.13.17 billion. Interest income increased 21.1 percent, but interest expenses rose faster at 33.1 percent, limiting net interest income growth to 7.1 percent at Rs.9.83 billion.\n\nNet fee and commission income rose 17.9 percent to Rs.2.32 billion, while trading gains surged to Rs.1.34 billion from Rs.76 million a year earlier. However, other operating income swung to a Rs.339 million loss from income of Rs.251 million.\n\nImpairment charges more than tripled to Rs.632 million from Rs.193 million, accounting for most of the Rs.733 million charge recognised in the first half. Operating expenses rose 9.5 percent to Rs.6.15 billion.\n\nThe second-quarter performance lifted first-half profit after tax by 10.8 percent to Rs.6.08 billion, while profit before income tax rose 10.1 percent to Rs.9.30 billion. Earnings per share increased to Rs.9.57 from Rs.8.63.\n\nFor the six months, net interest income grew 10.1 percent to Rs.19.56 billion. However, interest expenses increased 26.4 percent, outpacing the 19.1 percent growth in interest income and narrowing the net interest margin to 4.16 percent from 4.50 percent at end-2025.\n\nNet fee and commission income rose 20.9 percent to Rs.4.65 billion, while trading gains increased nearly sixfold to Rs.1.12 billion. Total operating income grew 13.3 percent to Rs.25.54 billion.\n\nOperating expenses rose slightly faster at 14.2 percent to Rs.12.28 billion, pushing the cost-to-income ratio to 48.09 percent from 44.52 percent."
  },
