    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "Iran war and China\u2019s Global Governance Initiative\n\nPlumes rise into the sky after a US attack on an Iranian facility in southern Iran\n\nYet another round of escalation, and yet another cycle of hope and despair. Now in its sixth month, the US war on Iran is hitting the poor the hardest. From transport to daily bread, they pay more than they did for essentials before the US\u2011Israeli war of aggression on Iran began on February 28.\n\nTo end the war\u2014even if only temporarily\u2014the warring parties attempted mediation, but only half\u2011heartedly. The outcome was a vaguely worded memorandum of understanding, which lasted only three weeks before collapsing, triggering the current round of what some critics call Donald Trump\u2019s \u201cforever war.\u201d\n\nThe trust deficit, instead of being bridged, keeps widening. It is now as unbridgeable as the contrast between the two countries\u2019 ideologies: one rooted in Shiite Islamic principles, the other in neo\u2011Western civilizational values. Together, they risk igniting Samuel Huntington\u2019s much\u2011criticized \u201cclash of civilizations.\u201d\n\nAlso widening in tandem with the trust deficit are the war\u2019s geographic contours. Saudi Arabia, Yemen, and Iraq have all become theatres where scores are being settled. Adding to the complexity, Ukraine last Saturday attacked an Iranian ship in the Caspian Sea, claiming it was carrying military cargo to Russia. Iran warned Ukraine that the attack would not go unpunished.\n\nA three\u2011day pause in the fighting raised hopes for peace this week. But it was not to be. Iran launched what the US now calls a \u201csurprise attack\u201d on American military targets in Jordan. An angry Trump vowed a fitting response, saying: \u201cWe are going to beat the f**king sh*t out of them. We\u2019ll be hitting them hard. They\u2019re going to get a beating.\u201d"
  },
