    "md5": "ad53484b975b6120f30d428c015f96d4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2011,
    "chunk_text": "Such a China-led international diplomatic effort would be in consonance with President Xi Jinping\u2019s Global Governance Initiative (GGI), built on new pancha seela or five core concepts: sovereign equality, the international rule of law, multilateralism, a people\u2011centred approach, and real action.\n\nThe Iran war offers China an opportunity to address the concerns stipulated in the white paper through the GGI. In line with this vision, China has already established the International Organisation for Mediation (IOMed) in Hong Kong to help resolve disputes between states.\n\nPeace in the Gulf region, needless to say, serves China\u2019s national interest. Stability would ensure low energy prices and the free flow of oil from West Asia to energy\u2011dependent China and other countries, especially in Asia.\n\nSome analysts, however, contend that the war also serves China\u2019s interests, since it keeps the US distracted from the Indo\u2011Pacific, particularly the South China Sea, China\u2019s backyard. With Washington shifting its THAAD missile systems from South Korea to West Asia and relocating thousands of military personnel from Japan to the region, China can only be pleased to see the United States\u2019 Pivot to Asia defence doctrine crumbling.\n\nSince the peace dividends far outweigh whatever leverage the war may bring, can China accept the peace challenge and join Pakistan, Qatar, and Oman to end the conflict as early as possible? Such a role is imperative, given that the war fires now engulf the vital Bab al\u2011Mandeb region and are spreading to other countries.\n\nThe war now hinges on one thorny issue\u2014Iran\u2019s claim to control the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nThe Trump administration and US warmongers must also learn to relish peace and realise that America\u2019s victory in this war lies not in defeating Iran but in reviving the Memorandum of Understanding and implementing Clause 5, which allows Iran and regional countries to work out a mechanism to ensure freedom of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz."
