    "md5": "114d5a8129f024f52a65689706113c03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "Judicial reforms do not mean retirement age extension\n\nThe debate over extending the retirement age of superior court judges has brought into focus a much larger question that Sri Lanka has struggled with for decades: how to create a judicial system that delivers timely, accessible and credible justice. While the proposal to retain experienced judges beyond the current retirement age may appear to offer a quick solution to the mounting backlog of cases, it risks addressing only the symptoms rather than the underlying causes of judicial delays.\n\nA strong judiciary is not measured by the number of years judges remain in office, but by the efficiency, independence and accessibility of the justice system as a whole. The challenge before Sri Lanka is therefore not simply about the retirement age of judges. It is about whether the entire judicial process is designed to deliver justice without unnecessary delays.\n\nThere is no doubt that experienced judges possess valuable institutional knowledge. Years spent interpreting the Constitution, developing legal principles and handling complex cases contribute significantly to the strength of the judiciary.\u00a0 Therefore, any discussion on retirement age should recognise the importance of preserving judicial experience.\n\nHowever, judicial efficiency cannot depend on extending the service period of a limited number of judges. If the system itself remains burdened by procedural delays, administrative weaknesses and inadequate resources, keeping judges in office for a longer period will provide only temporary relief.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s courts face a long-standing backlog of cases. A person seeking resolution of a property dispute, a criminal case, or a fundamental rights application cannot be expected to wait indefinitely while procedural obstacles continue to slow down the process."
  },
