    "md5": "6344ce7eeb983d8764d37917ae2fdcab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s courts face a long-standing backlog of cases. A person seeking resolution of a property dispute, a criminal case, or a fundamental rights application cannot be expected to wait indefinitely while procedural obstacles continue to slow down the process.\n\nOne of the major issues affecting the judiciary is the outdated administrative structure surrounding courts. Modern judicial systems around the world increasingly rely on technology-based case tracking, electronic filing systems and digital records to reduce delays and improve transparency. Sri Lanka must accelerate similar reforms.\n\nThe appointment process and institutional independence of the judiciary are equally important. Any reform affecting judges\u2019 tenure must be approached carefully because judicial independence is a fundamental pillar of democracy. Judges must not feel that their career progression, continuation in office or retirement arrangements are influenced by political considerations. Public confidence in the judiciary depends not only on actual independence but also on the perception that judicial institutions are free from external pressure.\n\nTherefore, discussions about retirement age extensions must be conducted through a transparent and inclusive process involving legal experts, judges, civil society and other stakeholders. Constitutional changes affecting the judiciary should not be viewed merely as administrative adjustments. They have implications for the balance of power among state institutions.\n\nThere is also a broader question about whether extending the retirement age could unintentionally limit opportunities for younger judicial officers and reduce institutional renewal. A judiciary that preserves expertise while encouraging the growth of younger legal professionals will be stronger and more dynamic."
  },
