    "chunk_size_bytes": 1177,
    "chunk_text": "There is also a broader question about whether extending the retirement age could unintentionally limit opportunities for younger judicial officers and reduce institutional renewal. A judiciary that preserves expertise while encouraging the growth of younger legal professionals will be stronger and more dynamic.\n\nThe objective should not simply be to keep judges longer in service; it should be to build a justice system where cases are resolved efficiently regardless of who occupies the bench. A modern republic requires institutions that function effectively through strong systems rather than dependence on individuals.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s judicial reform agenda must therefore move beyond short-term fixes. The country needs a comprehensive strategy that combines institutional independence, technological modernisation, administrative reform and adequate investment in the justice sector.\n\nThe credibility of democracy ultimately depends on the public\u2019s faith that justice will be delivered fairly and without unreasonable delay. Achieving that goal requires courage to confront structural weaknesses rather than relying on limited measures that offer temporary solutions."
