    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Judges\u2019 retirement age extension Lal Kantha asked me to keep silent:  Lal Wijenayake",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Judges-retirement-age-extension-Lal-Kantha-asked-me-to-keep-silent-Lal-Wijenayake/231-347012",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785453384.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-65bf21f0-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8c4b321b23cc72d629a035e07e073f5a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1788,
    "chunk_text": "Judges\u2019 retirement age extension Lal Kantha asked me to keep silent:  Lal Wijenayake\n\nTo create a civilised country and produce responsible citizens, we need the independence of the judiciary\n\nSenior lawyer Lal Wijenayake, who, as a member of the leadership council, the executive committee and the national operation committee of the ruling National People\u2019s Power (NPP), raised objections to the government\u2019s move to extend the retirement age of judges. In an interview with Daily Mirror, he detailed out reasons that led to his decision and implication of interference with the judiciary by any ruler. Excerpts:\n\nQ. What are the circumstances that led to your dissent as a member of the Executive Committee and the Leadership Council of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP)?\n\nWe have been fighting for judicial independence for a long time. Whenever there have been attacks on the independence of the judiciary, we have come forward and fought against them.\n\nI have practised law for fifty-odd years. First, Felix Dias Bandaranaike attacked the judiciary, and we fought against that. Similarly, there have been several instances where the independence of the judiciary was challenged.\n\nWhat we feel is that unless you have an independent judiciary, you cannot build a society where there is equality and democracy. To create a civilised country and produce responsible citizens, we need the independence of the judiciary.\n\nThere are three arms of government \u2014 the Executive, the Legislature and the Judiciary. The Executive and the Legislature have their own functions. Then you have the citizens. Citizens may come into conflict with the Legislature as well as the Executive at certain points in their dealings with society. It is the Judiciary that adjudicates those disputes."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-65bf21f0",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-65bf21f0",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Judges\u2019 retirement age extension Lal Kantha asked me to keep silent:  Lal Wijenayake",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Judges-retirement-age-extension-Lal-Kantha-asked-me-to-keep-silent-Lal-Wijenayake/231-347012",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785453384.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-65bf21f0-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "399262784d9da679cbd21ef35fa24643",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "There are three arms of government \u2014 the Executive, the Legislature and the Judiciary. The Executive and the Legislature have their own functions. Then you have the citizens. Citizens may come into conflict with the Legislature as well as the Executive at certain points in their dealings with society. It is the Judiciary that adjudicates those disputes.\n\nOne of the pillars of judicial independence is that during the period of service of judges, their service conditions should not be interfered with. Otherwise, the Executive and the Legislature can influence the judiciary by either offering benefits or withdrawing existing benefits. They can use such measures as a threat to interfere with judicial independence.\n\nThey can say, \u201cWe will increase your salary if you do this, or reduce it otherwise.\u201d Felix Dias Bandaranaike attempted something similar in 1972 or 1973 when he tried to withdraw the official vehicles provided to judges.\n\nOne important condition for protecting judicial independence is that the service conditions of judges should not be changed during their period of office. If a judge is appointed for four years, nobody should be able to remove that judge before the completion of those four years. This is one of the pillars that protects judicial independence, democracy and other rights.\n\nFor example, if I apply for a licence to build a house and my application is rejected in an unjustified manner, I should have access to a remedy. I should be able to approach the Judiciary. The Judiciary then decides the dispute between the State, which refuses to grant the permit, and me, and determines what is legally correct.\n\nOne way of interfering with the Judiciary is by interfering with the conditions of service of judges. In this case, they say they are going to extend the period of service. But the next government could say they are reducing the retirement age and remove some judges from office."
  },
  {
