    "md5": "e5492939a6908025bd7e5d510f08ed38",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "One way of interfering with the Judiciary is by interfering with the conditions of service of judges. In this case, they say they are going to extend the period of service. But the next government could say they are reducing the retirement age and remove some judges from office.\n\nQ. As a member of the Leadership Council of the NPP, when did you raise this matter with the party? When were you asked to keep quiet?\n\nActually, internally, I was discussing this matter with our members, though not officially. During those discussions, I said that this should not be done because it would be detrimental to the independence of the judiciary.\n\nThey must have come to know about it. One member gave me a call one day \u2014 a member of the Leadership Council, the Political Bureau and other bodies. He did not say anything like he was a dictator or anything of that nature. He simply said, \u201cWhy don\u2019t you keep silent on this matter?\u201d\n\nYes. Then I told him, \u201cI have been fighting for the independence of the judiciary. So how can I not fight against something that affects it?\u201d\n\nThen they called for a meeting of the leaders of what they call the \u201cNational Operation Committee.\u201d They summoned that meeting.\n\nIn the meantime, I wrote to the member who spoke to me. Over the telephone, I did not want to speak for long because I knew things could be misunderstood. So I wrote a letter explaining my position and sent copies to President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, as the leader of the party, and Secretary Dr. Nihal Abeysinghe, along with a few others.\n\nThen they called a meeting. I attended that meeting. They asked me to say what I had to say. I referred to the letter I had sent and almost read it out.\n\nThen President Anura Kumara Dissanayake explained his reasons for wanting this change. He gave his arguments and was critical of certain legal provisions as well as aspects of the legal system."
  },
