    "md5": "10696c65264131a2c1ff92ddf5b7b96c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1950,
    "chunk_text": "Then they called a meeting. I attended that meeting. They asked me to say what I had to say. I referred to the letter I had sent and almost read it out.\n\nThen President Anura Kumara Dissanayake explained his reasons for wanting this change. He gave his arguments and was critical of certain legal provisions as well as aspects of the legal system.\n\nAfter that, they did not take a decision. They moved on to discuss other matters, such as the economy. The meeting ended there.\n\nNo decision was taken by any of those bodies \u2014 either the Leadership Council or the Executive Council. They never formally took a decision. Therefore, I assumed to a certain extent that they had given up the idea.\n\nLater, I came to know that the proposal was going to be introduced. Therefore, I had to publicly state that I was against it.\n\nWhenever people tried to interfere with the judiciary in the past, we openly criticised them and fought against such moves. I am against this as well. That is what happened.\n\nQ. Previous governments made similar attempts. You are aware of the repercussions they faced. How do you relate the current situation to your past experiences?\n\nThe impeachment of former Chief Justice Shirani Bandaranayake by the Mahinda Rajapaksa government was one such instance where we fought against interference with the judiciary.\n\nFelix Dias Bandaranaike was a very powerful person. It was difficult to find anyone who could challenge him. He was powerful and popular within his circles. We fought against him. Mrs. Bandaranaike intervened and, to some extent, tried to correct the situation.\n\nAt the next election, what happened? He lost his seat. After that, he could not return to politics.\n\nSimilarly, those who tried to interfere with the judiciary have not survived politically. It is the same story with Mr. Mahinda Rajapaksa. Every time a person in government attempts to interfere with the judiciary, they have not survived politically."
