    "md5": "f287493cd2e83c969fea5bfdaea5a1a4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1497,
    "chunk_text": "We do not want, at this stage of our lives, to become a party responsible for destroying that. Therefore, I have to do whatever is possible on my part to protect it.\n\nQ So now, do you actually think the government will back down because a lot of pressure is being brought against it?\n\nI have been in the Lanka Sama Samaja Party for about 50 years. I am a breakaway from the Sama Samaja Party because they decided to support Mahinda Rajapaksa, and that is how I separated from them. So, I have experience of working within the Sama Samaja Party and understand how decisions were taken in a traditional left party.\n\nFor example, in 1964, when we were at university, I was a member of the Sama Samaja Party. There was a proposal for the party to support Mrs. Bandaranaike\u2019s coalition government. The party held a conference that continued for four days. Only after four days of discussion did they take a vote and decide that the party should join the coalition.\n\nThose who opposed the decision, including myself and Professor Kumar David, who was also a faculty member at the university, expressed our views at that conference.\n\nI am therefore used to a system where decisions are taken after wide-ranging discussions with all interested parties.\n\nHowever, in the NPP, I do not see that kind of process. I doubt whether they will be influenced by outside pressure because decisions seem to be taken somewhere, but it is not clear where those decisions are actually being made. That is the problem."
