    "chunk_size_bytes": 1506,
    "chunk_text": "JVP, CPI(M) discuss economic reforms and governance during Colombo talks\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic stabilisation efforts, governance reforms and public welfare agenda featured prominently during talks between leaders of the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) and the Communist Party of India (Marxist) (CPI(M)) in Colombo this week.\n\nThe discussions focused on the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Government\u2019s measures to stabilise the economy, initiatives to improve public welfare, the increase in plantation sector wages and efforts to strengthen governance by addressing corruption and dismantling privilege-based politics.\n\nThe meeting was held on Monday at the JVP headquarters, where JVP General Secretary Tilvin Silva hosted senior CPI(M) leaders Prakash Karat and Brinda Karat. JVP Politburo member and Minister Bimal Ratnayake also participated.\n\nThe two sides also exchanged views on the political developments that led to the JVP and NPP\u2019s emergence as Sri Lanka\u2019s governing coalition and discussed broader political developments in the region.\n\nPrakash Karat served as CPI(M) General Secretary from 2005 to 2015 and later assumed the role of interim coordinator following the death of Sitaram Yechury in April 2025. Brinda Karat is a member of the CPI(M) Politburo and a former General Secretary of the All India Democratic Women\u2019s Association.\n\nThe meeting formed part of continuing engagement between the Sri Lankan and Indian leftist parties on political, economic and governance issues."
