    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6243df00e5833e1cd47811962c0dfc03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1678,
    "chunk_text": "AI as force for advancing human civilisation\n\nChinese President Xi Jinping\u2019s vision that artificial intelligence should be guided by human wisdom and international consensus is a compelling one. AI has the potential to become much more than a tool for improving productivity or generating economic value. If developed responsibly and used fairly, it could become a powerful force for advancing human civilisation, improving access to education, healthcare and legal services, accelerating scientific discoveries, strengthening food security, increasing agricultural productivity while using fewer resources, reducing the waste of natural resources and helping societies better respond to challenges such as natural disasters and disease.\n\nHowever, the more difficult question is how realistic it is to achieve this vision in the current global environment. The principle is widely shared: that AI should serve humanity and improve the wellbeing of all. Yet translating this principle into practice will require overcoming significant geopolitical, economic, social and environmental challenges.\n\nToday, many countries understandably pursue policies based on their own national interests, which do not always align with the broader interests of humanity as a whole. The world is facing increasing polarisation, conflicts, economic uncertainties and social divisions. Even within developed countries, many communities continue to experience inequality and limited access to opportunities. There is therefore a real challenge in ensuring that the benefits of AI do not become concentrated among a small group of countries, corporations or individuals, but are shared more widely."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-e653b380",
    "num": "dailyftlk-e653b380",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "AI as force for advancing human civilisation",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/AI-as-force-for-advancing-human-civilisation/4-795383",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785457218.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-e653b380-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "54a43e41a798acbcd4d29c7c9998ed56",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1334,
    "chunk_text": "Today, many countries understandably pursue policies based on their own national interests, which do not always align with the broader interests of humanity as a whole. The world is facing increasing polarisation, conflicts, economic uncertainties and social divisions. Even within developed countries, many communities continue to experience inequality and limited access to opportunities. There is therefore a real challenge in ensuring that the benefits of AI do not become concentrated among a small group of countries, corporations or individuals, but are shared more widely.\n\nAt the same time, AI development itself must not create new challenges or come at the expense of basic human needs, particularly in developing countries such as Sri Lanka. The rapid expansion of data centres required to support AI systems brings significant demands for energy and water. If not managed responsibly, these demands could place additional pressure on natural resources and affect communities that are already vulnerable. The advancement of AI must therefore go hand in hand with sustainable development and ensure that technological progress does not deepen existing inequalities. It is imperative for Sri Lanka to ensure long-term environmental sustainability and energy security when establishing large-scale data centres in the Island."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-e653b380",
