    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1e61cce89525eace533536ac07060eeb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2000,
    "chunk_text": "At the same time, AI development itself must not create new challenges or come at the expense of basic human needs, particularly in developing countries such as Sri Lanka. The rapid expansion of data centres required to support AI systems brings significant demands for energy and water. If not managed responsibly, these demands could place additional pressure on natural resources and affect communities that are already vulnerable. The advancement of AI must therefore go hand in hand with sustainable development and ensure that technological progress does not deepen existing inequalities. It is imperative for Sri Lanka to ensure long-term environmental sustainability and energy security when establishing large-scale data centres in the Island.\n\nAt the same time, we should not lose faith in humanity\u2019s ability to work together for the common good. History has shown that when communities and nations face major crises, such as natural disasters, people often come together to support one another beyond borders and differences. Sri Lanka has experienced this on several occasions (e.g. Boxing Day tsunami, Cyclone Ditwah). This spirit of solidarity reflects the potential for human compassion and cooperation, which must continue to guide the development of AI.\n\nThe question before us is whether we can act with foresight today to shape the future we want, or whether we will only respond after problems emerge and the consequences become difficult to reverse. While the gap between vision and reality remains significant, having a shared vision is an essential first step. The challenge is to turn that vision into collective action, so that AI strengthens human capabilities, reduces inequalities and benefits both current and future generations. Sri Lanka must actively pursue its place around the table to ensure AI serves humanity and improve the wellbeing of all.\n\n(The author is Professor of Computing, and Dean of Faculy of Computing, Law and Psychology, University of Buckingham)"
  },
