    "md5": "635d09d701213dcb6126c37f6e2b1dfb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "The likely path to consensus would be a sequenced reform program. First, fill the existing vacancies, invest in digital and procedural modernisation, and strengthen court administration. And only then consider constitutional changes, if evidence shows they are needed.\n\nWhether the Government adopts that sequence will determine whether this episode becomes a collaborative judicial reform process or a more contentious constitutional confrontation\u2026\n\nViewed through a constitutional rather than partisan lens, there are at least three plausible ways of interpreting the Government\u2019s proposals to reform the judicial system that prevails at present.\n\nAnd any disinterested observer should resist assuming either benevolent reformism or hidden political intent without evidence.\n\nThe significance lies less in what the Government says its objectives are than in whether the proposed measures demonstrably advance those objectives while preserving judicial independence.\n\nWe first essay a SWOT analysis of the Government\u2019s position:\n\nRetaining experienced judges could preserve institutional continuity during broader reforms.\n\nRetirement-age reform has only an indirect relationship to reducing delays.\n\nRisks appearing person-specific if judges nearing retirement would benefit immediately.\n\nDoes little to address lower-court backlogs, where most delay actually occurs.\n\nIntroduce digital courts, improved case management, and procedural reforms simultaneously.\n\nStrengthen public trust by involving the Judiciary and Bar in designing reforms.\n\nPolarisation between Government and the legal profession could derail wider justice reforms.\n\nInternational concern regarding judicial independence could affect Sri Lanka\u2019s rule-of-law reputation.\n\nIt is not impertinent to also examine possible governmental motives. Several motives are consistent with the available evidence. They are not mutually exclusive. Nor should they irritate the more defensive elements in NPP ranks."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-27d0edf0",
