    "md5": "1b4845104386c2c04ae20ef112310ea8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1623,
    "chunk_text": "Managing Yala for a better experience by thinking beyond Leopards\n\nAs Sri Lanka marks Sri Lanka Leopard Day on 1 August, this article forms part of a special series by the Wildlife and Nature Protection Society (WNPS), bringing together the voices of scientists, researchers and conservationists to highlight the many challenges facing the country's only big cat. Through evidence-based perspectives, the series aims to foster greater public understanding and inspire the collective action needed across research, policy, law enforcement, habitat protection and community engagement to secure a future for Sri Lanka's iconic leopard\n\nIn the early 2000s I worked with the teams at Jetwing Hotels and Jetwing Eco Holidays to convince the tourism industry that Sri Lanka could market itself as a destination for leopard safaris. The situation now has reached the other extreme, with the issue of overcrowding in Yala and the scramble to show a leopard having been a problem for over a decade. Sri Lanka has no shortage of bright and capable people who know what needs to be done. But there seems to be little progress in implementing good solutions. One factor could be concern from the hundreds of safari vehicle drivers to maintain the status quo of not having limits on the number of vehicles entering the park. But there are practical solutions which can maintain the current levels of employment of safari drivers whilst improving the experience for wildlife and for visitors and for improving the country\u2019s brand image. In this article I will consider solutions that will produce a better outcome for all stakeholders."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-84ae28c0",
