    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2253106885bc2e9e2bcfe0e568621a63",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1509,
    "chunk_text": "In the early 2000s I worked with the teams at Jetwing Hotels and Jetwing Eco Holidays to convince the tourism industry that Sri Lanka could market itself as a destination for leopard safaris. The situation now has reached the other extreme, with the issue of overcrowding in Yala and the scramble to show a leopard having been a problem for over a decade. Sri Lanka has no shortage of bright and capable people who know what needs to be done. But there seems to be little progress in implementing good solutions. One factor could be concern from the hundreds of safari vehicle drivers to maintain the status quo of not having limits on the number of vehicles entering the park. But there are practical solutions which can maintain the current levels of employment of safari drivers whilst improving the experience for wildlife and for visitors and for improving the country\u2019s brand image. In this article I will consider solutions that will produce a better outcome for all stakeholders.\n\nThere are two broad approaches to dealing with this. Firstly, mitigating the adverse visitor experience of too many vehicles by managing how vehicles are distributed. I know a blanket reduction in the overall number will be met with resistance, and I will explain alternatives. Secondly, turning the emphasis away from pursuing a leopard sighting and instead making it more about experiencing Yala as a park. Although I reference Yala here, everything I say here could be applied to any other national park or reserve."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-84ae28c0",
    "num": "dailyftlk-84ae28c0",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Managing Yala for a better experience by thinking beyond Leopards",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Managing-Yala-for-a-better-experience-by-thinking-beyond-Leopards/4-795390",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785462495.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailyftlk-84ae28c0-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b00fa3780328981cf1da258693aa87d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1808,
    "chunk_text": "There are two broad approaches to dealing with this. Firstly, mitigating the adverse visitor experience of too many vehicles by managing how vehicles are distributed. I know a blanket reduction in the overall number will be met with resistance, and I will explain alternatives. Secondly, turning the emphasis away from pursuing a leopard sighting and instead making it more about experiencing Yala as a park. Although I reference Yala here, everything I say here could be applied to any other national park or reserve.\n\nOf the two approaches I recommend, the first to manage the distribution of vehicles, has two strands. Firstly, the entire Yala Protected Area Complex of around 1,500 square kilometres has to be managed as a cohesive unit with many more points of entry and exit and allowing vehicles entering from one gate to leave through another. This alone will spread out the vehicles and make the park feel less crowded. Secondly, there should be a system of numbered routes with differential pricing and with different numbers of vehicles allowed on each route. This can be done in such a way that there is no blanket reduction in the total number of vehicles which will make it more acceptable to the local safari driver community. Numbered routes with individual vehicle limits can provide a better experience for those willing to pay for it. The numbered routes can be designed in a democratic way such that they all have a fairly similar likelihood of coming across a charismatic species such as a leopard, elephant or sloth bear. We need to avoid criticisms that only the rich get to see the iconic species. But what is different now is that you pay for the quality of the experience as each route will permit a different number of vehicles. This is no different to everyday life as a customer."
  },
