    "chunk_text": "Of the two approaches I recommend, the first to manage the distribution of vehicles, has two strands. Firstly, the entire Yala Protected Area Complex of around 1,500 square kilometres has to be managed as a cohesive unit with many more points of entry and exit and allowing vehicles entering from one gate to leave through another. This alone will spread out the vehicles and make the park feel less crowded. Secondly, there should be a system of numbered routes with differential pricing and with different numbers of vehicles allowed on each route. This can be done in such a way that there is no blanket reduction in the total number of vehicles which will make it more acceptable to the local safari driver community. Numbered routes with individual vehicle limits can provide a better experience for those willing to pay for it. The numbered routes can be designed in a democratic way such that they all have a fairly similar likelihood of coming across a charismatic species such as a leopard, elephant or sloth bear. We need to avoid criticisms that only the rich get to see the iconic species. But what is different now is that you pay for the quality of the experience as each route will permit a different number of vehicles. This is no different to everyday life as a customer.\n\nIn fact, a short guided excursion, in the drive-in area ($10 in this example), or a guided walk in the enclosed area, may be a more enjoyable experience rather than a long and tiring game drive spent in pursuit of a leopard sighting."
