    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "In fact, a short guided excursion, in the drive-in area ($10 in this example), or a guided walk in the enclosed area, may be a more enjoyable experience rather than a long and tiring game drive spent in pursuit of a leopard sighting.\n\nAs the entrance ticket costs are borne by the visitors, the safari vehicle drivers can still make the same amount of money for the vehicle hire, irrespective of whether the visitors may now cover less distance on a game drive. Therefore, they are more profitable. A key element of the shifting the focus away from leopards is that there will need to be skilled guides.\n\nEven the safari routes can be managed in a way to take the focus away from the pursuit of charismatic mammals. There could be a few large hides where up to a hundred people can alight from vehicles and go inside for watching birds and other mammals. Trained naturalist guides can be stationed in these hides to provide interpretation for visitors. The waterholes and hides can be constructed such that the birds and the mammals which are less shy will be coming to the water at close range. Some of the \u2018hide guides\u2019 can even be equipped with telescopes to provide stunning views of birds that have come to the water. Small clay pots filled with water can be placed close to the hide for enhanced views of birds and small mammals who come to bathe or drink.\n\nInside the park there could be designated picnic sites with clean toilets and running water, picnic tables and benches for visitors to relax. There could be viewpoints and points where people spend time to take their Instagram pictures. People relaxing in these picnic spots or large hides will reduce the number of vehicles that are dashing around at speed looking for a leopard. Needless to say, the park will need to implement a robust plan for litter collection, several times a day not only to keep the place clean and tidy, but to avoid animals foraging on discarded food."
