    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "World-famous climber Nirmal Purja among 10 missing after avalanche atop Pakistan peak\n\nNew York Post - Ten mountaineers, including a Texas woman, led by world-famous climber Nirmal Purja, are missing after an avalanche on Pakistan\u2019s Broad Peak \u2014 and an experienced American alpinist familiar with the conditions told The Post he feared the mountain could become a death trap.\n\nA snowslide struck the ten-person team as they trekked over the 26,400-foot peak in the Karakoram mountain range around midday Thursday, according to the Alpine Club of Pakistan.\n\nPurja led the expedition consisting of Mallory Geis of Texas, Sohail Sakhi of Hunza, one Omani climber, one Chinese climber identified as \u201cMr. Wang,\u201d one unidentified foreign climber, and five Nepalese mountaineers.\n\nPurja, a former Royal Marine, is a 43-year-old Nepalese climber famous for ascending 14 of the world\u2019s tallest mountains. His astonishing climbs were featured in the Netflix film \u201c14 Peaks: Nothing is Impossible.\u201d\n\nAuthorities have received no communication from the crew atop the world\u2019s 12th tallest mountain and have mobilized an all-out search-and-rescue mission.\n\nA high-altitude climber who led an expedition up Broad Peak last week told The Post Thursday he feared recent weathe conditions could spell trouble.\n\n\u201cIt had been raining every day, which means that at high altitude, it would have been snowing,\u201d said Napier Sandford Fuller, 53.\n\n\u201cI remember thinking, sh-t, they\u2019re going to have a hell of a problem because if it\u2019s raining \u2026 for a week straight, it\u2019s going to have a huge snow load on that mountain,\u201d added Fuller.\n\nThe dad of four is no stranger to dangers while climbing the same mountain.\n\nHe said he had a near-death experience on the mountain while leading the team under a contract with Seven Summit Treks, a Nepalese travel company that organizes expeditions on high-altitude peaks."
