    "chunk_text": "The dad of four is no stranger to dangers while climbing the same mountain.\n\nHe said he had a near-death experience on the mountain while leading the team under a contract with Seven Summit Treks, a Nepalese travel company that organizes expeditions on high-altitude peaks.\n\n\u201cUsually that would be fatal, but I was able to do what\u2019s called self-arrest. You flip a few times and then just grab the snow and kick your feet in like a little freight train; you just stop,\u201d he said.\n\nAfter the distressing ordeal and concerns about the equipment\u2019s quality on the mountain, Fuller and his sherpa decided not to scale the remaining vertical gap.\n\nA picture taken by the climber captured Geis, a travel photographer based in San Antonio who is a member of the missing group, camped out on a mountain ridge.\n\nGeis struggled with altitude sickness during an initial attempt to ascend Broad Peak \u2014 her first try at climbing a mountain of that monstrous height, Fuller said.\n\nFuller was Geis in her previous climb as they stopped at campsites to acclimate to the altitude.\n\nGeis hesitated about going up the mountain a second time but was convinced by a local guide that she was strong enough to do it.\n\n\u201cMallory, she\u2019s a Texan, a real nice young lady, her first 8,000 [meter],\u201d Fuller said. I was talking to her like she didn\u2019t want to climb an 8,000 \u2026 and met this guy, the guide, he\u2019s a local, and he convinced her that she was strong enough to climb an eight. But it\u2019s not really her idea at all.\u201d\n\nThe Alpine Club of Pakistan said in a statement that \u201cevery possible effort\u201d was being made to secure helicopter support and that \u201call available resources are mobilized at the earliest opportunity.\u201d"
