    "md5": "a34e44dd3ba3667a192ce4c63e6b1d41",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1771,
    "chunk_text": "RDA tender process under fire\n\nColombo, July 31 (Daily Mirror) - As Sri Lanka moves ahead with the Central Expressway Project (CEP 4) and the Ruwanpura Expressway, a senior engineer involved in the project has questioned why the Road Development Authority (RDA), the Ministry of Transport and Highways, and the National Procurement Commission have not allowed proper competition to be followed in the tender process.\n\nAccording to the senior engineer who wished to be anonymous, the issue is not just procedure. It is the possible loss of billions of rupees in public funds due to what appears to be a deeply flawed prequalification system.\n\nThe warning signs are already clear in the RDA\u2019s own numbers, he said.\n\nIn CEP 3 Section 2, the RDA allowed only six local contractors to qualify for seven contract packages. The result was predictable. With fewer bidders than contracts, competition was effectively eliminated. Each contractor was almost guaranteed a share of the work.\n\nThe outcome? Every single bid came in above the Engineer\u2019s estimate \u2014 by 16% to 21%. The total cost rose to Rs. 112.56 billion, compared to an estimate of Rs. 94.69 billion. That is an excess of Rs. 17.87 billion that will ultimately be borne by the public.\n\nThe Engineer explained that yet, in a striking contrast, the same RDA oversaw CEP 3 Section 1, where the number of bidders exceeded the number of packages. There, bids came in dramatically lower \u2014 between 38% and 42% below the Engineer\u2019s Estimate. The government saved nearly Rs. 5.9 billion.\n\nThe conclusion is unavoidable. Where competition exists, Sri Lanka saves. Where it is restricted, the country pays more.\n\nSo the question must be asked as to why did the RDA design a system that limited competition in Section 2?"
  },
  {
