    "md5": "2146e088a2b662e9b89b04735daf842a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "The conclusion is unavoidable. Where competition exists, Sri Lanka saves. Where it is restricted, the country pays more.\n\nSo the question must be asked as to why did the RDA design a system that limited competition in Section 2?\n\nWere these thresholds genuinely necessary? Or were they set in a way that effectively pre-selected a small group of contractors?\n\nThe Ministry of Transport and Highways and the National Procurement Commission must answer this, the senior engineer told the Daily Mirror.\n\nMore importantly, did any authority question why not a single bid came below the Engineer\u2019s Estimate? In any competitive system, that alone should have triggered alarm.\n\nInstead, the process appears to be moving forward without scrutiny.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s own experience proves that broader participation works. In CEP 3 Section 1, around fifteen contractors \u2014 including mid-tier firms \u2014 were allowed to compete. Many had no prior expressway experience. Yet they delivered the project successfully, on time and to standard, the senior engineer explained.\n\nIn fact, several of today\u2019s established expressway contractors gained their experience from those very contracts.\n\nHad the current restrictive criteria been applied then, many of them would never have qualified.\n\nSo why is the system now closing the door on the next tier of contractors?\n\n\u201cAs the RDA prepares to roll out CEP 4 and the Ruwanpura Expressway, there is a real risk that the same flawed model will be repeated, this time involving even larger sums of public money,\u201d the senior engineer alleged.\n\nThe engineer further explained that there are practical ways to bring more competition into expressway tenders without lowering the standard of work.\n\nFirst, allow international contractors who are already operating in Sri Lanka to bid. These companies already understand local rules, labour, materials, and environmental requirements, so their participation would not create extra administrative burden."
  },
