    "md5": "dfffb487ca05343500808290ad947f43",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1629,
    "chunk_text": "First, allow international contractors who are already operating in Sri Lanka to bid. These companies already understand local rules, labour, materials, and environmental requirements, so their participation would not create extra administrative burden.\n\nThird, encourage joint ventures between Sri Lankan CS2 contractors and experienced international expressway builders. These partnerships combine international technology, project management experience and financial strength with local knowledge, workforce and resources - a model that has worked well in many other countries.\n\nThe National Procurement Commission has a clear mandate to ensure fairness, transparency and competition. The Ministry has a responsibility to safeguard public funds. And the RDA, as the implementing agency, must ensure that its processes do not undermine these principles.\n\n\u201cThe solutions are not complicated. Broaden the prequalification criteria. Allow more CS2 contractors to participate. Encourage joint ventures with foreign firms. Permit specialized subcontracting. And most importantly, ensure that the number of bidders exceeds the number of contracts,\u201d the senior engineer said.\n\nAt a time when Sri Lanka is under pressure to maintain fiscal discipline, losing Rs. 17.87 billion due to weak competition cannot be justified.\n\nThe public deserves answers. And more importantly, it deserves a procurement system that works in its interest, not one that quietly inflates costs.\n\nIf the lessons from CEP 3 are ignored, the consequences will not just be financial. They will further erode public trust in how national projects are managed."
