    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o threat grows as Sri Lanka braces for climate risks\n\nColombo, July 31 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka must prepare for possible severe weather impacts from the developing El Ni\u00f1o climate phenomenon, which could trigger extreme weather conditions globally, a senior climate expert has warned.\n\nSpeaking to media, Senior Professor Buddhi Marambe of the University of Peradeniya said the current El Ni\u00f1o event developing in the Pacific Ocean is presently at a weak level but is expected to intensify in the coming months.\n\nEl Ni\u00f1o is a natural climate pattern caused by the abnormal warming of surface waters in the central and eastern tropical Pacific Ocean. It can disrupt normal weather patterns worldwide, often leading to droughts in some regions, excessive rainfall and flooding in others, as well as impacts on agriculture, water resources and ecosystems.\n\nProfessor Marambe said several parts of Sri Lanka are already experiencing severe dry weather conditions, with farmers reporting that water sources have dried up, affecting cultivation lands.\n\nMeanwhile, Disaster Management Centre (DMC) Director General Major General (Retd.) Sampath Kotuwegoda said authorities are preparing plans to address possible disasters arising from El Ni\u00f1o-related climate changes.\n\nHe made these remarks while responding to questions before the Committee on Public Finance regarding Sri Lanka\u2019s preparedness to face potential disaster situations.\n\nDuring the committee meeting, it was also revealed that the Disaster Management Fund, established under an Act of Parliament, has not yet been fully operationalized.\n\nMeanwhile, Environment Minister Dr. Dammika Patabendi said Sri Lanka could experience significant weather changes in the coming months as the El Ni\u00f1o phenomenon continues to strengthen, with its peak expected earlier than previously forecast.\n\nEl Nino to trigger unpredictable rainfall later this year, steps taken for food security"
