    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "Nine dead as thousands of migrants enter Spanish enclave from Morocco\n\nsky news - The announcement comes after local authorities in Ceuta had asked the central government in Madrid for reinforcements to manage a border crisis that had been escalating before boiling over on Thursday, with large crowds breaching the border fence.\n\nSpain's government said on Thursday evening it would send the armed forces to help the Civil Guard \"to maintain security in the city of Ceuta\".\n\nIt also announced that Spain's Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez would join interior minister Fernando Grande-Marlaska and visit Ceuta on Friday.\n\nRachid Sbihin, head of the association that represents Spain's Civil Guard officers who police the border in Ceuta, told the Associated Press: \"The situation is absolute chaos.\n\n\"It's not possible to give precise numbers, but there are thousands of migrants crossing.\"\n\nAchraf Maimouni, a member of the Moroccan Association for Human Rights in northern Morocco, said the situation was ''exceptional\".\n\n\"The Tarajal border was opened under unusual circumstances this morning and people crossed,\" he said.\n\nSpain's interior ministry said earlier it would guarantee the safety of citizens and the integrity of the nation's border, but that the government could not declare a national emergency - as local authorities had asked - over migration concerns.\n\nCeuta is located on the northern tip of Morocco, at the point where the Strait of Gibraltar and the Alboran Sea meet in the western Mediterranean.\n\nVideo showed crowds of people, mainly Moroccans, walking around the breakwaters at Tarajal beach onto local roads in the Spanish North African enclave, along with pictures of migrants celebrating their safe arrival.\n\nWhile the majority appeared to be young men, there were also families with women and children. \"Viva Espana!\", some shouted, according to a freelance photographer working for The Associated Press."
