    "md5": "df4fea07ecfb98e9563a27bb7e6a3c83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s Credit Rating: Progress Worth Recognizing, But Growth Requires Much More - Former Diplomat Kananathan\n\nColombo, July 31 (Daily Mirror) - S&P Global Ratings\u2019 decision early this week\u00a0 to affirm Sri Lanka\u2019s long-term and short-term foreign and local currency sovereign credit ratings at CCC+/C, with a stable outlook, is undoubtedly a positive development. The agency cited the country\u2019s resilience despite significant external challenges, reflecting the progress made since the economic collapse and sovereign default in 2022.\n\nThe Government deserves recognition for several important achievements. Fiscal discipline has improved, inflation has been brought under control, foreign exchange reserves have strengthened, and, importantly, the country\u2019s domestic revenue collection has improved significantly. Strengthening internal income through better tax administration and fiscal management has been one of the key successes of the economic reform programme. This has contributed to restoring confidence in the country\u2019s public finances and has helped stabilize the economy.\n\nHowever, while Sri Lanka has clearly moved away from the brink of default, it is equally important not to overstate where the country stands today. A CCC+ sovereign credit rating represents an improvement from default, but it remains firmly within the highly speculative category. It is not yet a rating that signifies a strong, sustainable recovery.\n\nA CCC+ rating indicates that the country continues to carry very high credit risk and remains vulnerable to adverse economic or financial developments. Should economic conditions deteriorate, there remains a real possibility of financial distress. Consequently, international investors and lenders continue to view Sri Lanka as a high-risk destination."
