    "md5": "ee1717e2b6537a606f0ac700493e5e52",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1650,
    "chunk_text": "A CCC+ rating indicates that the country continues to carry very high credit risk and remains vulnerable to adverse economic or financial developments. Should economic conditions deteriorate, there remains a real possibility of financial distress. Consequently, international investors and lenders continue to view Sri Lanka as a high-risk destination.\n\nOne of the most important drivers of that growth is Foreign Direct Investment (FDI).\n\nWhile improved domestic revenue is essential for fiscal sustainability, it is not sufficient on its own to generate the level of investment needed to transform the economy.\n\nUnfortunately, Sri Lanka\u2019s current sovereign credit rating remains a significant obstacle to attracting large volumes of FDI.\n\nA CCC+ rating signals to international investors that the country presents a high level of financial risk. Investors therefore demand substantially higher returns before committing capital. International banks will not comfortable to\u00a0 provide financing. Even in extra circumstances they do, such lending usually comes with higher interest rates, stronger collateral requirements, stricter loan covenants, shorter repayment periods and tighter lending limits. These conditions inevitably increase the cost of doing business and reduce Sri Lanka\u2019s competitiveness as an investment destination.\n\nGlobal investors have many options. When countries with stronger credit ratings offer lower risk and greater policy certainty, capital naturally flows towards those markets. This is why improving Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign creditworthiness is not merely a matter of national prestige\u2014it is an economic necessity."
