    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "Global investors have many options. When countries with stronger credit ratings offer lower risk and greater policy certainty, capital naturally flows towards those markets. This is why improving Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign creditworthiness is not merely a matter of national prestige\u2014it is an economic necessity.\n\nThe Government has already made meaningful progress in restoring macroeconomic stability, and that progress deserves recognition. But stabilisation is only the foundation. The ultimate objective must be a stronger economy capable of attracting substantial Foreign Direct Investment, creating productive employment, increasing exports and delivering sustained improvements in the quality of life of all Sri Lankans.\n\nThe affirmation of the CCC+/C rating should therefore be viewed neither with undue pessimism nor excessive celebration. It is an important milestone on the road to recovery\u2014but it is not the destination. The real measure of success will be when Sri Lanka earns investment-grade confidence from global investors, becomes a preferred destination for Foreign Direct Investment and achieves the sustained economic growth that its people deserve.\n\nThe writer is former Diplomat\u00a0 Ambassador Kana Kananathan is a businessman and lobbyist with over four decades of experience in the African continent. A long-time resident of Africa, he served as Sri Lanka\u2019s envoy to Kenya, with concurrent accreditation to 22 African Nations and was the permanent representative to UN Habitat and UN environmental Programme. Over the years, he has been the Elections Monitor across the continent, working closely with African governments, and built enduring partnerships with African leaders. He also served as Economic and investments Advisor to former President Professor Alpha Cond\u00e9 of the Republic of Guinea)"
