    "md5": "4be219f4c908cfe3be3a27c4044c3a85",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1654,
    "chunk_text": "What\u2019s  brewing at the Bar?\n\nToday a large section of the people in our country live in dire poverty\n\nGovernment is proposing bringing in an Amendment to the Constitution extending the retirement age of Supreme Court and Appeal Court Judges. A large number of lawyers affiliated to the Bar Association of Sri Lanka oppose the move. Members of the parliamentary Opposition stringently voice their opposition to the government\u2019s proposal.\n\nThe Bar Association and members of the Opposition claim the proposed Amendment to the Constitution would undermine public confidence in the impartiality of the Superior Courts. Neither body however, has let on, as to how by extending the age of retirement of Superior Court Judges the public will lose confidence in the impartiality of the Courts of law.\n\nGovernment on its part claims the need to extend the retirement age of Judges of the Superior Courts is to help clear the backlog of pending cases awaiting conclusion. Today there is a backlog of approximately 1.1 million pending cases. Government claims extending age of retirement of judges is part of an effort to improve the efficiency of Sri Lanka\u2019s judicial system and address a backlog.\n\nThe parliamentary Opposition on the other hand claims the government wants to extend the age of retirement of superior court judges merely to extend the tenure of a particular Justice. But, by making such claims,\u00a0 are not members of the Bar Association and Members of Parliament (MPs) themselves bringing into disrepute a senior member of the judiciary by making unsubstantiated charges against him? Is it not like cutting off one\u2019s nose to spite the face?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-9adb582f",
