    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "What\u2019s  brewing at the Bar?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Whats-brewing-at-the-Bar/172-347095",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785516711.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-9adb582f-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0607e679aefe7fd848ef90a21e7ae4c9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "The parliamentary Opposition on the other hand claims the government wants to extend the age of retirement of superior court judges merely to extend the tenure of a particular Justice. But, by making such claims,\u00a0 are not members of the Bar Association and Members of Parliament (MPs) themselves bringing into disrepute a senior member of the judiciary by making unsubstantiated charges against him? Is it not like cutting off one\u2019s nose to spite the face?\n\nThis means only around one-seventh of its members voted to oppose the proposal to extend the retirement age of Superior Court Judges. Again,\u00a0 the idea of extending the age of retirement of Judges is not a new one. As columnist M.S.M. Ayub points out, a Parliamentary Subcommittee consisting of members of all parties in the House, including the current Opposition parties, way back in 2016 themselves recommended the extension of the retirement age of judges!\n\nToday a large section of the people in our country live in dire poverty. Nearly 25% of our children, according to UNICEF,\u00a0 are malnourished and families are coping with rising costs by cutting down on meals. Small and Medium Enterprises (SME) which form the backbone of our industrial production \u2013 producing 51% and employing over 45% of the workforce -- are facing closure due to price fluctuations caused by the US war on Iran.\n\nFor a majority of these people whether a particular Justice retires on or before he reaches the age of 60 is of no consequence. Their problem is one of keeping the wolf from the door. It is a majority of these people who are often brought to courts on trivial charges of minor crime\u00a0 who are part of the over million pending cases.\n\nMany of these helpless souls awaiting \u2018justice\u2019, languish in our over-crowded prisons, either unable to make bail payment or afford to pay lawyers\u2019 fees when cases are postponed for one reason or another. A number of them were probably among those who died during the recent riot at the Negombo prison."
  },
