  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-e643a44e",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-e643a44e",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Betel Chew Ban Raises Serious Questions",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Betel-Chew-Ban-Raises-Serious-Questions/172-347096",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785516776.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-e643a44e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "276b87513dc4505dbb8d821498a286b9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "Betel Chew Ban Raises Serious Questions\n\nThe sudden Gazette notification prohibiting the inclusion of tobacco in betel victuals\u00a0 has left both vendors and consumers confused. A traditional betel chew consists of betel leaves, areca nut, chunnam (slaked lime), and tobacco. For many users, particularly among low-income communities, it serves as an inexpensive intoxicant and a substitute for alcohol.\n\nWhile protecting public health is a legitimate objective, the Government appears to have acted without fully considering the wider economic and social consequences. Sri Lanka, like India, Pakistan and Bangladesh, has a long tradition of betel chewing, and thousands of livelihoods depend on this trade.\n\nThe ban is likely to affect more than 100,000 betel vendors and around 10,000 tobacco growers, many of whom rely on this industry for their daily income. Furthermore,\u00a0 banning tobacco in beetle victuals could encourage consumers\u00a0 to\u00a0 shift to illicit liquor. Before introducing such a far-reaching regulation, the authorities should have conducted a comprehensive impact assessment and explored measures to help those whose livelihoods that will be affected.\n\nThe policy also raises an important question of consistency. If the objective is to reduce the health risks associated with tobacco, why has the Government targeted only tobacco used in betel victuals while cigarettes, which are equally harmful, continue to be sold legally? Is this because of the substantial tax revenue generated by cigarette sales, or is there a reluctance to confront the powerful multinational company that dominates the tobacco market?\n\nPublic health policies should be consistent, evidence-based and balanced. They should protect public health without placing an unfair burden on small vendors and farmers while leaving larger commercial interests untouched."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-3c6fa930",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3c6fa930",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Advancing Digital inclusion in Sri Lanka From Digital Divide to Digital Opportunity",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Advancing-Digital-inclusion-in-Sri-Lanka-From-Digital-Divide-to-Digital-Opportunity/172-347097",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785516848.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-3c6fa930-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f0c4c998a134d5914f4ef4abb453e434",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1794,
    "chunk_text": "Advancing Digital inclusion in Sri Lanka From Digital Divide to Digital Opportunity\n\nAs Sri Lanka increasingly embraces Artificial Intelligence, the importance of inclusive and trustworthy data becomes even greater\n\nThe true measure of success is not the number of online services or AI applications introduced, but whether they improve the lives of all citizens\n\nSri Lanka is entering a new era of digital transformation. From online public services and digital payments to the growing use of Artificial Intelligence (AI), technology is reshaping how citizens interact with government and businesses. Yet, the success of this transformation depends not only on technological advancement but also on ensuring that every citizen has the opportunity to participate in and benefit from the digital economy.\n\nThis important theme was highlighted during the National Workshop on Data Governance and Artificial Intelligence for Public Sector Transformation (July 20-22, 2026), jointly organised by the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs (UN DESA) and the United Nations Project Office on Governance (UNPOG). The workshop brought together policymakers, public sector officials, academics and international experts to discuss how Sri Lanka can build an inclusive and trusted digital future.\n\nOne of the greatest challenges facing Sri Lanka is the digital divide. While internet connectivity has expanded significantly, unequal access to digital devices, reliable connectivity and digital skills continues to affect many communities, particularly in rural and estate areas. Digital inclusion is therefore about much more than internet access. It means ensuring that every citizen, regardless of age, gender, location or ability, can confidently access and use digital technologies."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-3c6fa930",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3c6fa930",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Advancing Digital inclusion in Sri Lanka From Digital Divide to Digital Opportunity",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Advancing-Digital-inclusion-in-Sri-Lanka-From-Digital-Divide-to-Digital-Opportunity/172-347097",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785516848.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-3c6fa930-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "55d6b9b1a6f3f3c4bf3538b58c03ff84",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "One of the greatest challenges facing Sri Lanka is the digital divide. While internet connectivity has expanded significantly, unequal access to digital devices, reliable connectivity and digital skills continues to affect many communities, particularly in rural and estate areas. Digital inclusion is therefore about much more than internet access. It means ensuring that every citizen, regardless of age, gender, location or ability, can confidently access and use digital technologies.\n\nBy strengthening digital public infrastructure and enabling secure interoperability between government systems, Sri Lanka can deliver faster, simpler and more citizen-centred public services. Instead of asking citizens for the same information multiple times, government institutions can securely share authorised data, improving efficiency while reducing inconvenience.\n\nDigital inclusion also creates opportunities for better governance. High-quality, inclusive and interoperable data allow policymakers to make evidence-based decisions, allocate resources more effectively and design services that better reflect the needs of diverse communities. Ensuring that vulnerable and underserved groups are represented in national data systems is essential for creating policies that leave no one behind.\n\nAs Sri Lanka increasingly embraces Artificial Intelligence, the importance of inclusive and trustworthy data becomes even greater. AI systems are only as reliable as the data used to develop them. Responsible AI requires accurate, representative and unbiased data, supported by strong governance frameworks that promote fairness, transparency and accountability. When implemented responsibly, AI has the potential to improve healthcare, education, agriculture, disaster management and many other public services."
  },
