    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1b2a57e49b326ef42c8be11f24313895",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s hospitality leaders to chart Vision 2030 roadmap at global conference\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s hospitality industry is set to take a major step towards shaping its long-term future with more than 200 senior industry leaders expected to convene at the International Hospitality Leaders Conference (IHLC) 2026, scheduled for September 29 at the Cinnamon Grand Colombo.\n\nOrganised by the Sri Lanka Chapter of the Institute of Hospitality (IOH), the conference aims to move the industry beyond post-crisis recovery and towards a strategic Vision 2030 roadmap focused on global competitiveness, innovation and sustainable growth.\n\nBacked by the Institute of Hospitality UK, a globally recognised professional body with more than 80 years of international standing, the conference will bring together hotel owners, developers, general managers, tourism policymakers, investors, academics and commercial leaders to formulate what organisers describe as the country\u2019s first collective hospitality industry roadmap.\n\nConference Chairman Refhan Razeen, who is also General Manager of Shangri-La Hambantota, said the sector had successfully navigated one of the most difficult periods in its history and the focus must now shift towards sustained performance and future readiness.\n\n\u201cAs we move beyond recovery, the conversation must shift from resilience to long-term performance. Vision 2030 is about ensuring that our industry remains globally competitive, commercially resilient and prepared for the future. The International Hospitality Leaders Conference has been created to bring together the leaders who will help shape that future,\u201d he said.\n\nOrganisers said Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism industry is now at a critical juncture as it seeks to respond to digital disruption, rapidly changing traveller expectations and increasingly stringent global sustainability requirements."
  },
