    "description": "Uga Resorts launches community elephant coexistence project 2nd phase",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Uga-Resorts-launches-community-elephant-coexistence-project-2nd-phase/273-347113",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785518380.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-ae7453ea-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4ed108d4b037f034f7d7aea823e2ffd2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1740,
    "chunk_text": "Uga Resorts launches community elephant coexistence project 2nd phase\n\nNew fencing conservation initiative at Uga Ulagalla will protect 65 farming families while helping safeguard Sri Lanka\u2019s elephant population\n\nUga Resorts, the award-winning Sri Lankan hospitality group renowned for its authentic luxury experiences, has reached another significant conservation milestone with the completion of a second community elephant fence near its five-star Uga Ulagalla resort in Anuradhapura.\n\nOfficially opened on July 15, 2026, the five-kilometre electric fence has been developed in partnership with the local community and the Centre for Conservation and Research (CCR).\n\nThe initiative will help protect 65 farming families and around 132 acres of agricultural land while supporting peaceful coexistence with Sri Lanka\u2019s endangered elephant population.\n\nRepresenting a total investment of approximately Rs.4,500,000, Uga Resorts contributed more than Rs.3,175,000, with the local community contributing Rs.1,324,500 towards the project.\n\nConstruction began in February 2026 and forms part of Uga Ulagalla\u2019s long-term commitment to reducing human-wildlife conflict through practical conservation initiatives.\n\nUnlike the traditional boundary fencing, the community elephant fences are strategically positioned around villages and cultivated land, helping to prevent elephants from entering the settlements while allowing them to continue moving naturally through their habitat.\n\nRather than creating an impenetrable physical barrier, the fences act primarily as a psychological deterrent, reducing conflict without causing harm to the elephants and creating a sustainable solution that benefits both the wildlife and local communities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-ae7453ea",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-ae7453ea",
    "date_str": "2026-07-31",
    "description": "Uga Resorts launches community elephant coexistence project 2nd phase",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Uga-Resorts-launches-community-elephant-coexistence-project-2nd-phase/273-347113",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785518380.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-31-dailymirrorlk-ae7453ea-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9bf4905667dc3ff79f5d3c49e699d372",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1729,
    "chunk_text": "Rather than creating an impenetrable physical barrier, the fences act primarily as a psychological deterrent, reducing conflict without causing harm to the elephants and creating a sustainable solution that benefits both the wildlife and local communities.\n\nThe new fence builds upon the work of the Elephant Research Centre at Uga Ulagalla, established in 2020 by Raaben in partnership with renowned Sri Lankan elephant conservationists Dr. Prithiviraj Fernando and Dr. Jennifer Pastorini of the CCR.\n\nThe centre supports elephant conservation and research while helping to reduce human-elephant conflict in the Anuradhapura region, with its team continuing to monitor elephant movements and share insights with guests through visits to the centre.\n\nUga\u2019s primary electric fencing project at Pittala Konderama and Karambe villages, where Uga Ulagalla is located, spans 3.4km and has transformed everyday life for the 70 families in these village communities since being installed in January 2023.\n\nGuests staying at Uga Ulagalla can gain a deeper understanding of Sri Lanka\u2019s elephants through visits to the resort\u2019s Elephant Research Centre, where Katharina and her team share insights into Sri Lanka\u2019s elephant conservation efforts.\n\nUga Resorts owner and Managing Director Priyanjith Weerasooria said, \u201cAt Uga Resorts, we believe tourism has a responsibility to create a positive and lasting impact on the destinations where we operate. Supporting initiatives like this community elephant fence allows us to protect local livelihoods while safeguarding one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most iconic species. It\u2019s a powerful example of how conservation, communities and tourism can work together to create meaningful change.\u201d"
  },
