    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "Higher tea volumes meet sluggish demand at Colombo auction\n\nThe auction offerings totalled 6.0 million kilogrammes (M/Kgs), up from the 5.3 M/Kgs on offer the previous week.\n\nThe Large and Semi-Leafy Orthodox teas realised a price advantage once again vis-\u00e0-vis the Orthodox/Rotovane BOP/BOPFs, which continued to witness a sluggish market.\n\nThe Ex-Estate offerings were fairly similar to last and comprised of 0.9 M/Kgs. The teas from the Western slopes showed a marginal improvement, whilst the Nuwara Eliya/Uda Pussellawas showed no change. The Uvas had on offer a selection of seasonal teas.\n\nA few select Western BOP/BOPFs in the Best category appreciated by Rs.20-40 per kilogramme, whilst the others were irregular. In the Below Best category, select BOPs in the higher price bracket were firm and Rs.20 per kilogramme dearer, whilst the others were irregular and often easier.\n\nThe corresponding BOPFs were barely steady. At the lower end of the market, a majority of the BOPs were unsold due to a lack of suitable bids.\n\nThe corresponding BOPFs were firm for most, whilst the poor leaf invoices declined by up to Rs.20 per kilogramme. The Nuwara Eliya BOP/BOPFs did not attract any useful interest, perhaps following the relatively uninteresting quality.\n\nThe Uda Pussellawas were often neglected, following plainer quality. The Uvas \u2013 Seasonal teas, continued to sell reasonably well though at irregular rates, following the quality/overseas bids.\n\nOther non-seasonal types \u2013 BOPs, were mostly unsold, due to a lack of suitable bids, whilst the corresponding BOPFs declined by Rs.50-100 per kilogramme.\n\nThe CTCs \u2013 High and Mid Grown BP1s, had hardly any offerings. The PF1s \u2013 Better sorts, were generally firm, whilst the others were mostly irregular. The corresponding Low Growns \u2013 BP1s, were up to Rs.50 per kilogramme easier. The PF1s \u2013 Select Best, were firm, whilst the others were Rs.20-40 per kilogramme easier."
  },
