    "md5": "52a8f51cde1b1f70886b1fce30cab1d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "The program is being organised by the Hunupitiya Gangaramaya Temple under the guidance of Ven. (Dr.) Kirinde Assaji Thero, Chief Incumbent of the Hunupitiya Gangaramaya Temple, Colombo, and Sanghanayaka of the Colombo Nawa Koralaya under the Malwatta Chapter.\n\nThe trustees, devotees, donors and well-wishers of Gangaramaya Temple are supporting the organisation of the program.\n\nOne of the most important ways in which the service of Podi Hamuduruwo continues is through the Galboda Siri Gnanissara Memorial Scholarship Trust Fund, known as \u2018Nena Isura\u2019.\n\nToday, \u2018Nena Isura\u2019 supports over 420 students from rural and less privileged areas in different\u00a0 parts of Sri Lanka. These students have passed the Grade Five Scholarship Examination and\u00a0 need assistance to continue their education up to the G.C.E. Advanced Level.\n\nThe students come from areas including Monaragala, Kirinda, Padukka, Medagama,\u00a0 Ridimaliyadda, Sooriyawewa, Akurassa and Kalutara.\n\nThe program also supports students from families assisted by the Defence Ministry Seva Vanitha Unit, the Police Seva Vanitha Unit and the Tri-Forces Seva Vanitha Units.\n\nThe scholarship students will come to Colombo with their parents on 2 August. They will first visit the Colombo National Museum and learn about the history, culture and heritage of Sri Lanka.\n\nThey will also attend a special lecture by a university professor. The lecture will explain the importance of education and how it can help young people improve their lives and create a\u00a0 better future.\n\nAfterwards, the students and their parents will visit Gangaramaya Temple. A special lunch will be provided for them at the temple.\n\nThe students will also receive educational books, school supplies and other items they need\u00a0 for their studies.\n\nThe day will conclude with the \u2018Nena Isura\u2019 Scholarship Awards Ceremony from 2:00 p.m. to 3:30 p.m. at the Conference Hall, Temple Trees."
