    "md5": "b23c077d8ee30cfd5b4f5c08dfe695b8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 637,
    "chunk_text": "Great countries need great schools\n\nHow S. Thomas\u2019 College,\u00a0175 years old, four campuses, a stellar alumni body, can turn its inheritance into a national instrument of excellence and service\n\nNations are not built by treaties or treasuries alone. They are built, generation after generation, in the quiet work of forming young people who think clearly, serve honestly, and lead well. At Independence, it was schools like S. Thomas\u2019 College that supplied the country its first generation of statesmen, jurists, civil servants, and public intellectuals, formed not merely to hold office, but to understand office as a form of service."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-dailyftlk-05ac1c32",
    "num": "dailyftlk-05ac1c32",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "Great countries need great schools",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Great-countries-need-great-schools/4-795456",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785534541.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-dailyftlk-05ac1c32-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "34161c05cf463f452fcb43473ed0fbf2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "Nations are not built by treaties or treasuries alone. They are built, generation after generation, in the quiet work of forming young people who think clearly, serve honestly, and lead well. At Independence, it was schools like S. Thomas\u2019 College that supplied the country its first generation of statesmen, jurists, civil servants, and public intellectuals, formed not merely to hold office, but to understand office as a form of service.\n\nThe measure of a great school, in the end, is not the excellence it keeps for itself. It is the excellence it sends out into the world.\n\nThis matters nowhere more than in Sri Lanka, still overwhelmingly rural, where a child\u2019s postcode too often predicts their future more reliably than their ability does. AI has a genuine role in closing that gap: adaptive tutoring that meets a child at her actual level, gap-analysis that flags a struggling learner before any teacher notices, assessment that scales personal attention to thousands at once, at negligible cost. Used well, it dissolves the excuse that a rural child without a laboratory has nothing to offer the world. STC\u2019s four campuses are already a physical and digital spine reaching into the terrain where opportunity is thinnest; the task is to extend them, treating every gifted, underprivileged child they discover as the mission itself.\n\nGreat countries, in the end, are not the sum of their armies, their exports, or even their governments. They are the sum of what their schools were willing to demand of their young people, and how far they carried that demand beyond their own gates. Sri Lanka does not lack the raw material of greatness; it lacks institutions patient enough to go and find it, wherever it is hiding, and finish what they find"
  },
