    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "Nor does this outward mission need to be bounded by faith, race, or region. A country as plural as Sri Lanka is best served by an institution reaching across every divide, Sinhala, Tamil, and Muslim children alike, on the same terms, with genuine proficiency in learning other subjects through English, from O/Level through A/Level, since that is the real gatekeeper to the world\u2019s best universities. Done at scale, this produces something Sri Lanka has not had in generations: leaders from every district who never had to leave home to get there, each carrying the Satellite Scholar name as a mark recognised nationally and internationally, found, formed, and finished to lead on a global stage.\n\nThis is not a new idea for STC to discover. Bishop Duleep Chickera once urged Thomians to cross the road, to step out of a well-resourced campus into the world of those who have far less, not as occasional charity but as a defining posture. Writing as an Old Boy who once boarded within these gates, I do not raise this lightly. At 175, the school stands at the milestone that demands the question be asked in full: not whether STC remains excellent, but whether it will finally cross that road all the way, building an entire architecture, digital, physical, and human, that lets it remain exactly where it is and still arrive, fully, wherever the road has not yet reached.\n\nIt would be dishonest to present this as simple. An institution this size does not turn quickly, and every idea here is easier to write down than to fund and sustain past an initial burst of enthusiasm. The real risk is not a lack of resources; it is that a vision this size quietly narrows into a slogan and a handful of pilots while the architecture itself never gets built. Guarding against that takes the same discipline STC already applies to cricket and academics: clear targets, honest reporting, and the willingness to kill what doesn\u2019t work."
