    "md5": "7c818bf93021a59dcb84b1284f6b7ba3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1724,
    "chunk_text": "India\u2019s Cockroach movement: Lessons for South Asia\n\nFew political movements have attracted as much curiosity through their name as India\u2019s Cockroach Movement. At first hearing, it sounds more like internet satire than a serious political campaign. Yet behind its unusual title lies one of the most significant youth-led movements in contemporary India, raising important questions about governance, accountability and the relationship between governments and an increasingly assertive generation of young citizens.\n\nWhat began as public anger over alleged irregularities and leaks in India\u2019s National Eligibility cum Entrance Test (NEET), the highly competitive examination for admission to medical colleges, rapidly evolved into a nationwide campaign demanding transparency, accountability and institutional reform. Thousands of students protested in New Delhi and other cities, while millions followed the movement online. The Government subsequently announced reforms aimed at restoring confidence in the integrity of the examination system.\n\nFor neighbouring countries, however, the movement\u2019s significance extends well beyond India\u2019s domestic politics. It offers valuable lessons about youth expectations, digital mobilisation and the evolving relationship between governments and citizens across South Asia.\n\nCompetitive examinations occupy a unique place in Indian society. For millions of families, they represent the principal pathway to higher education, professional advancement and social mobility. More than two million students sit the NEET examination each year, investing years of preparation, considerable financial resources and immense personal sacrifice in pursuit of a place in medical school."
  },
  {
