    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ebc5fdd98ee5762d6db74d565a4bb994",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1837,
    "chunk_text": "Competitive examinations occupy a unique place in Indian society. For millions of families, they represent the principal pathway to higher education, professional advancement and social mobility. More than two million students sit the NEET examination each year, investing years of preparation, considerable financial resources and immense personal sacrifice in pursuit of a place in medical school.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the principal lesson from India\u2019s experience is not about replicating another country\u2019s response but recognising a common regional reality. Like India, Sri Lanka has a highly educated and increasingly connected younger generation whose expectations continue to evolve. Young people today compare opportunities, governance standards and public services not only within their own country but also across the region and the wider world\n\nThe movement therefore evolved beyond demands for a fresh examination. Protesters called for stronger institutional accountability and reforms to safeguard the integrity of India\u2019s education system. At its core, the controversy became a national conversation about public trust.\n\nIndia has a long history of public movements shaping national policy. From Mahatma Gandhi\u2019s freedom struggle and Jayaprakash Narayan\u2019s movement against corruption during the 1970s to Anna Hazare\u2019s anti-corruption campaign in 2011 and the nationwide farmers\u2019 protests of 2020\u20132021, Indian democracy has repeatedly demonstrated its capacity to absorb public mobilisation within constitutional politics.\n\nThe Cockroach Movement represents the latest chapter in this tradition. Unlike many earlier campaigns, however, it did not originate with political parties or established civil society organisations. Instead, it emerged organically through digital platforms before moving onto the streets."
  },
