    "md5": "1c3eca43eb0045a823133029f643c313",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "The Cockroach Movement represents the latest chapter in this tradition. Unlike many earlier campaigns, however, it did not originate with political parties or established civil society organisations. Instead, it emerged organically through digital platforms before moving onto the streets.\n\nPerhaps the movement\u2019s most enduring significance lies not in its immediate demands but in the generation that carried it forward.\n\nIndia is home to approximately 1.46 billion people and one of the world\u2019s youngest populations. Generation Z alone numbers well over 500 million people, making it the largest such cohort anywhere in the world. This demographic reality is reshaping political expectations and public discourse.\n\nUnlike previous generations, today\u2019s young citizens are digitally connected, globally informed and accustomed to immediate access to information. They compare government performance across countries, organise rapidly through social media and expect timely responses from public institutions.\n\nGovernments no longer enjoy a monopoly over public narratives. Every smartphone has become a platform for public debate, enabling information and opinion to spread almost instantly. This transformation need not weaken democratic institutions. Managed wisely, it can strengthen accountability and improve governance. It does, however, require governments to communicate more openly, respond more transparently and engage more effectively with legitimate public concerns.\n\nThe Cockroach Movement therefore reflects a reality extending far beyond India. Generation Z is no longer preparing to inherit public life; it has already become an influential participant in shaping it.\n\nAlthough the movement emerged from a controversy within India\u2019s education sector, its implications extend well beyond national borders."
  },
