    "md5": "514696a6c425d769c232a0d01e99953d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1927,
    "chunk_text": "The Cockroach Movement therefore reflects a reality extending far beyond India. Generation Z is no longer preparing to inherit public life; it has already become an influential participant in shaping it.\n\nAlthough the movement emerged from a controversy within India\u2019s education sector, its implications extend well beyond national borders.\n\nSecond, meritocracy remains one of the strongest pillars of social stability. Across South Asia, education is widely regarded as the principal pathway to social mobility and economic opportunity. When merit appears compromised, frustration grows; when fairness is protected, public confidence is strengthened.\n\nThird, Generation Z has fundamentally altered the relationship between governments and citizens. This generation expects transparency, accountability and timely responses. Governments that recognise these expectations are more likely to earn public trust.\n\nThe future competitiveness of South Asian nations will depend not only on economic performance but also on the confidence that young citizens place in their public institutions. Governments should therefore regard young people not merely as beneficiaries of public policy but as partners in national development. When their aspirations are recognised and their voices are heard, they become contributors to stability rather than sources of discontent\n\nFourth, digital mobilisation is now an enduring feature of democratic life. While social media presents challenges, including misinformation and polarisation, it also offers governments new opportunities to communicate directly with citizens, explain policies and respond to concerns more effectively.\n\nFinally, governments become stronger\u2014not weaker\u2014when they listen. Responsiveness should never be mistaken for weakness. Governments that acknowledge legitimate concerns and take timely corrective action reinforce rather than diminish democratic legitimacy."
  },
