    "chunk_text": "Finally, governments become stronger\u2014not weaker\u2014when they listen. Responsiveness should never be mistaken for weakness. Governments that acknowledge legitimate concerns and take timely corrective action reinforce rather than diminish democratic legitimacy.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the principal lesson from India\u2019s experience is not about replicating another country\u2019s response but recognising a common regional reality. Like India, Sri Lanka has a highly educated and increasingly connected younger generation whose expectations continue to evolve. Young people today compare opportunities, governance standards and public services not only within their own country but also across the region and the wider world.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s own history of youth movements and demands for institutional reform demonstrates that public confidence is among the State\u2019s greatest assets. Whether in education, public administration, the judiciary or economic management, institutions function effectively only when citizens believe they are fair, transparent and accountable.\n\nThe future competitiveness of South Asian nations will depend not only on economic performance but also on the confidence that young citizens place in their public institutions. Governments should therefore regard young people not merely as beneficiaries of public policy but as partners in national development. When their aspirations are recognised and their voices are heard, they become contributors to stability rather than sources of discontent.\n\nDuring more than three decades in diplomacy, I have observed governments and societies across diverse political systems and cultures. One enduring lesson stands out: the strength of a nation is measured not only by the size of its economy or the power of its institutions, but also by the confidence its citizens place in those institutions."
