    "chunk_text": "For contemporary readers, Jonklaas\u2019s influence might be compared\u2014despite differences in period and design vision\u2014to that of Shanth Fernando and Paradise Road. Each changed the visual culture of domestic life in Sri Lanka, making a new form of contemporary living desirable and recognizable. Jonklaas gave material form to the relaxed sociability emerging around Independence: a pre-air-conditioned world of home bars, dining rooms, verandahs and gardens, clubs, race meetings and entertaining, where growing informality coexisted with rituals of dress, status and display.\n\nAdvertisement in the catalogue for Aubrey Collette\u2019s 1955 Faces exhibition, with Jonklaas\u2019s name prominently displayed. (Courtesy of Cresside Collette)\n\nTerry Jonklaas as Citizen Perrra and Aubrey Collette as Mandrake the Magician at Havelock Golf Club. (Courtesy of Cresside Collette)\n\nThe interiors he designed for Mubarak Thaha offer a vivid example. Thaha was a well-known figure with interests in Colombo clubs and horse-race betting. The eight-foot-long bar occupied the space beneath a staircase. Nearby stood elongated swivel chairs with moulded high backs, upholstered in saffron-yellow corduroy and supported on cast-iron pedestal bases, alongside locally made interpretations of the Barcelona chair. The imposing curved bar was faced with panels of speckled Formica edged in brass; a brass foot rail ran along its base, while the countertop, raised on recessed iron supports, appeared to float above the structure. The bartender\u2019s side was as carefully resolved as the public face, with finely finished solid-teak carpentry. As a final flourish tailored to the owner, Jonklaas designed a plaster-of-Paris horse\u2019s head for the counter-top."
