    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-06655c16-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b4ba65a882b602a6e6d3ddfbb0598cec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "Reckless motorcyclists continue to endanger lives on Sri Lanka\u2019s roads\n\nMotorcycles have become one of the most common and affordable modes of transport in Sri Lanka, providing convenience for millions of commuters, students, delivery riders, and workers. Their ability to navigate through heavy traffic and reach destinations quickly has made them an essential part of daily life. However, alongside these advantages has come a growing concern: the increasing number of reckless motorcyclists who continue to put their own lives and the lives of others at risk.\n\nRoad safety experts have repeatedly warned that dangerous riding habits are contributing significantly to Sri Lanka\u2019s road accidents. From speeding through crowded streets to ignoring traffic signals, many riders demonstrate a lack of discipline that often results in serious injuries and fatalities. These incidents not only affect victims and their families but also place a heavy burden on hospitals, emergency services, and the country\u2019s economy.\n\nOne of the most common forms of reckless riding is excessive speeding. Many motorcyclists treat public roads as racing tracks, weaving through traffic at high speeds with little regard for the safety of pedestrians or fellow motorists. Speeding reduces the rider\u2019s reaction time and makes it difficult to avoid unexpected obstacles such as pedestrians crossing the road, vehicles changing lanes, or animals wandering onto highways. Even a minor mistake at high speed can have devastating consequences.\n\nAnother dangerous habit frequently observed is overtaking vehicles from the wrong side. Instead of waiting patiently for a safe opportunity, some riders attempt risky manoeuvres between buses, lorries, and cars. This behavior often surprises other drivers, increasing the likelihood of collisions. Narrow roads and congested urban areas make such actions even more hazardous."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-06655c16",
    "num": "islandlk-06655c16",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "Reckless motorcyclists continue to endanger lives on Sri Lanka\u2019s roads",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/reckless-motorcyclists-continue-to-endanger-lives-on-sri-lankas-roads/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785600480.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-06655c16-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7857e7d419684b6c5b51183f35db9510",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "Another dangerous habit frequently observed is overtaking vehicles from the wrong side. Instead of waiting patiently for a safe opportunity, some riders attempt risky manoeuvres between buses, lorries, and cars. This behavior often surprises other drivers, increasing the likelihood of collisions. Narrow roads and congested urban areas make such actions even more hazardous.\n\nHelmet usage remains another area of concern. Although helmet laws are enforced in Sri Lanka, not every rider wears one correctly. Some fasten the helmet loosely, while others wear damaged or low-quality helmets that provide inadequate protection. In many cases, passengers also neglect to wear helmets, leaving them highly vulnerable in the event of an accident. A properly fitted, certified helmet can significantly reduce the risk of fatal head injuries.\n\nThe rise of food delivery and courier services has also added pressure on motorcycle riders. Tight delivery schedules sometimes encourage speeding, abrupt lane changes, and risky overtaking. While many delivery riders operate responsibly, others feel compelled to take unnecessary risks in order to meet deadlines and satisfy customers. Employers and delivery platforms have an important role to play by promoting safety over speed and ensuring realistic delivery expectations.\n\nYoung riders are particularly vulnerable to reckless behavior. Newly licensed motorcyclists may overestimate their abilities while underestimating the dangers of busy roads. Peer pressure, thrill-seeking, and overconfidence can encourage risky stunts such as riding on one wheel, racing with friends, or using mobile phones while riding. These actions often end in tragedy, affecting not only the riders but also their families and communities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-06655c16",
    "num": "islandlk-06655c16",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "Reckless motorcyclists continue to endanger lives on Sri Lanka\u2019s roads",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/reckless-motorcyclists-continue-to-endanger-lives-on-sri-lankas-roads/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785600480.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-06655c16-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f5603272f3bbea328c3c99afa865ba4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1690,
    "chunk_text": "Young riders are particularly vulnerable to reckless behavior. Newly licensed motorcyclists may overestimate their abilities while underestimating the dangers of busy roads. Peer pressure, thrill-seeking, and overconfidence can encourage risky stunts such as riding on one wheel, racing with friends, or using mobile phones while riding. These actions often end in tragedy, affecting not only the riders but also their families and communities.\n\nWeather conditions in Sri Lanka further increase the risks associated with reckless riding. Sudden rain showers can make roads slippery, reducing tyre grip and increasing stopping distances. Riders who fail to adjust their speed during wet weather are more likely to lose control of their motorcycles. Poor visibility at night, combined with speeding or inadequate lighting, creates additional hazards.\n\nLaw enforcement agencies continue to conduct traffic operations aimed at reducing dangerous riding practices. Police regularly issue fines for speeding, reckless overtaking, riding without helmets, and other traffic violations. While enforcement plays a crucial role, long-term improvements require greater public awareness and responsible attitudes among road users. Education campaigns in schools, driving schools, and communities can help instill a stronger culture of road safety.\n\nParents also have a responsibility to educate young riders before allowing them to use motorcycles. Emphasizing defensive riding, respect for traffic laws, and the importance of wearing protective gear can make a lasting difference. Similarly, experienced riders should set positive examples by following road rules and encouraging others to do the same."
  },
