    "chunk_size_bytes": 1713,
    "chunk_text": "Parents also have a responsibility to educate young riders before allowing them to use motorcycles. Emphasizing defensive riding, respect for traffic laws, and the importance of wearing protective gear can make a lasting difference. Similarly, experienced riders should set positive examples by following road rules and encouraging others to do the same.\n\nImproving road infrastructure can also contribute to safer travel. Well-maintained roads, visible lane markings, proper street lighting, and clear traffic signs reduce confusion and help all motorists navigate more safely. Dedicated motorcycle lanes, where practical, could further reduce conflicts between motorcycles and larger vehicles.\n\nEvery journey begins with a choice. Riders can choose to obey speed limits, wear proper safety equipment, avoid distractions, and ride responsibly. These simple decisions may seem small, but they can save lives. Reckless riding may offer a few seconds of excitement or convenience, but the consequences can last a lifetime.\n\nAs motorcycle ownership continues to grow in Sri Lanka, fostering a culture of responsible riding has become more important than ever. Through stricter enforcement, better education, improved infrastructure, and personal responsibility, the country can reduce preventable road accidents and create safer roads for everyone. Ultimately, arriving safely is far more important than arriving quickly.\n\nOPA Facilitates Brainstorming Session on Harnessing Digital Transformation for Inclusive Development\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
