    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "Lanka explores passenger drones for future transport system\n\nThe Civil Aviation Authority of Sri Lanka (CAASL) has begun exploring the regulatory and operational requirements for introducing electric Vertical Take-off and Landing (eVTOL) aircraft, commonly referred to as passenger drones, into the country\u2019s aviation system.\n\nThe CAASL held a high-level technical discussion with representatives of EHang Intelligent and key aviation stakeholders to examine the potential integration of Advanced Air Mobility (AAM) technologies in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe meeting, held at the CAASL, followed an earlier discussion between EHang representatives and Deputy Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Eng. Janitha Ruwan Kodithuwakku, as the government evaluates emerging aviation technologies through a safety-focused approach.\n\nDuring the discussions, EHang presented its eVTOL technology and global developments, including obtaining a Type Certificate from the Civil Aviation Authority of China (CAAC), becoming the first eVTOL manufacturer to receive such certification from its State of Design.\n\nThe company also proposed establishing an eVTOL Regulatory Sandbox in Sri Lanka to support research, development, demonstrations and trial operations before possible commercial deployment.\n\nDiscussions focused on developing a regulatory framework, certification and licensing requirements, airspace integration, infrastructure readiness, safety oversight and security considerations.\n\nThe meeting was chaired by CAASL Director General and Chief Executive Officer Capt. Daminda Rambukwella. Officials from the Ministry of Defence, Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority, Board of Investment, Airport and Aviation Services (Sri Lanka) (Pvt) Ltd and EHang Intelligent participated.\n\nCAASL Director General and Chief Executive Officer Capt. Daminda Rambukwella at the meeting"
