    "md5": "a65dcca68c2371635f696f6dbf595e33",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1643,
    "chunk_text": "A Tribute to Sir Christopher Ondaatje \u2013 A Diagonal Journey across Sri Lanka\n\nSir Christopher Ondaatje was an enigmatic and illustrious figure, remembered as a financial magnate, a passionate explorer, a celebrated author, and perhaps the greatest \u2018Global Ceylonese\u2019. Born in Ceylon on 22nd February 1933, he left his homeland in 1947 to attend school in England. After spending five years in the City of London, he moved to Canada, where he built a vast fortune\u2014first in finance, and later in publishing. At the height of his financial power, he bravely sold all his companies to return to his first passions: writing and exploration. His passing on 20th July 2026 marks the end of an era, but his profound legacy endures through his extensive literature, his philanthropy, and his devoted family.\n\nI first met Christopher in 2004 when I was in London. He comes from the famous Ondaatje family. He has written a number of books, but the book closest to his heart was The Man-Eater of Punanai (1992), which recounts the tale of a man-eating leopard that terrorised the tiny village of Punanai in the early 20th century until an English tea planter, Shelton Agar, shot it.\n\nFar from being only the story of this cunning leopard, Ondaatje uses the narrative of his return to Sri Lanka after 38 years to pick up the pieces of his early family life. He uses the story of the man-eater as a metaphor for his relationship with his tyrannical but much-loved father. It is a classic parable and love story that has sold over a hundred thousand copies, describing a way of life that has now completely disappeared on the island he loved so much."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-e2e88e61",
    "num": "islandlk-e2e88e61",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "A Tribute to Sir Christopher Ondaatje \u2013 A Diagonal Journey across Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/a-tribute-to-sir-christopher-ondaatje-a-diagonal-journey-across-sri-lanka/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785601920.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-e2e88e61-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "32c704c24b7f3df641e25491c7ba979e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1763,
    "chunk_text": "Far from being only the story of this cunning leopard, Ondaatje uses the narrative of his return to Sri Lanka after 38 years to pick up the pieces of his early family life. He uses the story of the man-eater as a metaphor for his relationship with his tyrannical but much-loved father. It is a classic parable and love story that has sold over a hundred thousand copies, describing a way of life that has now completely disappeared on the island he loved so much.\n\nOndaatje stayed on the island, married a Portuguese lady in court named Magdalena de Crooz, and thus began the Ondaatje dynasty. His original name was Ondachi, but he converted to Christianity, and the family became known for their intellect and commitment to literature, learning, and religion. One of his descendants was even the first person to translate the Bible into Tamil.\n\nThe Ondaatjes frequently intermarried with Europeans, and almost certainly the most famous historical Ondaatje was Peter Philip Juriaan Quint Ondaatje. He left Ceylon when he was 12 years old on a scholarship to a university in The Hague. Sponsored by burgomaster Hendrik Hooft, he became head of the Patriot Party in Holland and led the uprising against the House of Orange (circa 1785).\n\nA powerful politician, he was exiled to France when the Patriot Party and the supporting Austrians were overthrown, but he returned to Holland with the advent of Louis Napoleon (Bonaparte\u2019s brother) when Louis returned to power in 1806. At that time, Quint Ondaatje was one of the six most powerful people in Holland and briefly ran the Dutch East India Company. Eventually, Bonaparte\u2014suspected of being jealous of his brother\u2019s popularity in Holland\u2014returned Louis to France and exiled Ondaatje to Java, where he died."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-e2e88e61",
    "num": "islandlk-e2e88e61",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "A Tribute to Sir Christopher Ondaatje \u2013 A Diagonal Journey across Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/a-tribute-to-sir-christopher-ondaatje-a-diagonal-journey-across-sri-lanka/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785601920.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-e2e88e61-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e94175f024bdd1fdfc57c6bdc4fd29f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1467,
    "chunk_text": "A powerful politician, he was exiled to France when the Patriot Party and the supporting Austrians were overthrown, but he returned to Holland with the advent of Louis Napoleon (Bonaparte\u2019s brother) when Louis returned to power in 1806. At that time, Quint Ondaatje was one of the six most powerful people in Holland and briefly ran the Dutch East India Company. Eventually, Bonaparte\u2014suspected of being jealous of his brother\u2019s popularity in Holland\u2014returned Louis to France and exiled Ondaatje to Java, where he died.\n\nThe best of Christopher Ondaatje\u2019s books are The Man-Eater of Punanai and Woolf in Ceylon. Christopher switched between living in Canada, London, and The Bahamas with his wife, Valda. Their three children live in California and Greenwich, Connecticut, in the USA, and in England.\n\nGiven his love for the land of his birth, it wasn\u2019t difficult for me to convince Christopher to return to Sri Lanka for a journey of discovery. The only challenges were when and for how long, as he was heavily involved with organizations like the Royal Geographical Society, the National Portrait Gallery, and the Somerset County Cricket Club.\n\nIt took 14 years for me to organize this diagonal journey in 2018, binding together many of Christopher\u2019s famous haunts: Yala National Park, Gal Oya Valley, Sigiriya, Anuradhapura, Wilpattu National Park, and eventually Colombo. This journey ultimately proved to be his last visit to the land of his birth."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-e2e88e61",
    "num": "islandlk-e2e88e61",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "A Tribute to Sir Christopher Ondaatje \u2013 A Diagonal Journey across Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/a-tribute-to-sir-christopher-ondaatje-a-diagonal-journey-across-sri-lanka/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785601920.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-e2e88e61-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1ce5d0eb61ca692ec0b215bd0fd55f77",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1696,
    "chunk_text": "It took 14 years for me to organize this diagonal journey in 2018, binding together many of Christopher\u2019s famous haunts: Yala National Park, Gal Oya Valley, Sigiriya, Anuradhapura, Wilpattu National Park, and eventually Colombo. This journey ultimately proved to be his last visit to the land of his birth.\n\nThe journey took us to Yala National Park, where we met with wildlife personnel and visited the Ondaatje Bungalow, a property Christopher had gifted to the park in 1992. Returning to Hambantota Rest House was an emotional event. The aftermath of the 2004 tsunami was shocking; the old pier had given way to a massive breakwater of stone across the mouth of the old Moorish harbour. The old crescent-shaped beach was still there, but not as beautiful as described by Leonard Woolf in the early 1900s. Here, Christopher nostalgically recounted how his father used to sit on the lawn to watch him swim as a 12-year-old boy, remarking that the Rest House was \u201cprobably the place that brings back most memories\u201d.\n\nWe then moved to the rugged terrains of Kumana, a picturesque haven for avifauna and migratory birds, offering an authentic and arduous camping experience. Heavy October rains turned the tracks to mud and made the tents damp, but Christopher thrived. He delighted in the camp food, roaring campfires, and daily safaris. We witnessed a rare sighting of the Ceylon Sloth Bear and watched in awe as veteran wildlife expert Shirley Perera calmly reasoned with an aggressive bull elephant, successfully asking the giant to step back and let the jeep pass. We spotted leopards in the rain and in a glade, prompting Christopher to remark, \u201cThis is the best life in the world\u201d."
  },
