    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "We then moved to the rugged terrains of Kumana, a picturesque haven for avifauna and migratory birds, offering an authentic and arduous camping experience. Heavy October rains turned the tracks to mud and made the tents damp, but Christopher thrived. He delighted in the camp food, roaring campfires, and daily safaris. We witnessed a rare sighting of the Ceylon Sloth Bear and watched in awe as veteran wildlife expert Shirley Perera calmly reasoned with an aggressive bull elephant, successfully asking the giant to step back and let the jeep pass. We spotted leopards in the rain and in a glade, prompting Christopher to remark, \u201cThis is the best life in the world\u201d.\n\nThe final leg tested our endurance in Wilpattu National Park. Flooded causeways forced us to abandon our camping plans for a small, rented bungalow nearby. Despite heavy rains and rough game drives, spirits remained high as we tracked the Wilpattu leopard and shared tales of the mysterious \u2018Devil-bird\u2019. At Sigiriya, Christopher astutely observed that the oft-ignored north-eastern side of the rock clearly displays the full head and flowing mane of the lion for which it is named. He remained a visionary, endlessly fascinated by the island\u2019s mysteries.\n\nIn Colombo, over final dinners shared with naturalists and friends, we reflected on Christopher\u2019s profound impact. He demonstrated the importance of reinventing oneself and taught those around him the value of honour and returning generosity without expectation.\n\nSir Christopher Ondaatje\u2019s illustrious life was defined by extreme experiences, bold adventures, and a deep love for his island home. Yet, his final parting words to us revealed his true priorities:\n\n\u201cMy legacy is my family. If I am going to be remembered, I want to be remembered for my family. Nothing else matters. If I have failed with them \u2013 then I have failed in life \u2013 and that is a dangerous option.\u201d"
  },
