    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "Working out my future with my father\n\nIt was a hot, sunny afternoon. A few dark clouds appeared and disappeared on the horizon. Humidity was beyond bearable as the pre-monsoon heat peaked. I sat in the shade of a passion fruit vine that wove a complicated pattern on the trellis connecting the back door of our house to the outdoor kitchen.\n\nA reluctant, intermittent breeze occasionally brought some conmfort. I suffered in my sarong, hoping for evening lightning, thunder, and a heavy shower to cool the surroundings, lazily watching the shadow of the trellis on the ground, as it changed with the setting sun.\n\nI could see my mother through the narrow kitchen door. She sat on the kitchen floor cutting vegetables. She was in a sweat, and every two minutes she stopped her work to wipe her face and acknowledge my presence with a smile. Strong sunlight barely let me see inside the kitchen. I wondered how she felt, sweating in front of the naked flames in the hearth of the windowless small kitchen.\n\nGradually, the sun penetrated the shade of the passion fruit vine and exposed me to burning heat. I could not move my chair, as it had only three legs; the fourth was missing. Five bricks stacked vertically supported it. It was a rickety contraption with a rough, thick plank on top, because its old rattan weave and the dirty cushion had already disintegrated. Next to it was my father\u2019s throne, with two armrests and a broken rattan seat. A flat, hard cushion stuffed with coconut fiber sat on it; its cover torn to expose the matted fiber underneath.\n\nMy father designed the house we lived in and two carpenters built it in 1961. He said he had built it to satisfy some of his senior relatives, who reminded him that his four sons had no home to call their own. They also reminded him that the children did not own even a single coconut tree to pluck from. He blamed Eusabius Uncle for not supporting him financially to buy a piece of land and build a house."
  },
