    "chunk_size_bytes": 1784,
    "chunk_text": "My father designed the house we lived in and two carpenters built it in 1961. He said he had built it to satisfy some of his senior relatives, who reminded him that his four sons had no home to call their own. They also reminded him that the children did not own even a single coconut tree to pluck from. He blamed Eusabius Uncle for not supporting him financially to buy a piece of land and build a house.\n\nHe borrowed three thousand rupees from a local bank, bought 38 perches of land at an auction at Maradanawatte in Hendala, and built the house in three weeks. Two problems in occupying it were the water supply and the toilet. Father bought three large concrete rings and sank them one on on top of the other in sandy ground. The well was six feet deep and provided brackish water, which we had to boil for several hours before it was potable. It served as our drinking water source and as a place to bathe and wash our clothes.\n\nConstruction of the toilet was a challenge. Father thought we could use an open pit without a roof to meet the immediate need. He said he would borrow more money to build a water sealed toilet. It took 12 months to borrow the money from his bank to construct a toilet. The walls of the house and the toilet were half-an-inch thick wooden planks. A mason cemented the floor. Rubberized sheets, usually used as roof material for cattle sheds and outdoor buildings, covered the roof.\n\nThe doors and windows were bought from a timber yard. They were of odd shapes, but the two carpenters fixed them as my father instructed. During the monsoon rains, heavy winds sometimes blew several sheets off the roof. My younger brother, Gamini, always volunteered to climb up in the rain, pull the displaced rubberized sheets back in place and nail them together."
