    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "The doors and windows were bought from a timber yard. They were of odd shapes, but the two carpenters fixed them as my father instructed. During the monsoon rains, heavy winds sometimes blew several sheets off the roof. My younger brother, Gamini, always volunteered to climb up in the rain, pull the displaced rubberized sheets back in place and nail them together.\n\nOne bedroom was my parents\u2019, with two beds. They kept their trunk full of clothes in their room, and father\u2019s weekly clothes on hooks above it. My paternal grandmother lived with us and had her bed in the second bedroom. Nihal, my elder brother, occupied the other bed in that room. I slept on a mat in the sitting room with Gamini. The youngest brother, Parakrama, slept with my parents.\n\nMy mother\u2019s kitchen was her domain. It was small, and she organized it well. She kept rice in a large wooden box with a tightly fitted lid. I spent my evenings sitting on the box, watching her cook. She offered me a piece to munch on when she cut a carrot or a cucumber. I eagerly waited for a piece of umbalakada from her. She occasionally taught me how to cook vegetables, meat, and fish, and, more importantly, how to start a fire. I enjoyed the aroma coming from the clay pots when she cooked food. I thought my mother was the most talented cook in the world.\n\nIt was a Saturday afternoon. Father slept a few hours after lunch on weekends, especially on Sundays. I worried about how he slept in the scorching heat. The rubber mattress on his bed was loosely spread over a wooden frame in a tiny room, just big enough for two narrow beds. On one bed, he sweated profusely but slept well, perhaps because the warped wall planks left enough space for a breeze to enter."
