    "chunk_size_bytes": 1682,
    "chunk_text": "It was a Saturday afternoon. Father slept a few hours after lunch on weekends, especially on Sundays. I worried about how he slept in the scorching heat. The rubber mattress on his bed was loosely spread over a wooden frame in a tiny room, just big enough for two narrow beds. On one bed, he sweated profusely but slept well, perhaps because the warped wall planks left enough space for a breeze to enter.\n\nHe could not sleep at night because of his worries. He often woke up his mother for a chat through the wooden wall that separated them. I thought afternoon siestas would compensate for his sleep deficit. I pushed away the idea that he could die of a heart attack. I consoled myself by thinking he was still young, in his forties. I never discussed my fears about his health with anybody.\n\nOnce, after a two-hour siesta, my father occupied his throne and demanded a cup of tea from my mother. Mother asked me whether I also wanted a cup. Strong black tea with lots of sugar was our afternoon tea. While sipping his tea, father turned toward me and tried to catch my eye. He, too, was in a sarong and had a small piece of thin cloth around his shoulders. He wiped his face with the fabric and blamed the weather for making him uncomfortable.\n\nMy armless chair kept me a few inches above him, which he disliked. He again told me that I should find another chair to sit comfortably. He wanted to talk to me without turning his head and looking up. \u2018Now you are a man!\u2019 my father uttered without warning. My birthday was three days before, and I had turned 13. It passed without a party, kisses, a cake, or gifts. In our home, nobody talked about birthdays, cakes, or parties."
