    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "My armless chair kept me a few inches above him, which he disliked. He again told me that I should find another chair to sit comfortably. He wanted to talk to me without turning his head and looking up. \u2018Now you are a man!\u2019 my father uttered without warning. My birthday was three days before, and I had turned 13. It passed without a party, kisses, a cake, or gifts. In our home, nobody talked about birthdays, cakes, or parties.\n\nOn our way, we watched colorful toys and beautiful clothes on the pavements and in shop displays. Neither he nor we stopped to touch them or check their prices. We did not want to spoil our chance of getting an ice cream cup by angering our father. He believed that his children should suffer as he did. His philosophy was that poverty triggers inner strength which, in turn, propels a kid to greatness.\n\nMy father\u2019s declaration on my manhood stunned me. He repeated it, looked closely at my face, and asked, \u2018Now you are a man, and you should know what you will do with your life. What are your plans for life?\u2019 I smiled, and he smiled too, but hesitantly. \u2018You think about it and tell me later.\u2019\n\nWe drank tea peacefully and then discussed my homework. He then listened to my mother\u2019s gossip. She did not raise her head from cooking but eagerly shared any new information she had picked up with my father. She complained that the next door neighbors were too noisy and wished they had fewer children. She said another neighbor was scouting the road in front of our house to see whether we had bought a new radio. She refused to go to church on Sundays because a cad raised his sarong to wipe his face whenever she passed his house. My father ignored her complaints.\n\nThree days later, after school, my father and I sat on our chairs behind the house. It was a beautiful evening; the sun was setting, and a fresh breeze brought us some comfort. My mother made aggala, sweet fried rice balls with leftover rice and jaggery."
