  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-d9d7c6ab",
    "num": "islandlk-d9d7c6ab",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "Hilton Samarasinghe: The man who gave faces to the dead",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/hilton-samarasinghe-the-man-who-gave-faces-to-the-dead/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785603060.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-d9d7c6ab-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d31a52e3a26221406eb75b7b84f6c90b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1789,
    "chunk_text": "Hilton Samarasinghe: The man who gave faces to the dead\n\nSri Lanka faced its worst air disaster over 50 years ago when all 191 passengers and crew of a Martinair flight were killed. The island was then confronted with the grim task of identifying the disintegrated remains long before DNA testing was invented.\n\nEnter Hilton Samarasinghe, one of the most respected forensic technical officers of the time. He was also the only Sri Lankan police officer then trained at Scotland Yard in facial reconstruction and identification, a giant leap in crime solving.\n\nThe Martinair DC-8 aircraft was approaching the Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA) on its way to Jeddah with 182 passengers and nine crew when it went down in Maskeliya. It crashed into the fifth mountain in the Seven Virgins range on the night of December 4, 1974.\n\nThe chartered aircraft was due to stop at BIA for refuelling after taking off from Surabaya, Indonesia. It came down 40 nautical miles (75 kilometres) from the runway at BIA.\n\nSamarasinghe was tasked with identifying the mutilated victims. Using technology considered cutting-edge before DNA testing, he painstakingly sorted the remains. He photographed skulls and other bone fragments and superimposed them on photographs supplied by next of kin. His work helped provide closure for grieving families.\n\nBefore photographing the skulls, he had to remove the remaining traces of flesh. Some of this work was carried out in Samarasinghe\u2019s workshop at his home on Vihara Mawatha in Kolonnawa.\n\nAs a neighbour and classmate of his son Sujeeva \u2013 who later became a surgeon before giving up medicine to pursue a more lucrative career in IT \u2014 I had the rare opportunity to witness the grisly, gut-wrenching process of handling cadavers of air crash victims."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-d9d7c6ab",
    "num": "islandlk-d9d7c6ab",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "Hilton Samarasinghe: The man who gave faces to the dead",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/hilton-samarasinghe-the-man-who-gave-faces-to-the-dead/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785603060.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-d9d7c6ab-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b8dd9c4ce51d82e01bb9a1cca9d1a22d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "As a neighbour and classmate of his son Sujeeva \u2013 who later became a surgeon before giving up medicine to pursue a more lucrative career in IT \u2014 I had the rare opportunity to witness the grisly, gut-wrenching process of handling cadavers of air crash victims.\n\nOn his centenary birth anniversary, I cajoled Sujeeva to share some of his memories of his father who was a powerful force in criminal investigations during his time. He was born on May 15, 1926 and passed away on March 12, 1979 just as Sujeeva entered his GCE AL class.\n\nProfessionally, Samarasinghe was a senior technical officer in the Police Department, working with the Criminal Investigation Department (CID) as its official photographer. He was the first to be trained in extracting finger prints from decomposed bodies to enable positive identifications.\n\nSamarasinghe was a silent hero of the Police Department at a time when integrity and fairness not only mattered but were fiercely guarded. It was an era when expert witnesses did not bow to political pressure. In more recent times, by contrast, Judicial Medical Officers have doctored autopsy reports to suit political masters, as in the murders of Lasantha Wickrematunge and Wasim Thajudeen. The credibility of expert witnesses like Samarasinghe was unquestioned. Sadly, that level of steadfast integrity has become a distant memory.\n\nOne of the first cases Samarasinghe helped solve was the 1954 \u201cBalagollagama murder\u201d of a jeweller, K. Rasiah alias Arunachalam, in Puttalam district. He was able to positively identify the murder victim using the super imposition of a photo of the victim over a photo of the skull that was recovered.\n\nIn April 1966, Dodampe Mudalali died after \u201cfalling from the fourth floor of police headquarters\u201d. He had been interrogated by the CID following his arrest on charges of conspiring against the then UNP government. Police insisted that Dodampe Mudalali had jumped from a window and committed suicide, a conclusion upheld at the first inquest."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-d9d7c6ab",
    "num": "islandlk-d9d7c6ab",
    "date_str": "2026-08-01",
    "description": "Hilton Samarasinghe: The man who gave faces to the dead",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/hilton-samarasinghe-the-man-who-gave-faces-to-the-dead/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1785603060.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-01-islandlk-d9d7c6ab-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9392c2dd16f90435dd379e10b8a7ceb0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1523,
    "chunk_text": "In April 1966, Dodampe Mudalali died after \u201cfalling from the fourth floor of police headquarters\u201d. He had been interrogated by the CID following his arrest on charges of conspiring against the then UNP government. Police insisted that Dodampe Mudalali had jumped from a window and committed suicide, a conclusion upheld at the first inquest.\n\nSamarasinghe had constructed a dummy matching Dodampe Mudalali\u2019s physique and by dropping it from the same height, he demonstrated the clear difference between a voluntary jump and a body being forcibly pushed. He could have created a case to exonerate his fellow CID colleagues, but he stood on the side of justice.\n\nIt was Hilton Samarasinghe who also helped identify the remains of his CID colleague Inspector T. L. B Bastianpillai, one of the early victims of Sri Lanka\u2019s Tamil separatist war in April 1978.\n\nAs a fingerprint expert, he established the identities of the mutilated bodies of Bastianpillai, his Sub Inspector Perampalan, Sergeant Balasingham and police driver Siriwardena. Their bodies had been dumped in a well at Murunkan in the Mannar district.\n\nApart from his crime-busting work, Samarasinghe was also a legendary photographer, well known for documenting wildlife as well as for taking wedding photographs in colour \u2014 a rarity then.\n\nHe had a passion for wildlife photography, which he pursued with a budding politician at the time \u2014 Ranasinghe Premadasa \u2014 who went on to become president in 1989, nearly a decade after Samarasinghe\u2019s death."
  },
