    "md5": "2ab27d835074003ec51ab84c960eacf4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1455,
    "chunk_text": "Contributions of Chanaka Amaratunga towards Constitutional Reform\n\nThe people of Sri Lanka owe a significant debt of gratitude to Chanaka Amaratunga for his efforts to gather a group of scholars, politicians and concerned citizens to formulate ways in which the Constitution of the country could be amended to protect democratic freedoms . The series of seminars which he, and a group of members of the Council for Liberal Democracy, organized between November 1987 and June 1989 as recorded in the book on Ideas for Constitutional Reform which emerged from the seminars, remains a vital resource and a continuing stimulus for further thought and consideration on building new structures which could better protect individual liberties.\n\nI will begin by recalling the fundamental objectives which Chanaka Amaratunga espoused in leading this venture almost forty years ago. As he clearly stated in the introduction to the book, the seminars were organized with the objective that out of \u2018. . . . the free exchange of ideas, to articulate the principles and discover the specific proposals which must be the basis of a reform of the current Sri Lankan Constitution. He and his compatriots in the Council for Liberal Democracy were also clear on the objective of constitutional reform: \u2018. . . we should begin the process of examining the Constitution of this country in its widest sense, from a standpoint of safeguarding the rights of the individual.\u2019"
  },
