    "language": "en",
    "md5": "84f2162ad071c9275c31dce473791f87",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1610,
    "chunk_text": "I will begin by recalling the fundamental objectives which Chanaka Amaratunga espoused in leading this venture almost forty years ago. As he clearly stated in the introduction to the book, the seminars were organized with the objective that out of \u2018. . . . the free exchange of ideas, to articulate the principles and discover the specific proposals which must be the basis of a reform of the current Sri Lankan Constitution. He and his compatriots in the Council for Liberal Democracy were also clear on the objective of constitutional reform: \u2018. . . we should begin the process of examining the Constitution of this country in its widest sense, from a standpoint of safeguarding the rights of the individual.\u2019\n\nOn the other hand, he also cautioned us against ethnic and religious divisions. As he pointed out, \u2018Racial mistrust, racial hatred, and a vision of a future based on racial lines has been evoked among the Sinhalese and the Tamils, and now even among the Muslims.\u2019\n\nHe clearly saw a need for a practical outcome in his inaugural speech on 28 November 1987. In his own words. . . \u2018. . . the crisis that rages in Sri Lanka at the present time could never have reached the gigantic proportions of today but for the regressive nature of the Constitution of 1972 and the far more authoritarian and illiberal nature of the Constitution of 1978. . . . so, we must recognize that if individual and political freedom, racial harmony, justice and peace are our objectives, they are unattainable without the enactment of a Constitution that is compatible with the attainment of such objectives.\u2019"
  },
  {
