    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4e1894c87551364b2beae1c0bf498f33",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1663,
    "chunk_text": "The extension of the life of the legislature elected in 1977 for five years, all the way up to 1989 through a referendum, was also clearly the use of the political process for partisan advantage. While the single university was replaced by separate universities with some degree of autonomy, those of us who were in the academic world wore disheartened when an amendment to the Universities Act in 1985 abolished the University Courts and returned the control of the appointment of Vice Chancellors to the President of the country. There were a number of important specific recommendations that emanated out of the presentations and discussions of the seven seminars that Chanaka Amaratunga organized.\n\nMembers of the legislature who resigned or were expelled from the party through which he or she was elected were to be protected from expulsion from the legislature. It was also agreed that legislation should be subjected to judicial review and should not have provisions which violated basic human rights, that a comprehensive bill of rights should be included in the Constitution and that the independence of judges should be strengthened. The Executive Presidency was to be replaced by a Cabinet of Ministers headed by a Prime Minister.\n\nIt was also agreed that devolution of power was to be safeguarded by protecting the powers and functioning of Provincial Councils. There was a useful discussion on \u2018group rights\u2019 and the consensus was that while the rights of groups should be protected in the Bill of Rights, \u2018where there is any conflict between group or community rights and the rights of individuals, the rights of individuals shall prevail.\u2019"
  },
