    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ad81cb4c057db735669ab7820bcddd22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1434,
    "chunk_text": "It was also agreed that devolution of power was to be safeguarded by protecting the powers and functioning of Provincial Councils. There was a useful discussion on \u2018group rights\u2019 and the consensus was that while the rights of groups should be protected in the Bill of Rights, \u2018where there is any conflict between group or community rights and the rights of individuals, the rights of individuals shall prevail.\u2019\n\nLater, in 2016, a parliamentary steering committee, chaired by Ranil Wickremesinghe who was the prime minister at the time, received recommendations from six parliamentary committees with proposals for a transition from the executive presidency to a parliamentary model, changes in the electoral system, and the strengthening of judicial independence (including more leeway for judicial review of legislation), as well as further devolution of power and protection of human rights. However, no effective action was taken before that government led by President Sirisena ended. The current ruling political alliance, known as the National People\u2019s Power,pledged before its victory in parliamentary elections in November 2024 that it would enact a new constitution abolishing the executive presidency, reforming the electoral system, and ensuring meaningful devolution. No concrete steps have as yet been taken to implement this promise but there have been continuing efforts to continue discussions on the issue."
  },
