    "md5": "19443f4d9ba86d6d6aee7ad18bcb7050",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1617,
    "chunk_text": "Later, in 2016, a parliamentary steering committee, chaired by Ranil Wickremesinghe who was the prime minister at the time, received recommendations from six parliamentary committees with proposals for a transition from the executive presidency to a parliamentary model, changes in the electoral system, and the strengthening of judicial independence (including more leeway for judicial review of legislation), as well as further devolution of power and protection of human rights. However, no effective action was taken before that government led by President Sirisena ended. The current ruling political alliance, known as the National People\u2019s Power,pledged before its victory in parliamentary elections in November 2024 that it would enact a new constitution abolishing the executive presidency, reforming the electoral system, and ensuring meaningful devolution. No concrete steps have as yet been taken to implement this promise but there have been continuing efforts to continue discussions on the issue.\n\nOne of the recommendations of the seminar series that saw swift implementation was the reduction of the threshold limit of 12.5% of votes for consideration of the award of seats to any list in the election to the legislature. This was proposed by both Victor Gunawardana and me in our presentations and generally won support among seminar participants. Despite some criticism that such a reduction would encourage the multiplication of contesting lists and fissiparous tendencies, the threshold was reduced from 12.5% to 5% through the 15th Amendment to the Constitution certified on December 17, 1988."
