    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018There is no agreement\u2019: Iran rejects Trump claim of Strait of Hormuz reopening deal\n\nFirst Post - Iran on Sunday denied reports that it had agreed to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, rejecting US President Donald Trump\u2019s claim that an outline of a deal with Tehran included the \u201cimmediate, complete, and total\u201d reopening of the strategic waterway.\n\nIranian state media also dismissed reports of an agreement as false, while officials warned that the strait would remain closed as long as the United States continued what Tehran described as hostile actions.\n\nThe semi-official Fars news agency, citing sources close to Iran\u2019s negotiating team and military, said there was no agreement to reopen the Strait of Hormuz.\n\n\u201cThere is no agreement on reopening the Strait of Hormuz,\u201d Fars reported, quoting an informed source.\n\nThe source said the waterway would remain closed while US \u201chostile actions\u201d continued.\n\nIranian officials also rejected reports that Tehran had approved any plan to change its policy on the strategically important shipping route.\n\nAccording to Fars, vessels would only be permitted to pass through a designated route after coordinating with and receiving approval from the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps Navy.\n\nThe agency quoted a military source as warning that vessels attempting to use other routes could face \u201cincidents\u201d.\n\nThe comments underscore Tehran\u2019s continued control over access to the waterway amid heightened tensions with Washington.\n\nIranian military officials also rejected Trump\u2019s claim that Tehran had asked him to halt a planned US attack.\n\nMehr News Agency quoted officials as describing the claim as \u201canother lie\u201d and an attempt to pressure Gulf countries through threats.\n\nThe officials said Iran\u2019s armed forces remained at the \u201chighest level of readiness\u201d and were prepared for any scenario."
