    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "Inflation hits three-year high as CB\u2019s target breach stretches to fourth month\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s headline inflation accelerated to 7.3 percent in July 2026, up from 6.8 percent in June \u2014 the highest reading since June 2023, when inflation was still unwinding from the 2022 economic crisis \u2014 marking the fourth consecutive month the Colombo Consumer Price Index (CCPI) has run above the Central Bank\u2019s 5 percent target, as food prices surged and wage growth failed to keep pace with the cost of living.\n\nData released by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) showed the CCPI, on a year-on-year basis, has climbed steadily since crossing the target threshold in April this year - from 5.4 percent in April to 5.5 percent in May, 6.8 percent in June and now 7.3 percent in July. Food inflation nearly doubled month-on-month in year-on-year terms, jumping to 6.3 percent in July from 3.6 percent in June, which the Central Bank attributed largely to a base effect. Non-food inflation eased slightly to 7.8 percent from 8.4 percent, though it remained the bigger driver of the latest monthly increase, with an adjustment to housing rents contributing 0.23 percentage points of the CCPI\u2019s 0.24 percent month-on-month rise.\n\nCore inflation, which strips out volatile food and energy items, also accelerated, rising to 4.4 percent in July from 4.0 percent in June, pointing to broader, more entrenched price pressures across the economy.\n\nThe pain is uneven across household budgets. Transport costs remain the standout burden, up 17.2 percent year-on-year in July, even after easing from 18.7 percent in June. Restaurants and hotels rose 10.6 percent, education climbed to 7.2 percent from 6.5 percent, and miscellaneous goods and services rose to 6.2 percent from 5.8 percent - all categories that eat directly into discretionary middle-class spending."
  },
