    "chunk_size_bytes": 1835,
    "chunk_text": "The pain is uneven across household budgets. Transport costs remain the standout burden, up 17.2 percent year-on-year in July, even after easing from 18.7 percent in June. Restaurants and hotels rose 10.6 percent, education climbed to 7.2 percent from 6.5 percent, and miscellaneous goods and services rose to 6.2 percent from 5.8 percent - all categories that eat directly into discretionary middle-class spending.\n\nThe sustained breach of the 5 percent target \u2014 agreed under the Monetary Policy Framework Agreement (MPFA) in October 2023 \u2014 comes just as the Central Bank is reassessing whether that target is still the right one. In June, the Bank opened a public consultation on the inflation target and related parameters ahead of a new MPFA expected around October 2026, when the current agreement lapses under the three-year review requirement in the new Central Bank Act; some critics have argued the 5 percent target is too high and should be lowered to as low as 2 percent.\n\nThe Bank\u2019s own quarterly fan chart, published alongside the July monetary policy round, shows inflation crossing the target line around the second quarter of 2026 and continuing to climb, with the central forecast path appearing to peak in the high single digits around the third or fourth quarter before easing through 2027 and converging back near target only by early 2028 \u2014 with the confidence bands around that path widening sharply the further out the projection runs, underscoring how uncertain even the Bank considers its own medium-term outlook to be.\n\nThe Bank has been explicit that the chart is a projection rather than a commitment, and has flagged elevated uncertainty stemming from the fluid Middle East conflict and its spillovers into global shipping, energy prices and domestic economic activity as key risks to that outlook."
