    "md5": "2c2e9095efa48c9007739799b216596a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "External deficits ease from April\u2019s peak, but remain sharply wider than a year ago\n\nTrade and current account deficits narrow for a second straight month in June, easing from April\u2019s record highs\n\nFuel import costs, which surged after global oil prices climbed on escalation of the ME conflict, have driven the moderation\n\nYear-on-year and cumulative H1 deficits remain substantially wider than a year earlier, pointing to a slower pace of deterioration rather than a reversal\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s current account and trade deficits narrowed for a second consecutive month in June 2026, easing off the peaks recorded in April, even as both measures remained substantially wider than a year earlier - underscoring that the external sector\u2019s improvement is a moderation in the pace of deterioration rather than a reversal of the underlying pressure, according to the Central Bank of Sri Lanka\u2019s (CBSL) External Sector Performance report for June.\n\nThe trade deficit has narrowed in each of the past two months, from a record US$ 1.38 billion in April \u2014 the widest monthly trade gap since CBSL records began in 2002 \u2014 to roughly US$ 1.0 billion in May and US$ 828.5 million in June.\n\nThe current account deficit followed the same trajectory, easing from US$ 525.2 million in April to US$ 194.5 million in May and US$ 148.7 million in June. Fuel import costs, the single largest driver of April\u2019s blowout, have led the moderation: fuel expenditure fell from US$ 886 million in April to US$ 536 million in May and US$ 465 million in June, even as it remained 40.2 percent higher than a year earlier.\n\nGlobal oil prices began climbing sharply after the Middle East conflict escalated in late February 2026, and it was this spike, feeding through into Sri Lanka\u2019s import bill with a lag, that produced April\u2019s outsized fuel bill and, in turn, the record monthly trade gap."
  },
