    "md5": "dd1944b7e427ca049b035979a35ab5cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1636,
    "chunk_text": "Global oil prices began climbing sharply after the Middle East conflict escalated in late February 2026, and it was this spike, feeding through into Sri Lanka\u2019s import bill with a lag, that produced April\u2019s outsized fuel bill and, in turn, the record monthly trade gap.\n\nThe cumulative picture for the first half of the year tells the same story: the current account moved from a US$ 1.44 billion surplus in January-June 2025 to a US$ 245.4 million deficit in the same period this year, a swing of close to US$ 1.7 billion, while the cumulative trade deficit widened by 67.9 percent to US$ 5.49 billion, from US$ 3.27 billion a year earlier \u2014 already approaching the roughly US$ 6 billion trade gap recorded for the whole of 2024.\n\nThe deceleration, therefore, reflects fuel prices retreating from their April spike rather than a broader easing of the pressures the Central Bank has attributed to the Middle East conflict since it escalated in late February.\n\nThose pressures are still visible elsewhere in the external accounts. The services surplus \u2014 traditionally a cushion against the trade gap \u2014 contracted by 33.8 percent year-on-year in June and by 22.4 percent\u00a0 cumulatively in H1, as outbound travel spending by Sri Lankans rose 70.4 percent in June and 82.7 percent for the half-year, outpacing inbound receipts. Tourist arrivals fell 9.9 percent year-on-year in June to 124,551, with earnings down 10.8 percent to US$ 151.1 million, extending an 11.8 percent cumulative decline in H1 tourism receipts.\n\nTerms of trade also deteriorated further in June, as import prices continued to rise faster than export prices."
