    "chunk_text": "Those pressures are still visible elsewhere in the external accounts. The services surplus \u2014 traditionally a cushion against the trade gap \u2014 contracted by 33.8 percent year-on-year in June and by 22.4 percent\u00a0 cumulatively in H1, as outbound travel spending by Sri Lankans rose 70.4 percent in June and 82.7 percent for the half-year, outpacing inbound receipts. Tourist arrivals fell 9.9 percent year-on-year in June to 124,551, with earnings down 10.8 percent to US$ 151.1 million, extending an 11.8 percent cumulative decline in H1 tourism receipts.\n\nTerms of trade also deteriorated further in June, as import prices continued to rise faster than export prices.\n\nGross official reserves, which include the swap facility with the People\u2019s Bank of China, stood at US$ 6.5 billion at end-June, covering 3.2 months of imports. The rupee had depreciated 7.8 percent against the US dollar on a year-to-date basis by end-July, closing at Rs. 336.05, though the Central Bank says the pace of depreciation has moderated following recently implemented monetary, fiscal and macroprudential measures.\n\nA 27.1 percent month-on-month fall in vehicle import expenditure in June, to US$ 182 million, offers a further early sign of import compression, though it will take several more months of data to establish whether this marks a genuine turning point or simply a pause before the conflict\u2019s effects reassert themselves."
