    "language": "en",
    "md5": "053bb4d74ea37b9133299cf9d7cfa4be",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "Prisons of neglect!\n\nIf those in power continue to look away, the next riot will not be a surprise\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s prisons are not failing quietly. They are collapsing in full view of the State.\n\nAnd every time the system breaks, lives are lost. From Welikada to Mahara to Negombo, the pattern is painfully clear. The 2012 Welikada prison riot should have been a national wake up call. Twenty seven inmates were killed in a single incident that raised serious questions about command responsibility and use of force. A decade later, accountability came slowly and selectively, but the deeper issues remained untouched.\n\nGo back further to 1983 and Welikada again became a symbol of failure when detainees were massacred inside a high security facility. That tragedy was not just about ethnic violence. It exposed how easily control can be lost inside prison walls.\n\nIn 2020, Mahara prison told the same story in a different form. Overcrowding and fear of a COVID outbreak led to unrest. The response was live ammunition. Eleven inmates were killed. More than 100 were injured. Even then, the warning signs were clear. Prisons were overcrowded, under prepared and dangerously volatile. The recent Negombo riot there left at least 26 dead, including prison officers. More than 100 were injured. The violence was linked to gang rivalries and drug networks operating inside the prison. But the deeper cause was far more obvious. Negombo prison was holding nearly 2,400 inmates in a facility built for around 650.\n\nResponsibility cannot be pushed aside any longer. The Ministry of Justice, the Department of Prisons and successive governments must answer for the state of these institutions. Year after year, budget allocations for prison infrastructure and rehabilitation remain minimal compared to the growing prison population."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
