    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9481288bf5f1e4d9c4081a9ec2d63136",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "Responsibility cannot be pushed aside any longer. The Ministry of Justice, the Department of Prisons and successive governments must answer for the state of these institutions. Year after year, budget allocations for prison infrastructure and rehabilitation remain minimal compared to the growing prison population.\n\nDrug networks continue to operate with alarming ease. Organised gangs control sections of prisons. Those with money and connections gain protection. In Negombo, reports indicate that inmates suspected of cooperating with authorities were targeted during the violence.\n\nAnd when riots break out, it is not the powerful who suffer most. It is the ordinary inmates. First time offenders. Men serving short sentences and individuals who entered prison for minor crimes. Prison officers, too, are casualties of this failure. Understaffed and often under equipped, they are expected to manage thousands of inmates in high tension environments. In Negombo, several officers lost their lives trying to contain the unrest.\n\nSri Lanka has not adapted its legal framework to match reality. If prisons cannot cope with the numbers, then the answer cannot be to keep sending more people into them. Yet that is exactly what continues to happen. There are clear alternatives. House arrest for non violent offenders is one. With proper monitoring, it can reduce overcrowding without compromising accountability. Detention centres for minor offences can prevent low risk inmates from being exposed to hardened criminals. Rehabilitation centres, particularly for drug related offences, can address addiction instead of feeding criminal networks.\n\nThese are not new ideas. The Ministry of Justice must move beyond statements and begin structural reform. The Department of Prisons must be given the resources it needs, not just to contain inmates but to manage them safely. Parliament must take responsibility for updating outdated laws."
  },
