    "md5": "06a4b43a6bef022b4cb46c9b5310761e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1390,
    "chunk_text": "PC Elections Phobia?  Only Cure, Face It\n\nGovernments often fear that an election held at an unfavourable time may lead to defeat\n\nThe continued postponement of Provincial Council (PC) elections in Sri Lanka has become a matter of serious concern for democratic governance and public representation. Provincial Councils were established to promote devolution of power and enable citizens to participate in decision-making at the regional level. When elections are repeatedly delayed, these institutions lose their democratic legitimacy, accountability is weakened, and public confidence in representative governance is undermined.\n\nThe history of postponing PC elections reflects a recurring pattern of political, legal, and administrative challenges. Since 2017\u20132018, elections have been repeatedly deferred due to changes to the electoral system, delimitation issues, legislative amendments, and related administrative difficulties. As a result, several PCs have remained without elected representatives for extended periods, with their functions being carried out by Governors and government officials rather than elected members. This prolonged absence of elected provincial administrations has been criticised by civil society organisations, constitutional experts, and opposition parties as a setback to democratic decentralisation and public participation in provincial governance."
  },
