    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7f66ade312b2ab62b4397d27f530bde8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1913,
    "chunk_text": "The history of postponing PC elections reflects a recurring pattern of political, legal, and administrative challenges. Since 2017\u20132018, elections have been repeatedly deferred due to changes to the electoral system, delimitation issues, legislative amendments, and related administrative difficulties. As a result, several PCs have remained without elected representatives for extended periods, with their functions being carried out by Governors and government officials rather than elected members. This prolonged absence of elected provincial administrations has been criticised by civil society organisations, constitutional experts, and opposition parties as a setback to democratic decentralisation and public participation in provincial governance.\n\nGovernments have argued that electoral reforms, legal amendments, and administrative preparations are necessary before conducting elections. Such concerns may have some validity; however, critics maintain that they cannot justify indefinite postponement of elections. In a functioning democracy, elections are the primary mechanism through which citizens renew or reject the mandate given to their representatives. Prolonged delays risk weakening democratic institutions, reducing public trust, and undermining confidence in the rule of law.\n\nThe fear of facing voters at an unfavourable political moment appears to be a recurring phenomenon among political leaders. Unlike common psychological fears, this political anxiety, sometimes described as \u201cElection Phobia\u201d, is rarely acknowledged openly, yet it has become increasingly visible in Sri Lanka\u2019s political landscape. A phobia is generally understood as an intense and irrational fear that results in avoidance behaviour. The reluctance of political authorities to face the electorate at constitutionally determined intervals could reasonably be viewed as a political form of such avoidance."
  },
