    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "The fear of facing voters at an unfavourable political moment appears to be a recurring phenomenon among political leaders. Unlike common psychological fears, this political anxiety, sometimes described as \u201cElection Phobia\u201d, is rarely acknowledged openly, yet it has become increasingly visible in Sri Lanka\u2019s political landscape. A phobia is generally understood as an intense and irrational fear that results in avoidance behaviour. The reluctance of political authorities to face the electorate at constitutionally determined intervals could reasonably be viewed as a political form of such avoidance.\n\nSeveral fundamental rights petitions were subsequently filed in the Supreme Court challenging the government\u2019s actions. In its judgement delivered in January 1999, the Court examined the legality of the emergency declaration and the postponement of the election. Justice Mark Fernando, with Chief Justice G. P. S. de Silva and Justice Gunasekera concurring, criticized the failure to adequately protect the electoral process. The judgement emphasized that the Provincial Councils Elections Act was intended to ensure that elections would be held within a short period after dissolution,\u00a0 and that cancellation of the election could not be justified under the Public Security Ordinance.\n\nJustice Mark Fernando further held that the suspension of postal voting arrangements was unlawful, arbitrary, and not carried out in good faith. He stressed that the Elections Commissioner had been granted constitutional independence under Articles 103 and 104 to conduct elections according to law,\u00a0 and to prevent improper interference or unnecessary delays. The Commissioner had both the authority and the responsibility to determine a new election date despite disputes surrounding emergency regulations."
  },
