    "md5": "4203525c44f7912b4dafb29476bb53d8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "Justice Mark Fernando further held that the suspension of postal voting arrangements was unlawful, arbitrary, and not carried out in good faith. He stressed that the Elections Commissioner had been granted constitutional independence under Articles 103 and 104 to conduct elections according to law,\u00a0 and to prevent improper interference or unnecessary delays. The Commissioner had both the authority and the responsibility to determine a new election date despite disputes surrounding emergency regulations.\n\nThe Buddhist principle of Avirodha, included in the concept of Dasaraja Dharma, emphasises respecting the wishes of the people and avoiding actions that obstruct public welfare. In a democracy, the will of citizens can only be determined through free, fair, and timely elections. Attempts to avoid elections are therefore inconsistent with democratic values and weaken the relationship between the government and the people.\n\nSri Lanka has witnessed similar debates in relation to national elections. In 1982, the government of President J. R. Jayewardene conducted a referendum to extend the life of Parliament instead of holding a general election. Critics argued that the move was intended to preserve the government\u2019s strong parliamentary majority and avoid the uncertainty of an election under the newly introduced proportional representation system. The Liberal Party, led by Dr. Chanaka Amaratunga and Dr. Rajiva Wijesinghe, broke away from its alliance with the UNP and described the postponement as a threat to democratic rights.\n\nSimilarly, in 1975, the United Front government under Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike used its parliamentary majority to extend the life of Parliament by two years. When elections were eventually held in 1977, the government suffered a historic defeat. These examples demonstrate that postponing elections may provide temporary political relief but can also deepen public dissatisfaction and increase demands for change."
  },
