    "md5": "381b577a3faf5855dbdb5ae26e04deaf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1496,
    "chunk_text": "Similarly, in 1975, the United Front government under Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike used its parliamentary majority to extend the life of Parliament by two years. When elections were eventually held in 1977, the government suffered a historic defeat. These examples demonstrate that postponing elections may provide temporary political relief but can also deepen public dissatisfaction and increase demands for change.\n\nA defeat at a PC election may influence future presidential or parliamentary contests, but avoiding elections does not remove public dissatisfaction. Instead, it can strengthen opposition movements and increase distrust in government. Political leaders must recognise that authority maintained through avoidance rather than public consent is neither stable nor sustainable.\n\nThe most effective solution to \u201cElection Phobia\u201d is to face the electorate. Conducting PC elections on time, respecting the people\u2019s mandate, and allowing democratic institutions to function are essential for restoring public confidence and safeguarding the long-term health of Sri Lanka\u2019s democracy.\n\nBreaking this cycle requires a genuine commitment from all political parties, whether in government or opposition. Respect for constitutional electoral timelines, transparent electoral reforms, and the timely conduct of PC elections are necessary to restore democratic legitimacy and ensure that provincial governance reflects the will of the people rather than political convenience."
  },
