    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "The only difference is the medium through which they learn. Instead of printed pages, they read and write using Braille. It was a simple yet powerful reminder that blindness does not lower the standard of education, it simply changes the way that education is accessed.\n\nThe afternoon brought one of the day\u2019s highlights: a game of cricket.\n\nFor us Wesleyites, it was a reminder that ability often depends far more on opportunity and adaptation than on the assumptions we make.\n\nThe rest of the afternoon passed over cups of tea, pieces of cake, and countless conversations. By then, it hardly felt like two different schools spending a few hours together. It simply felt like children being children\u2014sharing jokes, asking questions, teasing one another, and enjoying each other\u2019s company.\n\nNo invisible lines were separating the seeing from the visually impaired. There were only students.\n\nReflecting on the visit, Vice Principal Mr. Iresh Ravindra described it as \u201ca meaningful outreach programme aimed at cultivating empathy, compassion and a deeper understanding of others among students.\u201d He noted that through singing together, playing cricket, sharing experiences, and enjoying tea together, the day became \u201ca powerful lesson that went beyond academics, reminding everyone that true education lies in developing kindness, empathy, and humanity towards others.\u201d\n\nSenior Prefect of Wesley College, Master Joel De Silva, also reflected on the experience: \u201cIt is difficult to leave a place like the Blind School without learning something about yourself. What stood out to me wasn\u2019t what the students couldn\u2019t do, it was everything they could. They welcomed us so warmly that it never felt like we were visitors. We spent the day playing, laughing, and talking just as any group of students would. It was an experience that reminded us that some of the strongest people you\u2019ll ever meet simply see the world differently.\u201d"
  },
