    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6224f0bd73c638b547364db4c2ac5bb6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "Post-IMF Road Ahead\n\nThe IMF agreement was signed after a tremendous balance of payment crisis followed by Sri Lanka\u2019s first ever default in April 2022\n\nThe NPP has an overwhelming mandate\u2014like no other government in the history of the country. On a trying global pitch, the government has played a very cautious first innings over the last two years. The second innings cannot be the same\n\nThe ongoing IMF programme has a much stronger hold on Sri Lanka than any previous agreement, because it came after a default. The IMF in its role as arbiter of debt restructuring could more or less hold the country to ransom\n\nWe are in uncharted economic waters. The formula for growth with development finance and international investors has become much talk with little substance\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 17th IMF agreement that ends in March 2027 is no doubt the most consequential international agreement in the history of the country. It has drastically redrawn the workings of public finance and state institutions; from the Central Bank and the financial sector to the State Owned Enterprises (SOEs) and welfare programmes. The question before the country now is the post-IMF road ahead.\n\nThe 2020s marked by the Covid pandemic, tremendous balance of payment crisis followed by Sri Lanka\u2019s first ever default in April 2022, and then the IMF\u2019s austerity measures have contracted Sri Lanka\u2019s economy. Can Sri Lanka come out of this economic depression towards an economic trajectory of growth with equity? What is to be done with the state institutions that had been run to the ground, including near liquidation, by successive governments since liberalisation in 1977, and are further suffocated by the ongoing IMF programme? How can our working people facing gruelling poverty and malnutrition be provided relief, but also sustainable livelihoods and employment?"
  },
