    "chunk_text": "The 2020s marked by the Covid pandemic, tremendous balance of payment crisis followed by Sri Lanka\u2019s first ever default in April 2022, and then the IMF\u2019s austerity measures have contracted Sri Lanka\u2019s economy. Can Sri Lanka come out of this economic depression towards an economic trajectory of growth with equity? What is to be done with the state institutions that had been run to the ground, including near liquidation, by successive governments since liberalisation in 1977, and are further suffocated by the ongoing IMF programme? How can our working people facing gruelling poverty and malnutrition be provided relief, but also sustainable livelihoods and employment?\n\nWhen it comes to Sri Lanka\u2019s external debt on which it defaulted close to 52% were commercial borrowing, inclusive of the 40% in ISBs. I have argued it is this high interest cost of ISBs borrowed extensively from 2007 to 2019 that was a major cause for debt distress and then default; a similar dynamic is also present in other countries like Ghana and Zambia. ISBs are sovereign debt borrowed in the international capital markets in New York and London, where five or ten year loans have to be paid back in foreign currency, usually USD. And in the case of countries like Sri Lanka at interest rates as high as 6 to 8 percent, over a ten year period.\n\nThe compounded interest itself could be equivalent to the principal, where such returns and particularly foreign currency earnings are unlikely, resulting in eventual default. However, the IMF programme has set a target of returning Sri Lanka to borrow ISBs again in 2027. Indeed, the IMF Report of June 2026 projects sustainability of Sri Lanka\u2019s debt with external debt servicing of USD 2.417 billion covered through borrowing USD 1.5 billion in ISBs in 2027. This amounts to returning the sheep to the predatory wolves again."
  },
  {
