    "language": "en",
    "md5": "44ecca880facaec121c00bfc06759a88",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1793,
    "chunk_text": "The compounded interest itself could be equivalent to the principal, where such returns and particularly foreign currency earnings are unlikely, resulting in eventual default. However, the IMF programme has set a target of returning Sri Lanka to borrow ISBs again in 2027. Indeed, the IMF Report of June 2026 projects sustainability of Sri Lanka\u2019s debt with external debt servicing of USD 2.417 billion covered through borrowing USD 1.5 billion in ISBs in 2027. This amounts to returning the sheep to the predatory wolves again.\n\nWhen AKD was elected with great expectations two years ago, the NPP had just three members in parliament. The new president ahead of parliamentary elections was under tremendous pressure by the Western establishment to stick to the IMF programme signed eighteen months earlier and an unfair debt restructuring agreement signed two days prior to the elections.\n\nOver the last two years since the presidential election, Sri Lanka has faced one shock after another; Trump\u2019s tariffs, Cyclone Ditwah and the energy crisis with the US and Israel war on Iran. Managing such shocks even as the country attempts to emerge out of the worst economic crisis since Independence is no easy task. It has for months at a time pushed the government into a reactive corner.\n\nIn this context, four years is a long time in the political life of a government. Another IMF programme, shackling national policies to the priorities of global finance, will seal the fate of the government. Furthermore, coming out of an IMF programme alone will not be enough. The years of damage with the many laws and policies passed under the IMF programme have to be reviewed. The Central Bank Act, Public Finance Management Act and a whole range of other laws will have to be repealed or amended."
  },
