    "md5": "beb38aaacbb735f0749fa60b3e4540fc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1974,
    "chunk_text": "In this context, four years is a long time in the political life of a government. Another IMF programme, shackling national policies to the priorities of global finance, will seal the fate of the government. Furthermore, coming out of an IMF programme alone will not be enough. The years of damage with the many laws and policies passed under the IMF programme have to be reviewed. The Central Bank Act, Public Finance Management Act and a whole range of other laws will have to be repealed or amended.\n\nNothing short of a fresh vision for our institutions, economy and society are necessary. The government alone cannot do this, and it will require not just much national debate and discussion, but also social mobilisation.\n\nWe are in uncharted economic waters. The formula for growth with development finance and international investors has become much talk with little substance. We are now forced to think about how we can use our domestic resources, whether it is our natural resources or our domestic finances, towards production, employment and the welfare of our people. Of course, every avenue to bring in foreign earnings should be pursued, but that will only be meaningful and sustainable if such foreign exchange is not squandered in luxurious or unproductive imports; how we spend our imports should be prioritised.\n\nWe need new or reformed institutions to develop our country at the crossroads, even as we draw on the lessons from the past. The Central Bank will have to play a developmental role. A twenty first century development bank is a must. Industrial and trade policies amidst a global economy coming apart are necessary. State institutions including SOEs have to be reformed and updated for our contemporary needs, and certainly not privatised and left to the whims of extractive financiers. Agricultural transformation is necessary to ensure a secure food system. Mobilisation of society with a new generation of co-operatives can be a way forward."
  },
