    "md5": "e2505072f7751d4b34015e070e2d7f62",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "Mahara Prison riot exposes overcrowding crisis, causes heavy destruction\n\nWhile security in islandwide prisons tightened yesterday with the unrest at Mahara Prison, which left one inmate dead and several injured, rights groups have once again called on the Government to solve the severe overcrowding and poor living conditions in prisons in the country immediately.\n\nThe riot, which erupted on Saturday afternoon and continued until the early hours of Sunday, had been brought under control through a joint security operation involving the Department of Prisons, Police, the Special Task Force (STF), and the tri-forces. The Sri Lanka Air Force also deployed a Bell 412 helicopter and surveillance drones to monitor the situation from the air.\n\nCommittee for Protecting Rights of Prisoners (CPRP) Chairman Senaka Perera alleged that the unrest was the result of years of severe overcrowding and deteriorating prison conditions.\n\nAccording to Perera, Mahara Prison, which has the capacity only to accommodate 991 inmates, is sheltering 4,447 prisoners.\n\n\u201cThe prison authorities are attempting to conceal the actual conditions inside the Prison,\u201d Perera alleged, adding that inadequate accommodation, limited sanitation facilities, and poor living conditions had created mounting tensions among inmates.\n\nPerera further alleged that although the unrest began around 1 p.m. on Saturday, prison officials failed to take immediate action.\n\n\u201cInstead of immediately containing the situation, prison officers withdrew from the premises, allowing the confrontation among inmates to escalate,\u201d he claimed.\n\nThe riots at the Mahara Prison erupted less than a month after the country\u2019s deadliest prison riot in years at Negombo Prison."
  },
