    "chunk_size_bytes": 1881,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cInstead of immediately containing the situation, prison officers withdrew from the premises, allowing the confrontation among inmates to escalate,\u201d he claimed.\n\nThe riots at the Mahara Prison erupted less than a month after the country\u2019s deadliest prison riot in years at Negombo Prison.\n\nMeanwhile, the Department of Prisons yesterday transferred 104 inmates from Mahara Prison to seven other correctional facilities following the unrest.\n\nDepartment of Prisons Prison Commissioner \u2013 Operations and Media Spokesman A.C. Gajanayake said the inmates had been transferred to Dumbara, Welikada, Boossa, Kalutara, Badulla, Batticaloa, and Polonnaruwa prisons.\n\nAccording to Gajanayake, a list of the transferred inmates had been displayed near the roadblock at the entrance to Mahara Prison to enable relatives to obtain information on their family members.\n\nGajanayake also said visits by relatives to inmates at Mahara Prison would remain suspended on today (3) and tomorrow while repair work was carried out, adding that normal visiting arrangements would resume once the essential restoration work had been completed.\n\nHe said investigations to identify the trigger for the violence are continuing and the extensive damage caused during the riot is also being assessed.\n\nAccording to Department of Prisons sources, the unrest began when a group of inmates, who had climbed the roofs, started pelting stones from inside the Prison during family visiting hours and had set fire to a building within the Prison premises while several prisoners attempted to escape by breaching a temporary wall under construction.\n\nRelatives gathered outside the Prison demanding information about inmates, prompting authorities to impose an overnight police curfew in three Grama Niladhari Divisions surrounding the Prison before lifting it on Sunday afternoon after order had been restored."
