    "chunk_text": "Relatives gathered outside the Prison demanding information about inmates, prompting authorities to impose an overnight police curfew in three Grama Niladhari Divisions surrounding the Prison before lifting it on Sunday afternoon after order had been restored.\n\nHe added that authorities were working to restore normal operations while ensuring the welfare of inmates. Meanwhile, the Department of Prisons said prison property had suffered extensive destruction, with officials are continuing a comprehensive assessment of the damage. Sources said they had damaged the offices which had official documents, the prison hospital, kitchen, and rehabilitation centre.\n\nA total of 20 inmates have been transferred to Boossa Prison while investigations continue.\n\nSri Lanka Podujana Peramuna MP Namal Rajapaksa said the incident reflected institutional failures rather than an isolated breakdown in security.\n\n\u201cThis is the second major prison disturbance in a matter of weeks. It is no longer an isolated incident. It is the result of poor management, negligence, and a prison system that is under immense strain,\u201d he said in a post on \u2018X.\u2019\n\nQuestioning why the Department of Prisons still lacked a permanently appointed Commissioner General, Rajapaksa asked: \u201cEven today, the Department of Prisons continues without a permanently appointed Commissioner General, despite the crisis unfolding before us. Why has this critical position still not been properly filled?\u201d\n\nWhile acknowledging that increased security deployments could restore order temporarily, he claimed that lasting solutions required addressing overcrowding, inadequate infrastructure, poor healthcare, delays in rehabilitation programs, and broader institutional weaknesses.\n\n\u201cThe Government must stop firefighting and start fixing a prison system that is showing clear signs of institutional failure,\u201d Rajapaksa said."
