    "chunk_size_bytes": 1705,
    "chunk_text": "This result surpasses the country\u2019s previous record in the preliminary rounds of the WSDC, which was fourth place in 2023, a particularly impressive feat given the rapidly expanding level of international competition. The achievement reinforces Sri Lanka\u2019s standing on the global stage and sets a strong benchmark for future teams.\n\n\u201cPlacing first in the world after the preliminary rounds is a special result for Sri Lanka. It really shows how much talent and hard work allows a small, non-English-speaking nation to compete and win against the best. The country should be proud of what they\u2019ve achieved on the world stage,\u201d said 2026 National Team Coach Shalem Sumanthiran.\n\nIndividually, Savith Wijesundara (Elizabeth Moir School) finished 4th Best Speaker in the world and 1st in the English as a Second Language (ESL) category \u2014 another national first \u2014 while Rudhesh Ram (Colombo International School) placed 7th Best Speaker in the world, and Adam Dhilshan (Royal College) was ranked 6th Best ESL Speaker.\n\nRepresenting Sri Lanka at the WSDC is the product of a demanding, year-long process overseen by the Debaters\u2019 Council of Sri Lanka, the organisation that administers the national schools debating circuit. Debaters take part in more than 15 Debaters\u2019 Council-endorsed tournaments annually, before applications for the National Pool are opened. From that pool, candidates go through training and trial rounds before the final team of five is selected.\n\nThis year\u2019s team \u2014 Savith Wijesundara, Rudhesh Ram, Adam Dhilshan, T. Tharaniharan (Royal College), and Apoorva Jinadasa (Hillwood College) \u2014 was coached by Shalem Sumanthiran, Sachin Tissera, and Kavindra Senaratna."
  },
