    "chunk_text": "Directors must focus on governance, not compliance costs: Minor Group\u2019s Rajakarier\n\nMinor International Group CEO and Minor Hotels Thailand CEO Dillip Rajakarier\n\nSays regulation lends to strong governance that ultimately protects financial performance\n\nSays companies assess financial risk but often overlook reputational risk\n\nDirectors should be less concerned about the cost of complying with governance requirements and more focused on ensuring boards discharge their responsibilities properly, Minor International Group CEO and Minor Hotels Thailand CEO Dillip Rajakarier said, arguing that strong governance protects corporate reputation and ultimately financial performance.\n\nSpeaking during a panel discussion at the recent Sri Lanka Corporate Director Summit 2026: \u2018Future-Ready Sri Lankan Directors,\u2019 Rajakarier dismissed suggestions that governance requirements had become an unnecessary burden on business, saying the real issue was how boards approached their responsibilities rather than the regulations themselves.\n\nHis remarks came in response to an all-too-familiar question on whether increasingly stringent governance and compliance requirements had become a hindrance to doing business, following the withdrawal of an international bank from Sri Lanka\u2019s retail banking sector after more than a century amid rising compliance costs.\n\n\u201cI think governance is the key these days,\u201d Rajakarier said.\n\nDrawing on his experience serving on boards across several countries, he said the quality of governance often depends less on regulation than on the attitude of directors.\n\n\u201cIn some countries, governance becomes a tick-box exercise. In most countries, it becomes a truly governance agenda as well. Again, it depends on your company board; as to how strong they are and how they govern the company,\u201d he said.\n\nRajakarier said weak boardroom practices, rather than regulation, were often the bigger concern."
