    "chunk_text": "\u201cIn some countries, governance becomes a tick-box exercise. In most countries, it becomes a truly governance agenda as well. Again, it depends on your company board; as to how strong they are and how they govern the company,\u201d he said.\n\nRajakarier said weak boardroom practices, rather than regulation, were often the bigger concern.\n\nRajakarier said such an approach reflected a failure to appreciate the importance of proper board oversight.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s really annoying that people really don\u2019t give the importance of independence and to ensure that the boards are properly governed,\u201d he said.\n\nRajakarier also questioned whether some directors described as independent were exercising independent judgement.\n\n\u201cAs I said, independents are truly independent as well. It\u2019s not someone who\u2019s picked up their friends and they echo their voice and they say yes to everything,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said directors ultimately carry responsibility not only for governance and compliance but also for safeguarding corporate reputation.\n\n\u201cThe responsibility they have towards the reputation of the company is really key because not many companies actually assess the risk of reputation,\u201d Rajakarier said.\n\nWhile companies devote considerable attention to financial controls through auditors, audit committees, and nomination and governance committees, Rajakarier said reputational risk often receives far less attention despite its direct impact on business performance.\n\n\u201cMost companies assess the risk of financial discipline. Of course, the auditors are there, the audit committee is there, the nomination and governance committee is there, but not many people look at the reputation. If your reputation goes under, you\u2019re dead,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said that is precisely where governance matters most. \u201cSo, I think it\u2019s really important and that\u2019s where governance comes in.\u201d"
