    "chunk_size_bytes": 1705,
    "chunk_text": "Binod Chaudhary\u2019s recipe for realising your dreams\n\nDr. Binod Chaudhary (left) presenting his book \u201cMade in Nepal\u201d to the British High Commissioner to Sri Lanka Andrew Patrick\n\nI first heard Dr. Binod Chaudhary speak at the Sri Lanka Corporate Director Summit 2026. His name was familiar, for my father had often spoken of him, but it was only after listening to his address that I fully grasped the magnitude of his journey. Inspired by his story, I immediately picked up his latest book, Made in Nepal. This article reflects on both the man and his manifesto.\n\nThere is something compelling about leaders who build enduring institutions from modest beginnings. Dr. Chaudhary\u2019s Chaudhary\u2019s story is not merely about becoming Nepal\u2019s first billionaire. It is proof that geography does not dictate destiny: companies from small economies can become global brands, and courage, discipline, resilience and vision often matter more than abundant resources.\n\nAddressing directors and business leaders in Colombo, Dr. Chaudhary challenged a common weakness in modern boardrooms. Too often, he argued, directors become consumed by compliance, governance and risk management while forgetting that one of their core responsibilities is to enable growth and encourage ambition.\n\n\u201cEntrepreneurs bring ambition. Boards provide discipline. The two should complement each other, not work against each other,\u201d he said.\n\nIt was perhaps the defining message of the summit. Governance without ambition breeds stagnation, while ambition without governance invites recklessness. Sustainable success demands both.\n\nHis own life demonstrates this balance. In 2000, at the height of Sri Lanka\u2019s civil conflict,"
