    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "It was perhaps the defining message of the summit. Governance without ambition breeds stagnation, while ambition without governance invites recklessness. Sustainable success demands both.\n\nHis own life demonstrates this balance. In 2000, at the height of Sri Lanka\u2019s civil conflict,\n\nThe same thinking fuelled the extraordinary success of Wai Wai noodles. Industry experts insisted Nepal was too small to create a global food brand. Dr. Chaudhary ignored conventional wisdom and built the business with international markets in mind from the outset. Today, Wai Wai is sold in over 100 countries, with annual sales exceeding three billion packets.\n\nHis message was simple: if the board thinks small, the company will remain small.\n\nThe Chaudhary Group\u2019s partnership with John Keells to distribute BYD electric vehicles in Sri Lanka is another example of calculated ambition. Despite limited experience in the automotive industry beyond introducing Suzuki to Nepal decades earlier, the venture exceeded expectations. Under the leadership of his son, Nirvana Chaudhary, the business expanded into 25 countries within a year.\n\nWhen asked whether they possessed all the necessary capabilities, Dr. Chaudhary\u2019s answer was refreshingly honest: \u201cPerhaps not. But we had the determination to build those capabilities.\u201d\n\nThat determination runs through every page of Made in Nepal. Unlike many business memoirs that celebrate wealth, this one celebrates perseverance. It traces the transformation of a modest family trading business into CG Corp Global, a diversified enterprise of more than 200 companies operating across Asia, Europe and Africa.\n\nAt its heart are the values inherited from his father: discipline, prudence, humility and long-term thinking. These principles became the foundation of sustainable growth."
