    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "Raja looked at these claims with his trademark scientific scepticism and found the secular palace theory completely lacking in evidence. Armed with physical data, architectural layouts, and a deep knowledge of Mahayana symbolism, he thoroughly turned this conventional wisdom upside down. He argued boldly that Sigiriya was never a secular pleasure palace. Instead, he proved that it was an extensive, highly sacred Mahayana Buddhist monastery complex.\n\nFurthermore, Raja extended this religious interpretation to the entire landscape of Sigiriya. Wijeyratne notes that Raja identified the extensive networks of water, fountains, ponds, and deep wells in the Sigiriya gardens as an integral part of Mahayana ritual worship. In Mahayana monastic architecture, water was not merely an aesthetic luxury for a king\u2019s bath; it held profound symbolic value, representing spiritual purification, clarity, and the flow of divine wisdom.\n\nThe complex hydraulic systems were designed to create a meditative, heavenly environment that mirrored Buddhist descriptions of cosmic paradises. By reinterpreting Sigiriya from a lonely fortress of terror into a bustling, magnificent centre of Mahayana devotion, Raja shook the foundations of Sri Lankan historiography. He faced immense pushback from traditionalists, but because his arguments were rooted in structural and textual logic, his theories could not be easily dismissed.\n\nRaja\u2019s deep connection to Sigiriya was not just academic; it was also intensely practical and heroic. His finest hour as a chemical archaeologist arrived during a massive national crisis in October 1967. An unknown vandal sneaked onto the high ledges of the Sigiriya rock fortress under the cover of night. In an act of sheer malice, the culprit splashed thick, commercial green paint over several of the priceless fifth-century frescoes and violently scraped away portions of the ancient plaster."
