    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "Raja\u2019s deep connection to Sigiriya was not just academic; it was also intensely practical and heroic. His finest hour as a chemical archaeologist arrived during a massive national crisis in October 1967. An unknown vandal sneaked onto the high ledges of the Sigiriya rock fortress under the cover of night. In an act of sheer malice, the culprit splashed thick, commercial green paint over several of the priceless fifth-century frescoes and violently scraped away portions of the ancient plaster.\n\nAs the newly appointed Commissioner of Archaeology, Raja immediately undertook this massive, terrifying restoration effort himself. The pressure from the public, the media, and the Government was suffocating. Understanding the global value of the monument, he coordinated with UNESCO to bring the top international experts to the island. Among them was the legendary Italian art conservator Luciano Maranzi.\n\nRaja and Maranzi spent weeks living and working on the windy, precarious scaffolds high up on the rock face. Raja\u2019s extensive background in chemistry became the ultimate weapon. They conducted delicate micro-chemical tests to analyse the exact composition of the commercial paint versus the ancient plaster.\n\nTogether, they devised a brilliant, highly precise method using specialised chemical solvents. They applied these solvents with extreme care, softening the vandalism layer by layer and gently wiping it away without altering or fading the original fifth-century pigments underneath. Their flawless scientific collaboration was a resounding success, fully restoring the world-famous Sigiriya ladies to their original, breathtaking glory. It remains celebrated as one of the greatest rescue operations in the history of global art conservation."
