    "chunk_size_bytes": 1733,
    "chunk_text": "Together, they devised a brilliant, highly precise method using specialised chemical solvents. They applied these solvents with extreme care, softening the vandalism layer by layer and gently wiping it away without altering or fading the original fifth-century pigments underneath. Their flawless scientific collaboration was a resounding success, fully restoring the world-famous Sigiriya ladies to their original, breathtaking glory. It remains celebrated as one of the greatest rescue operations in the history of global art conservation.\n\nBefore Raja\u2019s systematic interventions, mainstream historical wisdom placed the birth of advanced civilisation in Sri Lanka around the arrival of North Indian settlers, as described in the ancient Mahavamsa chronicle. Anything before that period was often brushed aside as a primitive Stone Age era populated by simple hunter-gatherers.\n\nWhen Raja excavated the deep burial sites at Ibbankatuwa, he found a massive, highly organised complex of stone cist graves spread across a large area. Inside these tombs, he discovered beautifully crafted clay pottery, iron tools, copper ornaments, and highly sophisticated beads made of semi-precious stones such as carnelian and agate. Some of these stones were not native to Sri Lanka, proving that these ancient people engaged in long-distance international trade.\n\nCrucially, scientific dating methods proved that the Ibbankatuwa megalithic tombs significantly predated the written, chronicle-based history of Sri Lanka. They proved beyond all doubt that a highly advanced, technologically capable, and socially stratified society lived, traded, and buried its dead on the island centuries before the traditional historical timeline even began."
