    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "Compounding these external pressures is a structural, internal crisis created by information asymmetry. Management teams tend to filter data, presenting Boards with polished, highly curated narratives. When Boeing faced global grounding orders for its 737 MAX aircraft following tragic design failures, investigations revealed that the Board had been severely insulated from internal engineering red flags. Modern Boards are learning the hard way that relying solely on management\u2019s perspective is a dereliction of duty.\n\nRegardless of whether a company is a large banking institution in London, an artificial intelligence (AI) startup in Silicon Valley, a multinational retail giant in Tokyo, a port operator in Colombo, or a small-to-medium-sized enterprise in Hambantota, every director is bound by three core legal principles, these being the Duty of Care, the Duty of Loyalty, and the Duty of Obedience. Under \u2018Duty of Care,\u2019 directors must act in good faith, with the care an ordinarily prudent person would exercise under similar circumstances. In the modern era, this includes active inquiry. A director cannot claim ignorance if he/she fails to look at dashboards, ask tough questions, or demand deeper reporting. As per \u2018The Duty of Loyalty\u2019 directors must place the interests of the organisation and its shareholders above their own personal or professional gain. This means rigorous management of conflicts of interest and a refusal to engage in self-dealing. In operationalising \u2018The Duty of Obedience,\u2019 the Board must ensure the organisation remains compliant with applicable laws and stays true to its charter, bylaws, and mission. When these duties are abandoned, the corporate body rots from the head down."
