    "md5": "5660369eb3e69d25f0816b7cca6ba84c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "Tea Board clarifies article on GI application\n\nWith reference to the news article titled \u201cYet to receive GI application for Ceylon Tea: NIPO\u201d published in the Daily FT on 27 July 2026, the Sri Lanka Tea Board has sent the following clarification.\n\n\u201cWe believe that this statement, when read without the broader context of the Ceylon Tea Geographical Indication (GI) initiative, may unintentionally create the impression that the Sri Lanka Tea Board has not commenced or pursued the GI registration process in Sri Lanka. As you are aware, this does not accurately reflect the considerable progress made over the past several years.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Tea Board has already submitted the application for GI protection for Ceylon Tea to the European Union (EU), and the application is currently at its final stage of evaluation. At the same time, the Tea Board has actively worked with the National Intellectual Property Office and other stakeholders in developing the domestic legal framework required for the implementation of the GI system in Sri Lanka.\n\nFurthermore, Ceylon Tea is already protected as a Certification Mark under Class 30 in terms of the Intellectual Property Act, No. 36 of 2003.\n\nAs discussed, and agreed among the relevant stakeholders, including the National Intellectual Property Office, and based on the guidance provided by the French Agricultural Research Centre for International Development (CIRAD), the technical expert supporting the EU GI registration process, the domestic GI application was intentionally planned to be submitted after the completion of the EU registration process. The\n\nThe NIPO is fully aware of this implementation process, as the DG of NIPO is a member of the EU GI project steering committee. This sequencing formed part of the agreed implementation strategy and followed the same approach adopted for the registration of Ceylon Cinnamon."
  },
  {
