    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "Expired dengue kits in use?\n\nColombo, Aug. 3 (Daily Mirror) - In a backdrop where the dengue epidemic has claimed over 60lives, speculation is rife as to whether private medical institutions are forced to use expired diagnostic devices such as dengue NS1 antigen kits due to lack of regulations and standards.\n\nBack in 2017, the then government introduced a price ceiling for full blood count tests at Rs. 250 and dengue NS1 antigen tests at Rs. 1000. In 2020, the Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA) issued a new maximum retail price for full blood count tests at Rs. 400 and dengue NS1 antigen tests at Rs. 1200 as per gazette notification no. 2186/17. Following recent raids conducted by CAA, the Authority clarified that the maximum retail price for full blood count tests and Dengue NS1 antigen tests haven\u2019t changed since 2020 and that action would be taken against any medical institution charging patients beyond these price limits.\n\nAccording to Private Health Services Regulatory Council (PHSRC) secretary Dr. Priyantha Tennakoon, the Council has been established under the Private Medical Institutions (Regulations) Act No. 21 of 2006. He said that all private medical institutions should maintain their own standards and that standards vary according to different institutions.\n\n\u201cFor the past 20 years we haven\u2019t set standards that are suitable for Sri Lanka,\u201d he told Daily Mirror. \u201cMost private hospitals and laboratories have been accredited under the Sri Lanka Accreditation Board (SLAB) or have maintained international standards. So we don\u2019t see the need to create a different set of standards. There are different standards for the healthcare sector such as Joint Commission International (JCI), Accreditation Commission for Health Care (ACHC) and so on. Hospitals can choose an accreditation system of their choice and obtain certificates,\u201d he said."
