    "md5": "be5037f453fe9ea544b6f781ea409555",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "BASL seeks meeting with President over judges\u2019 retirement age amendment\n\nColombo, July 3 (Daily Mirror) - The Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL) has formally requested a meeting with President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to discuss the Government\u2019s proposed constitutional amendment seeking to increase the retirement age of judges.\n\nThe BASl has urged the President that no further steps be taken on the proposal until it has had an opportunity to present its views.\n\nIn a letter addressed to the President, the BASL said constitutional amendments affecting the Superior Judiciary should never be introduced in an ad hoc manner or in response to temporary circumstances. It stressed that reforms of such constitutional significance should only be undertaken where there is a clearly demonstrable institutional necessity, transparent public justification and broad stakeholder consultation.\n\nThe BASL further stated that public confidence in constitutional governance depends not only on the substance of an amendment but also on the process by which constitutional changes are introduced.\n\nThe BASL said it had previously conveyed its concerns to the President in writing but had not received any response or official observations. It noted that, despite the absence of a response, the Cabinet of Ministers had proceeded to consider and approve the proposal without first giving the Association an opportunity to present its concerns.\n\nAccording to the letter, the BASL was subsequently granted an opportunity to present its initial submissions to Justice Minister Harshana Nanayakkara. During that meeting, the Minister assured the Association that its position would be conveyed to the President and that a request would be made for a meeting between the President and the BASL to discuss the matter.\n\nThe BASL also recalled that on April 9, 2026, the Justice Minister had written to the President\u2019s Private Secretary requesting that such a meeting be arranged."
