    "chunk_size_bytes": 1818,
    "chunk_text": "Security tightened across all prisons following Mahara Prison unrest\n\nColombo, Aug. 3 (Daily Mirror) - Authorities have stepped up security at prisons across the country after the violent unrest at the Mahara Prison left one inmate dead, several others injured, and caused extensive damage to prison property.\n\nSenior Deputy Inspector General of Police (SDIG) Sajeewa Medawatta said special security measures and close monitoring have been implemented at all prisons nationwide.\n\nHe said that additional attention has been directed towards key facilities, including Welikada, Magazine, Kalutara and Negombo prisons, to prevent similar incidents.\n\nThe unrest, which began on Saturday (01) afternoon at the Mahara Priso continued until the early hours of yesterday before security forces succeeded in restoring order.\n\nA joint operation involving the Department of Prisons, Sri Lanka Police, the Special Task Force (STF), and personnel from the Sri Lanka Air Force and Navy brought the situation under control at around 6.00 a.m.\n\nOne inmate was killed and six others were injured during the incident. Prison Department Spokesperson A.C. Gajanayake said an investigation has been launched to determine the cause of the unrest. He also said that inmates caused extensive damage to prison property during the violence.\n\nAccording to authorities, the disturbance began when a group of inmates hurled stones from inside the prison while relatives were visiting.\n\nSeveral inmates reportedly attempted to escape by breaking through a temporary wall under construction, while another group set fire to a prison building. Prison officers positioned near the main entrance opened fire to contain the escalating situation. During the unrest, some inmates also allegedly assaulted fellow prisoners who refused to join the violence."
