    "md5": "934b77d15dae64ea481e5289f0c24b3c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Communist Party urges President to act on Indian trawler issue affecting Jaffna fishermen\n\nColombo, Aug. 3 (Daily Mirror) - The Communist Party of Sri Lanka (CPSL) has urged President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to take immediate steps to address the longstanding issue of Indian fishing trawlers entering Sri Lankan waters and affecting the livelihoods of fishermen in the Jaffna District.\n\nIn a letter to the President, CPSL General Secretary Dr. G. Weerasinghe said the issue was raised during discussions held with fishing community leaders in Jaffna during the party\u2019s 83rd anniversary celebrations.\n\nThe party said Indian high-powered trawlers equipped with bottom-trawling technology were entering Sri Lanka\u2019s Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) and engaging in destructive fishing practices, causing serious damage to marine resources and local fishing activities.\n\nAccording to the letter, the arrival of large numbers of such vessels, their powerful engines and iron hulls had created difficulties for traditional fishermen operating wooden boats. It said fishing nets belonging to local communities were frequently damaged or destroyed as a result.\n\nThe CPSL warned that the problem was not limited to economic losses faced by fishermen but also posed a threat to the sustainability of marine ecosystems.\n\n\u201cThese destructive and illegal fishing practices are damaging the biological activities of the seabed and could lead to the depletion of future fish stocks,\u201d the party said.\n\nIt said the fishing industry remained one of the main livelihoods in the Northern Province and that communities dependent on it were facing increasing hardships due to unregulated fishing activities.\n\nThe party also raised concerns over possible links between illegal maritime activities and the movement of narcotics and other illicit goods through unregulated vessels."
  },
