    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka faces US $ 10bn second-half export test to meet record 2026 target\n\nSri Lanka will need to generate about US $ 10 billion in export earnings during the second half of 2026, to meet its record full-year target of US $ 19 billion, after bringing in nearly US $ 9 billion in the first six months.\n\nThe target requires the export earnings in the second half to exceed the first-half performance by more than 11 percent, placing greater pressure on merchandise and services exports to maintain growth, despite the uncertain global trading conditions.\n\nExport Development Board (EDB) Chairman and CEO Mangala Wijesinghe said the agency\u2019s broader ambition was to reposition exports as a central driver of Sri Lanka\u2019s economy.\n\n\u201cOur primary vision is to transform Sri Lanka into the world\u2019s premier export hub for exceptional goods and services,\u201d Wijesinghe said in a statement marking the EDB\u2019s 47th anniversary.\n\nThe EDB expects the full-year performance to set a new export earnings record. However, the statement did not provide a breakdown of the nearly US $ 9 billion earned during the first half or specify the contribution from the merchandise and services exports.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s longer-term export strategy is built around the National Export Development Plan 2026-2030, launched in June. It aims to increase the total export earnings to US $ 36 billion by 2030, almost double the amount targeted for this year. The statement listed the 2030 targets as US $ 27 billion from the merchandise exports and US $ 8 billion from services. These figures add up to US $ 35 billion, leaving a US $ 1 billion discrepancy that requires clarification.The plan also seeks to widen Sri Lanka\u2019s export basket beyond the traditional industries. Eight new sectors have been selected for promotion, with the EDB identifying electronics, food and beverages, information technology and logistics as the areas already recording growth."
  },
