    "chunk_text": "Prison riots not isolated from systemic issues\n\nThis trauma might turn the prisoners riotous when the facilities in prisons are trimmed due to congestion\n\nThe National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government is under pressure after the second major prison riot within a month took place on Saturday (August 1), causing extensive damage to the prison buildings and facilities within the premises.\n\nHowever, unlike the Negombo prison riot that raged on July 5 and 6, leaving over 20 inmates and 8 prison officers, the human cost was minimal in the Mahara incident,\u00a0 where only one inmate had lost his life.\n\nThe acute congestion in prisons that has created an unbearable situation in them is always blamed for such riots,\u00a0 while the law delay is mainly blamed in turn for the congestion. Underscoring the severe delays plaguing the justice system, while speaking at a recent event Supreme Court Justice Yasantha Kodagoda said that completing a single cycle of a criminal case in Sri Lanka takes an average of 10 years and 2 months. As an example, he stated that if a burglary is reported today, the first phase of the case would only conclude in August 2036.\n\nHe highlighted that there are currently 1.1 million cases pending across the country\u2019s courts. These are handled by just 333 courts, including 80 magistrate courts, two child magistrate courts, 64 district courts, 94 combined district magistrate courts, 53 High Courts, five commercial courts, 23 civil appellate High Courts, seven judges at the Court of Appeal, and five divisions of the Supreme Court. \u201cIf distributed evenly, each court would be handling over 3,300 cases,\u201d he said.\n\nThese are not mere numbers. They point the severe trauma the prisoners, both remand and convicted, undergo. The trauma and the frustration are manyfold when the people who are remanded for a long time, especially under the draconian Prevention of Terrorism Act, for offences that they have never committed."
  },
