    "md5": "b28525d8ac81c23c8250234c4380c95e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "These are not mere numbers. They point the severe trauma the prisoners, both remand and convicted, undergo. The trauma and the frustration are manyfold when the people who are remanded for a long time, especially under the draconian Prevention of Terrorism Act, for offences that they have never committed.\n\nDuring a speech in Parliament on June 23, 2021,\u00a0 the then Youth and Sports Minister Namal Rajapaksa said that some of those who have been detained under the PTA have been in remand prisons longer than his age (35 years then). However, it was his party, Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) that ruled the country for the succeeding three years, but they did not take action to resolve the issue.\n\nThe repercussions of the law\u2019s delay are very serious. Former Justice Minister Mohamed Ali Sabri told Parliament on December 2, 2020 that 4,620 cases were being heard in Sri Lankan courts for more than 20 years while 3,418 were being heard for more than 15 years and 8,947 cases for more than 10 years.\n\nNot all accused of these cases are languishing in prisons, but a court case against a person is a Sword of Damocles hanging over his head every day, especially if he is an accused.\n\nThe amendment brought in to the Poisons, Opium and Dangerous Drugs Ordinance in 2022 aggravated the situation as it allowed the Court of Appeal to hold exclusive jurisdiction to release a suspect on bail when the\u00a0 quantity of the dangerous drug trafficked, imported, exported, or possessed is 10 grams or more. And determining the purity of drugs takes a long time due to shortage of staff at the Government Analyst Department.\n\nThis trauma might turn the prisoners riotous when the facilities in prisons are trimmed due to congestion. Sometimes, Outside influences, as Justice Minister Harshana Nanayakkara suspects, might be another stimulus for the prison riots. Therefore, the prison riots are not isolated incidents. They are the results of more serious issues in the administration of justice."
  },
