    "md5": "fa3dd63860a695fd26fac7021d562b6f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1506,
    "chunk_text": "Controversy over extension of judges\u2019 retirement age: Rage Over Age: the Need for Comprehensive Reform\n\nThe doctrine of separation of powers in democratic societies operates in a constitutional ecosystem of checks and balances of one another\u2019s power, however less ideal the ecosystem is.\n\nIn some constitutional or legal systems, there is a glaring absence of a built-in institutional mechanism or avenue for \u2018judging judges\u2019 in the higher judiciary\n\nIn Sri Lanka, parties in power often treat weakening the judiciary as an easy route to sustained, smoother governance. Of late, an aspersion has arisen as to whether the NPP is trying this tactic using the retirement age of judges.\n\nAttempts at weakening the judiciary, especially since the 1970s, have followed a clear set of interests and priorities underpinning the agendas of governing parties. It is ironic, however, that they were often clothed in salutary principles.\n\nThere is also a speculation whether the current attempt represents a larger move, of which weakening the judiciary remains only a part. The government has failed to cogently articulate rational justification or an objective need for the change it seeks, thereby providing grist to the rumour mill. No rationale has been set out in a policy/position paper, amplified by wider consultations.\n\nIn fairness to the judiciary, no evidence suggests that a need for increasing the age of retirement has been raised by it although it is perhaps not the judiciary\u2019s call to make."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
