    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b74d47f10e91e19a5d807cc04626f540",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1802,
    "chunk_text": "There is also a speculation whether the current attempt represents a larger move, of which weakening the judiciary remains only a part. The government has failed to cogently articulate rational justification or an objective need for the change it seeks, thereby providing grist to the rumour mill. No rationale has been set out in a policy/position paper, amplified by wider consultations.\n\nIn fairness to the judiciary, no evidence suggests that a need for increasing the age of retirement has been raised by it although it is perhaps not the judiciary\u2019s call to make.\n\nIn some constitutional or legal systems, there is a glaring absence of a built-in institutional mechanism or avenue for \u2018judging judges\u2019 in the higher judiciary \u2013 a mechanism or avenue operating before, or independently of, the ultimate removal process. A Parliament-initiated impeachment or removal process can only be the last resort. When a judge of the higher judiciary is alleged to conduct himself/herself in such a manner or indulge in such activities that may risk undermining public confidence in his/her impartiality, integrity or independence, the need for such an institutional mechanism or avenue, short of impeachment or removal, appears more pronounced.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, such a mechanism is available for lower court judges only, and the Judicial Service Commission, despite its drawbacks, is designed to serve that purpose. It is public knowledge, however, that such drawbacks have led to considerable criticism from time to time.\n\nThere are several reasons why the NPP\u2019s move to increase the retirement age of judges of the higher judiciary \u2013 later, other echelons, too, have been added \u2013 should be viewed with caution. They are iterated without going into the legal complexities surrounding the issue."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
