    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3f08121c9fa8887f302d32642e9aa0d9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "There are several reasons why the NPP\u2019s move to increase the retirement age of judges of the higher judiciary \u2013 later, other echelons, too, have been added \u2013 should be viewed with caution. They are iterated without going into the legal complexities surrounding the issue.\n\nSecond, seeking to increase the retirement age in particular, without simultaneously addressing other urgent issues affecting the judiciary as a whole, could only reflect a narrow approach. The citizens of Sri Lanka need to be taken into confidence as to why the government thinks that by addressing this particular aspect, other systemic and structural issues affecting the judiciary will be remedied, and how.\n\nThird, the constitutional principle of judicial independence is sacrosanct, and it cannot be addressed in a haphazard manner, specifically focusing on retirement age. In case it is to be done for an objective need, it should be ensured in a manner that doesn\u2019t affect the promotional prospects of judges in the lower echelons of the system (even though, perhaps as an afterthought, their age of retirement was also proposed to be increased). Although appointments are made following the approval of the Constitutional Council, that doesn\u2019t extinguish the principle of legitimate expectations operating in a moral domain when it comes to the appointment of judges to the superior courts.\n\nFourth, the doctrine of separation of powers in democratic societies operates in a constitutional ecosystem of checks and balances of one another\u2019s power, however less ideal the ecosystem is. When one aspect pertaining to a particular branch of government is addressed without any rational justification or objective need \u2013 there is no evidence in the public domain that the judiciary is seriously constrained by the existing requirements relating to the age of retirement \u2013 it cuts deeply to the core of that constitutional ecosystem."
  },
