    "md5": "ca2e7a3dc2bf8dabe3f893368ff9bfae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1508,
    "chunk_text": "Fourth, the doctrine of separation of powers in democratic societies operates in a constitutional ecosystem of checks and balances of one another\u2019s power, however less ideal the ecosystem is. When one aspect pertaining to a particular branch of government is addressed without any rational justification or objective need \u2013 there is no evidence in the public domain that the judiciary is seriously constrained by the existing requirements relating to the age of retirement \u2013 it cuts deeply to the core of that constitutional ecosystem.\n\nConsidering an institutional mechanism or avenue which could consider complainants/appeals from aggrieved parties may strengthen safeguards in favour of the independence of the judiciary as well as the security of judicial tenure.\n\nHowever, these and any other challenges affecting the judicial system would require reforms that go far beyond the increase in the age of retirement alone. The constitutional ecosystem is larger and would correspondingly emphasise reforms to other branches of government as well. It would undoubtedly involve enlarging freedom and human security. Constitutionalism in particular calls for wide consultations and participation.\n\nStrikingly, however, this is where promises were made from time to time by successive governments including the incumbent one. And this is where they still remain yet to see the day of light.\n\nA.L.A. Azeez is a former Permanent Representative of Sri Lanka to the UN in Geneva & Former Ambassador to Vienna"
