    "language": "en",
    "md5": "281826200850e8bab09656501222c7bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1768,
    "chunk_text": "Overreacting to student anger during India\u2019s \u2018Cockroach\u2019 Protests\n\nThe degree of violence applied by police forces during unarmed protests depends a great deal on the kind of orders they get from their governments. This does not mean that in democracies, the police will always act with restraint\n\nThe sheer scale of India\u2019s student protests may have come as a surprise to the Indian government, but it was no surprise to those observers in India,\u00a0 as well as outsiders,\u00a0 who monitored mounting student anger and frustration over high cost of education, cost of living and injustices in the education system over the years.\n\nIndian social media is full of reports about students groaning under multiple economic burdens --\u00a0 the cost of living in India\u2019s sprawling cities due to high rents,\u00a0 other expenses and a system unforgiving to those who make mistakes. One recent video shows a father and daughter pleading outside the closed gates of an examination centre to let them in. The gates were closed because they had arrived two minutes late, after travelling a long way on a motorcycle,\u00a0 delayed due to rain and a flat tyre on the way.\n\nEarlier, seeking jobs in UK was an alternative for many young Indians. But new and draconian immigration laws has led to many Indian youth working at UK jobs\u00a0 in medical and IT sectors being deported. Last year, the Home Office passed a harsh new law prohibiting the employment of any foreign national whose arrival and UK visas were found to be\u00a0 facilitated by\u00a0 unregistered agencies. If unable to find alternative jobs within 30 days, they would be deported. This is harsh treatment of young hopefuls who spend minor fortunes looking for greener pastures, often getting into serious debt to pay agency sharks."
  },
