    "chunk_size_bytes": 1570,
    "chunk_text": "Earlier, seeking jobs in UK was an alternative for many young Indians. But new and draconian immigration laws has led to many Indian youth working at UK jobs\u00a0 in medical and IT sectors being deported. Last year, the Home Office passed a harsh new law prohibiting the employment of any foreign national whose arrival and UK visas were found to be\u00a0 facilitated by\u00a0 unregistered agencies. If unable to find alternative jobs within 30 days, they would be deported. This is harsh treatment of young hopefuls who spend minor fortunes looking for greener pastures, often getting into serious debt to pay agency sharks.\n\nThe vehemence with which Indian police cracked down on protesters shocked many. Indian police personnel wielding\u00a0 sticks (or canes) is a frequent sight in India. There are videos showing policemen carrying them even outside cinema halls, trying to keep order as frantic fans try to get tickets. But the brutality shown towards these young students, both male and female, was highly disturbing. One video showed a senior officer slapping a young woman who was merely standing by, not doing anything aggressive.\n\nBut police brutality in riot situations is not unique to India. We have seen it often enough in Sri Lanka. A press photo showing a policeman using a baton on several female student protesters in Colombo who were crouching down, trying to protect themselves with their hands, caused an uproar. During the atter stages of the 2022 Aragalaya, the police used rubber bullets as well as live bullets, killing a protester in the town of Rambukkana."
  },
