    "md5": "fcc5f3bab54245a4192c0140e7c4d797",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1766,
    "chunk_text": "But police brutality in riot situations is not unique to India. We have seen it often enough in Sri Lanka. A press photo showing a policeman using a baton on several female student protesters in Colombo who were crouching down, trying to protect themselves with their hands, caused an uproar. During the atter stages of the 2022 Aragalaya, the police used rubber bullets as well as live bullets, killing a protester in the town of Rambukkana.\n\nThe degree of violence applied by police forces during unarmed protests depends a great deal on the kind of orders they get from their governments. This does not mean that in democracies, the police will always act with restraint. But there is clearly a difference between how China\u2019s security forces reacted to peaceful protests in Tiananmen Square in June 1989 \u2013 according to independent estimates, over 2000 protesters were killed \u2013 and how India handled the protesters. At Tiananmen, it became a was a military rather than a police action.\n\nIn Iran, During massive civil unrest in January, police and security forces killed between 3000-40,000 people, and arrested protesters were executed on what human rights groups say were false charges.\n\nThese examples do not exonerate Indian police from what they did, but the kind of massacres reported from China and Iran would be unacceptable in India or Sri Lanka during a similar civil crisis. As Sri Lanka\u2019s\u00a0 youth-led Aragalaya gathered force in April-2022, many feared a strong backlash from \u2018strongman\u2019 President Gotabhaya Rajapakse who had full support of the military. But he hesitated to unleash heavy-handed police/military action on protesters possibly because he was unnerved by the sheer magnitude of public anger over his ruinous economic policies."
  },
