    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "These examples do not exonerate Indian police from what they did, but the kind of massacres reported from China and Iran would be unacceptable in India or Sri Lanka during a similar civil crisis. As Sri Lanka\u2019s\u00a0 youth-led Aragalaya gathered force in April-2022, many feared a strong backlash from \u2018strongman\u2019 President Gotabhaya Rajapakse who had full support of the military. But he hesitated to unleash heavy-handed police/military action on protesters possibly because he was unnerved by the sheer magnitude of public anger over his ruinous economic policies.\n\nProtests, riots, and demonstrations erupted against police brutality\u00a0 in Minneapolis in the United States on May 26, 2020, after the murder of George Floyd, a 46-year-old unarmed African American, by city police during an arrest. Police officer Derek Chauvin was recorded as kneeling on Floyd\u2019s neck for 9 minutes and 29 seconds; Floyd complained of not being able to breathe, and later died. Chauvin was convicted of murder.\n\nBut an already bad\u00a0 situation in the US became darker after Donald Trump became president and began his war against immigrants. He unleashed US Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) police on immigrants and protesters alike. ICE agents raid, operate and react outside legal limits and with excessive force, and have killed roughly 10 people in fatal operational shootings while\u00a0 over 90 people have died in ICE detention centres since 2020. These are gross human rights violations in a country which preaches human rights to the rest of the world.\n\nHowever, getting back to India, what happens now in the US or what happened in Tiananmen do not justify the way India\u2019s young \u2018Cockroach\u2019 protesters were beaten by India\u2019s\u00a0 police. That has to be judged in its own context."
  },
