    "chunk_size_bytes": 1832,
    "chunk_text": "Mounting global curbs on citizenship\n\nThe receiving countries, which are mostly of the First World, ignore the fact that they themselves could be the cause of the distress migration\n\nThe increasing movement of people across countries and continents due to a variety of push and pull factors is now being curbed the world over. The reasons are -- mounting concerns over security, and fears of demographic and economic changes threatening existing power structures.\n\nAffected countries and people do protest loudly, mostly on grounds of human rights sanctified by international conventions. But these protests have had little or no effect on governments that are\u00a0 increasingly pledged to safeguarding their national or domestic political interests.\n\nPeople migrate legally or illegally in search of opportunities, to escape from wars or instabilities created by political unrest, the effects of climate change and scarcities created by an unbridled increase in populations. People take advantage of improvements and increase in the modes of transport. But all these are negated by firewalls put up by receiving countries.\n\nOld liberal immigration and citizenship laws have been tightened and made more restrictive. Efforts to plug loopholes are on. Countries also dictate what kind of immigrants they will welcome and what kind they will bar. Receiving countries work out deals with the sending countries to tackle the problem at its source.\n\nThe receiving countries, which are mostly of the First World, ignore the fact that they themselves could be the cause of the distress migration. It is they which had ruined their colonies by exploitation over centuries thus indirectly causing migration. Migration from West Asia,\u00a0 on the rise now, can be attributed to the wars unleashed by imperialist powers moved by self-aggrandisement."
  },
