    "md5": "dfc0bdc00f10569966c5f93a09b63c65",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "The receiving countries, which are mostly of the First World, ignore the fact that they themselves could be the cause of the distress migration. It is they which had ruined their colonies by exploitation over centuries thus indirectly causing migration. Migration from West Asia,\u00a0 on the rise now, can be attributed to the wars unleashed by imperialist powers moved by self-aggrandisement.\n\nIn the United States, birthright citizenship has been a contentious issue for years. Under the 14th Amendment of the US Constitution, anyone born on US soil is automatically granted U.S. citizenship, regardless of their parents\u2019 immigration status. However, the practice of \u201cjus soli\u201d has faced increasing scrutiny in recent years, especially with the rise of anti-immigration sentiment and concerns over \u201cbirth\u00a0 tourism.\u201d\n\nIn 2025, the US President issued an Executive Order seeking to limit birthright citizenship by requiring that at least one parent be a US citizen or legal permanent resident for a child born in the US to receive citizenship. This executive order, which has been met with legal challenges, could fundamentally alter the future of US immigration law.\n\nSwitzerland is now further tightening its birthright citizenship laws. Historically, Switzerland followed the principle of \u201cjus sanguinis\u201d (right of blood), which granted Swiss nationality to children born abroad to Swiss parents. However, Switzerland did not have an unrestricted \u201cjus soli\u201d (right of soil) system\u2014meaning birth within Swiss territory did not automatically grant citizenship.\n\nIn 2026, Switzerland made headlines by joining several other European nations in tightening its stance on birthright citizenship. Children born in Switzerland to foreign nationals can only claim citizenship through naturalisation if they meet long-term residency requirements, further reinforcing the country\u2019s commitment to residence-based citizenship."
  },
