    "md5": "f67b10c35e6efb1c6d810d4d114f94eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1650,
    "chunk_text": "In 2026, Switzerland made headlines by joining several other European nations in tightening its stance on birthright citizenship. Children born in Switzerland to foreign nationals can only claim citizenship through naturalisation if they meet long-term residency requirements, further reinforcing the country\u2019s commitment to residence-based citizenship.\n\nThe changes in Ireland\u2019s birthright citizenship policy reflect broader concerns about immigration and the potential for \u201cbirth tourism\u201d\u2014the practice of foreign nationals traveling to Ireland to give birth, thereby granting their children Irish citizenship. This policy change has had a significant impact on the number of children born in Ireland who automatically receive citizenship, leading to debates about fairness and equality in immigration laws.\n\nHistorically, Germany had a mix of \u201cjus soli\u201d and \u201cjus sanguinis\u201d, where children born in Germany could acquire citizenship through birth if at least one parent had been living in Germany for a specified period. However, in 2000, the government passed legislation that tightened this provision and emphasised the importance of parental legal status in the country.\n\nIn 2026, Germany introduced further reforms that make it harder for children born to foreign nationals to acquire German citizenship. Children born in Germany to foreign parents must now prove a significant connection to the country through factors such as residency and education.\n\nThis regulation is part of Germany\u2019s broader effort to control immigration and prevent the creation of multiple generations of citizens without direct ties to the country."
