    "md5": "5a0bf042a26cb223eb857fb941d9fbf4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "In 2026, Germany introduced further reforms that make it harder for children born to foreign nationals to acquire German citizenship. Children born in Germany to foreign parents must now prove a significant connection to the country through factors such as residency and education.\n\nThis regulation is part of Germany\u2019s broader effort to control immigration and prevent the creation of multiple generations of citizens without direct ties to the country.\n\nAs with other countries, the Italian government is focused on ensuring that citizenship is tied to integration and residency, rather than simply place of birth.\n\nAustralia\u2019s shift toward stricter birthright citizenship laws began in 1986 when the country eliminated unconditional birthright citizenship. Now, a child born in Australia can only acquire citizenship at birth if at least one parent is an Australian citizen or holds permanent residency. This change is part of Australia\u2019s broader immigration policy, which prioritises skilled workers and long-term residents over casual visitors or migrants.\n\nIn 2026, Australia\u2019s government reinforced these rules, tightening the criteria for birthright citizenship. Children born to temporary visa holders or tourists in Australia are no longer automatically granted citizenship. This is consistent with Australia\u2019s long-standing policy of restricting citizenship to those with strong ties to the country, and it reflects the nation\u2019s ongoing commitment to managing immigration.\n\nFrance, one of Europe\u2019s most popular destinations for immigration, has a mixed approach to birthright citizenship. While France has long been a proponent of \u201cjus soli\u201d, where children born in the country could claim citizenship, recent reforms have introduced more conditions. Children born in France to foreign parents must now meet specific residency criteria and, in some cases, prove their long-term integration into French society before they can apply for citizenship."
