    "md5": "eb41e006341886c0632f46481fc4126a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2000,
    "chunk_text": "France, one of Europe\u2019s most popular destinations for immigration, has a mixed approach to birthright citizenship. While France has long been a proponent of \u201cjus soli\u201d, where children born in the country could claim citizenship, recent reforms have introduced more conditions. Children born in France to foreign parents must now meet specific residency criteria and, in some cases, prove their long-term integration into French society before they can apply for citizenship.\n\nAs these nations increasingly impose stricter rules on birthright citizenship, the implications for immigrants, particularly those from low-income and undocumented backgrounds, are profound. These changes are likely to affect millions of people seeking to provide their children with better opportunities.\n\nIndia does not allow immigration or give citizenship easily. There is a residence qualification for citizenship. There are barriers to citizenship for Muslims from Pakistan, Bangladesh and Afghanistan while non-Muslims from these countries are admitted more easily.\u00a0 Sri Lankans have problems getting citizenship even though many Tamil refugees have been demanding it citing decades of residence in India.\n\nMarriage to an Indian citizen does not automatically make a foreign national an Indian citizen. Under Indian law, marriage only creates eligibility, not citizenship itself, meaning that even after a valid and legally registered marriage, a foreign spouse does not automatically acquire Indian nationality, does not receive an Indian passport, and does not gain any immediate citizenship rights. Instead, the foreign spouse must strictly follow the Indian citizenship framework, which requires lawful and continuous residence in India for a prescribed period, compliance with visa and immigration rules, police and security verification, and formal approval by the Government of India. The final outcome depends on government discretion, not on marriage alone. India does not have birthright citizenship."
