    "language": "en",
    "md5": "43eac537d31cb4ab7f220834e4dbb98d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "QIrrespective of the opposition\u2019s claims that the government has failed to attract new investors, you stated a few weeks ago that the government plans to enact key reforms within the next three months to attract investment and boost exports. How do you intend to move forward with these reforms?\n\nThis year we will bring two more Acts related to investment. One is Investment Protection Act which has been drafted already. The other one is SOE Management Act which will also allow certain SOEs to have private public partnerships and manage these investments\n\nThere are reforms we have made. One is the Strategic Investment Act, we have already brought into Parliament. It clearly states for which category of investments and what the type of incentives are. Port City Act is amended to clearly articulate types of incentives. So now anybody investing in Sri Lanka it is not ambiguous.\n\nThis year we will bring two more Acts related to investment. One is Investment Protection Act which has been drafted already. The other one is SOE Management Act which will also allow certain SOEs to have private public partnerships and manage these investments.\n\nThirdly the most important one is Private Public Partnership Act. This will allow foreign investments to partner with the government in public infrastructure development. So these are the three changes that will be taking place within this year. Further these Acts will put us in line with the global best practices.\n\nQAccording to the National SME Strategy published by the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development in May 2026, one of the key strategic actions is to leverage digital tools to improve enterprise support and service responsiveness. Given the country\u2019s current resource constraints and the fact that public sector digitalization is still at an early stage, do you believe this objective is achievable?"
