    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4b8d64cda93ec02edbb488e445513a15",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1872,
    "chunk_text": "QAccording to the National SME Strategy published by the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development in May 2026, one of the key strategic actions is to leverage digital tools to improve enterprise support and service responsiveness. Given the country\u2019s current resource constraints and the fact that public sector digitalization is still at an early stage, do you believe this objective is achievable?\n\nWe are focusing on journey incentivizing and educating industries and telling them why they should join. And we are also in discussion with the Digital Ministry on of a particular platform that can do end to end servicing which will be part of the entity that is going to be called EITA- Entrepreneurship and Industry Transformation Agency. That will be digital first organization. With that we are confident.\n\nQDo you think creating infrastructure itself is sufficient? How about the reception of new technologies by the state sector employees are they trained?\n\nTechnological adaptation is anyway a difficult task irrespective of whether it is private or public sector. It is difficult with the Public sector because we are lagging. Therefore, we have already started an employee up skilling programme especially for our Development Officers, who are now around 1200 in numbers. This programme is done with Sri Lanka Institute of Marketing, and they are investing around 300 million rupees for the programme. That is one side. We also feel that today\u2019s technology is simpler and believe that people can learn how to use it better.\n\nThe strategy itself is having a technological platform. Because of that you could train people while on the job. What we feel is that the only way for government organizations to be efficient is by making their task simpler and bringing in technology. If other countries have done it we should also be able to do it too."
