    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9d26dbddf0d9fe05c52a2190e0866e89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "Then came the \u2018oil shock\u2019 that derailed many countries, followed by the Global Food Crisis. The five-year plan (1972-76) was derailed as well.\n\nThe nationalisation of the plantations, what brought it about, was required at the time and its repercussions, needs to be studied in depth. The export of non-traditional exports, saw some success.\n\nThe entire development process in East Asia, including Japan was well known, though the Developmental State Theory (DST) with Chalmers Johnsons \u2018MITI and the Japanese miracle came about much later in 1982.\n\nThe question that needs an answer is if Sri Lanka had studied and analysed the East Asian development process, before attempting the process?\n\nFrom what was seen and the manner in which Sri Lanka got going, it was clear that it had got the wrong end of it.\n\nWhen Sri Lanka changed its economic policy and decided on the \u2018open economy\u2019 Japan and East Asia was the model it said it hoped to follow, but was quite the opposite, being advised from the west, which had turned to neo-liberal thinking by then.\n\nHowever, the method chosen was infested with many fundamental mistakes. These mistakes could be identified in comparison with Japan, South Korea and Taiwan, and how they got about development. It should also be recalled that the \u2018foreign advice\u2019 at the time was not in favour of industrial development, but towards \u2018services\u2019.\n\nListed below are the major points, Sri Lanka did not seriously consider and became part of the many mistakes it made.\n\nSouth Korea and Taiwan had many development plans, while Japan followed what is called \u2018window guidance\u2019 via the Ministry of International Trade and Industry. A plan is also expected to motivate all sections of the population to work towards a common objective, and create the ground to reach the desired objective. If Sri Lanka had done so, in the correct spirit, there would have been no need for ethnic division and the war of three decades."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-04-dailyftlk-d468044a",
    "num": "dailyftlk-d468044a",
    "date_str": "2026-08-04",
    "description": "Can Sri Lanka overcome past mistakes and craft a path of development?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Can-Sri-Lanka-overcome-past-mistakes-and-craft-a-path-of-development/4-795528",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785785812.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-04-dailyftlk-d468044a-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d999fe8eb1e07aa7934ce6a9e9829e6e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "South Korea and Taiwan had many development plans, while Japan followed what is called \u2018window guidance\u2019 via the Ministry of International Trade and Industry. A plan is also expected to motivate all sections of the population to work towards a common objective, and create the ground to reach the desired objective. If Sri Lanka had done so, in the correct spirit, there would have been no need for ethnic division and the war of three decades.\n\nJapan was re-building after the war and knew that the market was not functioning, while South Korea and Taiwan, were under-developed economies and knew that they could not depend on an under-developed market and all three decided on state intervention to develop the economy and the market in the process.\n\nThere has also got to be a determination to develop, education and training together with directed research and development plays a crucial role.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s dual nature of the economy, one for the privileged class and the other for the rest, created a situation where urgency was not seen or felt.\n\nSri Lanka knew very well that local private investment would not flow into the required sectors, while keeping the lucrative import trade open. If FDI and local private investment was not available, who was going to fill the gap?\n\nThe Market vs Intervention debate was heavily one sided, without realising that intervention was required to develop the market, if the East Asian experience was taken into account.\n\nFinancing the development effort is always a major issue. Development Banks were set up for the purpose, with funding from the Central Bank. (The first development bank was set up in Canada in the 1940s, as a subsidiary of the Central Bank).\n\nSri Lanka, got rid of its two development banks, while it needed more for each selected sector. Commercial bank funding was selective and served only a small proportion of the would-be entrepreneurs, as collateral would be an issue."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-04-dailyftlk-d468044a",
    "num": "dailyftlk-d468044a",
    "date_str": "2026-08-04",
    "description": "Can Sri Lanka overcome past mistakes and craft a path of development?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Can-Sri-Lanka-overcome-past-mistakes-and-craft-a-path-of-development/4-795528",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785785812.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-04-dailyftlk-d468044a-0006",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4fd6ab4609e1980fd81a5938c998d4d4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka, got rid of its two development banks, while it needed more for each selected sector. Commercial bank funding was selective and served only a small proportion of the would-be entrepreneurs, as collateral would be an issue.\n\nThe development of industry follows a process with many stages. In an under-developed economy, industries start as infant industries and have to be developed and supported through various means.\n\nImport Substitution Industry (ISI) is the first stage, while product development with technical input and training of human resources, improves the product to be competitive in the global market, while the input costs are managed with state support.\n\nIn the initial phase, state support\u00a0 comes in many forms, with tariff barriers being one.\n\nThough the East Asian countries followed this process of industrial development, Sri Lanka expected local companies to engage in exports, without the development process. It was like asking a kid, not knowing how to swim to jump at the deep end and drown. Teaching a kid how to swim in the shallow end and then swimming to the deep end, is what the East Asian countries did.\n\nThe few private sector companies that were bold enough to attempt, were not supported as in the East Asian countries, but made to drown, by our own government, in its eagerness for foreign loans and short- term benefits.\n\nSri Lanka has first to decide which sectors of industry it needs to develop and work out a program. It is now clear that low-tech is not the way to go. Developing the High-Tech industry is not easy and requires a totally dedicated team. Are we anywhere close to such a situation?\n\nStrategic and innovative thinking is key to development. Can Sri Lanka change its thinking to move from just \u2018managing\u2019 the economy to \u2018developing\u2019 the economy?\n\nThis is Sri Lanka\u2019s missing-link, which has still not been understood."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-04-dailyftlk-7bf4e1a2",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7bf4e1a2",
    "date_str": "2026-08-04",
    "description": "What Sri Lanka can learn from India\u2019s youth uprising",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/What-Sri-Lanka-can-learn-from-India-s-youth-uprising/4-795529",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785786172.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-04-dailyftlk-7bf4e1a2-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a6138f8a943a1f35cac47e38c30be874",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "What Sri Lanka can learn from India\u2019s youth uprising\n\nProtesters participate in a demonstration demanding educational reforms as part of the Cockroach Janta Party\u2019s ongoing protest movement in New Delhi, India, 25 July 2026\n\nIndia\u2019s recent youth-led protest movement should not be viewed merely as a political episode confined to one country. It is a powerful reminder that when education systems fail to meet the aspirations of young people, the consequences extend far beyond classrooms and universities. Examination integrity, graduate employability, equal opportunity, institutional credibility and public accountability eventually become matters of national importance.\n\nThe movement, popularly identified with the self-styled Cockroach Janta Party, began as an online expression of frustration but developed into a nationwide youth campaign following allegations of leaks and irregularities surrounding a major medical entrance examination. The controversy affected approximately two million candidates and drew attention to wider concerns about unemployment, fairness and accountability within India\u2019s education system. After weeks of demonstrations, India\u2019s Education Minister resigned on 25 July 2026, and the protest organisers subsequently agreed to end their demonstrations following discussions with the government.\n\nThis outcome illustrates an important democratic principle: when large numbers of young people raise legitimate concerns through organised civic engagement, governments and public institutions must listen, respond and reform.\n\nIndia is home to more than 371 million young people\u2014the largest youth population in the world. This demographic strength can become one of the country\u2019s greatest assets or one of its most serious challenges, depending on how successfully the nation educates, empowers and employs its young citizens."
  },
