    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "46b9dcd00522d62f8b440592f0b0e6d8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "India is home to more than 371 million young people\u2014the largest youth population in the world. This demographic strength can become one of the country\u2019s greatest assets or one of its most serious challenges, depending on how successfully the nation educates, empowers and employs its young citizens.\n\nThe questions raised by India\u2019s young people are therefore equally relevant to Sri Lanka. They challenge us to ask a fundamental question:\n\nAre we preparing our young people for yesterday\u2019s economy\u2014or tomorrow\u2019s world?\n\nEvery nation invests heavily in roads, ports, airports, energy systems and physical infrastructure. These investments are necessary. Yet no investment generates more enduring national returns than investment in people.\n\nEducation is not merely a social service. It is the foundation of economic growth, technological advancement, social cohesion, democratic stability and national competitiveness. Countries that lead the twenty-first century will not necessarily be those possessing the greatest natural resources, but those capable of developing the most educated, innovative, ethical and adaptable people.\n\nSri Lanka has every reason to be proud of its free education system. According to the 2024 Census, 97.4 per cent of the population aged ten years and above is literate. This is a major national achievement.\n\nHowever, literacy and educational access alone are no longer sufficient measures of success. The challenge today is not simply getting children into classrooms. It is ensuring that they leave those classrooms with the knowledge, practical skills, confidence, creativity, values and adaptability required by a rapidly changing world.\n\nThe national challenge is therefore no longer simply to educate more students. It is to develop better learners, better professionals, better entrepreneurs, better innovators and better citizens."
  },
