    "md5": "8145816f5944078b816c0b2e5c44ef2c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "However, literacy and educational access alone are no longer sufficient measures of success. The challenge today is not simply getting children into classrooms. It is ensuring that they leave those classrooms with the knowledge, practical skills, confidence, creativity, values and adaptability required by a rapidly changing world.\n\nThe national challenge is therefore no longer simply to educate more students. It is to develop better learners, better professionals, better entrepreneurs, better innovators and better citizens.\n\nAcross South Asia, the working-age population has increased by approximately 16 million people annually since 2010, while the region has created only about ten million jobs each year. This means that millions of new labour-market entrants risk being excluded unless job creation, productivity and workforce preparation improve substantially.\n\nSri Lanka faces a particularly serious employment challenge. The World Bank estimates that nearly one million young Sri Lankans will enter the labour market during the coming decade. Without stronger economic growth and greater private investment, only about 300,000 new formal jobs may be created\u2014leaving a substantial majority of new entrants without access to quality formal employment.\n\nAvailable World Bank data also indicate that nearly one in five Sri Lankans aged 15\u201324 was not in employment, education or training in 2023. This is not simply an education statistic. It represents lost productivity, weakened confidence and unrealised human potential.\n\nSri Lanka therefore requires a comprehensive national education transformation focusing on:\n\nn Strengthening early childhood education and foundational literacy and numeracy.\n\nn Introducing age-appropriate and ethically grounded learning in artificial intelligence, coding and digital literacy.\n\nn Strengthening English-language proficiency while preserving excellence in Sinhala and Tamil."
  },
