    "md5": "ba574b1f9b53953687c5d20d016b775c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1740,
    "chunk_text": "A National Education Equity Guarantee should establish minimum standards for every school. These should include access to qualified teachers, safe classrooms, sanitation, libraries, science facilities, digital connectivity, counselling and learning support.\n\nThe Indian experience reminds us that young people are not demanding charity. They are demanding fairness.\n\nThey seek credible examinations, quality education, meaningful employment, transparent institutions and equal opportunities to realise their potential.\n\nSri Lankan youth share these aspirations. Their greatest expectation is not permanent dependence on government assistance. It is the opportunity to compete fairly, develop their talents, innovate confidently and succeed on merit.\n\nGovernments that listen before frustration becomes disillusionment strengthen democracy. Governments that ignore young people risk losing the confidence of an entire generation.\n\nEducation reform alone cannot solve unemployment. Even well-qualified graduates will struggle if the economy does not generate sufficient productive employment.\n\nEducation policy must therefore be connected directly to economic, investment, industrial and regional-development policy.\n\nSri Lanka must address both sides of the employment challenge:\n\nThe demand side: encouraging investment, enterprise growth, exports, innovation and the creation of quality jobs.\n\nn closer coordination among education, industry, labour and economic-development institutions.\n\nThe objective should not be merely to produce jobseekers. It should be to develop job creators, innovators and productive professionals capable of competing internationally.\n\nEducation should become one of Sri Lanka\u2019s foremost national priorities."
  },
