    "language": "en",
    "md5": "41c75d867401b7afb5359431da33e2ad",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "The objective should not be merely to produce jobseekers. It should be to develop job creators, innovators and productive professionals capable of competing internationally.\n\nEducation should become one of Sri Lanka\u2019s foremost national priorities.\n\nThose that postpone reform risk producing graduates whose qualifications no longer correspond with economic and social needs.\n\nEducation policy must therefore become a long-term national commitment rather than a victim of frequently changing political priorities.\n\nFor many young people, a national examination determines access to university, professional education or employment. Any failure involving question papers, marking, admissions or the release of results can affect years of effort and permanently weaken trust in public institutions.\n\nn firm accountability for misconduct without collectively punishing innocent candidates.\n\nExamination integrity is not merely an administrative matter. It is a national trust.\n\nYoung people should not merely receive policies. They should help shape them.\n\nSri Lanka should institutionalise youth participation through a National Youth Education Advisory Council, representation on education-reform bodies, district-level consultations, graduate surveys, digital submission platforms and regular dialogue among students, educators, employers and policymakers.\n\nParticipation must be genuinely inclusive. It should extend beyond urban university students to include rural youth, vocational trainees, school leavers, young entrepreneurs, young people with disabilities and those from economically disadvantaged communities.\n\nConsultation becomes meaningful only when public institutions explain how youth proposals have been considered and acted upon.\n\nEducational institutions, examination authorities and policymakers must be accountable for maintaining fairness, transparency and professional standards."
  },
  {
