    "md5": "baef7a2ea114e33f0207c670cd5e3872",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Every qualification should therefore develop a combination of subject knowledge, practical competence, digital capability, communication skills, teamwork, ethical judgement and the capacity to continue learning.\n\nEvery classroom shapes the future productivity, character and resilience of the nation.\n\nTeachers are therefore not merely employees delivering a syllabus. They are nation-builders.\n\nSri Lanka cannot achieve meaningful reform without investing in teacher education, professional development, modern teaching resources, appropriate recognition and accountable professional standards.\n\nCurriculum reform without teacher preparation will remain reform on paper.\n\nHigh-stakes examinations and intense academic competition place significant pressure on students and families. Examination uncertainty, repeated delays and institutional failures can deepen anxiety and weaken confidence.\n\nn timely assistance for students affected by administrative failures; and\n\nEducational achievement should not be pursued at the cost of student wellbeing.\n\nSri Lanka should seize this moment to launch its most ambitious education transformation since the introduction of free education.\n\nn Establishing an independent National Education Reform Commission supported by legislation and broad political consensus.\n\nnDeveloping a ten-year National Education Transformation Strategy, supported by three-year implementation plans.\n\nn Strengthening early childhood and primary education, with national targets for foundational literacy and numeracy.\n\nn Integrating artificial intelligence, coding, digital literacy and technology ethics into age-appropriate school education.\n\nn Strengthening English-language competency while preserving excellence in Sinhala and Tamil.\n\nn Reviewing the national curriculum every five years, supported by continuous annual adjustments where economic and technological conditions require them."
