    "chunk_text": "n Strengthening English-language competency while preserving excellence in Sinhala and Tamil.\n\nn Reviewing the national curriculum every five years, supported by continuous annual adjustments where economic and technological conditions require them.\n\nn Expanding technical, vocational, apprenticeship and work-integrated learning pathways in line with labour-market demand.\n\nn Modernising public examinations through greater transparency, professional security and competency-based assessment.\n\nn Investing substantially in teacher education, continuous professional development and digital teaching resources.\n\nn Embedding entrepreneurship, financial literacy, research and innovation across educational disciplines.\n\nn Establishing a National Graduate Employability and Job-Creation Strategy linking education directly to investment, enterprise development and economic growth.\n\nn Creating a National Education Equity Guarantee to establish minimum facilities and learning opportunities in every school.\n\nSri Lanka does not suffer from a shortage of policy documents. Its greater weakness has often been incomplete implementation.\n\nn representation from educators, students, employers, provincial authorities and professional bodies;\n\nA national education-performance scorecard should report annually on learning outcomes, teacher availability, school infrastructure, vocational participation, digital access, graduate employment, regional inequality and progress in implementing reforms.\n\nEvery major reform should identify what must be done, who is responsible, how it will be financed, when it must be completed and how success will be measured.\n\nWithout such accountability, education reform will remain an aspiration rather than a transformation.\n\nThe Indian experience also reminds us that democracy is strengthened when governments remain responsive, institutions remain independent and citizens exercise both their rights and responsibilities with maturity."
