    "md5": "ab61d1289b1e79f1b04fb3b08d89735b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "The Indian experience also reminds us that democracy is strengthened when governments remain responsive, institutions remain independent and citizens exercise both their rights and responsibilities with maturity.\n\nCitizens must recognise that lasting national progress depends upon peaceful civic engagement, respect for constitutional processes and commitment to the rule of law.\n\nGovernments must equally recognise that authority carries a duty to listen, explain, correct failures and remain accountable.\n\nDemocracy flourishes when governments listen, institutions act with integrity and citizens participate responsibly.\n\nSri Lanka stands at an important moment in its national journey. The country\u2019s future will not be determined solely by economic policies, political debates or infrastructure projects. It will depend substantially upon how successfully we educate, inspire, employ and empower the next generation.\n\nIndia\u2019s recent youth movement demonstrates that young people cannot be ignored. What began as frustration over examination integrity expanded into a broader demand for accountability and reform. The government\u2019s eventual response and the peaceful conclusion of the demonstrations demonstrate both the power of youth participation and the importance of institutional responsiveness.\n\nThe lesson extends beyond politics. It concerns governance, fairness, opportunity, human dignity and national vision.\n\nCountries that invest in young people invest in their own future. Countries that fail to modernise education, create employment and protect institutional trust ultimately mortgage that future.\n\nSri Lanka must therefore pursue education reform and job creation as two parts of the same national mission. Young people must be given not only qualifications, but capabilities; not only expectations, but opportunities; and not only promises, but credible pathways towards productive and meaningful lives."
