    "chunk_size_bytes": 1777,
    "chunk_text": "Unconvicted inmates make up over 72% of prison population\n\nColombo, August 04 (Daily Mirror) - Amid growing concerns over overcrowding in prisons across the country, it is identified that nearly three-fourths of inmates are remand prisoners with cases pending against them.\n\nThe authorities are now implementing a series of initiatives to increase capacity and strengthen prison services, following recent incidents of unrest at Negombo and Mahara prisons.\n\nAccording to the latest official statistics, the total prison population stood at 39,516 inmates as of August 1, 2026. The figures show a significant imbalance between convicted prisoners and those awaiting trial or sentencing, with unconvicted inmates accounting for more than 72 per cent of the total prison population.\n\nOf the total number of inmates, 28,646 are classified as unconvicted prisoners, including 26,935 males and 1,711 females. Meanwhile, the convicted prisoner population stands at 10,870, comprising 10,588 males and 282 females. Overall, male inmates make up the majority of the prison population, with 37,523 males compared to 1,993 female inmates.\n\nThe issue of overcrowding came into focus following a major riot at Mahara Prison on August 1, where one inmate died, and six others were injured. The unrest, which began at around 4.00 p.m. on Saturday, continued for more than 13 hours before security forces brought the situation under control by 6.00 a.m. the following day.\n\nThe Committee for the Protection of Prisoners' Rights has attributed the incident to severe overcrowding at the facility. Chairman of the Committee, Attorney-at-Law Senaka Perera, alleged that conditions inside prisons have deteriorated due to the excessive number of inmates being held beyond the facilities' capacity."
