    "chunk_text": "Dengue wards remain overcrowded despite decline in daily cases\n\nColombo, August 4 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Hospitals across Sri Lanka continue to face congestion in dengue treatment wards despite a noticeable decline in the number of daily infections, hospital sources told the Daily Mirror.\n\nAccording to official data, the number of daily dengue cases, which had peaked at around 1,000, has now dropped to approximately 500.\n\nHowever, sources said the reduction has not been sufficient to ease pressure on hospitals, with dengue wards in many state hospitals still operating beyond capacity.\n\nCommenting on the situation, Government Medical Officers\u2019 Association (GMOA) Secretary Dr Prabath Sugathadasa told Daily Mirror that the severe congestion experienced during the peak of the outbreak has eased slightly, but overcrowding remains a major concern.\n\n\u201cAlthough the number of new dengue cases has declined, the wards designated for dengue patients are still overflowing. Hospitals continue to experience congestion, particularly in units treating dengue patients,\u201d he said.\n\nDr Sugathadasa urged the public not to become complacent due to the recent decline in reported cases, as the risk of infection remains high and preventive measures must continue.\n\nDengue cases in Sri Lanka stand at 86,385 with 62 confirmed deaths.\n\nSri Lanka Dengue Cases 2026: Live Tracker and Latest Updates"
