    "chunk_text": "The zoo also said that four lions have shown signs of recovery, while six lions were in good health from the beginning. They will be placed in a private enclosure equipped with portable air-conditioners and industrial fans.\n\nHe explains that instead of sweating, lions take deep breaths to expel moisture from their airways. When the moisture evaporates, body temperature drops.\n\nBut the lions' body temperatures could not drop sufficiently in high humidity, he says.\n\nAnother factor could be the sudden spike in temperature in mid-July, leaving the animals insufficient time to acclimate to the heat, Karaki adds.\n\nTama Zoo houses around 260 species of animals. Its lions have long been a key attraction, having been bred and exhibited since 1964. According to the zoo's website, it offers a safari-style bus service for visitors to view the lions in their enclosure."
