    "md5": "19ec9cbc65d600e743acaefad74135ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "WhatsApp tests separate folder for business messages to reduce inbox clutter\n\nVarIndia - Meta-owned WhatsApp is testing a new feature designed to help users better manage business communications by automatically moving messages from large enterprises into a dedicated folder. The initiative comes as the platform experiences rapid growth in business messaging and AI-powered customer engagement, prompting efforts to improve the user experience without limiting enterprise interactions.\n\nThe feature follows Meta's strong second-quarter 2026 performance, during which CEO Mark Zuckerberg said revenue from the company's \"Other\" category within its Family of Apps business surpassed $1 billion, driven largely by WhatsApp's paid messaging services and subscription offerings.\n\nAs more organisations use WhatsApp to communicate with customers, users are increasingly receiving promotional offers, transaction alerts and service updates alongside personal conversations. Meta's latest experiment is intended to reduce inbox congestion while ensuring important business communications remain accessible.\n\nUnder the pilot programme, messages from larger businesses, including banks, airlines and other enterprises, will automatically move to a new \"Offers & Updates\" folder after a predefined period. Meta is currently evaluating different time intervals, with messages expected to be transferred within a few hours and up to 24 hours after delivery.\n\nUsers who prefer to keep business messages in their primary chat list will have the option to disable the feature. However, the timing of when eligible messages are moved during the trial is determined by WhatsApp rather than individual users.\n\nAccording to the company, separating promotional and transactional communications from personal conversations is expected to make the main inbox easier to navigate while giving businesses a dedicated space where customers can find their messages without them being buried among regular chats."
  },
