    "md5": "3bdd4d04d968aed01c4c3699d32f89da",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1818,
    "chunk_text": "According to the company, separating promotional and transactional communications from personal conversations is expected to make the main inbox easier to navigate while giving businesses a dedicated space where customers can find their messages without them being buried among regular chats.\n\nThe latest test builds on a series of measures WhatsApp has introduced over recent years to reduce unwanted business messaging. The platform has progressively expanded user controls, including options to unsubscribe from marketing messages, restrictions on broadcast messaging and limits on the number of unsolicited business messages that can be sent without customer engagement.\n\nThese initiatives reflect Meta's broader strategy of balancing commercial opportunities with user experience as WhatsApp continues to evolve into a major business communication platform.\n\nThe company's business ecosystem has also expanded rapidly with the global rollout of AI-powered business agents in June. Meta said more than one million businesses are already using these AI capabilities to automate customer interactions, sales and support functions. During the company's earnings call, Zuckerberg highlighted improved customer engagement and conversion rates achieved by Brazilian car rental company Movida through AI-driven interactions on WhatsApp.\n\nWith WhatsApp now serving more than three billion users worldwide, Meta faces the challenge of supporting increasing enterprise adoption while preserving the app's core role as a personal messaging platform. The new business message folder represents another step toward managing that balance, as AI-enabled business communications become a larger part of the platform's long-term growth strategy.\n\nWhy Countries Are Banning Children from Social Media and What It Means for Sri Lanka"
  },
