    "chunk_text": "The fraudsters then send the screenshots or recordings to the victim's relatives or friends through social media and demand money to prevent wider distribution. Initial demands typically range from Rs. 5,000 to Rs. 10,000 but quickly escalate, with some victims reportedly losing hundreds of thousands of rupees after making repeated payments.\n\nMany victims reportedly sought help from Sri Lanka CERT only after the extortion became continuous and financially overwhelming.\n\nAuthorities have urged social media users to exercise extreme caution when responding to messages, friend requests or video calls from unknown individuals. The public is advised to verify identities before engaging in private conversations, avoid sharing intimate images or participating in compromising video calls, and immediately report any attempted extortion to Sri Lanka CERT or the Sri Lanka Police.\n\nWhy Countries Are Banning Children from Social Media and What It Means for Sri Lanka"
