    "md5": "3e3c4f2968dc37c4fef80709d487ff64",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankans working in India can continue Sri Lanka EPF contributions\n\nColombo, Auguat 4 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lankan workers temporarily employed in India will be able to continue contributing to the Employees\u2019 Provident Fund (EPF) administered by the Sri Lankan Department of Labour while being exempted from contributing to the Indian Employees\u2019 Provident Fund under a proposed Social Security Agreement between Sri Lanka and India.\n\nThe agreement is expected to address key social security concerns faced by Sri Lankan workers employed in India and prevent them from having to make duplicate contributions to two separate social security systems.\n\nThe fourth round of bilateral talks between the Governments of Sri Lanka and India on the proposed agreement concluded successfully in Colombo on July 21 and 22, 2026, with both sides reaching agreement on outstanding issues related to the draft and finalising clauses to be included in the pact.\n\nNegotiations on the agreement began nearly a decade ago, with the latest discussions marking a significant step towards strengthening social security protection for workers of both countries.\n\nUnder the proposed arrangement, eligible Indian workers temporarily employed in Sri Lanka will also receive similar benefits, allowing them to continue contributions to their respective social security systems without being required to make additional contributions in the host country.\n\nThe agreement is also expected to provide relief to Sri Lankan workers who have previously contributed to India\u2019s Employees\u2019 Provident Fund but have been unable to withdraw their savings.\n\nUnder existing Indian EPF regulations, members are eligible to withdraw their benefits after reaching the age of 58. However, many Sri Lankan workers have faced difficulties in accessing their funds due to practical issues, including challenges in maintaining or operating Indian bank accounts."
