    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cI would say that it was a rebirth for all of us. There were screams and my elder daughter, who is five years old, was so scared, as were we all,\u201d he said.\n\nThe passenger also praised the pilot and cabin crew for helping passengers remain calm and called for a thorough inquiry into the incident.\n\n\u201cPeople sitting behind me also suffered injuries. A male passenger had blood on his face. We thought we would die. It was really scary. I was shaking and crying. It was horrible. I cannot hear properly right now,\" she added.\n\nSeven passengers from the flight were admitted to Fortis Hospital in Vasant Kunj for medical evaluation and treatment, the hospital said.\n\nThe passengers were brought to the emergency department after the hospital received an alert from Air India regarding the turbulence incident.\n\nA Code Yellow was subsequently activated to coordinate the medical response and deploy necessary resources, PTI reported.\n\nThe passengers underwent radiological investigations, including CT scans, MRIs and X-rays, where clinically required.\n\nThey were then shifted to inpatient rooms for further observation and treatment.\n\nFortis said doctors were continuing to assess the passengers and that the extent of their injuries and further treatment would become clearer after the investigations and specialist assessments.\n\nAir India said the aircraft experienced a \u201cbrief in-flight turbulence-related event\u201d during the cruise, resulting in a momentary change in altitude.\n\nThe airline said the aircraft landed safely in Delhi and all passengers and crew members disembarked.\n\n\u201cThere has been no report of serious injuries. A small number of passengers and crew members with minor injuries requiring medical assessment have been taken to a medical facility at the airport for precautionary examination and care by Air India\u2019s airport team and medical personnel,\u201d the airline said."
