    "description": "Has the Consumer Affairs Authority Gone into Hibernation?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Has-the-Consumer-Affairs-Authority-Gone-into-Hibernation/172-347408",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785879161.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-9ad4e0b5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ae05d9969b0094138c08d57988c13c4e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1720,
    "chunk_text": "Has the Consumer Affairs Authority Gone into Hibernation?\n\nEvery day, Sri Lankan consumers are being taken for a ride, and the Consumer Affairs Authority appears to be watching from the sidelines.\n\nI recently ordered a meal from an internationally renowned fast-food chain. The advertised menu price was Rs. 2,990.00. Yet, when the bill was presented, I found an additional Rs. 386.00 described as a \u201chandling fee\u201d\u00a0 Rs. 76.50 as a \u201cconvenience fee and\u00a0 29.00 as fuel surcharge. I accept the fuel surcharge. The amount I finally had to pay was an astonishing Rs. 511.50 more than the advertised price.\n\nThe obvious question is: What is the real price of the meal? Is it the price displayed on the menu, or the inflated amount extracted from the customer at the point of payment? These charges may have impressive names, but to the ordinary consumer they are nothing more than hidden costs that distort the advertised price. What is even more disturbing is the deafening silence of the Consumer Affairs Authority. Complaints about these questionable pricing practices have repeatedly appeared in newspapers and across social media, yet the Authority seems content to remain a passive observer.\n\nThe Consumer Affairs Authority was created to safeguard consumers\u2014not to preside over their exploitation. Its responsibility is to ensure transparent pricing, prevent unfair trade practices, and restore public confidence in the marketplace. The Authority must immediately investigate these pricing practices and require that the price displayed to the public is the final price payable, except for taxes or charges clearly disclosed before the order is confirmed. Anything less makes a mockery of consumer protection."
  },
