    "description": "When will South Asia wake up to climate reality?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/When-will-South-Asia-wake-up-to-climate-reality/172-347409",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785879371.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-454f5954-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "286a034194adfbc2a8c75c0655fd4b0a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "When will South Asia wake up to climate reality?\n\nIt no longer arrives as a distant warning. Climate change is here, it is relentless, and it is unforgiving. Across South Asia, from the flooded plains of Bangladesh to the heat-scorched regions of India and Pakistan, the signs are unmistakable. Yet, governments continue to react to disasters rather than prepare for them. Nowhere is this more evident than in Sri Lanka.\n\nFor years, scientists and environmentalists have sounded the alarm. Rising temperatures, erratic rainfall, prolonged droughts and intense storms were all predicted. In Sri Lanka alone, floods and landslides have become an almost annual tragedy. Entire communities are displaced, homes are destroyed, and lives are lost. Still, each disaster is treated as an isolated incident rather than part of a growing climate crisis.\n\nThe recent Ditwah disaster stands as a painful reminder. Hundreds of lives were lost. Key infrastructure collapsed. Roads were washed away, cutting off entire villages and leaving families stranded without access to basic necessities. It was not just a natural disaster. It was a failure of preparedness. It exposed how vulnerable the country remains despite repeated warnings.\n\nSouth Asia is one of the most climate vulnerable regions in the world. According to global climate risk assessments, countries like Sri Lanka, Bangladesh and India rank among those most exposed to extreme weather events.. When floods hit or droughts stretch for months, the impact is not just environmental. It is economic, social and deeply human. In Sri Lanka, the numbers paint a worrying picture. Thousands are affected every year by floods. Landslides alone have claimed dozens of lives annually in high-risk districts such as Kegalle, Kalutara and Nuwara Eliya. Yet, budget allocations for disaster preparedness and climate adaptation remain insufficient and often poorly utilised."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-454f5954",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-454f5954",
    "date_str": "2026-08-05",
    "description": "When will South Asia wake up to climate reality?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/When-will-South-Asia-wake-up-to-climate-reality/172-347409",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785879371.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-454f5954-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "88b3a760a53b386d4ab9492d3e6764ac",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "South Asia is one of the most climate vulnerable regions in the world. According to global climate risk assessments, countries like Sri Lanka, Bangladesh and India rank among those most exposed to extreme weather events.. When floods hit or droughts stretch for months, the impact is not just environmental. It is economic, social and deeply human. In Sri Lanka, the numbers paint a worrying picture. Thousands are affected every year by floods. Landslides alone have claimed dozens of lives annually in high-risk districts such as Kegalle, Kalutara and Nuwara Eliya. Yet, budget allocations for disaster preparedness and climate adaptation remain insufficient and often poorly utilised.\n\nFirst, governments must prioritise early warning systems. Technology exists to predict heavy rainfall, floods and landslides days in advance. Yet, gaps in communication mean that warnings often do not reach the most vulnerable communities in time. Second, infrastructure must be built to withstand extreme weather. Roads, bridges and drainage systems in Sri Lanka are not designed for the intensity of rainfall now being experienced. Poor urban planning has only worsened the situation, with wetlands reclaimed and natural waterways blocked. Climate-resilient infrastructure is no longer a luxury. It is a necessity.\n\nThird, land use policies must be enforced. Year after year, we see homes built in landslide-prone areas and flood plains. Regulations exist, but enforcement is weak. Political interference and short-term interests often override safety concerns. This must change.\n\nFourth, South Asia must invest in regional cooperation. Climate change does not respect borders. Sharing data, resources and strategies can significantly improve preparedness. A coordinated regional response could help mitigate the impact of disasters that affect millions."
  },
  {
