    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "388b2fe18e50de90bfd979430e28f278",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1120,
    "chunk_text": "Fourth, South Asia must invest in regional cooperation. Climate change does not respect borders. Sharing data, resources and strategies can significantly improve preparedness. A coordinated regional response could help mitigate the impact of disasters that affect millions.\n\nThe cost of inaction is far greater than the cost of preparedness. Every flood that destroys homes, every landslide that takes lives, and every drought that devastates livelihoods adds to an already heavy burden. For developing economies in South Asia, repeated disasters can stall growth and push vulnerable populations deeper into poverty.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s experience should serve as a wake-up call for the region. Climate change can no longer be treated as an environmental issue alone. It is a national security concern, an economic challenge and a humanitarian crisis.\u00a0 South Asia stands at a crossroads. It can continue down the path of reaction and regret, or it can choose preparedness and resilience. What is needed now is action.. The real question is whether we will act before the next disaster, or once again count the cost after it."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
