    "date_str": "2026-08-05",
    "description": "Decision to extend the retirement age of judges: A well-meaning policy implemented in Gotabaya style",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Decision-to-extend-the-retirement-age-of-judges-A-well-meaning-policy-implemented-in-Gotabaya-style/172-347410",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785879713.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-68b8b6e7-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "afac494a727be6b1d18d794e06e089a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "Decision to extend the retirement age of judges: A well-meaning policy implemented in Gotabaya style\n\nThe arbitrary extension of the retirement ages of judges, in the absence of any meaningful consultation, suggests its decision is no less tailor-made for narrow political ends than Gotabaya Rajapaksa\u2019s 20th amendment to the Constitution\n\nThe opposition to the government\u2019s proposal to increase the retirement ages of higher appellate court judges reveals a bitter irony of Sri Lankan politics. It is a classic case of how even well-meaning policies could be politicised to extreme ends\u2013\u2013 or, worse still, the same seemingly well-intentioned measures could be nothing more than haphazard choices of political expediency and guile.\n\nThe Cabinet last week approved extending the retirement age of all judges by two years. The Justice Minister, Harshana Nanayakkara, touted the decision as part of a broader structural reform package to clear an existing backlog of 1.1 million court cases. The Cabinet paper titled \u201cMake the Judicial Process More Efficient and Formal,\u201d envisaged two parallel pieces of draft legislation to extend the retirement of judges across all courts.\n\nFirst, a Constitutional Amendment Bill will extend the retirement ages of Supreme Court Judges from 65 to 67 and the Appeal Court judges from 63 to 65.\n\nA second bill\u2013The Judicial Act Amendment Bill stipulates extending the retirement of all lower court judges, similarly by two years. That would see the retirement age of High Court judges ( now 61) increased to 63 and District Court and Magistrate Court judges\u00a0 (now 60) increased to 62.\n\nThe two amendments were a judicial necessity, for the retirement ages of higher appellate court judges are hardcoded into Article 107 (5) of the Constitution and have remained unchanged since 1978. Any changes require a constitutional amendment. Conversely, the retirement ages of lower court judges are governed by Statutory Law under the Judicature Act."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-68b8b6e7",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-68b8b6e7",
    "date_str": "2026-08-05",
    "description": "Decision to extend the retirement age of judges: A well-meaning policy implemented in Gotabaya style",
