    "language": "en",
    "md5": "91d8d73cd1775fdec7b6acd7d15177bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1871,
    "chunk_text": "The two amendments were a judicial necessity, for the retirement ages of higher appellate court judges are hardcoded into Article 107 (5) of the Constitution and have remained unchanged since 1978. Any changes require a constitutional amendment. Conversely, the retirement ages of lower court judges are governed by Statutory Law under the Judicature Act.\n\nSecond, it is fair to argue that the extension of the retirement age of the judges of Higher Appellate Courts would not necessarily solve the mammoth 1.1 million unresolved court cases in the country. According to the Ministry of Justice, 818,000 cases are stuck in the magistrate courts, compared to only 5000 cases in the Supreme Court. The Sri Lankan judicial sector suffers from crippling staff shortages. A total of 2,647 vacancies in the judicial service are documented by the Ministerial Consultative Committee on Justice, the vast majority of which exist in clerical work, ranging from stenographers, typists and interpreters. Similarly, the government analyst\u2019s department is beset by staff shortage, antiquated equipment and persistent delays, contributing to the slow pace in the judicial process.\n\nThe recent interviews for 50 magistrate trial position manged to recruit only 33, in part owing to lower remuneration. The international benchmark for an efficient judiciary places 50 judges for every 1 million people. Sri Lanka only has 20 judges per every 1 million.\u00a0 The middle-income country average is 65 judges per million, and advanced developed economies have an average of 150 to 220 judges per million people, according to data from the Council of Europe.\n\nTherefore, it is no brainer that without sector-wide reforms, modernisation and efficiency gains,\u00a0 the extension of retirement ages of Higher Appellate Court judges would have minimal impact on the efficiency of the judicial process."
  },
