    "chunk_text": "Therefore, it is no brainer that without sector-wide reforms, modernisation and efficiency gains,\u00a0 the extension of retirement ages of Higher Appellate Court judges would have minimal impact on the efficiency of the judicial process.\n\nThe government decision has been opposed not only at home, vehemently by the Bar Association of Sri Lanka, the Judicial Services Association of Sri Lanka, which represents district court judges and magistrates, the political opposition and, according to the president of the Bar Association, Rajiv Amarasuriya, also Mahanayakas, who had written to the president.\n\nSimilarly, the decision has brought rebuke from international judicial bodies, including the French National Bar (the retirement age of whose senior judges of the Court de Cassation, equivalent of the Sri Lankan Supreme Court, is 70 years) and the Commonwealth Lawyers Association.\n\nHowever, the government decision is more in line with international norms than it was presented. Of the Commonwealth countries, the statutory retirement ages of all judges in the UK were increased in 2020 from 70 to 75. In Canada, the Supreme Court and upper-tier judges serve until 75. In Australia, the Federal and High Court judges must retire at age 70 and in New Zealand, too, it is 70.\n\nSo far so good. The government\u2019s decision across all these counts appears well-intentioned.\n\nThen alas, it fails a crucial smell test. The arbitrariness and unholy rush to push through a constitutional amendment, after 18 months in power, and having done nothing, suggests that the decision is tailor-made to the extension of the retirement age of the incumbent chief justice, who is due to retire in December should the government fail to pass the amendment.\u00a0 His retirement could then lead to the potential appointment of another Supreme Court judge, whom the government consider as sympathetic to the deposed Rajapaksa camp. The mad rush for a constitutional amendment is to avert that eventuality."
