    "chunk_text": "Then alas, it fails a crucial smell test. The arbitrariness and unholy rush to push through a constitutional amendment, after 18 months in power, and having done nothing, suggests that the decision is tailor-made to the extension of the retirement age of the incumbent chief justice, who is due to retire in December should the government fail to pass the amendment.\u00a0 His retirement could then lead to the potential appointment of another Supreme Court judge, whom the government consider as sympathetic to the deposed Rajapaksa camp. The mad rush for a constitutional amendment is to avert that eventuality.\n\nThat could, however, save the judiciary from being viewed yet again as an instrument of the party in political power, rather than one of the three pillars of the state that checks the power of the legislature and executive. The independence of the judiciary could not be sustained without public confidence in it. However, the government decision, if proceeded without extensive deliberation with the stakeholders, would risk fostering public perception of the judiciary as influenced and compromised\u2013 or worse still, creating plausible judiciary dependence on the government."
