    "chunk_size_bytes": 1646,
    "chunk_text": "A National Security Imperative:  When intelligence exists, but communication fails\n\nDuring Cyclone Ditwah meteorological and landslide warnings existed, yet many communities in the hill country and plantation areas received incomplete, delayed, or language-inaccessible alerts\n\nTime and again, critical information has been available, yet the absence of timely dissemination, confirmed receipt, swift response, structured feedback, and mutual trust between sender and recipient has turned preventable threats into national tragedies\n\nOne of the most critical weaknesses in current practice is the absence of disciplined, structured feedback loops. Dissemination without feedback is incomplete communication. Feedback without structure becomes noise\n\nEffective communication is the invisible backbone of national security. Without it, even the most accurate intelligence becomes useless, and coordinated action collapses into confusion. Sri Lanka has repeatedly paid a heavy price for this gap. Time and again, critical information has been available, yet the absence of timely dissemination, confirmed receipt, swift response, structured feedback, and mutual trust between sender and recipient has turned preventable threats into national tragedies. The Easter Sunday attacks of 2019 remain the starkest illustration. Foreign intelligence warnings reached the highest levels of the security apparatus days and even hours before the bombings. Names, locations, and the nature of the threat were known. Yet the system failed to convert that knowledge into decisive protective action. Information moved in fragments, often through informal channels."
