    "chunk_size_bytes": 1609,
    "chunk_text": "Effective communication is the invisible backbone of national security. Without it, even the most accurate intelligence becomes useless, and coordinated action collapses into confusion. Sri Lanka has repeatedly paid a heavy price for this gap. Time and again, critical information has been available, yet the absence of timely dissemination, confirmed receipt, swift response, structured feedback, and mutual trust between sender and recipient has turned preventable threats into national tragedies. The Easter Sunday attacks of 2019 remain the starkest illustration. Foreign intelligence warnings reached the highest levels of the security apparatus days and even hours before the bombings. Names, locations, and the nature of the threat were known. Yet the system failed to convert that knowledge into decisive protective action. Information moved in fragments, often through informal channels.\n\nThis pattern has not been confined to terrorism. Recent disaster responses have exposed the same structural weaknesses. During Cyclone Ditwah in late 2025, meteorological and landslide warnings existed, yet many communities, particularly Tamil-speaking populations in the hill country and plantation areas, received incomplete, delayed, or language-inaccessible alerts. Official channels issued contradictory assessments. Power and telecom outages further severed the link between national centres and ground responders. Once again, the sequence broke down: dissemination occurred in part, but receipt was not ensured, response was uneven, and feedback from the field struggled to reach decision-makers in time."
