    "md5": "0506df6d23b09f699356388851ac6872",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "This pattern has not been confined to terrorism. Recent disaster responses have exposed the same structural weaknesses. During Cyclone Ditwah in late 2025, meteorological and landslide warnings existed, yet many communities, particularly Tamil-speaking populations in the hill country and plantation areas, received incomplete, delayed, or language-inaccessible alerts. Official channels issued contradictory assessments. Power and telecom outages further severed the link between national centres and ground responders. Once again, the sequence broke down: dissemination occurred in part, but receipt was not ensured, response was uneven, and feedback from the field struggled to reach decision-makers in time.\n\nThese episodes share a common anatomy of failure. Intelligence or early warning is collected. It is partially disseminated. But the critical intermediate steps, ensuring the recipient has actually received and understood the message, confirming that appropriate action has been initiated, establishing a rapid feedback channel, and securing timely acknowledgement,\u00a0 are frequently missing or informal. In high-stakes environments, informal systems are fragile. They depend on personal relationships that may be strained by institutional rivalry, rank sensitivity, or past mistrust.\n\nAt the national security level, communication is not a support function. It is a core operational capability. A modern state facing hybrid threats, organised crime, terrorism, and climate-driven disasters requires a secure, resilient, and government-owned critical communication infrastructure that prioritises clear classification and urgency indicators, guaranteed delivery with confirmation of receipt, priority routing for operationally valuable intelligence, structured two-way feedback, and audit trails that support accountability without paralysing action."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-3a3bd8a8",
