    "md5": "804794bda3b05833828d9e80c5d6be9f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "At the national security level, communication is not a support function. It is a core operational capability. A modern state facing hybrid threats, organised crime, terrorism, and climate-driven disasters requires a secure, resilient, and government-owned critical communication infrastructure that prioritises clear classification and urgency indicators, guaranteed delivery with confirmation of receipt, priority routing for operationally valuable intelligence, structured two-way feedback, and audit trails that support accountability without paralysing action.\n\nA structured feedback loop should include the following essential elements:\n\nImmediate acknowledgement of receipt by the designated recipient or command centre.\n\nConfirmation that the content has been understood, including any clarification sought within a defined short timeframe.\n\nReporting of the initial action or decision taken in response to the information.\n\nPeriodic updates on progress or changing circumstances until the matter is resolved or formally closed.\n\nA final summary that records outcomes and lessons for institutional learning.\n\nThese steps must be supported by clear protocols on who is responsible for providing feedback, the maximum acceptable timeframes for each stage, and the channels through which feedback is transmitted. In operational environments, priority channels should allow near real-time two-way exchange, particularly when intelligence of high operational value is involved. Without such structure, critical information can disappear into organisational silence, leaving both the originator and the decision-maker uncertain whether anything has been done.\n\nStructured feedback is only effective when it is also secure. In national security work, the feedback channel itself can become a vulnerability if it is poorly designed. Secure feedback protocols must therefore combine speed, clarity, and protection."
  },
  {
