    "md5": "26fa2fd1689bafe203aa658fee9496f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1601,
    "chunk_text": "Protocols that exist only on paper fail under pressure. Periodic inter-agency exercises are necessary to test the system, expose weaknesses, and ensure that personnel remain proficient in both the technical tools and the procedural discipline required.\n\nNo technical system or protocol can succeed without a foundation of inter-agency trust and confidence. Trust is not built by circulars or occasional high-level meetings. It is cultivated through consistent, professional behaviour over time.\n\nTrust begins with reliability: when one agency shares information, the receiving agency must know that the information is accurate, timely, and shared in good faith. Reciprocity is equally important. Agencies that only take information without contributing in return eventually find themselves isolated. Transparency in handling shared intelligence, without compromising operational security,\u00a0 helps reduce suspicion. Joint training, combined exercises, and shared after-action reviews create personal and institutional familiarity that makes cooperation more natural under pressure.\n\nLeadership plays a decisive role. When senior officers publicly and privately reinforce the value of collaboration rather than institutional competition, trust grows. Conversely, when agencies are encouraged to protect their own information silos or to seek exclusive credit, confidence erodes. Clear and consistently applied protocols for information sharing, including defined roles and accountability, reduce the fear that cooperation will be misused or that individual officers will be blamed for systemic failures."
  },
