    "chunk_size_bytes": 1533,
    "chunk_text": "A deeply damaging habit still persists in many organisations: the tendency to report only what the superior is believed to want to hear, or to frame assessments in a manner designed to keep the decision-maker comfortable. This practice, often rooted in a culture of pleasing rather than informing, places the very person responsible for critical decisions in serious jeopardy. Incomplete, overly optimistic, or carefully filtered assessments deprive leaders of the accurate picture they need. The consequence is delayed or misguided decisions that later prove costly.\n\nThe relationship between sender and recipient remains decisive. When trust is high, information flows faster and is acted upon with greater confidence. When trust is low, even accurate intelligence is second-guessed, delayed, or quietly set aside. Building that trust demands consistent protocols, joint training, shared situational awareness, and leadership that rewards honest communication over institutional comfort.\n\nSri Lanka possesses the professional expertise within its security and disaster management institutions to close these gaps. What has been lacking is a coherent, whole-of-government approach that treats reliable communication as a strategic national asset rather than a technical afterthought, and that insists on continuous skill development among those who lead. The cost of continued failure is measured not only in lives and economic damage, but in the steady erosion of public confidence in the state\u2019s ability to protect its citizens."
