    "md5": "659d872b87077c311c9ef07818ee1656",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "Preventing any possibility of another July 1983\n\nA Government committed to national reconciliation must do more than promise equal treatment. It must take visible steps to provide justice for past wrongs while addressing the underlying causes of conflict so that the failures of 1983 are never repeated\n\nForty three years after the anti-Tamil violence of July 1983, Sri Lanka still carries the burden of an injustice that has never been adequately acknowledged or remedied.\n\nThe destruction of lives, homes and livelihoods on a hitherto unprecedented scale, in the capital city of Colombo and elsewhere, shattered the confidence of the Tamil community in the ability of the State to protect all its citizens equally.\n\nThe passage of time does not diminish the responsibility of the State, nor can what happened be glossed over in the name of moving forward. Successive governments have failed to provide adequate redress to those who suffered.\n\nThe unresolved issue of compensation for lives lost and property destroyed remains a source of deep grievance among survivors and their families, particularly when compared with the compensation provided to victims of more recent man-made disasters.\n\nAddressing the humanitarian consequences of July 1983, however, is only one part of the responsibility that falls upon the State. The deeper obligation is to address the political and institutional failures that made those events possible and to ensure they can never recur.\n\nLasting reconciliation requires minorities to have confidence that they have an effective voice in the governance of the country and that their concerns will be heard and acted upon. This is why the full implementation of constitutional guarantees, including meaningful devolution of power and the early holding of provincial council elections, is essential not only to reconciliation but also to preventing a repetition of the mistakes of the past."
  },
