    "md5": "ba2c3b6be58210d848ff4968373ccaa2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 768,
    "chunk_text": "Eight killed as adverse weather impacts over 11,800 people\n\nEight people have died following adverse weather conditions that affected several parts of the country over the past few days, according to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC).\n\nThe DMC said in its latest situation report issued yesterday (4) that the severe weather had impacted 56 Divisional Secretariat divisions across nine districts.\n\nA total of 11,832 people from 3,014 families have been affected by the prevailing conditions, while three others sustained injuries, the report said.\n\nThe adverse weather has also caused property damage, with one house completely destroyed and 290 houses partially damaged.\n\nThe DMC continues to monitor the situation and coordinate relief measures in affected areas."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-05-dailyftlk-7551fb9a",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7551fb9a",
    "date_str": "2026-08-05",
    "description": "Govt. says briefed Chief Prelates on 22A, no objections raised",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Govt-says-briefed-Chief-Prelates-on-22A-no-objections-raised/56-795616",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785896215.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailyftlk-7551fb9a-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ff0a66f3ae337d7ed5c73b79958204d7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. says briefed Chief Prelates on 22A, no objections raised\n\nCabinet Spokesperson says unaware of any letter sent by Chief Prelates\n\nCabinet Spokesman and Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa yesterday said he had not been informed of any letter reportedly sent by the Chief Prelates of the three Nikayas to President Anura Kumara Dissanayake opposing the proposed constitutional amendment to extend the retirement age of judges.\n\nResponding to a question during the weekly post-Cabinet media briefing, Dr. Jayatissa said he and Justice Minister Harshana Nanayakkara met the Chief Prelates of the Malwattu, Asgiri and Ramanna Nikayas on Monday to brief them on the proposed 22nd Constitutional Amendment.\n\nHe said the Government\u2019s position and its objective of expediting long-pending Court cases to provide timely justice to the public were clearly explained during the discussions.\n\nAccording to Dr. Jayatissa, the Chief Prelates, did not express any opposition to the proposed amendment at those meetings.\n\n\u201cThe amendment was not intended to benefit or target any individual judge, but would uniformly increase the retirement age of all judges, including those serving in the Supreme Court, Court of Appeal, Magistrates\u2019 Courts and other courts governed under the Judicial Service Act,\u201d he explained.\n\nQuoting the Malwattu Chief Prelate, the Cabinet Spokesman said even senior members of the Maha Sangha had acknowledged the serious delays in the judicial system, with some cases taking between 12 and 20 years to conclude, and recognised the need for reforms to improve the efficiency of the courts.\n\n\u201cThere is no opposition to judicial reforms aimed at speeding up the hearing of cases, not even from the Chief Prelates,\u201d Dr. Jayatissa said.\n\nThe Cabinet Spokesman and Justice Minister also met the Chief Prelate of the Amarapura Nikaya yesterday evening to continue discussions on the proposed constitutional amendment and the Government\u2019s judicial reform agenda."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
