    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "Projectile sinks Indian-flagged ship off Yemen coast\n\nBBC - An Indian-flagged vessel capsized and sunk after it was hit by a projectile off the coast of Yemen, officials say.\n\nAll 14 people on board the MSV \u200bFaize Noore Oliya - 13 of which were Indian nationals - were safely rescued, India's Shipping Minister Sarbananda Sonowal said.\n\nSonowal condemned the \"unprovoked attack\" and said steps would be taken to ensure the safety of seafarers in the region. It is not clear who struck the ship.\n\nIt is the latest in a spate of attacks on vessels in the Red Sea, an alternative waterway that some tankers had been using since Iran blocked the Strait of Hormuz in February.\n\nIndia's ministry of external affairs later released a statement calling attacks on commercial shipping in the region \"deeply worrisome\".\n\n\"The targeting of commercial shipping in the region must end, and free and unimpeded navigation and commerce through the international waterways in the region, in keeping with international law, must be restored at the earliest,\" the ministry said.\n\nMuch of the region's shipping routes have been disrupted since the US and Israeli strikes on Iran in late February.\n\nSince then, Iran has blocked the Strait of Hormuz, one of the world's busiest oil shipping channels, hugely impacting global oil prices.\n\nSome ships carrying oil from Saudi Arabia had instead passed through an alternative shipping lane in the Red Sea, but a recent spate of attacks by Yemen's Houthi fighters on Saudi tankers has further heightened risks in the region.\n\nYemen's transportation ministry, part of the Saudi-backed, internationally recognised government, accused \u200bthe Iran-backed Houthis of carrying out the \u200battack by using a boat laden with explosives, Reuters news agency reported.\n\nThe Houthis announced what it called a blockade on Saudi Arabia's Red Sea ports on 20 July.\n\nThe UK Maritime Trade Operations \u2060agency has reported several attacks on ships in the region over the past week."
