    "chunk_text": "A SpaceX rocket is about to crash into the moon tonight \u2014 Can you watch it?\n\nMEN'S JOURNAL - A SpaceX rocket stage is expected to slam into the moon early Wednesday morning in a rare event that astronomers have been tracking for months.\n\nThe Falcon 9 upper stage, which launched in January 2025 to send two private lunar landers toward the moon, is now on course to impact the lunar surface at approximately 2:35 a.m. ET on Aug. 5 after spending nearly 19 months drifting through space.\n\nWhile there\u2019s no danger to Earth, scientists say the collision offers a unique opportunity to learn more about the moon, and skywatchers may even have a chance to glimpse the aftermath.\n\nThe Falcon 9 rocket launched on Jan. 15, 2025, carrying Firefly Aerospace\u2019s Blue Ghost lunar lander and Japanese company ispace\u2019s Resilience lander.\n\nAfter sending both of the spacecrafts on their way, the rocket\u2019s upper stage was expected to eventually leave Earth\u2019s vicinity. Instead, a combination of gravity and solar influences gradually altered its path until it was pulled toward the moon.\n\nIndependent astronomers first predicted the impact earlier this year, and NASA later confirmed the rocket stage was indeed on a collision course. The booster is expected to strike the lunar surface near Einstein Crater, close to the moon\u2019s western edge.\n\nThe impact itself will occur on the moon\u2019s sunlit side, making the brief flash difficult to spot from Earth. Experts say the actual collision will likely last no more than a second.\n\nHowever, astronomers are hopeful that the collision will send a plume of debris high enough above the lunar surface that it could be visible through large telescopes, and possibly even binoculars under ideal viewing conditions.\n\nBecause the impact site sits near the moon\u2019s western limb, any bright ejecta could stand out against the darkness of space. Researchers around the world are expected to monitor the event, though there is no official public livestream of the impact itself."
