    "chunk_size_bytes": 1030,
    "chunk_text": "Because the impact site sits near the moon\u2019s western limb, any bright ejecta could stand out against the darkness of space. Researchers around the world are expected to monitor the event, though there is no official public livestream of the impact itself.\n\nScientists estimate the impact will create a crater roughly 65 to 90 feet wide, while ejecting lunar material hundreds of feet \u2014 and possibly much farther \u2014 into the air.\n\nNASA\u2019s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter won\u2019t witness the impact in real time, but it is expected to photograph the area afterward to compare before-and-after images and measure the newly formed crater.\n\nResearchers hope the collision will provide valuable data about how impacts behave on the moon, where the lack of an atmosphere creates very different conditions than on Earth.\n\nThe observations could also help scientists better understand potential hazards for future lunar missions, including NASA\u2019s Artemis program, as more spacecraft begin traveling to (and operating around) the moon."
