    "chunk_size_bytes": 2013,
    "chunk_text": "Ravi K seeks details on El Ni\u00f1o\u2019s economic impact\n\nColombo, Aug. 5 (Daily Mirror) - Opposition MP Ravi Karunanayake has questioned whether the government has assessed the likely economic impact of the forecast strong El Ni\u00f1o event expected to affect Sri Lanka during 2026/2027, including its impact on inflation, export crops, government revenue, disaster relief expenditure and the country\u2019s Gross Domestic Product (GDP).\n\nRaising the matter in parliament, Karunanayake asked whether the government had quantified the likely impact on inflation, the effect on export crops, additional expenditure required for disaster relief, revenue losses to the government and the expected GDP loss under both moderate and severe El Ni\u00f1o scenarios.\n\nCiting scientific forecasts warning of a strong El Ni\u00f1o event, the MP noted that Sri Lanka could face prolonged droughts, floods, food insecurity, shortages of drinking water, reduced hydroelectric power generation, adverse public health outcomes and economic disruption, requiring coordinated action by multiple ministries and government agencies.\n\nKarunanayake also sought to know whether the government had prepared and approved a National El Ni\u00f1o Preparedness and Response Plan, when it was approved, whether it had been made public and the key measures contained in the plan.\n\nHe further questioned which ministry or agency had been designated as the lead coordinating authority and what mechanisms were in place to coordinate the Department of Meteorology, Disaster Management Centre, the Ministries of Agriculture, Irrigation, Health, Water Supply, Power and Energy, the Mahaweli Authority and provincial administrations.\n\nThe MP also requested a sector-wise breakdown of funds allocated under the 2026 Budget for El Ni\u00f1o preparedness and response, including allocations for agriculture, irrigation, disaster management, drinking water, public health, electricity generation and emergency relief, together with any allocations expected to continue into 2027."
  },
