    "chunk_text": "Can Sri Lankan Police arrest you for not carrying an NIC?\n\nColombo, Aug. 5 (Daily Mirror) - A widespread belief among the public is that failing to carry a National Identity Card (NIC) in public is an offense that automatically leads to arrest. However, according to Attorney-at-Law Inspector of Police P. Amitha Abeysekara Silva from the Police Legal Division, simply not possessing an ID card is not an independent criminal offense under Sri Lankan law.\n\nUnder the Registration of Persons Act No. 32 of 1968 and the Police Ordinance, police officers hold the legal authority to question a person's identity whenever necessary for official duties, such as preventing a crime or conducting an investigation. While the lack of an ID on its own is not a crime, if an officer already has reasonable suspicion regarding an individual under specific circumstances, the inability to produce identification may reinforce that suspicion and provide legitimate grounds for arrest.\n\nPolice also said that under Section 33 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, an officer may arrest a person without a warrant if they refuse to disclose their name and residential address or intentionally provide false information when accused of or suspected of committing a non-cognizable offense.\n\nThey said that the purpose of such an arrest is solely to verify the individual's identity. Once their name and place of residence are verified, the police are required by law to release the individual on bail.\n\nFrequently Asked Questions: Carrying a National Identity Card in Sri Lanka\n\n1. Is it illegal to go out without carrying a National Identity Card in Sri Lanka?\n\nNo. Simply failing to carry a National Identity Card (NIC) in public is not an independent criminal offence under Sri Lankan law.\n\n2. Can the police arrest someone solely for not carrying an NIC?"
