    "md5": "e4369f5898ea69536f982dd5caee8cbe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. to continue investing in education despite economic challenges\n\nColombo, August 5 (Daily Mirror) - The Government will continue to prioritise investment in education despite Sri Lanka's economic challenges, Additional Secretary to the Prime Minister A.B.M. Ashraf said, stressing that the country's future depends on developing its human capital.\n\nSpeaking at a ceremony where 50 Lenovo laptops were distributed to outstanding G.C.E. Advanced Level students, Ashraf said Sri Lanka has never reduced spending on education, health and human development, even during periods of severe economic hardship.\n\n\"We may have faced financial difficulties and even bankruptcy, but we have never considered reducing investment in education. Instead, we have increased it because our future depends on our children,\" he said.\n\nAshraf said the Government's commitment to education would continue through a nationwide digital transformation programme aimed at modernising schools and improving access to technology.\n\nAs part of the initiative, all 10,000 schools in Sri Lanka are expected to be connected to the internet. He said schools would primarily be linked through fibre networks, while 4G, 5G and satellite technologies would be used in areas where fibre connectivity is not commercially viable.\n\nHe added that the Government also plans to upgrade local area networks, improve computer laboratories and ensure all secondary schools are digitally connected to meet the demands of modern education.\n\nAshraf said more than 1,500 secondary schools, mainly in remote and underserved areas, still lack computer laboratories. These schools will receive fully equipped computer labs, including computers, networking facilities and furniture, next year."
  },
