    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "Court seeks IRD clarification before ruling in national cricketers\u2019 tax dispute\n\nColombo, Aug. 5 (Daily Mirror) - The Court of Appeal today sought clarification from the Inland Revenue Department (IRD) on whether the recently enacted amendments to the Inland Revenue Act would affect the tax dispute involving Sri Lanka\u2019s national male and female cricket captains, before delivering its judgment.\n\nThe writ applications, filed by the national cricket captains, were taken up before a bench comprising President of the Court of Appeal, Justice Rohantha Abeysuriya PC, and Justice K. Priyantha Fernando.\n\nWhen the matter was called, the Court noted that Parliament had recently amended the Inland Revenue Act to specifically classify \u201csports persons\u201d as Independent Service Providers (ISPs), a category that does not attract Advance Personal Income Tax (APIT).\n\nThe bench sought clarification from Deputy Solicitor General Manohara Jayasinghe, appearing for the IRD, on whether the department would reconsider the decision being challenged in light of the legislative amendment.\n\nResponding to the Court, the Deputy Solicitor General maintained that the new amendment had no bearing on the present dispute. He argued that national cricketers are employees because they are engaged under contracts with Sri Lanka Cricket and submitted that the Court should proceed to deliver its judgment.\n\nAppearing for the two male national captains, Counsel Nishan Sydney Premathiratne referred to the relevant Parliamentary Hansard and argued that the amendment had been introduced specifically to address areas of uncertainty and dispute under the tax law.\n\nHe submitted that Parliament had now clearly categorised sports persons, including cricketers, as Independent Service Providers alongside professionals such as doctors, lawyers, architects, videographers and singers."
