    "chunk_size_bytes": 1702,
    "chunk_text": "He submitted that Parliament had now clearly categorised sports persons, including cricketers, as Independent Service Providers alongside professionals such as doctors, lawyers, architects, videographers and singers.\n\nHe contended that the latest amendment reflected Parliament\u2019s clear legislative intent that sports persons should not be treated as employees for tax purposes, but should instead remain subject to the withholding tax regime applicable to Independent Service Providers.\n\nThe petitioners also informed the Court that professional payments due to the players for the past seven months had been withheld as a result of the dispute, causing them significant hardship.\n\nThe Court informed the parties that although it had already prepared its judgment, it had postponed delivering the decision in order to obtain clarification from the IRD regarding the effect of the recent legislative amendment.\n\nThe bench granted both parties one week to file written clarification submissions and fixed the matter for judgment on August 31, 2026.\n\nCounsel Nishan Sydney Premathiratne appeared with Shenali Dias and Sidath Gajanayaka on the instructions of Gamindu Karunasena for the petitioners representing the male cricketers.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Dr. Harsha Cabral with Vikum Jayasinghe and Manith Dasanayake on the instructions of Gamindu Karunasena appeared for the petitioners representing the female cricketers.\n\nDeputy Solicitor General Manohara Jayasinghe appeared for the Attorney General representing the Inland Revenue Department.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Kuvera de Zoysa appeared with Sajana de Zoysa on the instructions of Sanjaya Fonseka, representing the former office bearers of SLC."
