    "md5": "c5dd2ffc94c6f9ec94374fadd26ae04c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "India calls for early Provincial Council polls, full implementation of Tamil rights\n\nColombo, Aug. 5 (Daily Mirror) - Provincial Council elections should be held at the earliest while constitutional provisions addressing the aspirations of the Tamil community should be fully implemented, Indian Foreign Secretary Vikram Misri said during his official visit to Sri Lanka on Wednesday.\n\nMisri also discussed issues related to fishermen, calling for the early release of detained Indian fishermen and boats while urging a humanitarian approach to resolving the matter.\n\nDuring the visit, India and Sri Lanka also agreed to advance negotiations on updating the India-Sri Lanka Free Trade Agreement (FTA) and welcomed the successful conclusion of negotiations on a bilateral Social Security Agreement, which is expected to be signed soon.\n\nOn his first standalone visit to Sri Lanka as Foreign Secretary, Misri called on President Anura Kumara Disanayake, Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya, Foreign Minister Vijitha Herath and Foreign Secretary Aruni Ranaraja. He also met Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa and held separate discussions with leaders representing the Sri Lankan Tamil and Indian Origin Tamil (IOT) communities.\n\nThe two sides reviewed progress on a wide range of bilateral projects and reaffirmed India's commitment to strengthening ties under its \"Neighbourhood First\" policy and Vision MAHASAGAR.\n\nThey expressed satisfaction over the implementation of reconstruction initiatives funded under India's USD 450 million assistance package announced following Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nAmong the key developments was the exchange of agreements for two Indian Rupee-denominated Lines of Credit worth USD 350 million, including USD 250 million for railway projects and USD 100 million for livestock and other priority sectors.\n\nBoth countries also confirmed that reconstruction of damaged houses and permanent bridges under a USD 100 million grant will begin shortly."
