    "md5": "76ef0659d848681780b3fd7ae5fdafad",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1642,
    "chunk_text": "Animal Welfare Bill : A new ray of hope for voiceless beings\n\nThis Bill therefore brings a fresh ray of hope for voiceless animals\n\nThe passage of the long awaited Animal Welfare Bill marks a significant legislative milestone for Sri Lanka. For over a century, animal rights was\u00a0 treated almost as an afterthought, governed by the obsolete Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Ordinance of 1907. Except for a handful of compassionate individuals who have been voicing for the rights of all animals, irrespective of whether it is an ailing stray dog on a street or an iconic elephant injured by consuming hakka patas, many others have chosen to cast a blind eye towards animal welfare.\n\nThis is evident when looking at the number of incidents of animal cruelty being reported on a daily basis. A recent video going viral on social media shows how a man and a woman obstructed an individual who usually bring food and water to feed stray dogs in his area. The woman is seen throwing away the food and water given to the dogs in protest while the man was seen chasing two dogs with sticks. Several stray dogs were also shot dead over the past few months, indicating deteriorating levels of compassion towards animals.\n\nAccording to the 1907 Ordinance, the maximum fine for a first-time animal cruelty or neglect offence was a paltry 100 rupees, alongside a possible prison term of up to three months. But now, any individual who is found guilty of animal cruelty would be charged Rs. 250,000 with a jail term of two years. Any individual who kills an animal in a cruel manner would be liable to a fine of Rs. 500,000 and a jail term of three years."
  },
