    "md5": "b5180b8930f3bf43f004468cf1ce9312",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1713,
    "chunk_text": "According to the 1907 Ordinance, the maximum fine for a first-time animal cruelty or neglect offence was a paltry 100 rupees, alongside a possible prison term of up to three months. But now, any individual who is found guilty of animal cruelty would be charged Rs. 250,000 with a jail term of two years. Any individual who kills an animal in a cruel manner would be liable to a fine of Rs. 500,000 and a jail term of three years.\n\nAfter a long delay, the then cabinet approved the Animal Welfare Bill in January 2022. Even though the draft Bill was gazetted and presented to Parliament, it was later referred to the Sectoral Oversight Committee on Food and Agriculture which recommended key adjustments.\n\nThe 2006 draft proposed to include the concept of \u2018duty of care\u2019 towards animals in the statute. Duty of care imposes a positive legal obligation on owners to actively ensure that the welfare needs of the animal under their care are met. Countries such as UK and Australia have established a legal duty of care for people in charge of animals grounded in the Five Freedoms of animal welfare. These include freedom from hunger and thirst, freedom from discomfort, freedom from pain, injury and disease, freedom to express normal behaviour and freedom from fear and distress.\n\nHowever, the Bill has now come under scrutiny for its provisions on chaining animals for instance. This concerns owners of domesticated elephants that have been chained for long years. Political groups have questioned provisions that restrict elephants to carry heavy loads. They argue that by indicating \u2018heavy loads\u2019, it could be interpreted in a way that restricts elephants from carrying the relics casket at processions."
