    "num": "dailymirrorlk-69e41fd2",
    "date_str": "2026-08-05",
    "description": "Are Easter Sunday victims happy with the death sentences?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-Easter-Sunday-victims-happy-with-the-death-sentences/172-347520",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785953351.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-69e41fd2-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c8fe1e4a013a3b79ec91ce2f3e09590f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "Are Easter Sunday victims happy with the death sentences?\n\nFormer Defence Ministry secretary Hemasiri Fernando and former police inspector-general Pujith Jayasundara were sentenced to death by the Colombo High Court in a 2-1 decision over the Easter Sunday terrorist attacks of 2019\n\nPeople were dumbfounded by the sentence not because of their approval or disapproval of it, but the court\u2019s verdict came as a bolt from the blue on them\n\nBy that time, a theory that accused the Rajapaksas of planning and executing the suicide bombings was developing and the leaders of the Catholic Community too were seemed to have been carried away by it\n\nLast Friday (July 31), Sri Lankans were thunderstruck with the news that former Defence Ministry secretary Hemasiri Fernando and former police inspector-general Pujith Jayasundara were sentenced to death by the Colombo High Court in a 2-1 decision over the Easter Sunday terrorist attacks of 2019.\n\nFernando and Jayasundara had separately been indicted by the Attorney General on charges including criminal neglect of duty by failing to take action to prevent the Easter Sunday terrorist attacks, despite receiving prior intelligence information that such attacks were imminent.\n\nPeople were dumbfounded by the sentence not because of their approval or disapproval of it, but the court\u2019s verdict came as a bolt from the blue on them. Even the journalist who cover the courts had failed to sense such a serious decision by the three-judge bench beforehand.\n\nMany people had expressed their views in mainstream media as well as on\u00a0 social media on it. Some people, while approving the judgement and the punishment, seemed to have bewildered by the fact that others who were ordered to pay compensation for the same offence \u2013 negligence and dereliction of duty \u2013 by the Supreme Court in 2023 had not been indicted in this case."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-69e41fd2",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-69e41fd2",
    "date_str": "2026-08-05",
    "description": "Are Easter Sunday victims happy with the death sentences?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-Easter-Sunday-victims-happy-with-the-death-sentences/172-347520",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785953351.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-69e41fd2-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "90bd40ed4a854c4a54245a07e77f24ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1955,
    "chunk_text": "Many people had expressed their views in mainstream media as well as on\u00a0 social media on it. Some people, while approving the judgement and the punishment, seemed to have bewildered by the fact that others who were ordered to pay compensation for the same offence \u2013 negligence and dereliction of duty \u2013 by the Supreme Court in 2023 had not been indicted in this case.\n\nUnderlining a legal point, the former Justice Minister Mohamed Ali Sabry, in a Facebook post, had stated \u201cNegligence, however serious, and dereliction of duty, however catastrophic their consequences, do not ordinarily constitute the mental element required for offences such as murder or attempted murder. A failure to act, however culpable, is not the same as an intention to kill or an intention to facilitate murder.\u201d\n\nHowever, an important segment of people \u2013 the Christian community that was made victim directly by the terrorist attacks \u2013 maintained a deafening silence over the sentence of corporal punishment on the two former high officials responsible for the public security. They - especially the Catholic community led by Cardinal Malcolm Ranjith that has been in the forefront in demanding justice for the victims of the Easter Sunday carnage \u2013 did not seem to be moved by the court\u2019s decision.\n\nThey neither issued any statement,\u00a0 nor did any journalist think it fit to seek their stance on it. In a way, one could argue that a community\u2014led by a leader who asked his followers, in the wake of the crime, to forgive the terrorists who brutally killed hundreds of men, women, and children\u2014cannot be happy with the death sentence imposed on people who failed to protect those innocent lives.\n\nIn fact, the victim community \u2013 which was mainly concerned about who the culprits behind the terrorist attacks were and what prompted them to kill nearly 300 innocent people \u2013 was sent from pillar to post by\u00a0 successive governments, giving false hopes."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
