    "description": "Are Easter Sunday victims happy with the death sentences?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-Easter-Sunday-victims-happy-with-the-death-sentences/172-347520",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785953351.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-05-dailymirrorlk-69e41fd2-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fe3e87febec1029145c019798896339b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "Rajapaksa and his party, the Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) assumed office in November 2019, and Cardinal Ranjith expressed satisfaction in the new government\u2019s actions towards revealing the mastermind of the attacks. Prominent politicians such as former Trade Minister Rishard Bathiudeen and his brother Riyad were arrested in connection with the terrorist attacks, on purported strong evidence against them.\n\nBy that time, a theory that accused the Rajapaksas of planning and executing the suicide bombings was developing and the leaders of the Catholic Community too were seemed to have been carried away by it. Towards the end of\u00a0 the Gotabaya Rajapaksa government, which was marked by the famous \u2018Aragalaya, \u201d they\u00a0 totally lost faith in the government and that continued during the Ranil Wickremesinghe administration that was formed with the support of the SLPP. Hence, they rejected the two committees that was appointed by Wickremesinghe to look into the intelligence failures in the run up to the terrorist attacks.\n\nIn the lead up to the last Presidential election, when the victory of the National People Power (NPP) had almost been sealed, the victim community was on the same wavelength with the NPP, with regard to the mastermind of the Easter Sunday attacks. However, they were again frustrated with the NPP administration as well, due to the delay in a breakthrough in investigations, until Suresh Sallay, the former head of State Intelligence Service (SIS), was arrested on February 25, this year.\n\nIt is against this backdrop that the Cardinal filed an intervening petition in the Court of Appeal, urging the dismissal of a writ petition by Suresh Sallay, challenging his detention. Therefore, the death sentences handed to Pujith Jayasundara and Hemasiri Fernando seem to have little real impact on the victim community."
  },
