    "md5": "67417ba6d97dde50f7d490e583871f00",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan by passport: Foreigner at uni gate: Betrayal behind Sri Lanka\u2019s expatriate  higher  education crisis\n\nPresidents of the Sri Lankan expatriate community in Qatar (from right) Sri Lanka Business and Professional Council Dr. Mohamed Rislan, Sri Lankan Cordinaton Committee Dulip Liyanage and Sri Lankan Community Benevolent Forum Ajith Ishantha\u00a0 hand over the proposal to Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya\n\nAre fathers who keep their children in Sri Lanka for higher education doing it at the unbearable cost of sacricing their futures?\n\nA reform submitted to the Prime Minister requests a clearly defined sub category for Sri Lankan expatriate children seeking uni entry\n\nUGC\u2019s Admission Handbook states that only 0.5% of the total intake is reserved, fee-free, for children of diplomatic personnel\n\nMost Sri Lankan state officials cannot afford to send their children to study in Sri Lankan universities\n\nAn engineer who has spent over twenty years pouring concrete and steel into buildings across the Gulf maintains that the hardest structure he never managed to build was a future for both his daughters. \u201cMy elder daughter studied at a Sri Lankan university. I paid fees so high they nearly broke me, month after month, just to keep her there. When it was my younger daughter\u2019s turn, I sat down and did the maths, and I knew I could not survive it twice. I sent her to Bangladesh instead.\u201d He pauses before adding the line that says everything: \u201cI gave one daughter her country. I could not afford to give the other the same gift.\u201d\n\n4.5 percent is set aside for foreign-quota university admissions, a category that also includes SAARC applicants, other foreign nationals, and, remarkably, Sri Lankan citizens living abroad. Our children, who possess Sri Lankan passports and have never renounced their nationality, are folded into this foreign quota and charged the very same fees as applicants with no Sri Lankan citizenship"
  },
