    "md5": "684fc3040ffc0d690ec3132882445800",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "4.5 percent is set aside for foreign-quota university admissions, a category that also includes SAARC applicants, other foreign nationals, and, remarkably, Sri Lankan citizens living abroad. Our children, who possess Sri Lankan passports and have never renounced their nationality, are folded into this foreign quota and charged the very same fees as applicants with no Sri Lankan citizenship\n\nAnother father did keep both his daughters studying in Sri Lanka, a rare achievement under the current system, but the cost was his life\u2019s work. \u201cI lost everything I had saved,\u201d he says simply. \u201cThe business I dreamed of starting when I came home no longer exists. Only on paper.\u201d Two daughters graduated. One dream did not survive.\n\nPerhaps the cruelest story belongs to a senior officer at a Sri Lankan diplomatic mission abroad, a man whose entire career has been built on representing his country\u2019s interests to the world. His own two daughters are studying in Estern Europe at present not because he wanted them a continent away from their grandparents, their language, their roots, but because even he, an official of the Sri Lankan state itself, could not afford to send them to a Sri Lankan university. \u201cI represent Sri Lanka every day of my working life,\u201d he says. \u201cBut when it came to my own children\u2019s education, Sri Lanka would not have me.\u201d\n\nFour families. Four professions. One devastating pattern, repeating itself in Doha, Kuwait, Riyadh and every international city where Sri Lankans work: parents who have given their country decades of labour and billions of dollars in remittances, only to find that when it is their own child\u2019s turn to walk through a Sri Lankan university gate, that gate treats them as strange foreigners."
  },
