    "md5": "232628e1f78d5c15c26c1de6071261a4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1402,
    "chunk_text": "Four families. Four professions. One devastating pattern, repeating itself in Doha, Kuwait, Riyadh and every international city where Sri Lankans work: parents who have given their country decades of labour and billions of dollars in remittances, only to find that when it is their own child\u2019s turn to walk through a Sri Lankan university gate, that gate treats them as strange foreigners.\n\nWorse still, Sri Lanka has made itself more expensive for its own citizens. A medical degree that costs an expatriate family nearly US$ 15,000 a year at home can be pursued for a fraction of that in Georgia, Malaysia, India, Armenia or Bangladesh. Families are not abandoning Sri Lanka by choice. Sri Lanka\u2019s own policy is pushing them out the door.\n\nThe reform submitted to Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya asks for something precise, and deliberately modest: a clearly defined sub category for Sri Lankan expatriate children, carved out from within the existing 4.5 percent foreign quota, not a single seat beyond it. Of that 4.5 percent, a minimum of three percentage points would be ring fenced for our children, with clear published eligibility rules, fees set at roughly a quarter of the current international rate, fee-free places for the highest achievers, guaranteed hostel accommodation for children arriving without parents, and honest information reaching families through our embassies abroad."
  },
