    "md5": "6b782a4e23a8a0f2e581a73e3a53d3ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "The reform submitted to Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya asks for something precise, and deliberately modest: a clearly defined sub category for Sri Lankan expatriate children, carved out from within the existing 4.5 percent foreign quota, not a single seat beyond it. Of that 4.5 percent, a minimum of three percentage points would be ring fenced for our children, with clear published eligibility rules, fees set at roughly a quarter of the current international rate, fee-free places for the highest achievers, guaranteed hostel accommodation for children arriving without parents, and honest information reaching families through our embassies abroad.\n\nIn conversations with policymakers, one mistaken fear keeps surfacing: that any new quota for expatriate children must come at the expense of local students, or strain university resources already stretched thin. This fear misunderstands the arithmetic entirely. We are not asking to expand the foreign quota from 4.5 percent to anything higher we are asking that three of those 4.5 percentage points, already set aside for non-local applicants, go to Sri Lankan citizens first, before any other foreign national. If a university currently admits, say, 45 foreign quota students out of a 1,000-seat intake, this reform does not ask for a 46th seat. It asks that roughly 30 of those same 45 seats go to Sri Lankan passport holders whose parents built this economy from abroad, rather than to applicants with no citizenship connection to Sri Lanka at all. Where policymakers should genuinely focus their energy is not on resisting this reallocation, but on the separate, larger question of whether university capacity overall needs strengthening more lecture halls, more laboratories, more academic staff\u00a0 an investment this very reform helps part-fund, since fee-paying expatriate seats generate revenue no local student\u2019s seat does."
  },
