    "chunk_text": "In conversations with policymakers, one mistaken fear keeps surfacing: that any new quota for expatriate children must come at the expense of local students, or strain university resources already stretched thin. This fear misunderstands the arithmetic entirely. We are not asking to expand the foreign quota from 4.5 percent to anything higher we are asking that three of those 4.5 percentage points, already set aside for non-local applicants, go to Sri Lankan citizens first, before any other foreign national. If a university currently admits, say, 45 foreign quota students out of a 1,000-seat intake, this reform does not ask for a 46th seat. It asks that roughly 30 of those same 45 seats go to Sri Lankan passport holders whose parents built this economy from abroad, rather than to applicants with no citizenship connection to Sri Lanka at all. Where policymakers should genuinely focus their energy is not on resisting this reallocation, but on the separate, larger question of whether university capacity overall needs strengthening more lecture halls, more laboratories, more academic staff\u00a0 an investment this very reform helps part-fund, since fee-paying expatriate seats generate revenue no local student\u2019s seat does.\n\nFor the nation, the gains run deep. Every family that no longer sends its savings to Tbilisi, Dhaka or Yerevan instead spends that money inside Sri Lanka on accommodation, on daily life, on family visits keeping hard earned foreign exchange circulating at home. Every expatriate child educated here grows up with genuine roots in Sri Lanka, not a fading, sentimental attachment to a homeland that once turned them away. These are tomorrow\u2019s doctors, engineers, accountants and entrepreneurs the ones most likely to return, to invest, to employ."
