    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "For the nation, the gains run deep. Every family that no longer sends its savings to Tbilisi, Dhaka or Yerevan instead spends that money inside Sri Lanka on accommodation, on daily life, on family visits keeping hard earned foreign exchange circulating at home. Every expatriate child educated here grows up with genuine roots in Sri Lanka, not a fading, sentimental attachment to a homeland that once turned them away. These are tomorrow\u2019s doctors, engineers, accountants and entrepreneurs the ones most likely to return, to invest, to employ.\n\nAn end to twenty-five years of hard earned savings vanishing into fees that should never have been so high for a Sri Lankan child in the first place.\n\nThe true value of this reform is best understood not in a single admission year, but across a generation. Today, Sri Lanka\u2019s expatriate community represents one of the country\u2019s greatest untapped resources. Many Sri Lankans living abroad have gained international exposure, advanced technical knowledge, professional experience and global networks built over decades of hard work in demanding foreign environments. Their children are growing up in multicultural settings, fluent in more than one system of education, with skills that can directly benefit Sri Lanka in fields such as technology, medicine, engineering, research, entrepreneurship and international business. A student who completes higher education in Sri Lanka, rather than abroad, may eventually become an entrepreneur, an investor, a researcher or a professional contributing directly to national development not from a distance, but from within. A fair admission pathway does more than fill a lecture hall: it encourages more expatriate families to maintain stronger educational and emotional links with Sri Lanka, generation after generation, rather than watching that link quietly fade."
