    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "The true value of this reform is best understood not in a single admission year, but across a generation. Today, Sri Lanka\u2019s expatriate community represents one of the country\u2019s greatest untapped resources. Many Sri Lankans living abroad have gained international exposure, advanced technical knowledge, professional experience and global networks built over decades of hard work in demanding foreign environments. Their children are growing up in multicultural settings, fluent in more than one system of education, with skills that can directly benefit Sri Lanka in fields such as technology, medicine, engineering, research, entrepreneurship and international business. A student who completes higher education in Sri Lanka, rather than abroad, may eventually become an entrepreneur, an investor, a researcher or a professional contributing directly to national development not from a distance, but from within. A fair admission pathway does more than fill a lecture hall: it encourages more expatriate families to maintain stronger educational and emotional links with Sri Lanka, generation after generation, rather than watching that link quietly fade.\n\nThis proposal backed by verified data and seven detailed reform measures has been formally handed over to the Prime Minister, and sent to the Offices of President, Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Ministry of Education and the Chairman of the University Grants Commission.\n\nBehind every clause of this document stands a real family: an engineer who could only afford to bring one daughter home to study. A surveyor who spent a lifetime of savings so his son could sit in a Sri Lankan lecture hall. A senior officer at diplomatic mission who serves his country by day and grieves, quietly, that his own daughters cannot study in it. A father who gave everything so both his girls could stay."
