    "md5": "5954db3fb95b998f3a42ee08bd1e640f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1116,
    "chunk_text": "Behind every clause of this document stands a real family: an engineer who could only afford to bring one daughter home to study. A surveyor who spent a lifetime of savings so his son could sit in a Sri Lankan lecture hall. A senior officer at diplomatic mission who serves his country by day and grieves, quietly, that his own daughters cannot study in it. A father who gave everything so both his girls could stay.\n\nA child holding a Sri Lankan passport, born to Sri Lankan parents and connected to Sri Lankan culture should not feel like a foreigner when seeking higher education in Sri Lanka. Correcting this policy gap is not about giving someone an advantage. It is about ensuring fairness. It is about protecting the rights of citizens. And ultimately, it is about strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s future by bringing its global community closer not pushing it away. The writer is President of the Sri Lankan Business & Professional Council (SLBPC), Qatar, and one of the signatories to the formal proposal submitted to the President and Prime Minister of Sri Lanka on behalf of the Sri Lankan expatriate community."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-06-dailyftlk-97d934b7",
    "num": "dailyftlk-97d934b7",
    "date_str": "2026-08-06",
    "description": "What if Sri Lanka\u2019s next economic breakthrough comes from you?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/What-if-Sri-Lanka-s-next-economic-breakthrough-comes-from-you/14-795646",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785973270.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-06-dailyftlk-97d934b7-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0f3f2ce319011eb8301df6bc343144e0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "What if Sri Lanka\u2019s next economic breakthrough comes from you?\n\nFTZMA Chairman Dhammika Fernando (left) presenting the proposal to BOI Director General Dr. Sulakshana Jayawardena as Chairman\u00a0Duminda Hulangamuwa looks on\n\nThe Window would replace luck with a process for which someone is responsible. It would not matter who the proposer is, who they know, or what letterhead they write on. It would matter whether the idea can strengthen the economy, whether someone credible is ready to implement it, and whether the obstacle in its way is one the Government can remove\n\nFTZMA hands BOI a practical proposal to turn promising ideas into action, strengthen foreign exchange earnings and help ensure that the queues of 2022 never return\n\nThe FTZMA proposes a national window where anyone, a professional, a factory, a university, a Sri Lankan abroad, can propose an idea that helps the country earn from the world. The earnings are what keep fuel in the pumps, gas in the kitchen and medicine on the shelves for\u00a0everyone, and what will repay the country's debts.\n\nThe Free Trade Zone Manufacturers' Association of Sri Lanka (FTZMA) officially handed over to the Chairman of the Board of Investment, Duminda Hulangamuwa, and the Director General, Dr. Sulakshana Jayawardena, a detailed proposal to establish a National Facilitation and Activation Window, a standing channel through which anyone can bring forward an idea capable of strengthening Sri Lanka's economy, above all ideas that create new foreign exchange inflows for the country.\n\nThe proposal, prepared and unanimously endorsed by the association's Executive Committee with the backing of its membership, sets out how the Window could be opened quickly, run by a small team of existing officers, and operated at almost no cost to the Treasury. The investment in every activated case would be private. What the State provides is the one thing only the State can provide, a cleared path."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-06-dailyftlk-97d934b7",
    "num": "dailyftlk-97d934b7",
    "date_str": "2026-08-06",
    "description": "What if Sri Lanka\u2019s next economic breakthrough comes from you?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/What-if-Sri-Lanka-s-next-economic-breakthrough-comes-from-you/14-795646",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1785973270.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-06-dailyftlk-97d934b7-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8c15c91cba28707fc5f75e8c213e51f1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "The proposal, prepared and unanimously endorsed by the association's Executive Committee with the backing of its membership, sets out how the Window could be opened quickly, run by a small team of existing officers, and operated at almost no cost to the Treasury. The investment in every activated case would be private. What the State provides is the one thing only the State can provide, a cleared path.\n\nAlthough the proposal has been placed with the BOI, the Window it describes is designed as a truly national door. Any Sri Lankan or foreign investor, business, professional, university, industry body or member of the diaspora could walk through it with an idea that can earn or save foreign exchange, or otherwise make the economy stronger and more resilient.\n\nIdeas that fall within the BOI's own authority would move directly under its facilitation. Ideas that belong to another institution would not be turned away. The Window would refer them to the authority legally responsible, and the BOI would remain the coordinator, following each case until the person who brought the idea receives a clear answer. No idea would die simply because its owner knocked on the wrong door. Under this design, there is no wrong door.\n\nThe Window is equally open to businesses already operating in Sri Lanka. An investor who is earning foreign exchange today, but is held back by an unclear rule, a stalled approval, a locked facility or a procedure no office will confirm in writing, could bring that exact block forward, together with the way out they can already see, and ask for the specific facilitation that would release more earnings. The association sees every small discouraging rule as a national cost, because every blocked path quietly redirects investment to another country, and every cleared path invites it back."
