    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "The Window is equally open to businesses already operating in Sri Lanka. An investor who is earning foreign exchange today, but is held back by an unclear rule, a stalled approval, a locked facility or a procedure no office will confirm in writing, could bring that exact block forward, together with the way out they can already see, and ask for the specific facilitation that would release more earnings. The association sees every small discouraging rule as a national cost, because every blocked path quietly redirects investment to another country, and every cleared path invites it back.\n\nThe reasoning is simple. The person who lives with a problem is usually also the person who knows the practical way out of it, and that knowledge is exactly what the Window asks to receive. Every submission must therefore arrive as a complete proposal: the activity that will earn or save foreign exchange, the person or organisation ready to implement it, the private resources standing behind it, and the exact facilitation requested from Government. A submission that only describes what is wrong, without proposing what can be made to work and who will make it work, would be courteously directed to the appropriate channel and would not enter the Window at all.\n\nThe Window's question to the country is not what is troubling you. It is what can you make work, and what one decision do you need to begin.\n\n\"The Government does not have to build the activity. It has to open the path, and private energy does the rest,\" said FTZMA Chairman Dhammika Fernando at the handover.\n\nThe heart of the proposal is speed with accountability. Every complete submission would receive a named BOI case officer and a clear preliminary decision within the least possible number of working days, and in every case within a month. The month is the outer limit, not the pace. A straightforward case could receive its answer within days, even within a single day."
