    "chunk_text": "Every reader in Colombo has, in fact, already seen this principle at work. For decades the marshy lands around the city were places people avoided. Then the State did one simple thing, it made them accessible. Walking tracks were laid, lighting was added, safe entry was provided. Diyatha Uyana, Weras Ganga Park, Beddagana, Diyasaru and the walkways around the lakes are the result, full from early morning, and in 2018 Colombo became the first capital city accredited as an International Wetland City under the Ramsar Convention. Nobody instructed the public to walk. The desire was always there, blocked only by mud. The State removed the mud, paved the way, and the people did the rest. Foreign exchange behaves exactly the same way.\n\nThe FTZMA represents export manufacturers who have operated in Sri Lanka's free trade zones for decades, earning foreign exchange every working day and employing hundreds of thousands of Sri Lankans directly and through their supply chains. These are enterprises that have kept their capital, their factories and their faith in this country through its hardest years. When an association of such investors studies a mechanism, endorses it unanimously and places it before the State, it speaks for people who have backed Sri Lanka with their own money and intend to keep doing so.\n\n\"This proposal did not come from frustration. It came from confidence,\" Fernando said. \"Our members have watched the country stabilise after its most painful crisis. We believe this recovery has created a short and precious window in which Sri Lanka can build the earning capacity it has lacked for nearly half a century, and we decided the most useful thing we could do was to put a complete, workable mechanism on the table of the institution best placed to run it.\""
