    "chunk_size_bytes": 1956,
    "chunk_text": "Consumers rarely purchase luxury products based solely on functional characteristics.\n\nA Kashmir sapphire commands exceptional value because of its origin.\n\nA Burmese ruby derives much of its reputation from Myanmar's history.\n\nHowever, the broader international recognition of Sri Lanka itself has yet to fully translate into higher commercial value across the gemstone industry.\n\nThis is precisely where nation branding and industry branding intersect.\n\nThey may then be cut, branded, designed into jewellery, marketed and eventually sold under internationally recognised luxury brands in New York, Geneva, Hong Kong, London or Dubai.\n\nAlthough the gemstone remains Sri Lankan, much of the value generated through branding and marketing is created elsewhere.\n\nAs a result, Sri Lanka frequently exports the raw beauty while importing little of the premium associated with the finished luxury experience.\n\nOur objective should be to export Brand Sri Lanka through every gemstone we sell.\n\nOur next challenge is to ensure that every sapphire, spinel, cat's eye and ruby leaving our shores also carries something even more valuable\u2014the reputation of Sri Lanka itself. If we succeed, we will not merely export gemstones. We will export trust. We will export heritage. We will export craftsmanship. And ultimately, we will strengthen Brand Sri Lanka\u2014one gemstone at a time\n\nToday's luxury consumer is fundamentally different from that of previous generations.\n\nIncreasingly, buyers ask questions beyond colour, clarity and carat weight.\n\nFortunately, Sri Lanka is well positioned to answer these questions.\n\nOur traditional mining heritage, experienced craftsmen and internationally respected gemological expertise provide an excellent foundation for building global trust.\n\nHowever, trust must be communicated consistently through certification, traceability and compelling storytelling.\n\nPositioning Sri Lanka as global home of natural coloured gemstones"
  },
