    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6fc51bb5fbd67c9ff8ec8d13569aef61",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cTo date, not a single drop of water has been supplied to the Northern and Eastern Provinces through the Mahaweli Authority. Yet, water has been provided under the Mahaweli Scheme to the districts of Polonnaruwa and Anuradhapura. This reflects a discriminatory approach towards the people of the North and East.\n\nHe alleged that the Mahaweli Authority\u2019s objectives had moved beyond water management.\n\n\u201cThis raises a fundamental concern that the Mahaweli Authority\u2019s primary objective has not been equitable water distribution, but rather the establishment of settlements that alter the ethnic composition of these areas.\u201d\n\nThe ITAK MP also raised concerns regarding the Mayilathamadu\u2013Mathavanai area, where livestock farmers have been engaged in disputes over access to grazing lands.\n\n\u201cThe Minister further stated that there is no place officially recorded as \u2018Mayilathamadu.\u2019 However, only this morning, cattle were reportedly killed in the Mayilathamadu\u2013Mathavanai area. Furthermore, illegal settlements continue to be established on lands belonging to the Mahaweli Authority.\n\nAs a result, livestock farmers and dairy farmers in the area are increasingly being denied access to their traditional grazing lands, placing their livelihoods at serious risk.\u201d\n\nRasamanickam said the Government had previously indicated that Mathavanai would be declared a designated grazing reserve but that no progress had been made.\n\n\u201cThe Government had previously stated that Mathavanai would be declared a designated grazing reserve through a Gazette notification. However, the Minister\u2019s response makes it clear that there has been no progress towards fulfilling that commitment. Indeed, the Minister confirmed that even the survey work for the area has yet to be completed.\u201d\n\nHe warned that continued occupation of Mahaweli Authority lands could increase tensions between communities."
  },
