    "chunk_text": "Are learner drivers barred from using expressways?\n\nColombo, Aug. 6 (Daily Mirror) - Vehicles displaying \u2018L\u2019 (Learner) plates and those driven by learner permit holders are not permitted to enter expressways, according to Police.\n\nA learner\u2019s permit is issued only for driving practice and does not authorise the holder to operate a vehicle on expressways, where a valid full driving licence is mandatory.\n\nPolice clarified that even if a fully licensed driver is seated alongside the learner, a vehicle displaying \u2018L\u2019 plates cannot be driven on an expressway.\n\n\"Vehicles driven by learner permit holders will be denied access at expressway entry points. If a learner-driven vehicle enters an expressway in violation of the law, the driver may face legal action, including fines or prosecution,\" Police said.\n\nPolice also warned that insurance claims arising from accidents involving vehicles being operated unlawfully may be rejected.\n\nNo. A learner\u2019s permit is issued only for driving practice. A valid full driving licence is required to drive on an expressway.\n\nNo. Vehicles displaying \u2018L\u2019 plates are not permitted to enter expressways.\n\nCan a learner drive on an expressway if a fully licensed driver accompanies them?\n\nNo. The presence of a fully licensed driver does not allow a learner or an \u2018L\u2019 plate vehicle to use an expressway.\n\nVehicles driven by learner permit holders will be denied access.\n\nThe driver may face legal action, including fines or prosecution.\n\nYes. Police warned that insurers may reject claims arising from accidents involving vehicles operated unlawfully."
