    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fd4d66b7f378572b0b3dd045be939bdd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1847,
    "chunk_text": "The meeting also reviewed the progress of programmes implemented under the Rs. 1000 million capital allocation provided for the Government\u2019s \u201cA Nation United\u201d national operation aimed at eliminating the drug menace.\n\nThe meeting also reviewed the public awareness and rehabilitation programmes implemented by the Bureau of Rehabilitation, as well as the initiatives of the Special Task Force to strengthen intelligence gathering on drug trafficking and enhance facilities required for operations against drug-related activities.\n\nAttention was also drawn to the need for constructing permanent buildings for police stations, as the Government currently incurs significant annual expenditure on rented premises and other buildings used by police stations among the 608 police stations operating across the island.\n\nThe purchase of new vehicles for the Sri Lanka Police was also discussed, considering that a considerable number of existing police vehicles are over 20 years old, resulting in increased maintenance costs.\n\nProgress on the proposed construction of the new Police Headquarters building, development of infrastructure facilities at the Police Hospital, and construction of the proposed Mirihana Police Sports Complex under police welfare allocations were also reviewed. It was noted that construction work currently underway at the Weligama and Mullaitivu Police Stations is expected to be completed and opened within the next few months.\n\nThe meeting also focused on the Sri Lanka Police digitalisation programme, modernisation of police uniforms and measures to address health-related issues arising from the demanding duties of police officers. The President\u2019s attention was drawn to proposals submitted by the National Police Academy to prevent the increasing prevalence of non-communicable diseases among police personnel."
