    "md5": "9c3be700ec401d6a9110fd527ba5cb1d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka strengthens business confidence through commercial mediation\n\nCorporate leaders who signed the Corporate Mediation Pledge at the inaugural ceremony held at Port City Colombo\n\nSri Lanka has taken a significant step towards strengthening business confidence with the establishment of a modern framework for commercial mediation, creating a structured, efficient and internationally aligned mechanism for businesses to resolve disputes while protecting valuable commercial relationships.\n\nThe milestone was marked at the inaugural Corporate Mediation Pledge Ceremony organised by the International Alternative Dispute Resolution Center, Sri Lanka (IADRC) at the Sales Gallery, Port City Colombo, where twenty-five corporates committed to recognising mediation as an option for resolving disputes arising from their business engagements.\n\nThe initiative follows the enactment of the Mediation (Civil and Commercial Disputes) Act, No. 13 of 2026, which came into operation on June 30, 2026. The legislation represents an important policy milestone by recognising mediation as a mainstream dispute resolution method for civil and commercial disputes, establishing standards governing the mediation process, defining the responsibilities of mediators and mediation service providers, and providing a framework for the recognition and enforcement of mediated settlement agreements.\n\nFor businesses, the introduction of this framework creates a new pathway to resolve commercial disputes in a manner that is confidential, collaborative and focused on achieving practical outcomes. Unlike traditional dispute resolution processes that can often result in prolonged proceedings, mediation enables parties to engage directly, explore mutually acceptable solutions and preserve important commercial relationships that may otherwise be affected by conflict."
  },
  {
