    "md5": "cf749b9d46b15b1ab94ab1b645dec7c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "Hela Apparel files for winding up after exhausting restructuring options\n\nHela Apparel Holdings PLC yesterday disclosed its move to wind up the company through the courts after its Board concluded that the Group is unable to pay its debts due to mounting liabilities and continuing liquidity constraints. This is despite pursuing debt restructuring and strategic investment options to preserve the business.\n\nIn a disclosure to the Colombo Stock Exchange, the company said its Board, having reviewed the financial position of the company and its subsidiaries, including their realisable assets, liabilities, liquidity levels, indebtedness, operating performance, expected cash flows and creditor obligations, including inter-company guarantees given to creditors, concluded that Hela Apparel Holdings PLC and its subsidiaries, Hela Clothing (Private) Limited and Foundation Garments (Private) Limited, are not able to pay their debts and are therefore unable to continue their businesses.\n\nAccordingly, the Board resolved on Aug. 4 to seek a court-supervised winding up of the company under the provisions of the Companies Act No. 7 of 2007. An application was filed before the Commercial High Court of the Western Province (Exercising Civil Jurisdiction) in Colombo on Aug. 5.\n\nHela Clothing (Private) Limited and Foundation Garments (Private) Limited have also filed applications before the same court on Aug. 5 to be wound up, following decisions by the respective boards of the two subsidiaries."
  },
