    "md5": "eb4acca2ddb5b989b705740d961c941c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1965,
    "chunk_text": "The longer the strait remains closed, the stronger Iran\u2019s bargaining power will be and the deeper the political crisis into which the Donald Trump administration will be plunged\u2014with a setback in the midterm election staring him in the face. If Democrats gain control of Congress, Trump will be unable to pass the national budget without conceding to their demands. This reality also spells disaster for the Republican Party in the 2028 presidential race. The pressure is on Trump to end the unpopular war and bring oil prices down, offering relief to the American middle class, who make up the bulk of the voting population. Oil was trading at US$ 80 yesterday as Iran and Oman held talks.\n\nThe United States remains a far stronger military power than Iran, but in asymmetrical warfare it is not armaments alone that decide the fate of a war or a nation\u2019s power\u2014it is political will to resist. If political will could be quantified, Iran would stand far ahead of its adversaries. Its determination to resist aggression is rooted in two factors: first, Shiite Islamic principles that emphasise sacrifice and perseverance for a better life in the hereafter; second, nationalism nurtured by a glorious civilisation with at least five millennia of history.\n\nAn Iranian surrender is therefore unlikely, even if the mercurial Trump, in desperation or sudden anger, were to order a nuclear strike on Iran.\n\nBut Iran\u2019s advantage in political will for perseverance does not mean it can afford to be overconfident. Such a foolhardy stance can misfire. Iran is seen as stretching beyond the breaking point its advantage over the Strait of Hormuz, its missile reach, and its proxy forces\u2014especially the Houthis in Yemen. The Houthis are ratcheting up pressure with attacks on Saudi-linked ships and threats to close the Bab al-Mandeb waterway. Iran\u2019s position is also bolstered by reports that the US faces a munitions shortage, though Trump denies such reports."
  },
