    "chunk_size_bytes": 1736,
    "chunk_text": "But Iran\u2019s advantage in political will for perseverance does not mean it can afford to be overconfident. Such a foolhardy stance can misfire. Iran is seen as stretching beyond the breaking point its advantage over the Strait of Hormuz, its missile reach, and its proxy forces\u2014especially the Houthis in Yemen. The Houthis are ratcheting up pressure with attacks on Saudi-linked ships and threats to close the Bab al-Mandeb waterway. Iran\u2019s position is also bolstered by reports that the US faces a munitions shortage, though Trump denies such reports.\n\nThis is why peace talks are strategically imperative, but weaponising them is unacceptable. Iran entered peace talks\u2014not once, but twice\u2014in good faith, only to be stabbed in the back by the US, raising questions about American trustworthiness.\n\nIs the US a nation of its word? Lies and deception have long been part of its war diplomacy and visible not only in Trump\u2019s nuclear talks deceit on Iran but also in President George W. Bush\u2019s false claims about weapons of mass destruction before the 2003 Iraq invasion, and in multiple false flag operations, including the Gulf of Tonkin incident in 1964.\n\nIt is indeed praiseworthy that Iran is negotiating a deal through third parties with an untrustworthy power. But it has no choice, because perpetual war is not an option. Nor can it sustain for long the economic harm caused by the conflict. Of course, the longer the war continues, the happier the US and Israeli military\u2011industrial complex will be\u2014about which President Dwight D. Eisenhower warned in 1961. The arms dealers, also known as agents of death, were surely elated to learn that Trump is pushing for an unprecedented US$1.5 trillion defence expenditure."
