    "chunk_text": "It is indeed praiseworthy that Iran is negotiating a deal through third parties with an untrustworthy power. But it has no choice, because perpetual war is not an option. Nor can it sustain for long the economic harm caused by the conflict. Of course, the longer the war continues, the happier the US and Israeli military\u2011industrial complex will be\u2014about which President Dwight D. Eisenhower warned in 1961. The arms dealers, also known as agents of death, were surely elated to learn that Trump is pushing for an unprecedented US$1.5 trillion defence expenditure.\n\nThe Iran\u2011Oman deal requires US endorsement, though Tehran insists it is not an agreement with Washington, despite the Trump administration\u2019s efforts to portray it as such. The deal fills gaps left by the vaguely worded memorandum of understanding signed in mid\u2011June. That MoU, though intended to last 60 days, collapsed within weeks\u2014not only because it lacked a mechanism to resolve disputes but also because of the Trump administration\u2019s afterthoughts once a \u2018victorious\u2019 Iran began reaping benefits and flaunting its control of the strait under clause 5 of the MoU.\n\nIran\u2019s actions have raised questions about the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, which seeks to balance state sovereignty with the principle of innocent passage. Yet the new security realities in West Asia after the US\u2011Israeli war of aggression have prompted Iran to interpret UNCLOS provisions more in favour of sovereignty and control over territorial waters. Concerns are also mounting about the Houthis\u2019 attempt to levy charges on ships crossing the Bab al\u2011Mandeb.\n\nGiven the deals within deals and battlegrounds within battlegrounds, optimism is increasingly difficult. When hostilities persist, deals only postpone the next round of conflict."
  },
