    "md5": "92f0eeea177ccdca88bcdb8f6a03de59",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1781,
    "chunk_text": "Judiciary not to be adjusted according to the convenience of the day\n\nThe government is apparently determined to proceed with the constitutional amendment to extend the retirement age of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges despite objections from relevant quarters including the legal fraternity. It is a test of the government\u2019s politeness, judicial independence and its understanding of the delicate balance of power that underpins a democratic state.\n\nWhether the extension is by two years or more is not the central issue. The real concern is whether the Executive and Legislature should alter the rules governing the tenure of sitting judges at a time when the judiciary is expected to function as the final guardian of the Constitution. Even if the government\u2019s intentions are entirely benign, the perception created by such a move is one that Sri Lanka can ill afford.\n\nThe judiciary is one institution that should remain beyond political calculations. Unlike other sectors of public administration where retirement ages can be revised according to labour market needs or demographic trends, the superior judiciary occupies a unique constitutional position. Judges are not ordinary public servants. Their independence depends not only on security of tenure but also on public confidence that neither rewards nor extensions are used to influence judicial conduct.\n\nGovernments across the world have learned that attempts to change judicial tenure invariably attract suspicion. It is not because every such reform is unconstitutional,\u00a0 but because judicial independence depends as much on perception as on legal safeguards. Justice must not only be done; it must also be seen to be done. Likewise, judicial independence must not only exist but also be seen to exist."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-07-dailymirrorlk-fe46b38c",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-fe46b38c",
    "date_str": "2026-08-07",
    "description": "Judiciary not to be adjusted according to the convenience of the day",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Judiciary-not-to-be-adjusted-according-to-the-convenience-of-the-day/172-347620",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786058648.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-07-dailymirrorlk-fe46b38c-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9c7f277992f4522058cd1cb67997fdfc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1896,
    "chunk_text": "Governments across the world have learned that attempts to change judicial tenure invariably attract suspicion. It is not because every such reform is unconstitutional,\u00a0 but because judicial independence depends as much on perception as on legal safeguards. Justice must not only be done; it must also be seen to be done. Likewise, judicial independence must not only exist but also be seen to exist.\n\nInternational experience provides ample lessons. In Hong Kong, former Chief Justice Geoffrey Ma chose to retire upon reaching the prescribed retirement age despite legislative changes that allowed extensions under certain circumstances. His decision reinforced an important principle\u2014that institutions are often strengthened not by exercising available powers but by exercising restraint.\n\nSri Lanka has struggled for decades to rebuild confidence in its democratic institutions.\n\nThe European Union, Commonwealth institutions and international legal organisations have consistently emphasised judicial independence as a cornerstone of democratic governance. While they may not directly intervene in domestic constitutional matters, developments that appear to weaken institutional safeguards often find their way into governance assessments, human rights reports and discussions on trade preferences. At a time when Sri Lanka is attempting to project itself as a stable democracy committed to reform, it would be unwise to invite unnecessary questions about its constitutional direction.\n\nThe government argues that experienced judges should be retained to reduce case backlogs and preserve institutional memory. However, extending the retirement age is a temporary administrative solution to what is fundamentally a structural problem. Judicial delays stem from procedural inefficiencies, inadequate staffing, outdated court administration, insufficient digitalisation and staff shortages."
