    "md5": "96bd81b083d16013c90bc43ec311fe15",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1133,
    "chunk_text": "The government argues that experienced judges should be retained to reduce case backlogs and preserve institutional memory. However, extending the retirement age is a temporary administrative solution to what is fundamentally a structural problem. Judicial delays stem from procedural inefficiencies, inadequate staffing, outdated court administration, insufficient digitalisation and staff shortages.\n\nThe government\u2019s overwhelming parliamentary majority provides it with the numbers to amend the Constitution. But constitutional legitimacy requires more than numerical strength. The judiciary has neither the purse nor the sword. Its authority rests entirely on public confidence in its impartiality. Once that confidence is weakened, rebuilding it becomes exceedingly difficult. Governments come and go through elections. Judges retire. Institutions, however, must endure.\n\nThis is why the government should reconsider its course. There are moments in governance when exercising power is less important than choosing not to exercise it. The judiciary is not an institution to be adjusted according to the convenience of the day."
