    "md5": "c0bdf73efae7654e4012de2d42e0a5de",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1909,
    "chunk_text": "Farmers have begun island wide protests urging your government to announce a guaranteed minimum price on paddy and reductions in the prices of fertilizer. They say the present proposed price by the government is still not sufficient and some mill owners do not purchase at the quoted rate. It was of course a welcome sight to see your Agriculture Minister K.D. Lal Kantha assuring that the government would blacklist the mill owners who do not adhere to the government rate but considering the present crisis it may be advisable to sit with the farmers and thrash out the issues. As a blow to our rice will be a blow to our stomachs.\n\nThen comes the matter of attracting large scale FDIs. Having a chat with a senior minister in your cabinet I was told that geopolitics is playing its dirty cards on the government when it comes to attracting FDIs. And I was also told that your eyeing newer markets to break away from this geopolitical issues where eventually the country benefits. But I was also told that your government is quite weak and laid back when it comes to decision making. Nothing happens swiftly and this is a huge discouragement for foreign investors whose patience Sri Lanka has anyway tested over the years. It is time you sit on the table and guide your government to attract good investments as you have already been over a year and a half in the seat. Do not send the investor from pillar to post. They will not hang around us for too long and will eventually go into other markets.\n\nThe matter of bureaucrats. Over and over I have written in this newspaper that corruption in this country has stemmed from bureaucrats. I am hearing reports that your ministers are having an issue with the bureaucrats and if this is so, the country does not lack talent. Change the system and show your power. If the bureaucratic system remains corrupt, it will eventually lead to the nation\u2019s downfall."
